window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"chant-requiem-aeternam-introit":["The chant that opens the Mass for the Dead and gives the Requiem its name: grant them eternal rest, O Lord, and let perpetual light shine upon them. Four minutes, unaccompanied, in the sixth mode — an unusually settled, low-lying melody that stays close to a single pitch and avoids leaps. The psalm verse Te decet hymnus is sung to a plain recitation formula, then the opening returns unchanged.","Funeral liturgy took shape slowly; the Mass for the Dead had no fixed form before the Carolingian reforms, and its chants were assembled from older material for a rite that grew ever more elaborate as monasteries took on the commemoration of donors. Cluny in the tenth century made prayer for the dead an industry, and the observance of All Souls' Day spread from there across Europe with these melodies attached.","Listen closer: unlike almost every other introit, this one omits the Gloria Patri doxology and any Alleluia. The liturgy strips out its own habitual expressions of praise, and the silence where they should be is doing the mourning. Note too how flat the melodic profile is — the chant refuses the rising gestures that elsewhere signal joy, circling instead within a narrow band of pitches.","Every later Requiem quotes or answers this melody. Ockeghem built his polyphonic Requiem on it phrase by phrase; Victoria did the same over a century later. From Mozart onward composers kept the words but abandoned the tune, and the modern argument is whether the concert Requiem, with soloists and orchestra, still belongs to the liturgy at all — a question Fauré answered one way and Verdi another.","Play this immediately before Ockeghem's Requiem introit and listen for the chant surfacing inside the polyphony, usually in one slow-moving voice. Then compare Fauré's setting of the same words: the mood is the same restrained one, six centuries later. Choose a monastic recording with a small group of men's voices rather than a large concert choir."],"chant-te-deum":["A long hymn of praise in prose, not verse, running some thirty lines from We praise thee, O God to a closing plea that we never be confounded. Eight minutes unaccompanied. The solemn tone builds most lines on a single repeated reciting note with a small turn at the end, so that the words move at speaking speed; a handful of phrases break into fuller melody. Two half-choirs usually alternate lines.","Legend had Ambrose and Augustine improvising it together at Augustine's baptism in 387; scholars now credit Niceta of Remesiana, a bishop in what is now Serbia, around the same years. It became the church's standard thanksgiving — sung at the end of Matins, at coronations, at the election of popes, and after military victories, a habit that persisted into the twentieth century.","Listen closer: the hymn changes genre halfway through. The first two-thirds are original prose praise; from Salvum fac populum tuum onward it is a chain of verses lifted straight from the psalms, added later and sung to a simpler formula. There are also two different chant melodies in circulation, a solemn tone and a shorter ferial one, and choirs sometimes switch between them.","Its ceremonial use made it the most politically loaded chant in Europe: a Te Deum ordered after a battle told a city who had won. Lully's version for Louis XIV and Charpentier's, whose prelude became the Eurovision theme, turned it into court spectacle, and Berlioz, Bruckner and Verdi each wrote versions that dwarf the liturgy. Scholars still debate whether the psalm verses were appended in Niceta's own century or later.","Hear a plainchant recording first so the shape is clear, then Charpentier's prelude: the fanfare is his, but the words underneath are these. Listen for the difference between the reciting lines, which rush, and the few phrases given real melody — those are the ones every later composer chose to expand."],"chant-salve-regina":["The Marian antiphon sung at the close of the day from Trinity to Advent: Hail, Queen, mother of mercy, our life, our sweetness and our hope. Five minutes in the first mode, unaccompanied. The melody opens with a rising gesture on Salve that recurs at each of the three closing invocations — O clemens, O pia, O dulcis Virgo Maria — each a step higher in urgency, ending on a phrase that falls away to nothing.","Attributed since the Middle Ages to Hermannus Contractus, the paralysed monk-astronomer of Reichenau who died in 1054, though the attribution is not secure and versions of the text circulated at Cluny and Le Puy. The Cistercians adopted it, then the Dominicans, who processed through the cloister singing it nightly; sailors sang it at sea, and Columbus's crews are recorded doing so.","Listen closer: the words describe exile and weeping — mourning and weeping in this valley of tears — yet the melody is warm and consoling throughout, never darkening. The tension between text and tune is the point; the singers are being comforted rather than made to feel their misery. Note also the simple version now used in most parishes, a sixteenth-century simplification of the far more ornate original.","It became the most set text in Catholic music after the Mass ordinary — Josquin, Lassus, Victoria, Pergolesi, Haydn, Schubert, Poulenc. The melody itself became a symbol: Poulenc gives it to the Carmelite nuns as they walk to the guillotine, the singing thinning voice by voice. Scholars now doubt the Hermannus attribution but have proposed no convincing alternative author.","Wait for the three O invocations at the end and notice how each is a fresh start, higher and slower than the last. Then hear Pergolesi's setting, and then Poulenc's scene from Dialogues des Carmélites, where the chant is sung by women being executed one at a time. Seek a recording of the long, ornate monastic version, not the parish tune."],"chant-veni-creator-spiritus":["A hymn in seven four-line stanzas invoking the Holy Spirit, sung at Pentecost and at every ordination, coronation and church council. Five minutes. The melody is compact and immediately memorable: the first phrase climbs stepwise, hangs at the top and settles back, and all seven verses use it unchanged, so the piece works by accumulation. Verses are usually alternated between two halves of the choir.","Probably written at Fulda or Mainz in the ninth century and often credited to Rabanus Maurus, Charlemagne's pupil and later archbishop. The Carolingian church needed a hymn for the moment when authority was conferred — on a bishop, on a king, on an assembly — and this one filled the role so completely that it has been sung at almost every papal conclave since.","Listen closer: the tune is not new. It is adapted from an older Pentecost melody, and its stepwise opening was so recognisable that Mahler could quote it as a public symbol. In his Eighth Symphony the first movement sets this Latin hymn entire, at enormous volume, and then binds it to the closing scene of Goethe's Faust — a ninth-century ordination chant made to carry a nineteenth-century idea of salvation.","Luther's German version, Komm, Gott Schöpfer, carried it into the Protestant chorale repertory, and Bach set the resulting tune for organ. The chant's ubiquity at ceremonies of power has drawn comment: it is invoked to sanction whatever follows, and its serene melody has accompanied coronations and inquisitions alike. Authorship remains open; Rabanus Maurus is a plausible but unproven candidate.","Learn the first phrase — it takes one hearing — then listen for it returning verse after verse, and notice how the meaning shifts as the words change beneath the same notes. Follow it with the opening minutes of Mahler's Eighth Symphony, where hundreds of voices hurl the same syllables at you. Any good monastic recording will do."],"chant-ut-queant-laxis":["A hymn for the feast of John the Baptist, four minutes long, in stanzas of three long lines and a short one. Its fame rests on the first verse. Each of the six half-lines begins one step higher than the last, so the opening syllables — Ut, Re, Mi, Fa, Sol, La — climb a six-note ladder in order. Sung plainly, it sounds like an unremarkable hymn until you notice the melody is a scale being spelled out.","The text is attributed to Paul the Deacon, a Lombard historian at Charlemagne's court. Around 1030 Guido of Arezzo, a Benedictine teaching choirboys and frustrated by the years it took to learn chant by rote, seized on this hymn as a mnemonic: sing the verse, and you have memorised the intervals. Guido also drew the staff of lines that made pitch visible, and claimed his pupils could now learn in months what had taken a decade.","Listen closer: Guido almost certainly adjusted the melody to make the trick work, so the tune we sing may be a teaching aid disguised as a hymn rather than the reverse. Ut later became Do, softer to sing, and Si — later Ti — was assembled from the initials of the last line, Sancte Iohannes. The whole of Western solfège, and the do-re-mi of every music lesson since, is the fossil of this one Italian monk's classroom shortcut.","Guido's letter to the monk Michael describing the method survives and is one of the most quoted documents in music history. Scholars debate how much of the hexachord system was his invention and how much he codified from existing practice, and whether the famous melody predates him at all. What is not disputed is the consequence: notated pitch and sight-singing made a repertory transmissible without a teacher's voice.","Sing along with the first stanza and stop after each half-line — you will hear the ladder. Then listen to any chant recording afterwards and notice that you can now anticipate where the melody will land. Seek a recording that includes a spoken or sung demonstration of the hexachord; several early-music groups pair the hymn with Guido's teaching exercises."],"chant-pange-lingua-gloriosi":["Six stanzas in praise of the Eucharist, sung in procession on Corpus Christi and, in its last two verses, at Benediction throughout the year. Five minutes unaccompanied. The melody is in the third mode and unusually wide-ranging for a hymn, with a distinctive falling opening on Pange that every Renaissance composer who used it kept intact. The final stanzas, beginning Tantum ergo, are the best-known music in Catholic devotion after the Ave Maria.","Thomas Aquinas wrote the text in 1264, when Urban IV instituted the feast of Corpus Christi and asked for an office for it. The hymn belongs to a moment when the consecrated host had become the central object of lay devotion, carried through streets under a canopy. Aquinas modelled his opening line on a sixth-century passion hymn by Venantius Fortunatus, borrowing its first three words outright.","Listen closer: the melody is probably not Frankish at all. Its shape resembles Mozarabic chant from Spain, and it may have entered the Roman books through Aquitaine, which would make this the one widely sung survivor of a suppressed Iberian tradition. Josquin took the tune and built an entire Mass from it, letting the chant migrate between voices instead of parking it in the tenor — the technique that ended the old cantus firmus mass.","Josquin's Missa Pange lingua, probably his last Mass, made this melody the most consequential hymn tune of the Renaissance; Liszt and Kodály returned to it centuries later. Aquinas's authorship is well documented, unusually for a medieval hymn, but the melody's origins remain contested — the Mozarabic hypothesis is attractive and unproven. Vatican II's reforms reduced its liturgical use without denting its popularity.","Listen to the chant once, then Josquin's Kyrie, and try to follow the falling opening figure as it passes from voice to voice — it is never in one place for long. Compare the last two stanzas, Tantum ergo, in their chant form and then in a nineteenth-century harmonised setting to hear what tonality does to a modal tune."],"chant-o-antiphons":["Seven short antiphons, one for each evening from 17 to 23 December, sung around the Magnificat at Vespers. Each opens with a held exclamation — O Wisdom, O Root of Jesse, O Key of David, O Dayspring — names the coming Christ in an Old Testament image, and ends with the imperative come. About twelve minutes for the set. All seven share the same second-mode melodic frame, so they sound like seven variations on one gesture of longing.","They were in use by the eighth century and were sung with ceremony: at Fleury and elsewhere, a different officer of the monastery intoned each evening's antiphon, the abbot taking one, the cellarer another, and each was expected to provide extra wine or food for his brethren afterwards. The days chosen are the darkest of the northern year, immediately before Christmas.","Listen closer: take the first letter of each title in reverse order — Emmanuel, Rex, Oriens, Clavis, Radix, Adonai, Sapientia — and they spell ERO CRAS, Latin for tomorrow I shall be. Whether the acrostic was designed or discovered is unknown, but the sequence is fixed and the pun lands exactly on Christmas Eve. The familiar hymn O Come, O Come, Emmanuel is a nineteenth-century verse translation of these seven texts.","Their liturgical position never wavered, and they survived the Reformation in translation and Vatican II intact. Musicologists note that the melodic uniformity is unusual for antiphons of such prominence and may indicate a single author or a deliberate cycle. Arvo Pärt set all seven in German as the Sieben Magnificat-Antiphonen, keeping the modal austerity and the sense of seven approaches to one idea.","Hear them as a set in one sitting rather than singly, and listen for the same opening gesture returning seven times with different words. Then play Pärt's Magnificat antiphons and notice how little he had to add. A recording by a monastic community that sings them at the right time of year carries the accumulating impatience the cycle is built on."],"chant-exsultet":["A nine-minute solo chant, sung by a deacon in darkness beside the newly lit paschal candle at the Easter Vigil. Let the angelic hosts of heaven exult, it begins, and it goes on to address the night itself. Long stretches move on a single reciting pitch in the manner of a preface tone, broken by flowering cadences; the deacon is alone, unaccompanied, with a congregation holding candles. The most theatrical piece of solo singing the liturgy contains.","Its text may go back to the fourth century; Augustine and Jerome both mention such a praise of the candle, and Ambrose has been proposed as author. In southern Italy from the tenth century it was sung from illuminated Exsultet rolls, parchment scrolls that the deacon let fall over the front of the ambo as he sang, so the congregation saw the pictures — printed upside down relative to his text — unroll in time with the words.","Listen closer for the line O felix culpa, O happy fault, that earned so great a Redeemer — a sentence that calls Adam's sin fortunate, and which theologians have argued over ever since. Hear too the passage praising the bees whose labour made the candle: an agricultural digression left standing in the middle of the year's most solemn proclamation, trimmed in some later revisions and restored in others.","The Exsultet rolls are among the great objects of medieval art and the clearest evidence we have of how chant was performed for an audience rather than merely sung. Liturgical revisions have repeatedly shortened the text, and the bee passage in particular has been cut and reinstated more than once. The Beneventan melody differs markedly from the Roman one, a reminder of how much local variety the standard books erased.","Listen in the dark, which is how it is meant to work. Follow the alternation between fast recitation and the moments where the deacon's line blossoms — those flowerings mark the sentences the text cares most about. Seek a recording of the older Beneventan version alongside the standard Roman one; the difference between them is the whole story of chant transmission in miniature."],"chant-dies-irae":["The sequence of the Mass for the Dead: nineteen three-line stanzas on the day of wrath that will dissolve the world in ashes. Seven minutes. The melody is built from three tunes used in rotation, each stanza taking one, so the same phrases circle back relentlessly. The opening eight notes — a falling step, a rise, a fall — are the most quoted in Western music. The last stanzas break the pattern and end on the word Amen.","Attributed to Thomas of Celano, the Franciscan who wrote the first life of Saint Francis, and datable to the mid-thirteenth century, when preaching about the Last Judgement was a mass phenomenon and Franciscan friars carried it into every Italian town square. The Council of Trent, which cut almost every sequence from the liturgy, kept this one; it stayed in the Requiem Mass until the reforms of 1969-70 removed it.","Listen closer: the first four notes alone are enough to signal death to a listener who has never heard the chant. Berlioz brings the whole melody in on tubas at the climax of the Symphonie fantastique and then speeds it up into a jig; Liszt built the Totentanz on it; Saint-Saëns, Rachmaninoff, Shostakovich and dozens of film composers followed. It is the closest thing Western music has to a fixed symbol with an agreed meaning.","Removed from the modern Catholic funeral for its terrifying theology, it survives entirely as a musical quotation, which is a strange fate for a liturgical text. Scholars question the Celano attribution and note that the text may adapt an older Italian trope on the Libera me responsory. Its melody's afterlife in horror film scores has arguably made it better known now than at any point when it was actually sung in church.","Learn the opening four notes, then go hunting: the Witches' Sabbath of Berlioz's Symphonie fantastique, Liszt's Totentanz, Saint-Saëns's Danse macabre. You will start hearing it everywhere. Hear the plain chant version first, sung slowly by men's voices, so you know what all those composers were distorting."],"chant-stabat-mater":["A sequence in twenty stanzas contemplating Mary standing at the cross, and asking to share her grief. Eight minutes. Like other sequences it pairs its stanzas, each pair sharing a melody, and the tune stays low and narrow, with a rocking figure at the start of each line that never resolves into anything triumphant. The rhyme scheme is insistent and the Latin unusually simple, which is why it translates and sets so easily.","Probably written around 1300 and associated with Jacopone da Todi, the Franciscan poet who abandoned law after his wife was killed in an accident. It belongs to the affective devotion the Franciscans spread across Europe: not doctrine but shared feeling, the believer invited to stand beside Mary and weep. It entered the Roman Missal only in 1727, long after the polyphonic settings had made it famous.","Listen closer: the poem never describes the crucifixion. Christ is barely present as a body; every image is of Mary looking, and of the singer asking to be allowed to look with her. That displacement — grief observed rather than suffering depicted — is what gives composers from Josquin to Pärt something to do, and why the settings tend to be intimate rather than monumental.","Trent cut it from the liturgy along with almost all sequences; it came back two centuries later, by which point Pergolesi's setting for two voices and strings, written as he was dying at twenty-six, had become the most frequently printed piece of music of the eighteenth century. More than two hundred composers have set it. Jacopone's authorship is traditional rather than documented.","Hear the chant first for the plainness of it, then Pergolesi's opening duet, where two voices grind against each other in suspensions that sound like sobbing. Then jump to Rossini for the operatic version of the same grief. Listen for what every setting keeps: the paired stanzas, and the refusal to describe anything but a mother watching."],"chant-quem-quaeritis-trope":["Three minutes of chant that are also, arguably, the first scene in European theatre. An angel asks the women approaching the tomb whom they seek; they answer, Jesus of Nazareth; the angel replies, He is not here, he is risen. Four short exchanges, sung by soloists rather than choir, originally attached to the beginning of the Easter Mass introit. The music is simple recitation with a rising question and a falling answer.","It appears in tropers from Limoges and St Gall in the early tenth century as an ornament to the introit. Within a century it had migrated to the end of Easter Matins and acquired stage directions: the Regularis Concordia, drawn up at Winchester around 970, tells monks to sit at the sepulchre in an alb, hold a palm, and speak in a soft voice — the earliest surviving European production notes.","Listen closer to what the words demand: an exchange between characters, in the present tense, with one singer pretending to be someone else. Nothing in the liturgy before this asks a monk to impersonate. Everything downstream — the Visitatio Sepulchri plays, the Passion plays, Hildegard's Ordo Virtutum, and eventually opera — depends on that small transgression, made inside a church by men who were not thinking of theatre at all.","Whether the trope caused liturgical drama or merely records a habit already in existence was fiercely argued through the twentieth century; Karl Young's account of a linear evolution from trope to play has been heavily qualified since. Its priority over other dialogue tropes is also disputed. What survives unchallenged is the Regularis Concordia's staging instructions, unambiguous evidence of enacted performance by 970.","Follow the four lines as a conversation and notice how the angel's question rises and the answer falls — the melodic shape is doing the acting. Then hear Hildegard's Ordo Virtutum, sung drama two centuries later, and consider what grew from thirty seconds of dialogue. Look for recordings that reconstruct the Winchester staging with separated singers."],"chant-kyrie-cunctipotens-genitor-deus":["A setting of the ninefold Kyrie eleison — Lord have mercy, Christ have mercy, Lord have mercy — with Latin verses inserted between the Greek acclamations, addressing God as almighty Father. Four minutes, sung in alternation. The melody is one of the most memorable in the chant books: a wide arching first phrase, repeated in threes, with the final Kyrie extended. It survives in the modern books as Kyrie IV, stripped of its added words.","Tropes — words and music inserted into existing chants — flourished in the tenth and eleventh centuries at St Gall, Winchester and above all in Aquitaine, where monks treated the standard liturgy as a frame to be decorated. The practice let local communities put their own poetry inside the universal rite. Trent abolished tropes in the sixteenth century, which is why the surviving Kyrie carries a name that no longer refers to anything sung.","Listen closer: the title is a ghost. Cunctipotens genitor Deus are the first words of the trope, deleted centuries ago, and the melody kept the label of the text it lost. The same tune then became a favourite skeleton for early polyphony — the organum settings in the Codex Calixtinus and the Aquitanian sources are built on it, and Guillaume de Machaut's Mass Kyrie uses this melody as its foundation.","Nineteenth-century restorers regarded tropes as corruptions of a pure Gregorian original and were glad they had been removed; scholarship since the 1970s has reversed that judgement, treating them as evidence of how living the repertory was. Reconstructing them means reuniting texts and melodies from separate manuscripts, and performances that restore the trope words remain relatively rare.","Hear the modern Kyrie IV first, then a recording that restores the troped words, and notice how the added syllables fill the long melismas — the melody suddenly explains itself. Then listen to Machaut's Kyrie, where this tune moves in enormous slow notes beneath three faster voices."],"notker-sancti-spiritus-assit":["A Pentecost sequence in paired verses: each melodic phrase is sung twice, to different words, by alternating halves of the choir, with single unpaired verses at the beginning and end. Six minutes. Because every syllable takes one note, the text moves briskly and clearly, quite unlike the long melismas of the chants around it. The effect is of a lucid, almost conversational hymn wedged into an elaborate liturgy.","Notker Balbulus — Notker the Stammerer — was a monk of St Gall who wrote in his preface that a refugee from the sacked abbey of Jumièges arrived carrying a book in which words had been fitted to the long Alleluia melismas, and that he took up the idea and improved it. His Liber hymnorum of the 880s, dedicated to Bishop Liutward, is the first named collection of sequences in European music.","Listen closer to the double-verse structure: it is a mnemonic device. Notker says outright that he wrote syllabic texts because he could not remember the wordless melismas, and that giving each note a syllable fixed them in his memory. A vast repertory of medieval poetry exists because one monk had a bad memory and wrote down his workaround.","How far Notker composed melodies rather than fitting words to existing ones is a long-running question; many of his sequences carry French melody names, supporting his own account of borrowing. His preface is one of the very few first-person statements by a ninth-century musician and is treated with corresponding care — and corresponding suspicion, since it also serves to justify a novelty. Trent later swept nearly all sequences away.","Count the repetitions: phrase, same phrase again with new words, then a new phrase twice. Once you hear the pairing, the whole form is transparent. Compare with the wordless Easter Alleluia jubilus to see what Notker was solving, then with Wipo's Victimae paschali laudes for the same technique a century and a half later."],"wipo-victimae-paschali-laudes":["The sequence of Easter Sunday, four minutes long, in paired verses. Halfway through it turns into dialogue: the choir asks Mary Magdalene what she saw on the road, and she answers in the first person — I saw the tomb of the living Christ, and the glory of his rising. Angels, the shroud, the empty tomb. The melody is bright and mostly one note per syllable, ending with a short Alleluia and Amen.","Attributed to Wipo of Burgundy, chaplain and biographer at the court of the emperors Conrad II and Henry III, writing around 1040. It is one of only four sequences the Council of Trent spared from the great cull of 1570, alongside the Dies irae, the Veni Sancte Spiritus and the Lauda Sion — a measure of how thoroughly it had lodged itself in the Easter of every Latin church.","Listen closer to the questions put to Mary. This is the Quem quaeritis dialogue grown up: a sung interrogation of a named character who answers in her own voice, inside the Mass rather than appended to it. One stanza, accusing the Jews of lying about the resurrection, was removed in 1570 and again after the Second Vatican Council — the chant carries the scar of its own reception.","Its dialogue form fed directly into the vernacular Easter plays of Germany, and Luther adapted it as the chorale Christ lag in Todesbanden, which Bach then used for an early cantata — a chain running from an imperial chaplain to Leipzig in 1707. Wipo's authorship rests on a single later ascription and is probable rather than certain.","Wait for the moment the texture changes from statement to question and back — that is where the drama sits, and it happens without any change of scenery. Then listen to Bach's Christ lag in Todesbanden and try to hear this melody's bones inside Luther's chorale. Small vocal ensembles serve it better than large choirs."],"ambrosian-aeterne-rerum-conditor":["A hymn for daybreak in eight four-line stanzas of short, regular verse — eternal maker of things, ruler of night and day — with the cock's crow as its central image. Five minutes. In its Milanese form the melody is more florid and wider-ranging than Roman chant of comparable function, with decorative turns at the ends of phrases. All stanzas share one tune, and the regular metre gives it a swing that Gregorian hymns rarely have.","Ambrose, bishop of Milan, introduced congregational hymn singing during the siege of his basilica in 386, when his people occupied the church to prevent its surrender to the Arian court and sang through the night to keep their spirits up. Augustine, present in Milan and not yet baptised, describes weeping at the sound. This is among the four hymns securely attributed to Ambrose himself.","Listen closer: the Milanese repertory is not a variant of Gregorian chant but a separate tradition that survived alongside it, protected by civic pride and papal indulgence, and still sung in Milan today. Its melodies are generally longer and more ornate, and its liturgy keeps items Rome discarded. When Charlemagne imposed Roman chant across his empire, Milan was the one great see that refused and was allowed to.","That Ambrose wrote hymns is beyond doubt; how much of the surviving Ambrosian melodic repertory dates from his lifetime is not, since the earliest notation is some six centuries later. Nineteenth-century scholars treated the tradition as a corrupt cousin of Gregorian chant. Modern comparative work reads it instead as an independent witness to what pre-Carolingian Western singing may have sounded like.","Play a Milanese recording beside a Roman hymn of the same function and listen for the extra curl at the end of each phrase. Notice the metre — this is verse with a beat, closer to song than to the free-flowing Roman chants. Ensembles specialising in the Milanese books, rather than general chant choirs, make the distinctness audible."],"old-roman-haec-dies":["The Easter Sunday gradual — this is the day the Lord has made, let us rejoice and be glad in it — in the version sung by the Roman basilicas rather than the one Charlemagne's empire adopted. Six minutes. The words and the broad melodic outline match the familiar Gregorian setting, but the surface is different: many more small notes, constant stepwise oscillation around each structural pitch, giving a rippling, almost tremulous sound.","Five manuscripts, all from Rome and none earlier than 1071, preserve a chant repertory that the city sang while the rest of Europe sang something else. The likeliest explanation is that Frankish singers received Roman chant in the eighth century, reshaped it in memory and in transmission, and sent the result back as the authoritative version; Rome's own singing survived only until the thirteenth century, when the imported Gregorian books displaced it at home.","Listen closer to the ornamental oscillation. Where the Gregorian version has one note, the Old Roman often has three circling around it, which suggests an oral practice of elaboration that Frankish notators either did not hear or deliberately smoothed. Whether the plainer Gregorian version is the earlier or the later of the two is the single hardest question in chant scholarship.","The relationship between the two repertories has been argued since Bruno Stäblein identified the Roman manuscripts as a distinct tradition in the 1950s. Positions range from Frankish embellishment of a simple Roman original to Roman elaboration of a shared archetype. The debate matters beyond chant, because it is a test case for what happens to music transmitted for two centuries without notation.","Listen to the Gregorian Haec dies first, then this, with the same words in front of you — the alignment makes the ornamental difference unmissable. Ensembles such as those directed by Marcel Pérès, who sing the Roman version with an ornamented, quasi-Eastern delivery, make the case audibly; other groups sing it plainer, and the contrast between interpretations is itself instructive."],"mozarabic-chant-old-hispanic-office":["The surviving music of the Iberian church before Rome replaced it: threni and preces for Lent, soni and laudes for the Office, sung unaccompanied and preserved in books such as the León Antiphoner of around 1069. Half an hour of it in a typical modern programme. The chants are long, heavily ornamented, and built on repeated refrains — the preces in particular return to a short congregational response after each verse, giving a litany-like insistence unknown in Roman chant.","The Visigothic church developed its own rite and music, and it continued under Muslim rule among the Christians the Arabs called musta'rib, arabised. In 1080 the Council of Burgos suppressed it in favour of the Roman rite; Cardinal Cisneros rescued a fragment for a chapel in Toledo Cathedral in 1500, where a version has been sung ever since, and six Toledo parishes preserved it into the modern era.","Listen closer, and be aware of what you cannot hear: almost all of this music is written in neumes that show melodic shape but not pitch, and no later pitched source exists to decode them. Only a couple of dozen melodies survive in readable form, transferred into Aquitanian notation. Everything else is a drawing of a melody nobody can now sing — one of the largest bodies of unrecoverable music in Europe.","Twentieth-century recordings of Mozarabic chant mostly present the Cisneros-era Toledo material, which is a Renaissance reconstruction rather than a medieval survival, and this is not always stated. Recent scholarship, notably the work on Old Hispanic notation led by Emma Hornby and Rebecca Maloy, extracts real structural information from the unpitched neumes without claiming to recover the tunes. The Pange lingua melody is often cited as a possible refugee from this tradition.","Approach it as an archaeology as much as a concert. Listen for the refrain structure of the preces, where a short phrase comes back after every verse — the one feature that transmits reliably. Compare with Gregorian chant of the same liturgical function and hear how much longer and more decorated the Iberian versions are. Choose recordings that say which sources they used."],"byzantine-akathistos-hymn":["A vast Marian hymn in twenty-four stanzas, one for each letter of the Greek alphabet in order, sung standing — akathistos means not seated. An hour in full. Twelve of the stanzas end in a cascade of salutations, each beginning Hail, piling image upon image; the other twelve close with a single Alleluia. In Byzantine performance a soloist chants the melody while other singers hold a continuous drone beneath, an unmoving low note under everything.","Traditionally sung in Constantinople in 626 to thank the Virgin for lifting the Avar and Persian siege, though the text is probably older and has been attributed to Romanos the Melodist among others. It is the central devotional hymn of the Orthodox year, sung in sections across the Fridays of Lent and complete on the fifth Saturday, and it passed into Slavic and Romanian practice with the liturgy.","Listen closer to the drone, called the ison. It is not harmony in the Western sense but a fixed reference pitch, and Byzantine theory treats the melody as moving in relation to it through intervals smaller than the Western semitone. When the ison shifts, the whole modal ground moves under your feet. Note too the acrostic: the twenty-four stanza openings run straight through the Greek alphabet.","Byzantine chant was long treated in the West as a frozen archaism; work on the medieval Middle Byzantine notation, which is decipherable, showed instead a repertory with its own history of composition and revision. A live argument concerns whether the ison is medieval or a later addition, since the notation records only the melody. The nineteenth-century Chrysanthine reform reshaped modern practice considerably.","Do not attempt the whole hour first time. Take one stanza with the Hail salutations and let the accumulation work on you, listening for the drone and how the melody circles above it. Then compare a Greek recording with a Russian one to hear how differently the same tradition developed. Recordings by Greek cantors preserve the microtones that choral arrangements smooth away."],"byzantine-cherubic-hymn":["The hymn sung at the Great Entrance of the Orthodox Divine Liturgy, as the bread and wine are carried through the church: we who mystically represent the cherubim now lay aside all earthly care. Ten minutes or more, because the melody has to last as long as the procession. A soloist spins an extremely slow, heavily ornamented line above a held drone; the text dissolves into extended vocalisation on single syllables while the clergy move.","Introduced into the liturgy of Constantinople under the emperor Justin II around 574, at a period when the ceremonial of the Great Church was being expanded into imperial spectacle inside Hagia Sophia. The hymn's function is architectural: it fills the time and space of a procession, and its length is determined by how far the deacons have to walk, which is why settings in large cathedrals grew so elaborate.","Listen closer for the kratemata — passages sung to meaningless syllables such as te-ri-rem, inserted when the words run out before the procession does. Byzantine composers treated these as compositional opportunities, and whole virtuoso genres grew from them. It is the Eastern equivalent of the Western jubilus: wordless singing given a liturgical justification, and then developed for its own sake.","Fourteenth-century composers of the so-called kalophonic or beautiful-sounding style, Ioannes Koukouzeles above all, elaborated the Cherubikon to lengths critics then and now have called excessive. Slavic churches later replaced the melodies with harmonised settings, and Tchaikovsky and Rachmaninoff wrote their own; whether such polyphony belongs in Orthodox worship at all remains contested among traditionalists.","Listen for the moment the words stop and the singer keeps going on nonsense syllables — that is the piece opening up. Follow the drone and notice how the melody keeps leaning away from it and returning. Compare a Greek cantor's version with Rachmaninoff's Vespers to hear the same tradition on two sides of a stylistic gulf."],"romanos-kontakion-nativity":["A sung sermon in twenty-four stanzas, opening: today the Virgin gives birth to him who is above all being, and the earth offers a cave to the unapproachable. Twenty minutes complete, though modern liturgy uses only the first stanza. A soloist delivers the narrative; the congregation answers each stanza with the same short refrain. Long stretches are dialogue — Mary speaks to the Magi, the Magi answer — carried on a flexible chanted line above a drone.","Romanos came from Emesa in Syria, probably of Jewish family, and worked in Constantinople under Justinian in the years the Hagia Sophia was being built. He wrote in Greek but in a metrical and rhetorical manner rooted in Syriac verse homilies, and legend has the Virgin appearing to him with a scroll and telling him to eat it. Around sixty of his kontakia survive.","Listen closer to the refrain. The same line returns after every stanza, so a congregation with no book can participate in an hour-long theological narrative simply by waiting for its cue. The technique is the same one Notker would arrive at independently three centuries later in the Latin West: put words to music people can hold in memory, and complicated doctrine becomes singable.","Romanos is the outstanding poet of Byzantine hymnography, and the kontakion's eclipse by the shorter kanon form in the eighth century is often treated as a loss. The music is a harder case: the earliest notated sources are four centuries after his death, so the melodies we sing are Middle Byzantine reconstructions of a tradition, not his own settings, and performances differ accordingly.","Listen for the refrain and start joining in mentally — that is how the form is designed to work. Take the Magi's dialogue section, where the piece turns into drama, and compare it with the Latin Quem quaeritis a few centuries later. Recordings by Greek ensembles that keep the drone and the microtonal inflections serve it far better than choral arrangements."],"kassia-hymn-of-kassiani":["A hymn for the woman who anointed Christ's feet, sung once a year at the service of Holy Wednesday: Lord, the woman fallen into many sins, perceiving thy divinity, takes up the myrrh-bearer's office. Eight minutes and often much longer in practice. It is chanted by a soloist in the fourth plagal mode over a drone, extremely slowly, with the phrase asking Christ not to despise his handmaid drawn out at extraordinary length.","Kassia was a Constantinopolitan noblewoman who, according to a much-repeated story, was a candidate at a bride-show for the emperor Theophilos, answered his misogynist remark about Eve too sharply, and was passed over; she founded a convent instead and wrote hymns. She is the earliest named female composer whose music survives in a form that can be performed, and around fifty of her hymns are preserved.","Listen closer to how the text is voiced: for most of its length the hymn speaks in the sinful woman's own first person, and a male cantor in an Orthodox church sings those words as her. Nothing in Byzantine practice is more direct about crossing that line. Whether Kassia's melody survives unchanged is another matter — the version now sung carries centuries of elaboration on top of hers.","The Holy Wednesday chanting of this hymn draws crowds in Greece who attend for it alone, and cantors are judged on their handling of it, which has given it an unusual status somewhere between liturgy and recital. Scholars are cautious about the biographical legends, which come from later chroniclers, but her authorship of a substantial body of hymns is supported by the manuscript tradition.","Listen to a single cantor rather than a choir, and give it time — the piece works by refusing to hurry. Wait for the closing plea and notice how the singer stretches it. Then hear Hildegard, writing three centuries later at the other end of Europe, and consider the two of them as the earliest named women whose music we can still sing."],"musica-enchiriadis-rex-caeli-domine":["Three minutes of the earliest written-down polyphony in Europe: a hymn melody sung by one voice while a second moves alongside it, mostly a fixed distance below, note against note. In the treatise's fuller example each of the two lines is doubled at the octave, making four parts moving in parallel. There is no independence between the voices — they are the same tune at two pitches, meeting at the beginnings and ends of phrases.","The Musica enchiriadis, a Frankish teaching manual of around 890, sets out the rules of this practice as something choirs already did, and gives worked examples in a special pitch notation of its own devising. It belongs to the same Carolingian project as the standardisation of chant: a culture writing down what it had previously done from memory, and in the act of writing it down, making it teachable and improvable.","Listen closer to the places where the lower voice will not move. Strict parallel motion at a fourth produces an interval the treatise's author considered unusable, so at those points the added voice simply stops on a pitch and waits for the melody to come back to it. That refusal is the first crack in parallelism, and the whole later history of independent voices comes through it.","It has been argued that the treatise describes an improvised practice far older than 890 and that its notated examples are illustrations rather than compositions — a fair point that does not change the significance of the document. What is unchallenged is priority: this is where the West first writes down more than one note at a time, and every subsequent European decision about harmony descends from it.","Play a plainchant hymn first, then this, and hear how little has been added and how much it changes. Listen for the moments where the two voices converge onto the same note at a phrase-end — that is the piece's only real event. Then jump straight to Pérotin to hear where two centuries took the idea."],"winchester-troper-organa":["The largest collection of early polyphony to survive from anywhere: some 174 two-voice pieces copied at Winchester around the year 1000, settings of tropes, alleluias, responsories and the Kyrie. Where they can be performed at all, the added voice weaves above and below the chant instead of tracking it in parallel, crossing it freely and arriving at unisons and octaves at cadences. A modern programme might offer forty minutes of reconstructions.","Winchester was the powerhouse of the English Benedictine reform under Bishop Æthelwold, the community that produced the Regularis Concordia and installed a famous organ in the cathedral. The manuscript belongs to a monastery deliberately building the most elaborate liturgy in the country, in a kingdom that had recently unified and wanted the ceremonial to prove it.","Listen closer, and know that you are hearing informed guesswork. The organa are written in unheighted neumes that indicate whether the voice rises or falls but not by how much, and the chant they accompany is often notated elsewhere or not at all. Scholars reconstruct the added voice by matching it against known chants and the intervallic rules of contemporary treatises; different editors produce audibly different music.","Long dismissed as unperformable, the repertory was opened up by Andreas Holschneider's work in the 1960s and then by Susan Rankin's, whose editions and facsimile made systematic reconstruction possible. Each performance is an argument about method as much as a piece of music, and ensembles are increasingly explicit about that. The manuscript remains the single most important source for polyphony before Notre-Dame.","Listen to two different reconstructions of the same item back to back — the disagreement is the most interesting thing about the repertory and tells you exactly where the evidence stops. Follow the added voice as it crosses above and below the chant, which is the clearest advance on the parallel organum of a century earlier."],"aquitanian-jubilemus-exultemus":["A Christmas versus for two voices, five minutes long, from the manuscripts associated with the abbey of St Martial at Limoges. It alternates two textures: passages where both voices move together syllable by syllable, and passages where the lower voice holds a note for a long time while the upper one runs above it in free, wandering figures. That second texture — one voice sustaining, one decorating — is new, and it is the sound of the next two centuries arriving.","Aquitaine in the eleventh and twelfth centuries was rich, cosmopolitan and musically inventive, the same soil that produced the troubadours. The versus was a semi-liturgical Latin song, sung at the great feasts and outside the fixed liturgy, which gave composers a freedom the Mass did not. Four Aquitanian manuscripts, once ascribed wholesale to St Martial, preserve the repertory; their actual origins are now thought to be more scattered.","Listen closer to the long-held notes in the lower voice. They are taken from chant, but stretched so far out of shape that the borrowed melody stops being recognisable as melody and becomes a foundation instead. Notre-Dame organum a century later does exactly this, at greater length and with more voices above. The rhythm of the upper voice is not notated, so every performance is a hypothesis.","The St Martial attribution has been substantially revised — the abbey collected the manuscripts rather than generating all the music — and scholars now speak of Aquitanian polyphony instead. The unsolved problem is rhythm: nothing in the notation specifies duration, and interpretations range from free declamation following the words to strict modal patterns imported backwards from Notre-Dame practice.","Wait for the switch between the two textures, which happens several times and is unmistakable once you know to expect it. Then listen to Léonin, where the sustaining voice becomes a drone lasting whole minutes. Compare two recordings with different rhythmic solutions to hear how much interpretive freedom the notation leaves."],"calixtinus-congaudeant-catholici":["A three-minute Latin song in praise of Saint James, notable for one thing: the manuscript writes three separate voice parts, in red and black ink, on the same staff. If all three are sung together it is the earliest surviving three-voice piece in Western music. The style is a bouncing, dance-like conductus with a refrain, and the voices move largely note against note, meeting on open-sounding intervals at the ends of lines.","The Codex Calixtinus, compiled around 1150, is the pilgrim's book of Santiago de Compostela: liturgy, sermons, miracle stories, an account of Charlemagne, and a practical guide to the road, complete with warnings about the water. Its music section gathers polyphony for the saint's feasts, several pieces ascribed in the manuscript to French composers, and this one to a Magister Albertus of Paris.","Listen closer, or rather read the argument: it is not certain the three voices were meant to sound at once. Some scholars hold that the red middle voice is an alternative to one of the others, offered as a choice rather than a third part, and that performing all three produces harshness no twelfth-century singer would have accepted. Recordings exist in both versions, and they are strikingly different pieces.","The claim to be the first three-voice composition has made it famous beyond its musical weight, and the dispute over whether the third voice is real remains open. The Calixtinus attributions to named Parisian musicians are also debated, though they support the picture of a repertory travelling the pilgrim roads from northern France to Galicia rather than growing locally.","Find a recording that performs all three voices and one that performs two, and decide for yourself which sounds like music a twelfth-century ear would accept. Listen for the refrain, which returns often and is the easiest thing to hold on to. Then hear Pérotin's four-voice organum, fifty years later, for the answer to the question this piece asks."],"calixtinus-dum-pater-familias":["The pilgrim hymn that closes the Codex Calixtinus: a single-line song in praise of Saint James with a refrain that mixes Latin and a cry in another tongue — Herru Sanctiagu, got Sanctiagu, e ultreia, e suseia, deus adiuva nos. Four minutes, strongly rhythmic, with short repeated phrases built for walking and for people who cannot read. The verses list the apostle's virtues; the refrain simply shouts onward and upward.","Santiago de Compostela was the third great pilgrimage of Christendom after Jerusalem and Rome, and hundreds of thousands walked the roads across France and northern Spain in the twelfth century. The codex was compiled for that traffic. The refrain's mixture of Latin with what appears to be German and Occitan reflects who was on the road: this is a song for a crowd with no common language.","Listen closer to the words ultreia and suseia. They are not standard Latin, and their exact meaning is uncertain — roughly further on and higher up, a walking cry rather than a prayer. Modern pilgrims on the Camino greet each other with ultreia still, having taken it back from the manuscript. The melody is monophonic in a manuscript otherwise notable for its polyphony, which suggests it was recorded because it was popular rather than because it was learned.","It is the best known piece in the codex among non-specialists, largely through its adoption by twentieth-century pilgrims and early-music groups, who often add drums and drones that the source does not specify. Purists object; others note that a walking song without a beat is not a walking song. The macaronic refrain has attracted linguistic study out of proportion to its four lines.","Play it while walking and the point becomes obvious — the phrase lengths match a stride. Listen for the refrain and join in on ultreia. Then compare a plain unaccompanied performance with a percussion-heavy one and consider what each is claiming about how medieval laypeople sang. Pair it with Congaudeant catholici from the same manuscript for the learned and popular sides of one book."],"bernart-de-ventadorn-can-vei-la-lauzeta":["Eight stanzas of Occitan verse sung to one melody that repeats unchanged for each, with a vielle or harp filling the gaps between verses; about seven minutes. The tune climbs through its first half and sinks back through its second, an arch that traces the opening image: a lark beating its wings up into the sunlight, then letting itself drop. A short closing stanza addressed to a hidden name dismisses love altogether and stops the song mid-thought.","Bernart was probably born a servant at the castle of Ventadorn in the Limousin, and made a living moving between courts, among them those of Eleanor of Aquitaine's family. The song belongs to the middle of the twelfth century, when Occitan poets were inventing the idea that unsatisfied desire refines the man who suffers it. Aristocratic households wanted love songs of exactly this kind; churchmen deplored them, which did the singers no harm at all.","It is copied in more manuscripts than any other troubadour song, and its melody was borrowed for fresh texts in French and Latin, the medieval equivalent of a cover version. Listen also to what the notation cannot tell you: the sources give pitches but no rhythm whatever. Every performance has silently decided, on no manuscript authority, whether the song dances in a strict beat or drifts in the rhythm of speech.","The song was a classic within a generation, quoted and imitated across Europe. Modern argument is almost entirely about rhythm. An older school sang troubadour melodies in strict dance metres derived from the poetic feet; performers since the 1970s have preferred free, speech-shaped declamation. Neither can be proved from the sources, and recordings of the two persuasions can sound like different pieces of music altogether.","Hear it once with a single unaccompanied voice, so the arch of the melody is bare, then with instruments and a drone. Follow a translation stanza by stanza and notice how the same tune has to carry hope, accusation and resignation in turn. Recordings by Paul Hillier and by the Studio der frühen Musik make the interpretive gulf audible."],"jaufre-rudel-lanquan-li-jorn":["Seven stanzas set to one repeating melody, roughly six minutes. Each stanza opens in May, with long days and birdsong heard from somewhere far off, then turns inward to a love the poet has never laid eyes on. The tune keeps to a narrow band of notes, moving mostly by step, lifting once near the middle of the stanza before settling again, so that every verse ends in the same quiet place it began.","Jaufre Rudel was a nobleman, lord of Blaye on the Gironde, and he went east on the Second Crusade around 1147. The refrain idea he made famous, amor de lonh, love from far away, sits oddly close to the crusading impulse: a longing for something across the sea that becomes more precious the less it can be had. He sang for the Aquitanian courts that had produced the first troubadours a generation earlier.","The melody had a long afterlife in another language. Some seventy years later a German crusade song, Walther von der Vogelweide's Palästinalied, uses a tune that fits Rudel's stanza shape exactly, so a Provençal song about a distant beloved seems to have been refitted with words about the Holy Land. Contrafacts like this are how melodies travelled when nobody thought of a tune as private property.","The medieval life attached to Rudel says he fell in love with the countess of Tripoli by reputation alone, sailed to find her, and died in her arms. Scholars now read that as a thirteenth-century biographer spinning a story out of the poem itself. It did not stop Heine, Carducci, Rostand and Kaija Saariaho from retelling it, which says something about how badly the legend was wanted.","Play it back to back with the Palästinalied and hear the shared bones of the melody. Follow the word lonh, far, as it lands at the end of line after line: the poem is built on that single sound. A slow unaccompanied performance suits it better than a driven one; this is a song about waiting."],"comtessa-de-dia-a-chantar":["Five stanzas and a short envoi, about six minutes, built on the standard troubadour plan: two matching opening phrases, then a longer stretch that runs to the cadence. The singer must sing, she says, of what she would rather not, and then lays out her case against a lover who has cooled: she has kept faith, she is worth more than he allows, and she says so without a trace of pleading.","Around twenty women troubadours, the trobairitz, are known by name from twelfth- and thirteenth-century Occitania, most of them noblewomen writing inside the same courtly circles as the men. The Comtessa de Dia is probably the Beatriz whose medieval life links her to the poet Raimbaut d'Aurenga; nothing about the identification is certain. Southern French courts of the period gave aristocratic women unusual room to speak in public, and this is what it sounded like.","Hers is the only trobairitz song whose music survives, and it survives in exactly one place: a northern French songbook copied a century later, where a scribe with no obvious reason to care preserved an Occitan woman's melody among his trouvère repertory. Notice too the reversal built into the words. Where a male troubadour begs a lady for mercy, here it is the woman who sets out the terms and names her own worth.","Nineteenth-century philologists rediscovered the trobairitz and largely read them as curiosities; the last fifty years have taken them seriously as poets. The unresolved questions are whether the medieval life can be trusted at all, and whether the manuscript's Occitan has been northernised in transmission. Performances range from a bare voice to full instrumental settings, and the song survives both.","Read a translation first, then listen for the moment the melody rises highest, which the poet saves for her flattest statement of her own value. Compare it with Bernart de Ventadorn's lark song and hear how identical the musical grammar is and how different the standpoint. Sinfonye's recordings are a good place to start."],"guiraut-de-bornelh-reis-glorios":["A dawn song in five stanzas, each ending with the same falling phrase on the same words: and soon it will be dawn. A watchman keeps vigil on the roof and calls down to a friend asleep in his lover's arms, warning him that the eastern sky is turning. The melody is broad and unhurried, rising over the first half of each stanza and dropping back for the refrain line, which lands in the same place every time.","Giraut was a commoner from the Limousin who taught in winter and sang at courts in summer, travelled to Spain and to the Holy Land, and was called by his contemporaries the master of the troubadours. The alba, or dawn song, was a recognised type with fixed furniture: two lovers, a friendly watchman, a jealous husband offstage and the light coming up too soon.","The song begins as a prayer. Its first stanza addresses God directly as glorious king, true light and clarity, asking him to protect a companion, and only afterwards do you learn what the companion is doing. Latin dawn songs on the same plan survive from two centuries earlier, so the trick of borrowing liturgical language for an assignation was already old when Giraut made it beautiful.","Dante, in his treatise on writing in the vernacular, made Giraut the poet of moral rectitude, ranking him beside Arnaut Daniel for love and Bertran de Born for arms, an assessment later readers have found generous. The melody survives in only a few sources and their readings differ, so editors have to choose; as with all troubadour song, rhythm is supplied by the performer.","Listen for the refrain line and let it become familiar, because the whole design is the wait for its return. Then notice that the watchman's warnings get more urgent while the music stays exactly as calm as before. Try a version with an instrumental interlude between stanzas, which is how the vigil's passing hours are usually suggested."],"raimbaut-de-vaqueiras-kalenda-maya":["Six long stanzas over a tune that behaves like a dance rather than a song: short phrases in pairs, each played twice, the first time with an ending that leads onward and the second with one that lands. The result is the most strongly pulsed piece the troubadours left, and the reason it is almost always performed with drums. The words begin by refusing May: neither the calends of May nor new leaves can bring the poet joy.","Raimbaut was the son of a poor knight from Provence who attached himself to Boniface of Montferrat in northern Italy, was knighted by him, and followed him east on the Fourth Crusade. The medieval commentary attached to the song says he wrote these words to fit an estampie that two French travelling players had performed on their fiddles at the Montferrat court, to please a lady who had refused him.","If that story is true, the music came first and the poetry second, which explains everything odd about the piece. The rhythmic drive, the paired phrases, the unusually irregular line lengths all belong to an instrumental dance, and the poet's job was to squeeze Occitan verse into a shape he had not designed. The lady behind it is addressed only by a code name, as troubadour convention required.","This is the troubadour song early-music groups reach for first, precisely because its dance origin licenses a beat where the rest of the repertory offers no rhythmic guidance at all. Sceptics point out that the commentary explaining its origin was written long after the fact by someone reconstructing a good story from the song, exactly as happened with Jaufre Rudel.","Notice how each phrase is stated twice with different endings, and you have heard the structural principle of medieval instrumental dance. Then put on one of the French royal estampies and hear the same machinery without words. Percussion-heavy versions are fun; a plainer one shows how much of the excitement is in the tune itself."],"marcabru-pax-in-nomine-domini":["A strophic song that opens, startlingly, with a line of Latin lifted from the liturgy before dropping into Occitan for the rest. Its governing image is a washing place: the campaign in Spain is a basin where a sinner can scrub his soul clean, and the poet keeps returning to the word. The melody is plain and rather hard, with none of the curving sweetness of the love songs, and it repeats without variation for every stanza.","Marcabru was among the first generation of troubadours, active in the second quarter of the twelfth century, and his medieval life claims he was a foundling of the lowest birth, which may be malice rather than fact. He sang for the courts of Aquitaine and Castile. The song belongs to the years around 1149, when the Christian kingdoms of Spain were pushing south and recruiters needed arguments as well as pay.","Marcabru is the great sceptic of the troubadour world. Elsewhere he attacks courtly love outright as a fraud that corrupts households, which makes him the internal opposition to the whole tradition. Hear how the Latin opening works: three words of liturgy, and a secular singer in a hall has borrowed the authority of the church for what follows. Only four of his melodies survive.","For a long time he was thought crabbed and obscure, and his deliberately knotted style, the trobar clus, kept editors busy and audiences away. Modern scholarship values him for exactly that difficulty and for the political bite that makes him unlike anyone else of his generation. His Occitan is hard enough that translations differ substantially over what several stanzas actually say.","Play it immediately after a love song by Bernart de Ventadorn and the difference in temperature is the point: same era, same region, opposite purposes. Follow the washing-place image through the stanzas and watch it turn from metaphor into recruitment. Look for a spare performance with little more than a drone behind the voice."],"richard-lionheart-ja-nus-hons-pris":["Six stanzas of complaint, about five minutes, over a melody that survives in two manuscripts whose readings differ slightly. No prisoner, the singer begins, can tell his story properly, unless he does it in a song. What follows is not self-pity but accounting: he names the men who owe him, points out that he has been in captivity two winters, and observes that his own people are in no hurry to buy him back.","Richard I of England was seized in Austria in December 1192 on his way home from the Third Crusade, handed on to the Emperor Henry VI, and held in Germany while an enormous ransom was collected in England and Normandy. He was a Poitevin as much as an Englishman, raised in a family of song patrons, and he wrote in French; a version in Occitan also survives, so the complaint was aimed at both halves of his lands.","The song is addressed, in its final stanzas, to his half-sister Marie, countess of Champagne, whose court was one of the engines of French courtly literature. That is not a private touch but a piece of politics: the message was meant to circulate among exactly the people who could pressure his brother John. The rhyme scheme drives the word for prisoner back into your ear at the end of stanza after stanza.","The attribution is usually accepted, unusually for a medieval song by a famous name, because the circumstances and the addressee fit so exactly. The romantic story of the minstrel Blondel wandering Europe and finding his king by singing outside castle walls is a thirteenth-century tale with no historical basis, though it has proved indestructible in opera and film.","Hear the French and Occitan versions in succession; the melody is the same and the vowels change everything. Listen for the last line of each stanza, where the rhyme lands on the same sound and the point about being abandoned is quietly made again. A harp accompaniment suits the courtly register better than drums."],"thibaut-de-champagne-ausi-conme-unicorne":["Five stanzas and a short envoi over a melody in the shape that would dominate European song for three centuries: two identical opening phrases, then a longer third stretch that goes somewhere new and closes. The image is from the bestiary. The unicorn, tamed by the sight of a maiden, falls asleep in her lap and is killed there; the singer says he was taken the same way, and then describes the prison of Love with its four jailers.","Thibaut IV was count of Champagne and, from 1234, king of Navarre, a great magnate who quarrelled with the French crown, led a crusade in 1239 and wrote songs all his life. More than sixty of his melodies survive, an unusually complete corpus, because his songs were gathered into handsome collected manuscripts of a kind normally reserved for saints and kings. Being both a king and a poet, he got the treatment twice over.","That two-phrases-then-a-longer-one design has a German name, Bar form, because the Meistersinger of Nuremberg codified it three centuries later and Wagner put it on stage. Its ancestry runs straight back through songs like this one. The unicorn comes from the Physiologus, the animal book every educated listener knew, so the audience recognised the creature's fate before the poet had finished describing it.","Dante, listing the best poets of the vernacular languages, named the king of Navarre among them, and Thibaut has kept a reputation for polish ever since. The nineteenth-century editors who revived him transcribed his melodies into strict rhythmic patterns derived from the poetry; most performers now find that straitjacket unconvincing and sing far more freely, which changes the songs completely.","Count the opening phrase: hear it, hear it again, then wait for the melody to break new ground. Once that shape is in your ear you will find it in Lutheran chorales and in Wagner. Follow the allegory of Love's prison and note that the poet never names an actual woman anywhere in the song."],"gace-brule-de-bone-amour":["Five stanzas to a single melody, unhurried, staying mostly in the middle of the voice and moving by step, with one reach upward in each stanza that is the song's only display. The poem turns on balanced oppositions: good love and true love, the heart that is given and the body that suffers, what he asks and what he receives. Nothing dramatic happens in the words or the music, which is exactly the intended effect.","Gace was a knight from Champagne, active in the decades either side of 1200, and one of the first northern French poets to take the Occitan love song and make it work in French. He moved in the circle of the court of Champagne and had connections at the royal court; nearly seventy songs are attributed to him, which makes him one of the best-preserved trouvères and one of the most imitated.","Dante quoted a French song in his book on the vernacular and credited it to Thibaut, king of Navarre; the song was almost certainly by Gace, whose work circulated under grander names because grander names sold books. Notice how little the melody does. It avoids leaps, avoids extremes, and lets the words carry the argument, which is the trouvère manner in a sentence.","Attribution is the permanent problem. The manuscripts disagree about which songs are his, so the size and shape of his output changes with every edition, and a piece confidently printed as Gace in 1900 may now be listed as anonymous. Performers have to choose a version of the melody as well as a version of the text, since the sources vary.","Take this as the baseline against which the flashier trouvères register. Play it after Bernart de Ventadorn and hear how French smooths off the Occitan edges. Follow the paired opposites in a translation; they are the structure of the poem, and the music simply keeps out of their way. A single voice with a vielle is enough."],"adam-de-la-halle-jeu-de-robin-et-marion":["A pastoral comedy of about an hour in which spoken verse dialogue alternates with more than twenty short sung numbers, most only a few lines long. Marion, minding sheep, turns down a knight who tries to carry her off; Robin arrives too late to help, and the second half is taken up with village games, a picnic and a dance. The tune everyone remembers, Robins m'aime, opens the play and comes back as a refrain.","Adam was a cleric and poet from Arras, the busiest song town of northern France, with its own confraternity of poets and musicians. He ended his career in Italy in the service of the count of Artois and the Angevin court at Naples, and the play was probably written there, for French speakers a long way from the meadows it describes. It is a piece of homesickness dressed as comedy.","Most of the songs are not Adam's inventions at all. They are popular refrains his audience already knew, quoted into the play the way a film uses hit records, which is why they are so short and so catchy. Note also what the manuscripts do not give: no instrumental parts, no indication of who plays what. Every band you hear in a modern performance is a scholarly guess.","Since the nineteenth century it has been sold as the first comic opera, a label that flatters it and misleads: the music decorates the play rather than carrying the drama, and nobody sings their way through a scene. It has nonetheless kept a place on stage, and productions differ enormously in how much instrumental music they invent to fill it out.","Start with Robins m'aime, learn it in thirty seconds, and then enjoy recognising it whenever it returns. Watch a staged production rather than only listening, because half the piece is business, games and dancing. Compare the courtly love songs elsewhere in this catalogue and notice that the knight, here, is the villain."],"adam-de-la-halle-tant-con-je-vivrai":["A rondeau of eight short lines lasting well under two minutes, for three voices in almost the same range. They move together, note against note, so that the words arrive in all three parts at once and the harmony changes on every syllable. The rondeau plan means you hear the opening music three times in ninety seconds: refrain, new lines, refrain, more new lines, refrain, with the tune barely altered.","Adam left sixteen three-voice rondeaux, the earliest surviving polyphonic settings of the French dance-song forms that would rule secular music for the next two hundred years. They were copied into a large author's collection devoted to his works, an honour almost unheard of for a poet-musician in the 1280s, and they come out of the professional song culture of Arras, where composers competed in public before an audience of colleagues.","The texture is the interesting thing. Church polyphony of the period liked to stack contrasting rhythms so the words blurred; here all three voices step in lockstep and the text stays perfectly audible, which is a decision about what secular song is for. And because the refrain returns twice, the piece spends most of its short life repeating itself, deliberately, like a dance figure.","Historians disagree about what these little pieces are. One view makes them the seed of the fourteenth-century formes fixes and so of Machaut and everything after; another treats them as a local Arras experiment that died with their author, with the later tradition growing from other roots. Performance is contested too: one voice per part, or voices doubled by instruments.","Listen twice. The first time, follow the top voice alone and hear a plain little tune; the second, listen to the three together and notice how the chords land on the beat of the words. Then play a Machaut rondeau and hear what the next century did with the same eight-line frame."],"chanterai-por-mon-corage":["Seven stanzas in the voice of a woman whose lover has gone on crusade, each ending with a refrain built on the pilgrims' shout, God, when will they cry Outree. The melody is unshowy and strophic, sitting low and moving in short arcs, so that the refrain feels less like a climax than like something the singer cannot stop saying. She veers between piety, resentment at the preachers, and plain physical longing.","The song belongs to the years around the Third Crusade, when a good part of the French nobility sailed east and a good part of France stayed behind. Several manuscripts give it to Guiot de Dijon, others leave it unsigned, and the attribution is not secure. Songs in a woman's voice were a recognised trouvère genre with their own conventions, almost all of them written by men.","The refrain word is borrowed. Outree, roughly onward, was a cry from the pilgrim roads to Compostela, preserved in a twelfth-century Spanish manuscript as an encouragement shouted on the march. Pulled into a song about a woman left at home, it becomes the sound of everyone else leaving. Notice also how the stanzas alternate between what she owes God and what she wants back.","The manuscripts' disagreement about authorship has kept it in the anonymous column for most modern editors, and a long argument continues about whether such songs preserve anything of a real women's tradition or are entirely male ventriloquism. Whatever the answer, the piece became a favourite of the early-music revival and is now among the most recorded trouvère songs.","Wait for the refrain in each stanza and hear how its meaning shifts as her mood does. Play it next to Richard the Lionheart's prison song and Marcabru's recruiting song, and you have three sides of the same crusade. A woman's voice with almost no accompaniment suits it best."],"estampie-royal-quarte":["One of eight royal estampies copied at the end of a great French songbook, and among the oldest instrumental music written down in Europe. It is built from short sections called puncta. Each is played twice, first with an ending that leaves the phrase hanging and leads on, then with one that closes it; the same pair of endings serves every section, so the piece feels like a chain of departures that keep returning to the same door.","The book that preserves them, compiled around 1300 for a noble or royal household, is otherwise a collection of trouvère and troubadour songs. Instrumental music in this period was normally improvised by professionals and never written at all, so having eight dances on parchment is an accident of preservation rather than evidence of a written tradition. What survives is a skeleton: one line of notes and nothing else.","Here is the difference from the songs around them. Troubadour and trouvère melodies are written without any rhythm, but these dances use measured notation, so for once the beat is given rather than guessed. Everything else is missing: no instruments named, no number of players, no indication whether a drone belongs underneath. The ubiquitous medieval drone of modern recordings is a twentieth-century convention.","A Paris theorist of the period remarked that the stantipes was so intricate that following it took the whole mind, which has been read both as evidence that it was not danced and as evidence that it was danced very well. Modern performances range from a single unaccompanied fiddle to full bands with bagpipes and drums, and the manuscript licenses neither.","Listen for the two endings, one open and one closed; once you hear the pattern, the whole form clicks into place and you can follow any medieval dance. Then compare it with the Italian Lamento di Tristano, which does the same thing at greater length. A performance with two instruments trading the tune shows the structure most clearly."],"walther-von-der-vogelweide-palastinalied":["The singer stands on the ground where the Gospel happened and says so: now at last my life has meaning, since these sinful eyes see the holy land. Stanza by stanza he lists what took place there, the birth, the baptism, the betrayal, the descent into hell, and then arrives at the awkward present, where Christians, Jews and Muslims all claim the same soil. The melody is broad and stately, two matching opening phrases and a longer close.","Walther spent his life as a professional singer with no land of his own, moving between the Babenberg court at Vienna and the courts of rival German princes, and writing political verse for whoever was paying. Late in life he received a small fief from Frederick II. This song attaches to the emperor's long-postponed crusade of 1228, though its dating has been argued over for a century.","The tune is almost certainly borrowed. It fits Jaufre Rudel's Occitan love song about a beloved across the sea so exactly that most scholars take the German crusade song to be a contrafact of it, which means the music for the holy places began life as music for unattainable desire. And the melody itself survives by luck, in a single manuscript leaf that only came to light in the twentieth century.","Of the many Minnesinger poems, this is effectively the one whose melody we have complete, which makes it carry a weight of representation it was never designed for. Nineteenth- and twentieth-century Germany claimed Walther as a national poet and put up monuments to him; the tune now has a second life with folk and metal bands, sung with an aggression the words half invite.","Listen for the repeat of the opening phrase, then the longer third limb, the shape that runs through German song for centuries. Follow a translation to the final stanzas and notice the argument turning political. Then play Jaufre Rudel's song and hear where the tune probably came from."],"walther-von-der-vogelweide-under-der-linden":["Four stanzas, four minutes, in the voice of a girl remembering an afternoon on the heath under a lime tree: the flowers and grass broken where they lay, a bed made of leaves, and a nightingale in the branches who saw everything and will not tell. Each stanza ends with a line of pure sound, tandaradei, imitating the bird. The melody usually heard is light-footed and dance-like, and lets the refrain syllables ring.","Walther wrote in two registers. One is the high courtly Minnesang of unreachable noble ladies; the other, of which this is the most famous example, sets ordinary young women in the open air and lets them speak for themselves. He was working around the Babenberg court in Vienna in his early years, in front of an aristocratic audience that evidently enjoyed hearing the elevated conventions sidestepped.","No melody is preserved with the poem. What performers sing is a French trouvère tune whose stanza shape matches Walther's line for line and syllable for syllable, close enough that many scholars think he wrote the German words to fit it. Whether that match is design or coincidence has never been settled, so the song you hear is a reconstruction wearing a borrowed coat.","Nineteenth-century readers took the poem's plainness for folk authenticity, and later German nationalism took Walther as a founding voice, an appropriation the poems have had to be dug out from under. Current criticism is more interested in the ventriloquism: a male court poet writing a woman's pleasure, for men, and how much of her voice is really hers.","Read a translation first; this is a poem that gives up almost everything on a first hearing once you know what happens. Then listen for the nonsense refrain and hear how the tune makes room for it. Compare it with Giraut de Bornelh's dawn song, the courtly version of the same secret."],"neidhart-meienzit-ane-nit":["A long summer song welcoming May, built from stanzas of many short rhyming lines that tumble over one another, set to a measured, foot-tapping tune of the kind almost no other Minnesinger left behind. The subject is a village dance: the meadow greens, the girls want to go out, the young men swagger, and somebody behaves badly. The music does not brood at all; it is written to be moved to.","Neidhart was a knight, Bavarian by origin and later in Austrian service, who invented a genre by taking the courtly love song out of the hall and setting it among peasants. His noble audiences found the transplant funny: boorish villagers aping courtly manners, and a knightly singer forever losing out to them. The songs stayed in circulation for two hundred years, longer than almost any other medieval secular repertory.","He is the only Minnesinger with a substantial body of surviving melodies, dozens of them across several manuscripts, which makes him disproportionately important to anyone who wants to know how German song of the period actually sounded. His output splits neatly in two: summer songs for outdoor dancing, like this one, and winter songs set indoors, where the tone turns sour and violent.","The permanent question is whether he is mocking the peasants or the courtly ideal, and good readers have argued both. There is also a textual problem: his fame kept the manuscripts growing after his death, so a large body of pseudo-Neidhart accumulated and the authentic core is hard to fix. By the fifteenth century he had become a folk character in his own right, hero of plays and pranks.","This is the medieval repertory that most rewards a band. Look for a performance with bagpipe or shawm and percussion and let the dance rhythm do its work. Then play Walther's Under der linden beside it and hear the same countryside from two social altitudes."],"oswald-von-wolkenstein-es-fuegt-sich":["Seven enormous stanzas, ten minutes or more, in which a man tells his own life. He left home at ten with nothing, served as a stable boy, a cook and an oarsman, learned languages he lists by name, was shipwrecked and clung to a barrel, lost the sight of an eye, came home, inherited, married. The melody is a single broad shape repeated seven times, and it has to carry an entire biography without flagging.","Oswald was a Tyrolean nobleman, a diplomat in the service of King Sigismund, a litigant in a ruinous land dispute that got him imprisoned and tortured, and a compulsive self-documenter. He had two large illuminated manuscripts made of his own songs, complete with a portrait of himself, at a time when almost no composer's face was recorded. The songs are the closest thing the Middle Ages produced to an autobiography set to music.","That portrait has a drooping right eyelid, matching what the poem says about his damaged eye, so the picture and the song corroborate each other across six hundred years. His manuscripts also contain polyphonic songs, several of them German words fitted to French and Italian pieces he had picked up on his travels, so the collection doubles as a record of what one restless man heard abroad.","Rediscovered in the nineteenth century and long treated as a colourful oddity, he is now read as the last major Minnesinger and the first European poet to write a life as ragged as a real one. Performance divides between rough, declamatory singing that plays up the autobiography and smoother approaches that treat him as a courtly composer; his Tyrolean dialect is a further obstacle.","Read a translation alongside the music or the point is lost, because the interest is entirely in what he claims happened. Notice how the melody's return at each stanza turns a list of disasters into something almost cheerful. Look for a recording that keeps the accompaniment sparse and lets the voice do the storytelling."],"alfonso-x-cantiga-100-santa-maria-strela-do-dia":["A song of praise rather than a miracle story, in Galician-Portuguese, laid out as a refrain and three stanzas. Everyone sings the refrain, calling Mary the star of day who shows the way to God; a soloist takes each stanza, whose first lines use new music before a final line swings back into the refrain tune. The melody is bright, symmetrical and instantly memorable, which is why it has become the emblem of the whole collection.","Alfonso X of Castile, called the Wise, gathered more than four hundred of these songs at his courts in Seville and Toledo in the second half of the thirteenth century, in manuscripts of extraordinary luxury. The same king ran a translation workshop staffed by Christian, Jewish and Muslim scholars and had law codes and star tables compiled in Castilian. The Cantigas were part of that programme: vernacular, systematic, and enormous.","The collection is organised by a numerical rule. Nine miracle stories are followed by a song of praise, over and over, which is why the round number 100 falls on a praise song rather than a narrative. One manuscript carries over a thousand miniatures, and another shows pairs of musicians with their instruments at every tenth song. Those pictures are the source of much of what is believed about medieval instruments.","How much Alfonso wrote himself is unresolved; he probably composed some and supervised the rest, as with everything else his workshops produced. The lavishly exotic instrumentations of 1970s recordings, drawn from those miniatures and from assumptions about Arabic influence, are now widely thought fanciful, and recent performers have gone plainer. Galician-Portuguese pronunciation is its own argument.","Learn the refrain on the first hearing, because you will sing it four times. Then listen for the moment in each stanza when the new melody bends back into familiar territory: that hinge is the form. Compare a big 1970s recording with a recent one for two entirely different ideas of what this music is."],"alfonso-x-cantiga-10-rosa-das-rosas":["Four stanzas framed by a refrain that piles up superlatives: rose of roses, flower of flowers, lady of ladies, lord of lords. Between them the singer declares himself Mary's man, sworn to serve no other and to leave every other love. The tune rises confidently at the start of the refrain and comes down in a long, satisfying descent, and the stanzas are shaped so that their last line hands you straight back to it.","Thirteenth-century Castile was saturated with Marian devotion: pilgrimage shrines, miracle collections, new cathedrals. Alfonso's political position depended on presenting himself as the Virgin's vassal, and these songs do that work in public. He asked in his will to be buried with the Cantigas manuscripts and for them to be sung on her feast days at his tomb, which tells you he regarded them as more than entertainment.","The vocabulary is lifted wholesale from secular love song. The word the refrain uses for lord is the same term a troubadour applies to the lady he serves, and the promise never to love another belongs to the etiquette of courtly devotion, not to prayer. The king is not writing a hymn so much as recasting himself as the Virgin's troubadour, with all the erotic apparatus intact.","This is the cantiga everyone records, to the point where it stands in for four hundred others that never get performed. Some scholars object that the standard drum-driven arrangement has turned a devotional song into a novelty number; others reply that the manuscripts, which give melody and text and nothing else, cannot settle the matter either way.","Count the four superlatives in the refrain and notice how the melody gives each one a step upward. Then read a translation of a stanza and spot the language a troubadour would use about a woman. Play it after Bernart de Ventadorn to hear the borrowing plainly."],"martin-codax-ondas-do-mar-de-vigo":["The first of seven short songs in Galician-Portuguese, sung by a girl on the Atlantic coast who asks the waves of the Vigo estuary whether they have seen her lover, and whether he will come back. Each stanza is a couplet plus a one-line refrain, and each repeats the last with a single word changed, so the song moves forward by inches. The melody is narrow, unaccompanied in most performances, and over in four minutes.","Nothing is known about Martin Codax except the name written at the head of the songs. He belongs to the Galician-Portuguese lyric tradition that flourished in the north-west of the peninsula in the thirteenth century, the same language and roughly the same decades as Alfonso X's Cantigas, but entirely secular and rooted in a much older popular form: the woman's song, sung to the sea, the dawn or a mother.","The manuscript surfaced in 1914, when a Madrid bookseller named Vindel found a single parchment leaf used as padding inside the binding of an old book. It carries the seven poems and music for six of them; the seventh has empty staves, ruled but never filled. The songs are also chained together, each stanza's second line becoming the next stanza's first, so the cycle interlocks.","Its authenticity was doubted at first, on the reasonable ground that a unique parchment turning up in a bookshop is exactly what a forgery looks like; it is now accepted. In Galicia the songs have become national emblems, set in stone and sung at festivals. Musically the debate is about mode and rhythm, since the notation gives pitches without a beat.","Have the words in front of you and watch the single-word substitutions: the pleasure of the form is that almost nothing changes. Listen to all seven songs together, about twenty minutes, rather than this one alone. An unaccompanied woman's voice is the version to seek out first."],"llibre-vermell-stella-splendens":["A dance-song for pilgrims in two voices that chase each other in close imitation over a swinging triple beat. A long refrain hails the star shining on the mountain, and between its returns come stanza after stanza cataloguing who climbs to the shrine: kings and queens, nobles and commoners, the rich, the poor, the crippled, the sick, all named in a rolling list that most performances cut down. It runs for as long as the stanzas last.","The book, copied at the Benedictine monastery of Montserrat in Catalonia around 1399, is called the Red Book after its nineteenth-century binding. A Latin note beside the songs explains their purpose with unusual frankness: pilgrims keeping night vigil sometimes want to sing and dance, so they should be given honest and devout songs rather than the vulgar ones they would otherwise choose. This is a solution to a discipline problem.","The rubric permits dancing in a holy place, which is exactly what the ten pieces in the book are designed for; several are canons that a crowd can pick up by ear. And the manuscript is a survivor. Napoleon's army sacked Montserrat in 1811 and destroyed most of the monastery's library; this book is among the few things that came through, which is why we have any of this repertory at all.","It has become the most recorded medieval music of the Iberian peninsula, helped by a rhythm that needs no scholarly reconstruction. Arguments concern tempo, the amount of percussion, and whether the imitation between the two voices should be sung by soloists or by a crowd. Some scholars think the pieces were collected from elsewhere rather than composed at Montserrat.","Follow the second voice entering behind the first and hear the canon lock into place. Then listen to the stanzas as a list and enjoy the sheer social range of the pilgrims named. A recording with a large, slightly rough chorus catches the point better than a polished small ensemble."],"llibre-vermell-ad-mortem-festinamus":["A single melodic line, sung in unison, driving forward on repeated notes in a marching triple rhythm, with a refrain that returns after every verse: we hasten towards death, let us stop sinning. The verses work through the catalogue of those who will not be spared, the rich, the young, the strong, the pope, and the tune never softens or slows. It stays inside a narrow span of notes so that any crowd can join it.","It closes the Montserrat book, the last of the ten songs given to pilgrims. Catalonia had been hit hard by the Black Death in 1348 and by repeated returns of plague for the rest of the century, and the visual arts of the period were full of skeletons leading the living away by the hand. This is the earliest surviving music for that idea, a dance of death with a tune.","Almost everything else in the book is polyphonic; this one is deliberately not. A single line in a small range, endlessly repeating, is what you write for a procession of people who cannot read music and are walking while they sing. Notice how the words of the refrain are the only ones that recur, so that the message survives even if the verses are lost in the noise.","Modern performances tend to pile on drums until the piece sounds like a war chant, which has made it a favourite of film and video-game soundtracks; the manuscript authorises none of that. Scholars point out that the pilgrims' repertory was intended as an alternative to secular dancing, so a certain amount of rhythmic energy is precisely the point.","Listen for the refrain and let it become inescapable, which is the design. Then play the Gregorian Dies irae, or any later setting of it, and hear a different medieval answer to the same fear. A performance with plain massed voices and light percussion serves it better than a heavily produced one."],"laudario-cortona-laude-novella":["The opening piece of the oldest Italian songbook, and a model of the form: a refrain, then a pair of matching short lines, then a line that leads the ear straight back to the refrain. The text is in Italian rather than Latin, addressed to the Virgin, and announces itself as a new song to be sung. The melody moves by step within a small compass, easy to catch after one hearing and easy to sing badly without ruining.","In thirteenth-century Umbria and Tuscany, lay confraternities called laudesi met in the evening to sing devotional songs in their own language, outside the liturgy and without clergy. Cortona is deep in Franciscan country, a generation after Francis of Assisi, whose movement put vernacular praise at the centre of religious life. A wave of penitential processions across northern Italy in 1260 spread the habit further.","The Cortona manuscript holds forty-six laude and is the earliest substantial collection of song in Italian, which makes it a foundation stone of Italian literature as well as of Italian music. But its notation gives pitches without rhythm, so the buoyant dance you usually hear is a modern editorial decision; sung in even, unmeasured notes it becomes something much closer to chant.","Performances have swung from processional drums and shawms in the 1970s to austere unison singing more recently, and the manuscript supports both equally badly. Scholars have proposed that some tunes are shared with the Cantigas de Santa Maria repertory across the Mediterranean, a connection that is suggestive rather than proven.","Sing along with the refrain, which is what it is for. Then compare it with a cantiga by Alfonso X and hear how similar the plan is, refrain and stanzas hinged the same way, in two languages a thousand kilometres apart. A recording with plain unison voices shows the melody most clearly."],"lamento-di-tristano":["Three sections in a slow, swaying triple time, each stated twice with a first ending that leads on and a second that closes, followed by a fast movement called La Rotta that takes the same material and runs with it in a brisk duple beat. Slow then fast, on one set of tunes: the whole idea of the paired dance is here. The manuscript gives a single line of notes and nothing else at all.","It is one of fifteen dances copied into a manuscript now in London, written in Tuscany around 1400 alongside the songs of the Italian trecento. The Tristan romance was enormously popular in Italy, retold in prose and painted on chests and walls, so a dance could be sold under his name in the way a tune might be named after a celebrity today. Whether it accompanied any telling of the story is unknown.","The word rotta means broken, and the trick of following a slow dance with a fast one built on the same material runs forward through the pavane and galliard of the sixteenth century to the paired movements of the Baroque suite. The manuscript's other dances, with names like Ghaetta and Chominciamento di gioia, are longer and more elaborate; this is the plainest of them and the one that travelled.","A handful of recordings of this piece have done more than any scholarship to fix what modern audiences think medieval music sounds like, and it turns up constantly in films and games set anywhere between 800 and 1500. Specialists are quick to point out that the drones, the hand drums and the plangent reed instruments are all twentieth-century additions to a bare notated line.","Listen first for the pair of endings in each section, then for the moment the tempo doubles and the same tune becomes a dance. Compare a stark version, one or two instruments only, with a fully arranged one; the gap between them is the size of the guesswork. Try the French royal estampies next."],"sumer-is-icumen-in":["One voice starts the tune, a second comes in behind it, then a third and a fourth, each entering at a fixed distance so that four versions of the same melody are running at once. Underneath, two more voices trade a short figure back and forth, over and over, without stopping. The Middle English words are about the cuckoo calling, the ewe bleating for her lamb and the bullock leaping. It can go round as long as the singers like.","The music was copied around the middle of the thirteenth century at Reading Abbey, into a miscellany of sermons, calendars, poems and medical notes rather than a music book. Beside it a scribe wrote out, in red ink, instructions for how the round is to be performed: how many can sing it, where each voice comes in, how it ends. Explicit performance directions of that kind are almost unknown from the period.","Written under the English words, in the same manuscript, is a Latin sacred text about Christ's sacrifice fitted to the identical music, so one page carries both a summer song and a Passion meditation. The two lower voices form a separate repeating idea of their own, the earliest written-out example in Europe of a figure that simply cycles beneath everything else, the ancestor of the ground bass.","Its date has been argued across a century. Nothing else in thirteenth-century Europe combines six voices with strict canon over a repeating bass, and one influential mid-twentieth-century argument moved it forward to about 1310 to make it less anomalous; palaeographers have since pushed back toward the earlier reading. Either way it remains a piece with no siblings, which is what makes it awkward.","Ask for the tune alone first, sung once through, and then hear the whole machine assemble as the voices enter. Try to hold on to the two lowest voices, which are doing something completely different. Then compare a boys' choir version with a small consort of adults; the piece changes character entirely."],"agincourt-carol-deo-gracias-anglia":["A carol, which in the fifteenth century meant a refrain called a burden alternating with narrative verses. Two soloists tell the story in English, stanza by stanza: the king sailing to Normandy, the taking of Harfleur, the battle and the ride home to London. Then everybody joins in the burden, giving thanks to God for the victory, and the writing opens out into a fuller, warmer sound built on sweet-sounding thirds and sixths.","Written within a few years of the battle of 1415, probably for one of the celebrations that greeted Henry V's return. Carols were not Christmas music at this date but a processional form, characteristically English, mixing English verse with Latin tags. English composers of exactly this generation were being copied on the continent for their full, consonant harmony, a manner contemporaries called the English guise.","The words hand the whole victory to God and give Henry almost no credit, which was the king's own line after the battle: he forbade songs about his deeds and ordered thanksgiving instead. The song obeys and simultaneously spreads his fame, which is a neat piece of work. The sources give the burden twice over, once for two voices and once for three, so a performance can grow as it goes.","It has served as patriotic music ever since, most conspicuously in William Walton's score for Olivier's wartime film of Henry V, which planted it in the national ear. Historians of the fifteenth century value it differently, as evidence of what English harmony sounded like just before Dunstaple's generation exported it to the continent.","Listen to how the burden fills out after the thinner verses; that expansion is the whole effect. Follow one verse with the text in front of you to catch the narrative. Then play Dunstaple and hear the same sweetness applied to sacred Latin, and you have the English style in two steps."],"there-is-no-rose-of-swych-vertu":["A short Christmas carol: a burden and five brief verses, three minutes in all. Each verse is in English except its last words, which drop into Latin, so the song swaps languages line by line. The rose of the title is Mary, and the verses move from wonder at the birth to the invitation to leave worldly pleasures. Two voices carry the verses, moving in parallel a third or a sixth apart; the burden adds a third voice.","It survives on a long parchment roll made in England around 1420, a format chosen so that singers could hold it up and read from it while walking in procession rather than gathering around a book. Carols of this kind belonged to processions and to the hall as much as to the choir, and the mixture of English and Latin in a single stanza was a fashion of the period.","The Latin phrases are not decoration. Each is a self-contained exclamation, a thing to marvel at, a matching form, let us rejoice, let us cross over, and they act as tiny liturgical anchors dropped into the vernacular story, so that the song moves between the street and the church every few seconds. That switching is the form's whole invention.","For most listeners today the text arrives through Benjamin Britten, whose Ceremony of Carols sets these words for boys' voices and harp and has made the medieval version sound like a curiosity, which reverses the actual order of things. Performance questions are about voice type, boys or adult men, and about how far to go with reconstructed Middle English pronunciation.","Hear the medieval setting straight after Britten's and the difference in temperature is the lesson: no expressive shading, just two lines moving sweetly together. Listen for the Latin words at the end of each verse. A small adult consort, one voice per part, suits it better than a large choir."],"carmina-burana-codex-buranus":["Not a piece but a book: over two hundred and fifty poems in Latin, Middle High German and a little French, copied around 1230. There are drinking songs, gambling songs, spring songs, seductions, ferocious satires on the papal court, and at the end two religious plays. Music is notated for only part of them, so a modern programme is a selection, usually an hour or so, of the songs whose tunes can actually be recovered.","The manuscript was probably made in the south Tyrol or Styria for a household wealthy enough to want an illuminated book of scandalous verse. The poems themselves come from the wandering scholars and unbeneficed clerics of the twelfth-century schools, men with Latin educations and no jobs, which explains both the classical polish and the anticlerical rage. The book was found at Benediktbeuern in Bavaria in 1803, and named after it.","Most of the melodies are written in staffless neumes, marks that show whether a tune goes up or down but not by how much, so they can only be sung when the same song survives elsewhere in staff notation. Everything you hear from this book is the lucky minority. The opening pages carry a wheel of fortune miniature, four figures rising and falling around a turning wheel, which set the tone for everything after.","Carl Orff took twenty-four of the texts in 1936 and set them to insistent new music of his own, and for most of the world Carmina Burana now means that cantata rather than this manuscript. Early-music groups have spent sixty years trying to reclaim the originals, with results that sound nothing like Orff and, being reconstructions, cannot claim to be authentic either.","Hear a reconstruction ensemble first, then Orff's O Fortuna: the gulf between them is the point of the exercise. Look for a programme that keeps the drinking songs and the satires alongside the love lyrics, since the mixture is what makes the book. Have translations to hand; the jokes are the substance."],"fauvel-porchier-miex-estre-ameroie":["A single unaccompanied voice, barely two minutes long, singing a short French refrain song: refrain, then a verse that borrows half of it, then the refrain whole again. The tune moves in narrow steps around a small compass and ends every phrase with the same falling turn. The singer says he would rather be loved as a swineherd, honest and poor, than serve at court. The plainness of the melody is itself the argument.","It comes from the illuminated copy of the Roman de Fauvel made in Paris around 1317, now Bibliothèque nationale fonds français 146: a satirical poem about a horse called Fauvel whom bishops and kings queue up to groom. A royal clerk added 169 pieces of music into the margins and gaps of the story. The court of Philip V, the fall of the minister Enguerran de Marigny and the whole machinery of royal government are the target.","Fauvel's name is an acrostic of six vices — Flaterie, Avarice, Vilanie, Variété, Envie, Lascheté — and the phrase for grooming him gave English its expression about currying favour. The manuscript deliberately mixes registers: this rustic-sounding one-line song sits a page or two from the most advanced polyphony written anywhere in Europe that decade. The juxtaposition is editorial, not accidental.","For a long time the Fauvel music was mined only for the pieces attachable to Philippe de Vitry, and the monophonic items were treated as filler between the motets. Recent work reads the manuscript as a single designed object in which the plain songs carry as much of the satire as the learned ones. Ensembles specialising in medieval song have given these pieces back their edge.","Hear it beside the three-voice motets from the same book: this is the manuscript talking to itself in two voices, high style and low. Notice how little the tune needs to make its point. The Clemencic Consort and, later, the Boston Camerata recorded generous selections from Fauvel, and either will place the song inside its story."],"fauvel-aman-novi-probatur-exitu":["Three voices, about three minutes. A slow tenor borrowed from chant lays down a repeating rhythmic pattern; above it two faster voices sing different Latin poems simultaneously, one about Haman hanged on his own gallows, the other about treacherous Fortune. The words collide, so no listener catches both. What registers instead is the grinding of the two upper lines against the fixed, unhurried steps of the bottom voice, and the sour clashes on the way into each cadence.","Written into the Roman de Fauvel manuscript in Paris around 1317, in the aftermath of the execution of Enguerran de Marigny, Philip the Fair's chief minister, hanged at Montfaucon in 1315. Haman, the courtier in the book of Esther hanged on the gallows he built for someone else, is the transparent mask. Court clerks read Latin fluently, and the veiled accusation was meant to be decoded by the people it concerned.","The tenor's pattern repeats exactly, then repeats again in halved note values, so the whole piece accelerates without any singer changing tempo — the technique later named isorhythm. Listen for the moment the repetitions start arriving twice as fast; that is the machine tightening. The borrowed chant words mean roughly \"alas, my soul is sorrowful\", so the oldest layer of the piece pronounces the sentence.","Attribution is the long argument. Philippe de Vitry's name attaches to a handful of Fauvel motets on stylistic and biographical grounds, and this is usually among them, but no medieval source signs it. Scholars now prefer to speak of a Parisian circle around Vitry rather than a single author, which suits a manuscript that was plainly the work of a committee of clerks.","Follow the lowest voice only, and count its long notes; when the same shape comes round faster you have heard isorhythm without needing the word. Then listen again for the rhymes in the upper poems. Pair it with Garrit Gallus from the same manuscript. Three solo male voices without instruments make the text collisions clearest."],"vitry-firmissime-fidem-adesto-sancta-trinitas":["A Trinity motet in three voices, around four minutes. The chant tenor, an Alleluia for the feast, is cut into a repeating rhythmic figure; over it the top voice rattles out a long doctrinal poem in short notes while the middle voice sings a slower hymn text. The number three governs everything — voices, texts, the divisions of the beat — and the piece ends not with a flourish but by simply running out of borrowed chant.","One of the motets copied into the Roman de Fauvel around 1317 and long credited to Philippe de Vitry, then a young clerk in royal service who would end as Bishop of Meaux. These same years produced the treatise called Ars nova, which gave the period its name: a new way of writing rhythm that let notes be divided in twos as well as threes, and made the fast chattering upper voice of a motet like this possible at all.","The tenor is not sung as free chant but measured, so a sacred melody becomes the piece's clock. Its rhythmic unit repeats regardless of where the borrowed tune has got to, which means the same notes return wearing different rhythms. That separation of pitch from duration is the first genuinely structural device in Western music; count the tenor's entries and you are reading the blueprint.","Theorists of the 1320s quoted Fauvel motets as specimens of the new notation, which is why the manuscript matters far beyond its satire. The conservative Jacobus of Liège attacked the new style at length for burying plainchant under fussy rhythm. Vitry's authorship here rests on citations in treatises rather than a signature, and a cautious modern editor still writes \"attributed\".","Try it directly after Pérotin: the same cathedral culture, but the rhythm has become a grid instead of a flow. Hear the tenor's long notes as a slow pulse under the chatter, then let the top voice's speed register as genuinely new. Ensembles that specialise in fourteenth-century motets, the Orlando Consort among them, sing this repertory one voice to a part."],"vitry-petre-clemens":["A ceremonial motet for the election of a pope, five or six minutes, with texted voices moving above a measured tenor. The upper part addresses Peter — Pierre Roger, taking the name Clement — by name and by pun; beneath it a second poem tells the mourners of the Church to dry their eyes. Long-breathed and dense with Latin rhetoric, it is built on rhythmic patterns that repeat and then contract, so the ending gathers speed.","Pierre Roger was elected Clement VI at Avignon in May 1342. Philippe de Vitry, by then a seasoned royal servant, poet and diplomat, had known him for years and wanted a benefice; the motet is a job application in Latin verse as much as a celebration. The papacy had sat at Avignon since 1309, and its court pulled in the best musicians, poets and painters in Europe.","The new pope's name is worked into the poetry, since Clemens is both a title and the word for mercy, so every promise of clemency is also a form of address. Vitry moved in the same Avignon circles as Petrarch, who was there at the time and called him the one true poet left in France. Listen near the end for the tenor speeding up by strict proportion while the upper voices carry on as before.","Vitry survives in fragments: about a dozen motets can be attached to him with confidence, out of a career that theorists treated as epoch-making. Debate turns on which pieces are really his, and on how much of the Ars nova treatise bearing his name he actually wrote. Petrarch's praise has kept the reputation alive in places where the music itself is thin on the ground.","Listen for the way the top voice's declamation sounds like speech at speed, and for the long notes underneath that never hurry. Then compare Machaut's Latin motets a generation later, where the same machinery carries prayer instead of politics. A one-voice-per-part recording with the texts in front of you is the only real way in."],"tournai-mass":["Six movements of the Mass Ordinary in three voices — Kyrie, Gloria, Credo, Sanctus, Agnus Dei and a dismissal — running about twenty minutes. They do not match one another. The Kyrie moves in slow blocks of sound like organum from a century earlier; the Credo rattles its text out syllable by syllable; the closing Ite missa est is a French motet with two texts sung at once. The joins are audible, and they are the point.","The manuscript belongs to Tournai Cathedral in what is now Belgium, and the movements were copied together in the middle of the fourteenth century, probably assembled for a weekly Lady Mass endowed there. Polyphony until then meant single feast-day items; a complete sung Ordinary in parts was a new kind of purchase, made possible by legacies from wealthy canons who wanted Masses sung for their souls in perpetuity.","The dismissal carries a French poem about the Virgin over its Latin, so the last thing sung at this Mass borrows the form of a love song. Listen for the sudden change of language and speed at the end: that movement belongs to a different decade from the Kyrie and almost certainly to a different composer. The cycle's unity is a bookbinder's achievement rather than a composer's.","For years it was called the earliest polyphonic Mass cycle and set beside Machaut's as a lesser cousin. Scholars now stress the difference in kind: Tournai is a compilation, Machaut's a single conception. Dating the individual movements is contested, with the Kyrie perhaps decades older than the rest. Comparable assemblies from Toulouse, Barcelona and the Sorbonne have since been recognised as the same phenomenon.","Hear the Kyrie and then jump straight to the final dismissal: fifty years of style change inside one Mass. Compare it with Machaut's Messe de Nostre Dame, written nearby and within the same generation. Small vocal ensembles with a low, unforced sound suit it, and the recordings worth having are the ones that do not smooth the seams away."],"barcelona-mass":["Another gathered Ordinary — Kyrie, Gloria, Credo, Sanctus, Agnus Dei — in three voices, about twenty minutes, preserved in a manuscript now in Barcelona. The writing is more even than Tournai's: a supple top line over two slower supporting parts, with the long Gloria and Credo broken up by passages where two voices trade fast phrases. The Credo gets through its text at speed and then stops dead for the words about the Incarnation.","The manuscript belongs to the orbit of the Aragonese royal chapel, whose kings collected French singers and instruments and corresponded constantly with the papal court at Avignon. From 1378 the Great Schism split the papacy between Rome and Avignon, and Aragon backed Avignon; music, notation and singers travelled the same roads as the diplomacy. A Mass like this shows how fast Parisian technique reached Catalonia.","Individual movements turn up in other manuscripts in other company, which means this \"Mass\" is a local act of collection rather than a work conceived whole. Listen for the moment in the Credo where everything slows to block chords at the Incarnation: that gesture, shared by all the composite cycles of the period, is the one thing their compilers seem to have agreed on.","The Barcelona, Tournai, Toulouse and Sorbonne Masses are usually discussed together as composite cycles, and their existence complicates any claim that Machaut invented the polyphonic Mass. Scholarship since the 1960s has tried to date the layers and produced ranges rather than years. Recordings are few, and most surround the cycle with chant propers to restore something of the liturgy.","Play it after the Messe de Tournai and listen for the smoother, more song-like top voice. Start with the Gloria: the text moves quickly, and the pairs of voices tossing phrases give it a lift the Tournai cycle lacks. Ensembles that add medieval fiddle and small organ to the vocal lines make the Aragonese court setting audible."],"machaut-hoquetus-david":["Four minutes of three-part music with no words at all beyond the single syllable \"David\" attached to the lowest line. That tenor walks through a chant melisma in long measured notes; above it two voices hocket, each resting while the other sounds, so a single melodic line is split between two performers like a ball passed back and forth. The effect is of one instrument breathing in gasps, and it never lets up.","Machaut spent his last decades as a canon at Reims, supervising the copying of his complete works into large illuminated manuscripts — an author's edition, almost unheard of for a musician. The Hoquetus stands at the end of the motet section in those books. It has often been linked to a great Reims occasion of the 1360s, perhaps the coronation of Charles V in 1364, though nothing in the sources proves it.","The borrowed chant is the melisma on the word \"David\" from an Alleluia for the Nativity of the Virgin — the same chant Pérotin had set at Notre-Dame nearly two centuries earlier. Machaut takes only its tail. Because the piece carries no text, medieval performers may well have played it on instruments; the manuscripts say nothing, so every modern recording is making an argument.","Nineteenth-century editors found hocket comic or primitive. The twentieth heard rhythmic sophistication instead and made this a favourite of early-music groups and, later, of composers hunting for medieval models. Whether it is vocal or instrumental remains open. Its position in the manuscripts, among the motets rather than the songs, suggests Machaut regarded it as high art and not a novelty.","Find the pulse and notice that the two upper voices never sound together on the beat: that stutter is the hocket. Then hear Pérotin's Alleluia nativitas, built on the same chant, to feel the two-hundred-year gap. Instrumental recordings with medieval winds are exhilarating; voices on a single syllable are stranger and probably closer to what the page says."],"machaut-felix-virgo-inviolata-genitrix":["The last and largest of Machaut's motets: four voices, about six minutes. Two upper parts sing different Latin prayers to the Virgin at the same time, the tenor carries a phrase of chant in long measured notes, and a fourth voice fills in beneath. Twice the whole texture breaks into hocket, the voices splintering into gasps before the long lines resume. It closes with a plea for peace for the people of France.","Written probably around 1360, in the wreckage after Poitiers: King John II a prisoner in England, mercenary companies loose in the countryside, the plague still recent. Machaut had lived through the siege of Reims in the winter of 1359–60 and wrote about it elsewhere. The Virgin's mercy and an end to the war are asked for in one breath, which is exactly what a cathedral canon was employed to pray for.","The tenor's borrowed words — to you we sigh, mourning and weeping — come from the antiphon Salve Regina, and they are the emotional key: the oldest layer says plainly what the composed voices only circle. The two hocket passages arrive precisely where the rhythmic scheme contracts, so the sound of panic is a by-product of arithmetic rather than a gesture added on top.","Machaut's motets were long treated as forbidding exercises next to his songs; the three Latin motets, of which this is the last, have since been reclaimed as his most ambitious music. Debate continues over whether they form a deliberate group and how far specific war events can be read into them. Performances differ over whether to give the lower parts to instruments.","Read the two poems side by side before you listen, then stop trying to follow both. Wait instead for the hocket: it comes twice and is unmistakable. Compare the Kyrie of his own Messe de Nostre Dame, built by the same means. The Hilliard Ensemble and the Orlando Consort have recorded the motets complete, one voice to a part."],"machaut-quant-en-moy-amour-et-biaute":["The first motet in Machaut's own ordering of his works, and a manifesto for what follows: three voices, six minutes, two French love poems sung simultaneously above a Latin tenor. The top voice describes how Love entered through the eyes; the middle voice answers in kind. Near the end the two split into hocket. Underneath, entirely unmoved by any of it, the tenor repeats a fragment of chant on two words meaning \"very bitter\".","Machaut placed his motets at the head of his collected manuscripts, before the songs and before the Mass, as the most learned thing he did. He served John of Luxembourg, the blind king of Bohemia, into the 1340s, travelling across Europe in his household, and afterwards settled at Reims. This is court music for readers: the pleasure of hearing two poems at once assumes an audience that already knows both.","The tenor's two words come from chant for the dead, and they undercut everything sung above them. French love poetry floats on a Latin reminder of mortality, and the joke — or the warning — is visible only to someone who reads the tenor's label in the manuscript. Several of Machaut's motets work this way, with the borrowed chant quietly delivering a verdict on the poetry.","For a century these pieces were considered unlistenable, edited but never performed. The change came with one-voice-per-part singing in the 1970s and 1980s, which made the texts audible and the dissonances sound deliberate. The current argument is about order: whether Machaut's numbering encodes a narrative running from courtly love to prayer across all twenty-three motets.","Listen once for sound alone; the sour clashes approaching each cadence are not accidents. Then listen again with the tenor's words translated in front of you and hear the whole thing turn ironic. Follow it with Puis qu'en oubli, where the same disillusion is said plainly in one short song instead of three simultaneous ones."],"machaut-foy-porter":["A single melody with no accompaniment written down, in the virelai shape: a refrain, two matching verses, a third section that leads back, then the refrain again. Four minutes at a walking pace. The tune is wide-ranging and springy, with long runs of notes at the ends of lines, and the refrain — a promise to keep faith, guard honour, obey and serve — comes round often enough to lodge in the memory after one hearing.","Machaut wrote most of his songs for one voice at a time when polyphony was the prestige medium, and went on doing so late in life. The virelai's shape descends from the round dances of the previous century, and the form still carries that swing. He was a cleric and diplomat who wrote for patrons and for himself, and the manuscripts he supervised present the songs as literature with music attached rather than the other way round.","The words are a list of feudal duties — faith, honour, obedience, service — transferred wholesale from a lord to a lady. Courtly love borrowed the vocabulary of vassalage, and this refrain is the clearest case of it in the repertory. Musically the giveaway is that the two halves of the melody end on different notes, so the refrain sounds unfinished until the very last time round.","Because the manuscripts give only a melody, everything else is a performer's decision: tempo, instruments, whether to add drones or improvise a second part. Recordings run from austere solo voice to full medieval band with percussion, and scholars have argued sharply against the busier approach, since no fourteenth-century source shows instruments accompanying songs of this kind.","Take it as a song first and try to hum the refrain back after one play. Then compare two recordings, one solo and one with instruments, and decide which you believe. It sits naturally beside Douce dame jolie, its more famous twin. The Orlando Consort sing it unaccompanied; other groups turn it into a dance, and both are defensible."],"machaut-comment-qu-a-moy-lonteinne":["A virelai for one unaccompanied voice, about five minutes, sung in a woman's person: however far away her lover is, her heart stays with him. The melody is long-breathed and mostly stepwise, falling further at the end of each phrase than it climbed, and the refrain returns four times without variation. There is no accompaniment in the manuscripts and no strongly marked pulse; the line simply unwinds and comes back.","Machaut wrote a great deal of poetry in a woman's voice, and built entire narrative poems around an exchange of letters with a young admirer, real or invented. The virelai belonged to a courtly world in which absence was the normal condition — a lover at war, a patron travelling, a clerk sent on embassy. Machaut himself spent years on the road in the service of John of Bohemia before settling at Reims.","With no harmony to lean on, range does all the expressive work: the melody saves its highest note for the final section, so the return of the refrain afterwards sounds like a settling rather than a repetition. The notation also carries fewer rhythmic signs than his polyphonic songs, which is why recorded performances can differ by a factor of two in speed and some singers treat the line as almost free.","The unaccompanied virelais were once dismissed as archaic survivals by a poet who had not caught up with polyphony. The modern view is that Machaut kept them deliberately as a different register — plainer, more personal, closer to speech. Editors still disagree about how to bar the melody, and some recent editions print it without barlines at all and leave the pacing to the singer.","Best heard alone and quietly: one voice, nothing else. Listen for the shape of the whole rather than for detail, since the refrain draws its meaning from how much time has passed since you last heard it. Then try Foy porter for the same form at a brisker tempo, and Puis qu'en oubli for Machaut in three parts."],"machaut-de-toutes-flours":["A ballade in three parts, about six minutes: one heavily decorated top line that carries all the words, and two slower parts beneath it. Each of the three stanzas runs to the same music and closes with the same line of text. The image is a garden in which every flower and fruit has been destroyed except a single rose, and Fortune is now digging at its roots. The top voice keeps stopping and restarting, as if short of breath.","The ballade was the aristocratic song form of the fourteenth century, and Machaut is where its polyphonic version becomes fully itself: one singer, two supporting lines, a text of high artifice. By the 1360s he was the most famous poet in France, his manuscripts copied for royal collectors, his forms imitated and his poems set by younger men. Fortune and her wheel are his recurring subject across poetry and music alike.","The garden is allegory rather than scenery: the surviving rose is the lady, and Fortune digging at the roots is the poem's real event. Notice how the long decorative runs fall on small unimportant words while the loaded nouns go past quickly — the mismatch is a courtly game. An Italian manuscript of about 1400, the Faenza Codex, contains an arrangement of this song for keyboard, proof that it crossed the Alps and outlived its language.","Machaut's ballades divided opinion early: an Italian theorist complained about French over-decoration while Italian scribes copied the songs anyway. Modern performance is split between all-vocal readings, with the lower parts sung to the same text, and mixed ones with fiddle and harp. Neither can be proved, since the manuscripts leave the lower lines untexted and say nothing else about them.","Follow the top voice through the first stanza and let the rest blur; on the second stanza listen only to the two lower parts and hear how independent they are. Then compare Ochi dolenti mie, a Landini ballata from the same decade, for the Italian way of decorating a song. Look for a recording that gives you all three stanzas rather than one."],"jacopo-da-bologna-non-al-suo-amante":["A madrigal for two voices, about three minutes: two stanzas to the same music, then a closing pair of lines in a different metre called the ritornello. The voices are equal partners, spinning decorated lines that meet at cadences and separate again. The text is Petrarch's, about Diana surprised while bathing, and the singer says he would rather watch his own lady washing a veil in a stream than see the goddess naked.","Jacopo worked at the Visconti court in Milan and the della Scala court in Verona in the middle of the century, when north Italian rulers competed for poets and singers as they competed for territory. Petrarch was alive, celebrated, and moving between Milan, Padua and Venice in exactly those years. This is the only fourteenth-century setting of a Petrarch text known to survive, which makes it a document of two arts brushing past each other.","Petrarch never mentions music in connection with his own poems, and there is no evidence he heard this one. Jacopo sets it as a musical madrigal because Petrarch called the poem a madrigal — the two forms share a name and little else. Listen for the ritornello at the end: the metre changes, the pace lifts, and the two voices suddenly move together, which is how these pieces sign off.","The piece carries a heavy load of significance for scholars precisely because of the Petrarch connection, and there has been long argument about whether the poet approved, disapproved, or never knew. Musically it is a fine example of its type rather than an outlier. Its survival in the Squarcialupi Codex, the great illuminated Florentine anthology of about 1410, kept it visible from the start.","Two voices only, so you can genuinely follow both. Track the lower one for a change: it is a second melody, not an accompaniment. Then hear a Landini ballata from a generation later and notice how the Italian style thickens. Recordings by trecento specialists, with countertenor and tenor and no instruments at all, are the clearest."],"jacopo-da-bologna-fenice-fu":["A two-voice madrigal of about three minutes on a text of shifting creatures: the speaker was a phoenix, lived pure and soft, and now finds herself turned into a turtledove. Two stanzas share the same music and a ritornello closes it. The upper line is heavily ornamented, spinning long runs on single syllables, while the lower moves more plainly; they cross often enough that the ear regularly loses track of which is which.","Heraldic beasts were political language in fourteenth-century Lombardy. The Visconti of Milan, whom Jacopo served, used the viper and the sun-ray as emblems, and courtly poems traded in such figures as compliments and as coded news. Madrigals like this were sung by two soloists at table or in a small chamber, reading from a single manuscript held between them, for an audience that could decipher the animals.","The bestiary is the message. A phoenix that becomes a turtledove — the bird of chastity and of mourning — reads as a change in a lady's state, a widowhood or a withdrawal, addressed to people who could supply her name. We cannot now, and the piece survives the loss: those long spinning runs are the sound of a courtly compliment drawn out as far as it will go.","Jacopo is the earliest trecento composer with a substantial surviving output, and modern assessments put him at the point where Italian secular polyphony takes its own road away from France — less rhythmically intricate, more melodically generous. Arguments about his dates rest almost entirely on the courts he can be placed at, since virtually nothing else about his life is documented.","Listen for the crossings: the two voices occupy the same range and swap places constantly, which is the characteristic trecento sound and quite unlike a French song with its clear top line. Play it directly after Non al suo amante. Ensembles who sing this with a slightly nasal, forward tone catch the idiom better than smooth cathedral blend."],"jacopo-da-bologna-oselletto-selvaggio":["A madrigal whose first stanza fills with birdsong: the voices imitate a wild bird singing through the season, chirping in short repeated figures and chasing each other's phrases. The second stanza turns sour and complains that every corner of Italy now produces a self-appointed master of music. Three minutes, two stanzas and a ritornello, with the bird noises returning at the end to mock the pretenders the words have just described.","Written in the middle of the fourteenth century for a north Italian court, at a moment when written polyphony was spreading fast and the number of people claiming to compose it was growing faster. Jacopo was both a court composer and a theorist who wrote about notation, and had a professional's contempt for amateurs. The second stanza is one of the earliest pieces of music criticism embedded inside a piece of music.","The birdcalls are argument, not decoration: the piece demonstrates real craft — close imitation between the voices, tight rhythmic control — before the text accuses everyone else of having none. The complaint also names the authorities the composer respects, so a listener in 1350 heard a joke and a hierarchy at once. It anticipates by two centuries the fashion for French chansons stuffed with birdsong.","The madrigal is quoted constantly in histories of the trecento for its text rather than its music, as evidence that composers had begun to think of themselves as a profession with standards. Other trecento madrigals grumble in similar terms, and scholars have tried to reconstruct the quarrel they imply. The imitative birdsong writing also feeds the long argument about how the caccia developed.","Wait for the change of tone: the bird is charming and then the words turn. Even without Italian you can hear the difference between the chirping figures and the flatter declamation that follows. Compare Gherardello's hunting caccia for the other way trecento music imitated the outdoors, and Janequin's birdsong chansons two centuries later if you know them."],"gherardello-tosto-che-lalba":["A caccia — a hunt — for two voices in strict canon over a slower third part, about four minutes. The second singer begins the same tune several beats after the first and never catches up, so the whole piece is a chase in sound as well as in subject. The text is a dawn hunting scene full of shouted dog names, horn calls and cries of encouragement; the overlapping voices turn it into something like a crowd.","Gherardello was a Florentine, a priest attached to the cathedral, and one of the first generation of trecento composers whose works fill the Squarcialupi Codex. Hunting was the aristocratic pastime and a favourite subject for realistic word-painting in music. The caccia flourished in Florence and the northern cities for about fifty years around the middle of the century and then vanished as completely as it had appeared.","The canon is exact — one melody, two singers, a fixed distance apart — which means every collision of words was composed in advance rather than improvised. Listen for the moments when both voices shout the same syllable simultaneously; those are the joints of the design. The slow untexted bottom line, probably played rather than sung, is what stops two identical voices turning into mush.","Cacce were long treated as picturesque curiosities, evidence of medieval realism, and only later recognised as among the most technically demanding music of their century, since strict canon over a free part is genuinely hard to write. Whether the tenor was sung or played is unresolved, as usual in this repertory. This caccia is Gherardello's most recorded piece by a wide margin.","Listen once for the sheer noise of the hunt, then again tracking only the second voice to hear it shadowing the first at a fixed distance. It works especially well live. Pair it with Lorenzo da Firenze's A poste messe, the other famous hunting canon, and with Jacopo's birdsong madrigal. Recordings that put a fiddle or organ on the bottom line give it more swing."],"lorenzo-da-firenze-a-poste-messe":["Another hunting canon, about four minutes: two voices following one another at a fixed distance over a slow supporting line. The text sets dogs on a boar — huntsmen posted, hounds named and urged forward, the noise building to the kill. Lorenzo writes the shouts as short repeated notes and the horn calls as leaping figures, and the canon turns both into echoes bouncing off the trees a beat or two later.","Lorenzo da Firenze was a Florentine cleric attached to the church of San Lorenzo and, according to a later chronicler, a teacher of Francesco Landini. He belongs to the second trecento generation, working in the decades either side of the plague's return, when Florence's guilds and confraternities supported an unusually dense musical life. His surviving output is small and, piece for piece, the most experimental of the period.","Lorenzo is the trecento's odd man out. Several of his works call for notes outside the standard medieval scale and for rhythmic devices borrowed from France, and his manuscripts carry accidentals that editors still argue over. Here the strangeness is rhythmic rather than harmonic: the canon's following distance is short enough that the two voices are often barely a beat apart, which is far harder to sing than it sounds.","Lorenzo's standing has risen with interest in the experimental fringes of the fourteenth century. Once a footnote to Landini, he is now discussed alongside the French ars subtilior composers for his willingness to break the rules of his own tradition. The Squarcialupi Codex reserves a decorated opening for him, which suggests Florence rated him highly around 1410.","Start with the last minute, where the chase closes in and the two voices are almost on top of each other, then go back for the build-up. Hear it beside Gherardello's Tosto che l'alba, written a decade earlier, and judge which hunt is wilder. Groups with sharp consonants and a bit of raw tone serve it far better than smooth ones."],"lorenzo-da-firenze-sovra-la-riva":["A two-voice madrigal of about three minutes set on a riverbank: two stanzas to the same music and a ritornello to finish. The upper voice moves in long ornamental spirals, the lower more slowly, and both are drawn through harmonies that keep sliding a half-step where the ear expects a whole one. The result is a pastoral scene with something distinctly uneasy underneath it, and cadences that arrive slightly off-colour.","Florence in the 1360s and 1370s was rebuilding after plague and war, and its cathedral, baptistery and parish churches employed singers who also wrote secular songs. Lorenzo, a cleric at San Lorenzo, was one of them. Madrigals were sung by two soloists reading from one book at private gatherings rather than in public, for an audience that knew the repertory and could hear a deviation from it.","The chromatic inflections are the thing to listen for. Fourteenth-century notation left many accidentals unwritten, to be supplied by singers according to convention, but Lorenzo writes some of his in — which means he wanted the strangeness heard rather than smoothed away. Editors who follow the manuscript literally produce a sound that startles listeners who expect medieval music to be modal and calm.","How much chromaticism to apply in trecento music is one of the field's oldest quarrels, and Lorenzo sits at its centre because his sources are unusually explicit. Performances differ audibly depending on the edition used. His small output, a couple of dozen pieces, has made him a test case rather than a repertory staple, though recordings have multiplied since the 1990s.","Listen for the moments where the harmony leans somewhere you did not expect; if a recording sounds bland, find one that uses a less cautious edition. Then hear a Jacopo madrigal of twenty years earlier for the plainer version of the same form. Two solo voices with no instruments is the arrangement that lets the inflections register."],"ciconia-una-panthera":["A madrigal in three voices, about four minutes, praising a panther that walks in the company of Mars and Jupiter and guards a city. Two upper voices decorate in close imitation over a slower tenor, and the writing is brighter and more clearly directed than a trecento madrigal of fifty years earlier, with cadences that land where the ear now expects them. The closing ritornello lifts into faster, dancing motion.","The panther was the heraldic beast of Lucca. Ciconia, a singer and priest from Liège, spent his career in Italy — Rome, probably Pavia, and from about 1401 Padua cathedral — at the moment when northern counterpoint and Italian melody fused into something new. Italian city states used ceremonial music as they used painted banners, and this madrigal is a civic advertisement for an occasion nobody has securely identified.","The panther is a coat of arms, not an exotic animal, and the poem's Mars and Jupiter are the planets governing the city's fortune: political astrology addressed to people who took it entirely seriously. Musically, listen for the two top voices imitating each other at close range. That northern habit, rare in earlier Italian song, is what makes Ciconia sound like the start of the next century rather than the end of this one.","Ciconia was rediscovered in the twentieth century and promoted as the missing link between the trecento and Dufay's generation, a role since questioned as too tidy. Which of the two men named Ciconia in the Liège documents wrote the music was disputed for decades; the later dates, putting his birth around 1370, are now generally accepted and make him a contemporary of the ars subtilior.","Notice how much more purposefully this moves than an earlier Italian madrigal — the harmony pulls forward instead of circling. Listen next to his motet Doctorum principem for the same voice in ceremonial mode, then to Dufay's Nuper rosarum flores, written a generation later for another Italian civic occasion. Recordings pairing voices with soft winds catch the outdoor character."],"ciconia-doctorum-principem":["A ceremonial motet for a living man, about five minutes: two upper voices singing different Latin poems in bright imitation over a supporting tenor. It opens with fanfare-like calls passed between the voices, an unmistakable imitation of trumpets, and ends with both voices calling the honorand's name together. Between those two markers the texture is unusually transparent for a medieval motet, and the words are actually audible.","Francesco Zabarella was the leading canon lawyer of his day, archpriest and later bishop of Padua, and the man who did most to broker an end to the Great Schism at the Council of Constance. Ciconia was a cantor at Padua cathedral under him from about 1401 until his death around 1412. Italian cities used motets of this kind at graduations, installations and formal entries into the city.","The trumpet figures at the opening are the giveaway: real trumpeters preceded a dignitary in procession, and Ciconia writes their calls into the voices so the music stages the arrival it celebrates. He also builds the piece in matching halves, the second reworking the first at speed. The slow tenor is probably newly composed rather than borrowed chant, which was still unusual for a motet.","These occasional motets were once dismissed as flattery set to music. They are now read as the earliest surviving music written for named living individuals on datable civic occasions, which makes them unusually precious to historians. Dating rests on the steps of Zabarella's career, and scholars disagree over which promotion this one was written to mark.","Listen first for the fanfare, then for the moment the two voices land on the name together — you will hear it without knowing any Latin. Compare Dufay's Nuper rosarum flores of 1436, the same civic genre a generation on. Recordings that double the lines with cornetts and sackbuts are historically arguable and thrilling; all-vocal ones are clearer."],"ciconia-le-ray-au-soleyl":["Three minutes of music written down as one line of notes. Three singers read that same line at three different speeds, in the proportion three to two to one, so a single melody becomes a three-part canon that stays consonant with itself from beginning to end. The text is a fragment about a sun-ray and a turtledove. What you hear is a slow bass, a middle voice at walking pace and a quick top line, all the same tune.","The sun-ray with a turtledove was a badge of Gian Galeazzo Visconti, ruler of Milan until his death in 1402, and the piece is generally taken as a compliment to him or his household. Ciconia was in Italy by the 1390s, inside a courtly culture that prized musical puzzles: notation had grown sophisticated enough to encode a trick, and demonstrating the solution was part of the entertainment.","Nothing in the manuscript states the speeds directly. They are given by mensuration signs, the medieval equivalent of time signatures, placed at the start of the single line. Solve the signs and the piece exists; misread them and it collapses into noise. That makes it a proportion canon a full century before Flemish composers made the device famous, and it earns the piece a place in every history of notation.","Puzzle pieces like this have been read two ways: as courtly party tricks, and as serious speculative music in the tradition that treated number as the link between sound and the cosmos. Both readings are probably right at once. Modern editions differ over the exact proportions and over whether all three voices should carry the text or one of them be played instead.","Try to hear the same tune three times at once: start with the slowest voice and follow it to the end, then rewind and follow the fastest. Machaut's palindrome Ma fin est mon commencement is the obvious companion, and Ockeghem's Missa prolationum is where the idea goes next. Any recording will do, because what you are listening to is a design."],"solage-fumeux-fume":["A rondeau in three voices, five slow minutes, written unusually low: the parts sit at the bottom of the male range and the sound is dark and clotted. The harmony slides by half-steps into regions fourteenth-century music was not supposed to reach, wandering and doubling back before every cadence. The text is barely more than a pun repeated — the smoker smokes through smoke his smoky thoughts — and the music takes it entirely seriously.","Solage is a name attached to ten pieces in the Chantilly Codex and to almost nothing else; he probably served Jean, Duke of Berry, the greatest collector of his age, in the 1380s and 1390s. Paris then had a mock-order of fumeurs, invented by the poet Eustache Deschamps, whose members cultivated a fashionable moody eccentricity. This song is their anthem, and its strangeness is a joke played with a straight face.","The chromaticism is not random. The piece descends through a chain of harmonies that keeps lowering the ground by half-steps, so the ear loses its bearings at exactly the rate the text loses its sense. Fumeux in Deschamps' usage means something like fuming, vaporous, given to black moods: the fumes are the humours of medieval medicine rising to the brain, not tobacco, which Europe had not yet seen.","The ars subtilior was long described as decadent, a late-Gothic dead end of over-refinement before Dufay restored good sense. That verdict has been abandoned, and the Chantilly repertory is now heard as a deliberate experimental phase with its own logic. Fumeux fume is the piece everyone cites, and it has drawn attention from composers and listeners well outside the early-music world.","Play it loud and low in a dark room. Do not hunt for a tune; listen instead to the ground sinking underneath. Then hear Gesualdo three centuries later for the other famous chromatic outlier, and Solage's Chantilly contemporaries for the rhythmic side of the same experiment. Recordings by low male voices, unaccompanied, are the ones to seek out."],"cordier-belle-bonne-sage":["A three-voice rondeau of about three minutes, a New Year's gift-song offering the singer's heart to a lady. The top voice is decorated with small quick figures that cut across the beat, while the two lower parts move more steadily beneath, and the whole thing is over almost before it has settled. Heard without knowing anything else, it is a light and charming song rather than a difficult one.","The Chantilly Codex, copied around 1410, gathers the most elaborate French songs of the previous thirty years. Cordier — probably Baude Fresnel, a harper and valet de chambre to Philip the Bold of Burgundy who travelled as far as Scotland and Cyprus — stands at the front of it. New Year gifts of jewels, poems and songs were a social obligation at the Burgundian and French courts, and this is one of them.","The famous thing is on the page rather than in the ear: the music is written out in the shape of a heart, with red note-heads picking out the syncopations and the word for heart in the text replaced by a drawn one. It is a visual pun only the performers, holding the book, could see. Notation as picture was a brief fashion around 1400, and this is its best-known survivor.","The heart-shaped page is reproduced in every general history of music, which has made the song far better known as an image than as sound. Identifying Cordier with Baude Fresnel, proposed in the 1990s on documentary grounds, is now widely but not universally accepted. Whether the eye-music affects performance at all is a fair question: heard blind, this is a good song, not a strange one.","Look at a photograph of the manuscript page first — it takes ten seconds and changes the listening. Then hear it beside Tout par compas, the circular canon by the same composer, and beside Senleches' harp-shaped virelai. Light, quick performances with one voice per part, perhaps with a harp, suit the New Year occasion best."],"cordier-tout-par-compas":["A three-voice rondeau, about three minutes, whose text announces what it is: composed all by compasses, and properly sung in a circle. The upper writing is a canon that arrives back where it began, so the singers can go round as many times as they like before stopping. The two supporting parts are unhurried, and the interest lies in that circling voice, which never quite cadences with any finality.","The same Cordier who wrote the heart-shaped rondeau, and probably the same New Year season at a Burgundian or French court. The two songs sit together at the head of the Chantilly Codex and were plainly copied as a matching pair of showpieces. Circle and heart are emblems as much as jokes: the circle stands for eternity, and a work drawn with compasses is by definition a well-made one.","The text is also a signature, since the composer names himself in it, which is rare enough to be worth noticing. The pun on compas covers both the drawing instrument and the idea of correct measure in music. In the manuscript the circle of notes is ringed by smaller circles carrying verse instructions. Because the canon is endless, no source tells the singers when to stop; that decision is left to them.","Like its companion, this is a piece whose fame rests on its page. Musicologists have used the pair to argue that late fourteenth-century notation had become a medium for wit, closer to emblem books than to modern scores. Sceptics reply that eye-music is a tiny fraction of the repertory and that the Chantilly Codex's real difficulty is rhythmic rather than visual.","Count how many times round the singers go and notice that you cannot hear a seam. Then compare Ciconia's proportion canon and Machaut's palindrome for the other ways medieval composers made music fold back on itself. Recordings differ in how many rotations they take, and a short one leaves you wanting the trick again."],"senleches-la-harpe-de-melodie":["A virelai in three voices, five or six minutes. The text describes making harmony on the harp of melody, well tuned, and the music answers with a top line of extreme rhythmic complication: figures that cut across the beat, notes grouped in fives and sevens against the steadier motion below. Two slower voices hold the frame while the surface refuses to line up with it, and the misalignment is the whole effect.","Jacob de Senleches was a harper as well as a composer, in the service of Eleanor of Aragon and later of Cardinal Pedro de Luna, the Aragonese churchman who became the Avignon pope Benedict XIII. The courts of Avignon, Aragon and Foix formed a circuit along which the most intricate French style travelled in the 1380s and 1390s, well away from Paris and largely independent of it.","One manuscript, now in Chicago, writes the piece out on the strings of a drawn harp, with a scroll of verse instructions wound round the frame telling the singers how to read it: the player's instrument becomes the page. The rhythmic notation is among the most demanding of the century, using coloured notes and unusual signs to specify divisions no earlier system could express at all.","The complexity of the ars subtilior was long taken as evidence of exhaustion. Since the 1970s, with an assist from composers writing very complicated music of their own, it has been reheard as virtuoso experiment. Whether singers of the 1390s could perform this accurately, or whether such pieces were partly meant to be admired on the page, remains open and rather pointed.","Keep your attention on one of the lower voices; the top line will then sound as though it has arrived from a different rhythm entirely, which is the intended effect. Look up the harp-shaped page. Then hear Cordier's heart and Solage's Fumeux fume for the two other faces of this repertory. Ensemble PAN and later groups have recorded it well."],"senleches-fuions-de-ci":["A ballade for three voices, about five minutes, in which the singer tells his fellow musicians to flee: the one who fed them is dead and there is nothing left here for any of them. The top line is elaborately syncopated in the manner of its decade, but the mood is grey rather than dazzling, and the refrain returns once per stanza, three times in all, each time onto the same resigned cadence.","Eleanor of Aragon, queen of Castile, died in 1382, and Senleches, a harper in her household, wrote this on the loss of his employer. Musicians in the fourteenth century were retainers: a patron's death meant unemployment, travel and a fresh search for a place. Senleches found one soon afterwards at the Aragonese court and later with Cardinal Pedro de Luna at Avignon, so the song records a career turning.","The poem names instruments and colleagues, so it doubles as a snapshot of a court music establishment — the harp, and the poor company of players who must now scatter and look elsewhere. It is one of very few medieval songs datable to a month by an external event, which makes it a fixed point for dating the whole ars subtilior style that surrounds it in the manuscripts.","The piece is prized by historians for its documentary value as much as its music, since it shows a professional musician writing about his own working conditions, something that almost never happens again before the sixteenth century. Attempts to identify the individuals named have produced plausible but unproven candidates. Its date anchors the chronology of the Chantilly repertory.","Read a translation first; this is a farewell letter and loses half its force without the words. Listen for the refrain each time it returns, and for how sparingly the elaborate rhythms are used for display here. Follow it with La harpe de melodie, by the same man in dazzling mode, to hear how differently he could use the same technique."],"power-missa-alma-redemptoris-mater":["Four movements of the Ordinary — Gloria, Credo, Sanctus, Agnus Dei — for three voices, about twenty minutes, all built on the same borrowed melody: the Marian antiphon Alma redemptoris mater, laid out in long notes in the middle voice. Because one tune underlies every movement, the Mass sounds unified in a way earlier cycles do not. The surface is smooth, full of sweet thirds and sixths, and the words are set clearly.","Leonel Power spent his career in English aristocratic and cathedral service, first in the household chapel of Thomas, Duke of Clarence, later at Canterbury, where he trained the choristers. English polyphony of the 1420s was admired on the continent for its sweetness, and manuscripts copied in northern Italy preserve much of it, including this Mass. The English sources themselves were largely destroyed at the Reformation.","The idea that makes it matter is simple and had not been done before: take one chant, run it through every movement, and a set of separate pieces becomes a single work. Listen for the antiphon's opening rise in the middle voice at the start of each movement. The chant is not decorated but stretched, so a melody lasting a minute in plainsong spreads across five.","Priority is disputed. This Mass and Dunstaple's cycles are the earliest surviving Ordinaries unified by a single borrowed melody, and at least one movement long given to Power is credited to Dunstaple elsewhere, a warning about English attributions in general. The technique passed to Dufay's generation and became the standard way of building a Mass for a century and a half.","Listen to the Gloria and the Agnus Dei back to back and try to hear the same slow tune underneath both. The English sound — thirds and sixths where French music of the same date is bare — registers immediately. The Hilliard Ensemble and Gothic Voices recorded this repertory memorably; a small group of male voices in a resonant church is the right frame."],"pycard-gloria-old-hall":["One of the most elaborate pieces in the Old Hall Manuscript: a Gloria in five voices, around six minutes, in which two parts sing a strict canon while three others weave freely around them. The text is divided between groups, so phrases are tossed from one side to the other and then gathered up by everyone at once. Long stretches move in quick, springing rhythm, and the sound is bright, busy and full of thirds.","The Old Hall Manuscript, copied in the 1410s and early 1420s, is the great surviving collection of English polyphony from before the Reformation, probably compiled for a royal household chapel; the names in it include one Roy Henry, thought to be Henry IV or Henry V. Pycard, whose first name is unknown and who may have belonged to John of Gaunt's chapel, contributes some of its most complicated music.","The canon is hidden in plain sight: two voices sing the same line at a fixed distance while the other three fill the space around them, so the trick is buried in the texture rather than displayed. Pycard wrote several such pieces, one of them a canon in four parts. English composers of this generation loved these puzzles as much as their French contemporaries, but wrapped them in far sweeter harmony.","Old Hall was long treated as a provincial curiosity until scholars noticed how advanced its notation and canonic writing actually are. Pycard's identity is unsettled — a Picard by origin, perhaps a keyboard player named in Gaunt's accounts — and his handful of pieces are discussed mainly as technical feats. The manuscript's importance is now beyond dispute: without it, English music before Dunstaple would be almost invisible.","Do not chase the canon; enjoy the surface, which is unusually energetic for church music of 1410. Listen for the passages where voices alternate in pairs, then for the sudden full sound when all five sing together. Afterwards try Power's Mass and Dunstaple's motets for where this school goes next. Groups with high, bright voices record it best."],"binchois-de-plus-en-plus":["A rondeau in three parts: a texted top line, and two lower voices that singers of the time were as likely to play as to sing. The refrain comes round three times in four minutes, each return carrying different words. What you hear is one long melody, gently syncopated, arching up and settling into soft cadences where two of the voices slide outward to an open fifth and octave. Nothing hurries, and nothing is repeated for emphasis.","Binchois spent his working life in the chapel of Philip the Good, Duke of Burgundy, the richest court in Europe, whose musicians moved with it between Lille, Bruges and Dijon. Chansons like this were copied into small, expensively decorated songbooks for aristocratic households, and the courtly-love text, a lover's promise that his desire grows and grows, was exactly the subject expected. He had reportedly been a soldier before he was a singer.","Ockeghem took the tenor line of this song and built an entire Mass on it, the Missa De plus en plus, the borrowed love tune stretched into slow notes underneath the Kyrie. Other composers quoted its opening phrase too. A four-minute court song became structural raw material, which in the fifteenth century was the highest compliment a melody could be paid.","Around 1441 the poet Martin le Franc wrote that Du Fay and Binchois had taken up the contenance angloise, the English manner of Dunstaple, and made their music sweeter for it. That one sentence has shaped every account of the period since, and it fixed Binchois as one of the two leading composers of his generation. Modern taste has generally preferred Du Fay's ambition to Binchois's economy.","Four minutes, so play it twice. The first time follow only the top voice and notice how long its phrases are before they come to rest. The second time follow the bottom line and hear how often it thins away to almost nothing just before a cadence. Ensembles disagree over singing all three parts or giving two to a harp and a fiddle; both are defensible and sound quite different."],"binchois-dueil-angoisseux":["A ballade, which means three stanzas to the same music, each ending with the same line of text: anguished grief, furious rage. The pace is slower than a rondeau and the top line moves in long, sagging phrases that keep falling further than you expect before turning back. Underneath, the two lower voices cross and uncross. Six minutes of one musical shape repeated three times, so that the words rather than the music supply the change.","The poem is by Christine de Pizan, the first woman in France to make a living by writing, and it comes from the sequence of ballades she wrote in widowhood after her husband died in an epidemic in 1390. Binchois set it perhaps forty years later for the Burgundian court. Setting a named living or recently dead poet, and a woman's voice of mourning, was unusual for the chanson repertory.","Listen to where the top voice sits at the start of each phrase and where it ends: almost every one begins high and slides down, so the melody keeps sinking. The final cadence of the refrain arrives on a bare open sound with no third in it, hollow rather than warm, which was already an old-fashioned effect by 1430 and here reads as deliberate coldness.","Binchois's reputation has always rested on melody rather than construction, and this is the piece usually produced as evidence. Early-music performers argue over tempo: taken briskly it becomes a courtly complaint, taken slowly it becomes something closer to a funeral song, and the manuscripts give no guidance. There is also no way to know whether Binchois knew Christine de Pizan's poem as a widow's lament or simply as a fine text.","Read the poem in translation first, because the music sets one stanza's worth of shape to three stanzas' worth of meaning and you will want to know what changes. Then listen for the refrain line each time it returns. Compare a version sung by a solo voice with lower parts played, and one sung by three voices; the first sounds like a person speaking, the second like a ritual."],"binchois-ave-regina-caelorum":["One of the four seasonal antiphons to the Virgin, sung at the end of the day's last office. Three voices, four minutes, and the plainchant is not hidden away in the bass but carried in the top line, decorated just enough that it still sounds like chant with an accent. The lower two voices move with it almost note for note, so the piece proceeds as a chain of full, sweet chords rather than as independent lines chasing each other.","Compline antiphons were sung daily by every religious house in Europe, and settings like this gave a chapel a way to make a routine moment sound special without much rehearsal. Binchois wrote a great deal of this kind of practical sacred music alongside his chansons, and it circulated widely in continental manuscripts. He ended his life as a canon at Soignies, in Hainaut, on a comfortable church income earned by decades of court service.","The harmony leans overwhelmingly on thirds and sixths, intervals that medieval theory had ranked as imperfect and slightly unstable, and that this generation heard as simply beautiful. That preference is the audible content of the contenance angloise the poets talked about. Notice too how rarely the three voices arrive at a cadence together: one usually holds on a beat longer, which keeps the music moving through what would otherwise be a full stop.","Ockeghem marked Binchois's death in 1460 with a lament, Mort, tu as navré de ton dart, which quotes plainchant for the dead and names him directly. That a younger composer took the trouble tells us how he was regarded. His sacred music was long treated as the lesser half of his output; recordings since the 1990s, particularly by ensembles built around a small consort of male voices, have argued otherwise.","Find a recording that alternates the polyphony with plainchant, which is how it would have been heard, and notice how much warmer the harmonised sections feel arriving after unison. Then listen only to the top voice: you are hearing the chant, lightly ornamented. Du Fay's Ave maris stella makes a natural companion piece and uses a related trick."],"busnois-in-hydraulis":["A Latin motet in two halves, four voices, six minutes. Three of the parts move freely; the tenor does something odd, repeating one short phrase over and over at different pitch levels and different speeds while everything else changes around it. The effect is of a machine running underneath a conversation. The second half opens with a change of metre that lifts the whole texture, and the piece ends by naming its own composer.","It is addressed to Johannes Ockeghem, the most admired musician in Europe, by a younger man who wanted to be taken seriously. Busnois calls himself in the text an unworthy musician of the Count of Charolais, the title Charles the Bold held only until he became Duke of Burgundy in 1467, which dates the piece. The opening lines describe Pythagoras discovering the ratios of the intervals, and a hydraulis, the ancient water organ.","The tenor's repeated phrase is not decoration but demonstration: it returns at intervals that spell out the very ratios the text credits Pythagoras with finding. So the motet argues its subject in sound while stating it in words. Busnois also builds his own name into the closing section, an act of self-advertisement inside a piece of praise, and one that later composers copied.","The motet is the main evidence for a link between Busnois and Ockeghem, and scholars have used it to argue that the younger man studied with the elder, though the text says only that he admires him. It also anchors the dating of much of Busnois's work. As a piece of music it is admired more than loved: the tenor scheme fascinates analysts and can sound arid on a first hearing.","Listen once for pleasure and once with the text in front of you, because half the point is the fit between what is said and what is done. The moment to wait for is the metre change at the start of the second half, where the music visibly relaxes. Follow it with Compère's Omnium bonorum plena, written a few years later, which does something similar with a whole list of composers."],"busnois-missa-l-homme-arme":["A complete Mass Ordinary in four voices, about half an hour, every movement built on the same short, blunt tune about a man in armour who is to be feared. Busnois puts it in the tenor in long notes, but keeps changing its speed and starting pitch from section to section, so it is sometimes a slow foundation and sometimes almost fast enough to sing along with. The upper voices are springy, angular and much busier than Du Fay's.","The armed-man tune generated something like forty Masses across a century and a half, an unmatched tradition, and its popularity has been connected to the Burgundian Order of the Golden Fleece and to crusade rhetoric after the fall of Constantinople in 1453. Busnois was in the service of Charles the Bold, the Order's sovereign, whose campaigns ended with his death in the snow outside Nancy in 1477.","Richard Taruskin argued in 1986 that Busnois did not merely use the tune but wrote it, and that this Mass is the head of the whole tradition rather than one entry in it. The evidence is circumstantial, resting partly on how completely the tune's phrases suit Busnois's habits of rhythm. If he is right, one composer invented the most-used melody in European sacred music and then hid inside its anonymity.","The Mass survives in the Vatican choirbooks and has been securely his since the nineteenth century. Taruskin's authorship claim remains contested and unresolved; so does the relationship between this Mass and the six anonymous L'homme armé Masses in a Naples manuscript, which some read as a cycle by Busnois himself. Performers argue about whether the tenor should be sung at all or played on a slide trumpet.","Learn the tune first, which takes thirty seconds, then start with the Agnus Dei, where Busnois works it hardest. The comparison to make is with Du Fay's Mass on the same melody, written a decade earlier and far more stately, and then with Josquin's, which dismantles it. Groups of four to six men, one to a part, get closer to what the Burgundian chapel sounded like than a full choir does."],"busnois-je-ne-puis-vivre":["A bergerette, a short song form with a single verse between two statements of the refrain, so the shape is compact and the whole thing takes four minutes. Three voices, the melody in the top part, and a rhythmic character quite unlike Binchois: clipped phrases, quick turns, cadences that arrive slightly before you brace for them. The lower voices are genuinely independent lines rather than harmonic support, and the bass leaps about.","Busnois wrote for the Burgundian court in its last brilliant decade, when Charles the Bold spent enormous sums on chapel and household while losing a war. Chansons of this kind were the currency of court flirtation, copied into songbooks that circulated as gifts. Several of Busnois's texts are built around wordplay on names, which suggests a specific social world where everyone knew who was meant.","The verse section conceals an acrostic: read down the opening letters of its lines and you get the name Jacqueline d'Hacqueville, a woman of the French royal circle who appears the same way in at least one other Busnois song. Nobody knows what the relationship was. The music gives nothing away, which is presumably the point of hiding a name where only a reader, not a listener, can find it.","The acrostics were noticed in the twentieth century and have driven a small industry of biographical speculation, most of it unprovable. What is not in doubt is the technical standard: Busnois's chansons were copied more widely than almost anyone's, reprinted by Petrucci a generation later, and used as raw material by Josquin's contemporaries. He was the model for what a secular part-song could be.","Listen for the bass line, which is where Busnois's personality sits: it jumps in ways Binchois's never does. Then find the text with the acrostic marked and read down the margin. Recordings that use one voice to a part with lute or harp doubling let you hear the counterpoint; larger vocal ensembles smooth it into something prettier and less sharp."],"isaac-innsbruck-ich-muss-dich-lassen":["A farewell to a city, three minutes long, in four voices. Two settings survive: one keeps the tune in the tenor with the other parts moving freely round it, the other puts the melody on top and lets all four voices move together, chord by chord, phrase by phrase, with a small breath between each line of text. It is the second that everybody knows, and its plainness is the whole effect.","Isaac was a Fleming who worked for the Medici in Florence and then became court composer to Maximilian I, whose Habsburg government sat at Innsbruck in the Tyrol. Courtiers followed the emperor around and were always leaving somewhere. The German part-song of the period was a middle-class as well as a courtly genre, printed in cheap collections, and this one entered that repertory and stayed in it.","The tune outlived its words. Lutherans replaced the text with O Welt, ich muss dich lassen, farewell to the world rather than to a town, and it became a chorale sung at deathbeds and funerals. Bach harmonised it twice in the St Matthew Passion. Brahms, at the very end of his life, wrote two organ preludes on it among his last completed pieces. A courtier's goodbye became four centuries of music about dying.","Whether Isaac composed the melody or arranged one already circulating is unresolved and probably unresolvable; the tune appears in sources close to his and the four-part settings are certainly his work. There is a further argument about the city: some scholars have proposed that the original text said Isbruck meaning Innsbruck, others that a different place name was fitted to it later. The music is unaffected either way.","Start with the homophonic setting, where all four parts move together, and simply follow the top line; it takes three minutes and needs no preparation. Then listen to a Bach chorale on the same melody, and then to Brahms's organ prelude, and hear four hundred years of the same eight phrases. Recordings by small mixed choirs suit it better than large ones."],"isaac-quis-dabit-capiti-meo-aquam":["A five-minute lament for four voices on a Latin poem beginning who will give water to my head, that I may weep. The upper parts move in close, chafing imitation, full of falling figures. The tenor is silent for long stretches, which leaves an audible hole in the middle of the sound, and when it finally enters it sings a single line of liturgy rather than the poem the others are singing. The ending is abrupt.","Lorenzo de' Medici died at Careggi outside Florence on 8 April 1492, and the city's cultural machine went into mourning. Isaac had been in Medici employment as a singer at the cathedral and the Baptistery and as a music teacher in the household. The poem is by Angelo Poliziano, Lorenzo's friend and the finest Latin poet in Italy, who died two years later. The two men were part of the same circle.","Poliziano's text has the laurel struck down by lightning, and laurel is Lauro, Lorenzo's name and personal emblem, so the image is a pun performed as an elegy. The tenor's late entry sings Et requiescat in pace, the words from the Mass for the dead, cutting across the classical Latin poetry of the other voices. Some sources notate the piece in black notes, a visual sign of mourning readable only by the singers.","The piece is one of the earliest surviving musical memorials to a named private individual rather than a monarch or a fellow composer, and it is regularly cited in arguments about when European music began to commemorate persons rather than offices. Its date is secure. What remains unclear is where it was sung, and whether at a public memorial or in the private setting of the Medici household.","Read the poem first, in any translation; it is short and its central image is the point. Then listen for the moment the low voice finally enters, roughly two thirds through, and notice that it is singing different words to different music. Josquin's lament for Ockeghem and Gombert's for Josquin make an obvious sequence to hear afterwards."],"isaac-choralis-constantinus":["Not a single work but a system: polyphonic settings of the Proper of the Mass, the texts that change every day, for the whole church year, in three enormous books. Roughly a hundred Mass Propers, each with its Introit, Gradual, Alleluia, Offertory and Communion, mostly for four voices, mostly built on the plainchant for that feast. Nobody performs it entire; recordings take one day of the calendar at a time, which lasts about forty minutes.","The chapter of Constance cathedral commissioned settings of their own local chant in 1508, and Isaac, by then court composer to Maximilian I, took the job and kept expanding it for the rest of his life. He died in Florence in 1517 leaving it unfinished. His pupil Ludwig Senfl assembled, completed and ordered the material, and it was finally printed in Nuremberg between 1550 and 1555, decades after both patron and composer had moved on.","Because the chant is local, the collection preserves the melodies actually sung in one south German diocese around 1500, some of which survive nowhere else. Isaac treats them differently from feast to feast: sometimes the chant is a slow foundation, sometimes it is passed between voices phrase by phrase, sometimes it is paraphrased so freely that it disappears. The set is effectively a catalogue of every technique available to him.","It was long treated as an industrial monument rather than music, admired for scale and left unplayed, and the standard modern edition took most of the twentieth century to complete. Attribution is genuinely mixed: the third book in particular contains work by Senfl, and separating master from pupil is an ongoing scholarly task. Recordings since the 1990s, taking single feasts, have made the case that the music itself is first rate.","Do not try to listen to it as a whole. Pick one feast, ideally Christmas or Easter, and hear the five Proper movements in liturgical order with the chant sung between them, which is how they were designed to work. The pleasure is in the alternation of plainchant and polyphony across an hour rather than in any one movement."],"agricola-de-tous-biens-plaine":["Agricola wrote at least five different pieces on the same borrowed melody, the tenor of a famous love song by Hayne van Ghizeghem. Each is two or three minutes long. The borrowed line walks calmly through the middle while the parts above and below it do something close to the opposite of calm: long, unbroken chains of running notes that leap, double back and refuse to cadence. Played on viols, they sound almost improvised.","By 1500 the tenor of Hayne's chanson had become a communal object, set by Josquin, Obrecht, Isaac, Ghiselin and many others, the way a jazz standard is a shared vehicle. Agricola spent his career moving between Milan, Florence, Cambrai and finally the Habsburg-Burgundian chapel of Philip the Handsome, and he died of plague at Valladolid in Spain in 1506 while the court was travelling.","In one of the settings the borrowed tenor is set against itself in canon, so the same tune runs twice at different distances while the free voice weaves through both. Across the group, Agricola never once repeats a solution. Because the pieces carry only the original song's opening words and no more, they were almost certainly meant to be played rather than sung, which makes them among the earliest instrumental variations in European music.","Agricola's contemporaries found him strange: sixteenth-century writers describe his style with words suggesting oddity and wildness rather than praise, and later historians followed them, treating him as an eccentric beside Josquin. That verdict has been revised since his five-hundredth anniversary in 2006, when consort ensembles recorded him extensively and the restlessness started to sound like a deliberate and consistent aesthetic.","Find a recording of Hayne's original chanson first, which takes three minutes, so that you know the tune Agricola keeps burying. Then hear two or three of his settings back to back and try to follow the middle part; the difficulty is the experience. A viol consort suits this music better than voices, and the best performances let the lines sound slightly untidy rather than polishing them."],"agricola-missa-in-minen-sin":["A complete Mass Ordinary for four voices, about half an hour, on the tune of a Dutch song about what is in my mind. The borrowed melody is handled loosely, appearing in different voices and often paraphrased past recognition. What marks the Mass out is its surface: lines that keep going when a cadence is due, ornamental runs in the middle of a solemn text, and duets between two voices that behave like the instrumental pieces Agricola wrote elsewhere.","Dutch and German songs supplied Mass tenors as readily as French ones did in this generation; Isaac set the same tune. Agricola was in his forties and in demand, poached between Italian courts and the Burgundian-Habsburg chapel, at a salary that suggests a considerable reputation. The Mass survives in manuscripts prepared in the Low Countries workshop of Petrus Alamire, whose scribes supplied the courts of Europe with luxurious choirbooks.","Compare the two-voice passages with the full-textured ones and you will hear two different composers at work. In the reduced sections Agricola writes as if for players, with long spinning lines and almost no regard for where the words fall; when all four voices sing, he behaves. That split between vocal decorum and instrumental habit runs through his whole output and is what his contemporaries found unusual.","Agricola's Masses were rarely printed and are still rarely sung, and the standard complete edition has taken decades. The recent case for him rests on the argument that his awkwardness is purposeful rather than incompetent, and that ensembles trained on Josquin's balance were simply performing him wrongly. Whether the Mass repertory sustains that case as convincingly as the instrumental pieces do is still open.","Start with the Credo, the longest movement, and listen specifically for the passages where the texture drops to two voices; those are the ones that sound like nobody else. Then hear Ockeghem's Missa Mi-mi, one generation earlier, to feel how much stranger Agricola's surface is. Ensembles of six to eight singers, one or two to a part, suit it."],"compere-omnium-bonorum-plena":["A four-voice motet in two large sections, seven minutes. It opens like any prayer to the Virgin, full of grace, and then does something no prayer does: it starts reading out names, asking her to intercede for a list of living musicians one after another. The music stays even and unhurried through the list, giving each name roughly equal weight, so the effect is of a roll call sung rather than declaimed.","The names are the profession's leadership around 1470: Du Fay first, then Busnois, Ockeghem, Regis, Caron, Tinctoris, Josquin as a young man, and Compère himself last, calling himself the least of them. Du Fay is prayed for as a living man, which puts the piece before his death in 1474. It has often been connected with a ceremony at Cambrai, where Du Fay was a canon, though the occasion is not documented.","Underneath the prayer, the tenor sings the melody of Hayne van Ghizeghem's love song De tous biens plaine, full of all good things, which supplies the motet's opening Latin words as a translation. So a chanson tenor holds up a Marian motet that is really a portrait of a profession, and the pun works in two languages at once. It is the densest piece of musical in-joking of its century.","The motet is the single most valuable document historians have for who was thought to matter in the 1470s, and it is the earliest surviving mention of Josquin by another composer, which fixes an otherwise vague early biography. Its date and occasion have been argued over for a century. As music it is less discussed, which does it a disservice: the writing is confident and the pacing unusually assured.","Get a text with the names marked and follow along; hearing Josquin's name sung by a colleague when he was perhaps twenty is the moment. Listen also for the tenor, moving slowly under everything, and try to hear that it is a love song. Busnois's In hydraulis, praising Ockeghem alone, is the obvious companion."],"larue-requiem":["A Mass for the dead in four and five voices, twenty-five minutes, and the first thing anyone notices is the pitch: the whole thing sits far lower than the period's normal range, with a bass line that at one point sinks below anything else written in the fifteenth or sixteenth century. The upper voices are dark too. Plainchant alternates with polyphony, and the polyphonic sections move slowly, in long overlapping arcs without sharp cadences.","La Rue spent nearly thirty years in the Habsburg-Burgundian court chapel, serving Philip the Handsome and then, after Philip's sudden death in Spain in 1506, his sister Margaret of Austria, regent of the Netherlands, who kept an intensely musical and famously mournful household. Court chapels of this period sang Requiems constantly, for named benefactors on their anniversaries, so a setting like this was working repertory rather than a single occasion.","The Offertory is where the bass goes lowest, on the words about delivering souls from the pit, and the sinking is plainly pictorial: the music descends as the text describes descent. Elsewhere La Rue uses the darkness structurally rather than illustratively, keeping all four voices packed close together in the bottom of their range so that the chords sound thick and slightly clouded rather than open.","The attribution is secure and the manuscripts luxurious, several from Margaret of Austria's own library. What is argued is pitch: whether such low writing was sung as notated by specialist bass singers, or whether it is a written convention meant to be transposed upward in performance. Recordings exist both ways and sound like different pieces. The connection to Philip the Handsome's funeral is plausible but unproven.","Listen to the Offertory first and simply wait for the bottom of the bass line; you will know it when it arrives. Then hear Ockeghem's Requiem, a generation earlier and much lighter in sound, to feel what La Rue is doing with register. Look for a recording using low male voices at the written pitch rather than a transposed one."],"larue-absalon-fili-mi":["Five minutes, four voices, all of them low, on King David's cry over his dead son: Absalom my son, would that I had died for you. The setting is almost entirely without imitation, the voices moving together in slow blocks of harmony. About two thirds through, on the words about going down into hell, the music begins to slide flatward, chord by chord, into regions of harmony almost nothing else in the period touches, and simply keeps sinking until it stops.","The motet appears in an English manuscript that was probably a diplomatic gift, and in a Netherlandish source close to the Habsburg court chapel where La Rue worked. Laments over the deaths of sons were painfully current: Philip the Handsome died at twenty-eight in 1506, and Margaret of Austria's circle used David's grief as a public model. Nothing ties the piece to a specific death, and it may have been written as devotional repertory.","The descent is written out with a key signature carrying more flats than any singer of 1500 would ordinarily meet, taking the music down through pitches that had no comfortable place in the modal system. It is not decoration; it is a structural fall lasting nearly a third of the piece, and it stops on the word for hell. Nobody attempted anything comparable again for at least fifty years.","For four centuries the piece was Josquin's, printed under his name and treated as evidence of his expressive daring. Joshua Rifkin argued in the early 2000s, from source transmission and from style, that it is La Rue's, and most specialists now accept the reattribution while a minority does not. The debate has quietly transformed La Rue's standing, and it is a good demonstration of how unstable Renaissance authorship is.","Do not read anything first; just listen and wait for the point, roughly three minutes in, where the harmony starts going somewhere the ear does not expect and refuses to come back. Then read the text and hear it again. Ensembles of four to eight low male voices at written pitch give the intended murk; brighter, higher performances lose the effect entirely."],"mouton-nesciens-mater":["Five minutes, eight voices, and one of the smoothest surfaces in Renaissance music: nothing sounds difficult, nothing calls attention to itself, the sound simply thickens and thins in long waves and cadences arrive softly. The text is a Christmas responsory about the Virgin giving birth without a husband. What you hear is eight parts, but what was written down is four, each shadowed by a second voice entering later and higher.","Mouton served Anne of Brittany, queen of France, then Louis XII and Francis I, in the royal chapel that travelled with the French court. He was the teacher of Adrian Willaert, who carried this manner of writing to Venice, and his music was among the first widely printed north of the Alps. French royal taste around 1510 favoured a clear, unstrained sound over the puzzle-making of the previous generation, and Mouton supplied it.","The whole piece is a quadruple canon: four written parts, each imitated exactly by a partner that starts two beats later and a fifth higher, holding that distance from beginning to end without a single adjustment. It is one of the most demanding constructions in the repertory, and it is entirely inaudible. Mouton spends his ingenuity on making the machinery disappear, which is the opposite of what Ockeghem's generation did with the same technique.","Sixteenth-century writers praised Mouton for a music that flows on an easy thread, and that praise has been used ever since to damn him as bland. The counter-argument is exactly this piece: extreme difficulty concealed rather than displayed is a deliberate aesthetic position, and it is the one Palestrina inherited. Mouton's reputation has risen as recordings have made the concealment audible to analysts and the beauty audible to everyone.","Listen first without knowing anything about the canon, and just enjoy how effortless it sounds. Then read what is happening and listen again, trying to pair each entering voice with the one it shadows; you will lose track, which is the point. Compare with Tallis's forty-voice Spem in alium, where the machinery is meant to overwhelm rather than hide."],"mouton-quis-dabit-oculis":["Six minutes for four voices, a Latin lament asking who will give our eyes a fountain of tears. The texture is close and mostly homophonic, the voices moving as a block and pausing together, so the words come through with unusual clarity for the period. Near the end the piece turns from lamenting to praying, and the music narrows to the plainest possible declamation of the words of the Mass for the dead.","Anne of Brittany, twice queen of France, died at Blois on 9 January 1514 after a reign in which she had run one of the best-endowed musical households in Europe. Mouton was her singer. The funeral observances for her lasted weeks and were among the most elaborate ever staged for a French queen. The text names Anne directly and calls on France and Brittany to mourn her, which makes the piece unusually specific for a Latin motet.","The final section abandons the poem and simply sets Requiem aeternam dona ei, grant her eternal rest, in slow chords. Switching mid-piece from a commissioned poetic text to raw liturgy turns a court compliment into an actual prayer. Some sources notate the piece in blackened notes, a purely visual signal of mourning that the audience could never see and only the singers would have registered as they sang.","This is one of a small group of political laments, alongside Josquin's for Ockeghem and Isaac's for Lorenzo de' Medici, that establish the deploration as a recognisable genre. Its documentary value is high: the text supplies details of Anne's mourning that historians use. Musically it is admired for restraint, and it is sometimes contrasted unfavourably with the more inventive laments of the previous generation.","Follow the words, which for once you will be able to hear, and wait for the switch into the Requiem text at the end; the change of register from poetry to liturgy is the whole design. Hear it alongside Isaac's lament for Lorenzo, written twenty-two years earlier, for two different answers to the same problem. A small choir in a resonant church suits it."],"brumel-missa-et-ecce-terrae-motus":["Twelve independent voices for three quarters of an hour, on a fragment of Easter chant: and behold, there was a great earthquake. Brumel takes about five notes of that chant and pushes them through the texture hundreds of times, so the same small shape keeps surfacing in different voices at different speeds until the sound is saturated with it. Whole sections thin out to three or four parts, then everything piles back in, and the full twelve-voice moments are overwhelming.","Nothing on this scale had been attempted. Four, five and six voices were normal around 1500; twelve required a chapel of exceptional size and skill, and probably a specific grand occasion, though which one is unknown. Brumel had been a singer at Chartres and Notre-Dame in Paris before taking charge of the chapel of Alfonso d'Este at Ferrara in 1506, one of the best-funded musical establishments in Italy.","The Mass survives complete in a single set of manuscript partbooks copied in Munich around 1570, more than half a century after Brumel's death, for a performance under Lassus. Those books carry annotations from that revival, so we know a specific later composer went to considerable trouble to hear it. Without that one Bavarian source the largest polyphonic Mass of its century would be lost.","The twentieth century rediscovered it slowly, and recordings from the 1990s onwards, particularly those that treat the twelve parts as a wall of sound rather than twelve soloists, made its reputation with general listeners. Arguments continue about whether all twelve lines were sung, or whether instruments doubled some, and about the date, which floats between Brumel's Paris years and his Ferrara appointment.","Start with the Credo, the longest movement, and give up any attempt to follow individual lines; listen instead to how the density changes, thinning to a couple of voices and then flooding. Then go back to the Kyrie and try to catch the earthquake motif, which is short and rises. Recordings made in large stone buildings serve it far better than dry studios."],"brumel-missa-pro-defunctis":["A Requiem in four voices, under half an hour, alternating chant with polyphony in the way a fifteenth-century Mass for the dead normally does. What is not normal is the presence of the Dies irae, the long rhyming sequence about the day of wrath, set in polyphony rather than left to plainchant. Brumel gives it plain, mostly chordal writing with the chant clearly audible, so that the text stays legible across its many verses.","The Requiem text was not fixed in 1500; different regions used different combinations of chants, and the sequence Dies irae was a comparatively recent and locally variable addition. Ockeghem's Requiem, the earliest that survives, does not include it. Brumel was working in French cathedral chapels where the Roman use was becoming standard, and setting the sequence was in part a decision about which liturgy to follow.","Because the Dies irae later became the most quoted melody in Western music, from Berlioz to horror films, this is the moment it first enters polyphonic art music. Brumel does not dramatise it. He keeps the chant near the surface and lets the verses go by in a level, unhurried tread, which makes the later tradition of using the tune as a sign of terror look like an invention of the nineteenth century.","Its status as the earliest surviving polyphonic Dies irae is regularly cited, though survival is not the same as priority and earlier settings may simply be lost. The Mass has been overshadowed by Brumel's twelve-voice Mass and by La Rue's Requiem, and is recorded far less than either. Scholars value it mainly as evidence for how the Requiem liturgy was consolidating around 1500.","Go straight to the Dies irae and listen with the knowledge that you are hearing a melody you already know from a hundred later pieces, before anyone had decided it should sound frightening. Then hear Berlioz or Rachmaninoff quoting the same tune and register the distance. Choirs that keep genuine plainchant for the alternating verses give the right shape."],"fevin-sancta-trinitas":["Four minutes, four voices, a prayer to the Trinity that opens with a rising phrase passed from voice to voice and then keeps returning to it. The writing is transparent: pairs of voices trade phrases, the full texture arrives for emphasis and withdraws again, and the harmony changes slowly enough that every entry can be heard. There is no cantus firmus holding it together, only the shapes of the phrases themselves, imitated and varied.","Févin served Louis XII in the French royal chapel and died young, around 1511 or 1512, probably in his early forties. The chapel he worked in had absorbed Josquin's manner completely, and printed music was just beginning to carry a composer's name across borders: Petrucci had started publishing in Venice in 1501, and motets like this travelled in his volumes and their successors far beyond the court that produced them.","The piece became a template. Later composers added extra voices to it, and others built entire Masses on it by taking its phrases apart and reassembling them, which is what parody technique means. So a short devotional motet turns into a quarry. Listen for how self-contained each phrase is, and you can hear why: every unit is short, clearly shaped and detachable, almost designed to be borrowed.","A sixteenth-century writer called Févin a fortunate imitator of Josquin, and the label has stuck for five hundred years, half compliment and half dismissal. Modern scholarship treats him as the composer who made Josquin's methods teachable, systematising the pairing of voices and the clear imitative entry into something any well-trained chapel could produce. He is much more often analysed than performed.","Listen for the opening phrase and count how many times it comes back; the piece is essentially one idea examined patiently. Then hear Josquin's Ave Maria, from twenty years earlier, and notice that Févin is doing the same thing with fewer moving parts and more repetition. Any good chamber choir of a dozen singers does it justice."],"gombert-musae-jovis":["Six voices, six minutes, a Latin poem calling on the Muses to weep because Josquin is dead. The texture is unusually thick: all six parts sing almost continuously, entries overlap before the previous phrase has finished, and there are very few clean cadences to breathe at. Underneath it, one voice repeats a phrase of plainchant from the burial service. The sound is dense, grave and slightly airless, which is exactly the intended effect.","Josquin died in 1521 in his home town of Condé-sur-l'Escaut, and the laments went on being written and published for decades afterwards, because by then he had become the standard against which composers were measured. Gombert had been a singer in the travelling chapel of the Emperor Charles V, and later claimed to have studied with Josquin. The piece was printed in Paris in the 1540s among a group of settings of the same poem.","The cantus firmus is drawn from the chant for the dead, so the mourning is doubly present: the poem's classical Muses on the surface, the Church's actual burial music underneath. Gombert also writes almost no rests into any part, which means no voice ever falls silent long enough for the ear to reset. That relentlessness, borrowed and intensified from Josquin, is the technical signature of his whole generation.","Gombert's life took a spectacular turn: he was condemned to the galleys for an offence against a choirboy in the imperial chapel, and, according to a sixteenth-century account, won his freedom by sending the emperor a set of Magnificats composed at sea. The story is repeated everywhere and rests on a single source. His music, by contrast, is well documented and was regarded across Europe as the model of the dense style.","Listen for what is missing: silence. Try to find a point where the music stops and re-starts, and notice how rarely it happens. Then hear Josquin's own lament for Ockeghem, written half a century earlier, which is sparer and clearer, and you have the whole shift of the intervening generation in two pieces. A choir of twenty or so keeps the lines separable."],"gombert-media-vita":["Nine minutes, six voices, on the antiphon that begins in the midst of life we are in death. Gombert never lets the texture clear. Voices enter on top of each other, phrases begin before the last has ended, and the harmony shifts by small degrees rather than by decisive cadences, so the music seems to be continuously in the middle of something. Three times the text turns to a plea, holy God, holy and strong, and the density thickens further.","Gombert served Charles V's imperial chapel, which travelled constantly with the emperor between Spain, Germany and the Low Countries, singing wherever the court stopped. The chapel had first-rate singers and no fixed acoustic. Music printing was by now industrial: Antwerp, Nuremberg and Venice presses carried Gombert's motets across Europe within a year or two of composition, and he was among the most widely reprinted composers of the 1540s.","Count the cadences. In nine minutes there are very few places where all six voices agree to stop together, and Gombert achieves this by starting a new phrase in one voice exactly where another is closing. The technique is called overlapping, and it turns polyphony from a conversation with pauses into a single unbroken stream. It is the direct ancestor of the long, seamless paragraphs of later sixteenth-century church music.","The theorist Hermann Finck wrote in 1556 that Gombert showed all musicians the way, filling his works with imitation and avoiding rests, and that description has organised every subsequent account of him. His reputation collapsed once Palestrina's clarity became the teaching model, and thick imitative writing came to be regarded as a fault. Recordings since the 1990s have restored him as the great alternative to that clarity.","Do not try to follow lines. Sit with the sound and notice how seldom it comes to rest, and how uneasy that makes a nine-minute piece feel. Then play Palestrina's Sicut cervus immediately afterwards, where everything breathes, and the contrast will explain a century of argument about church music. Large resonant spaces suit Gombert; close studio recordings expose the density unkindly."],"clemens-ego-flos-campi":["Seven voices, five minutes, on words from the Song of Songs: I am the flower of the field and the lily of the valleys. The scoring is unusual for its date, and Clemens uses the extra part to keep the sound continuously full while still moving voices in and out. The writing is warm rather than dense, with clear phrase shapes and a habit of repeating a short figure at successively higher pitches, which lifts the music without raising the volume.","The Song of Songs was read as a love poem addressed to the Virgin, so erotic Hebrew verse became Marian devotion. Clemens worked in the Low Countries towns of Bruges, 's-Hertogenbosch and Ypres, and had a close relationship with the great Marian confraternity at 's-Hertogenbosch, an immensely wealthy lay brotherhood that maintained its own singers. He also worked with the Antwerp printer Tielman Susato, who published him steadily.","The unusual number of voices may be a dedication in code: the confraternity had a small inner circle of sworn brothers, and seven parts for a Marian text written for their chapel would be a legible compliment. The suggestion is plausible rather than proven. Whatever the reason, the seventh voice changes the sound: with an odd number of parts there is no natural pairing, so the texture never quite settles into symmetry.","The nickname non Papa, not the Pope, distinguishes him from someone, but from whom is unsettled: the usual explanation, that it separates him from Pope Clement VII, has been doubted because the two were never plausibly confused, and a rival theory points to a poet-priest in Ypres called Jacobus Papa. His music was among the most printed of the mid-century and then almost entirely forgotten until the twentieth.","Listen for the rising repetitions, where the same short shape climbs step by step; it is the clearest thing in the piece and Clemens uses it as his main expressive device. Then hear Palestrina's Sicut cervus, on similarly gentle sacred verse, for a comparison between Flemish warmth and Roman clarity. A choir of about twenty, one or two extra on the top line, suits it."],"clemens-souterliedekens":["All hundred and fifty psalms, in Dutch, set for three voices, and every one of them built on a popular secular tune of the day carried in the middle voice. Each setting is short, a minute or two, and deliberately simple: no imitation to speak of, the borrowed melody plain and audible, the outer voices moving with it. Nobody performs the set complete; recordings offer a selection, and an hour of them is a generous programme.","The Dutch psalm translations had been printed in 1540 with the melodies of the street songs they were to be sung to, named in the book, so that any household could use them. Clemens set the whole collection in three parts for the Antwerp printer Tielman Susato, who issued them in 1556 and 1557 in his series of small, cheap songbooks. The market was domestic: families singing psalms at home in their own language.","The tunes are not devotional. They are drinking songs, love songs and dance tunes, and the printed books name them, so a reader could see exactly which tavern melody was carrying which psalm. Nobody involved seems to have thought this improper. The collection is consequently the largest surviving repository of sixteenth-century Netherlandish popular song, preserved by accident inside a book of psalms.","The Souterliedekens sit awkwardly in the history of Reformation music: Catholic in sponsorship, vernacular in language, popular in melody, and published in a city where confessional lines were about to harden. Historians have argued over whether they belong to Catholic devotional reform or to the Protestant psalm tradition, and the honest answer is that in 1556 Antwerp the distinction was not yet forced.","Pick any six or eight and listen to them in a row, following the middle voice, which is always the borrowed tune. Then find a recording that also performs the original secular song beside its psalm setting; hearing a dance tune turn into a psalm in the same breath is the point of the whole enterprise. Small mixed groups, one voice to a part, are ideal."],"willaert-salmi-spezzati":["Eight Vesper psalms set for two separate four-voice choirs that answer each other verse by verse and join for the closing doxology. The individual psalms run four or five minutes each. The writing is mostly chordal and syllabic, so the words are audible, and the interest lies in the exchange: one block of sound stops, another starts from a different direction, and every so often all eight parts sound at once and the room fills.","Willaert had been maestro di cappella at St Mark's in Venice since 1527, running the most prestigious church music post in Italy, and he held it until his death in 1562. Venetian Vespers on major feasts were state occasions as much as religious ones, attended by the Doge. The 1550 publication that contains these psalms is a joint volume, printing settings by Willaert alongside settings by Jachet of Mantua of the same texts.","The two choirs do not simply alternate. Willaert overlaps their endings and beginnings, so the second group is often already singing before the first has finished, which stops the exchange sounding mechanical. He also keeps the two groups in the same range rather than making one high and one low, so what changes between them is position rather than colour: the effect depends on where the sound comes from.","The romantic account has the two choirs in the opposing organ lofts of St Mark's, the architecture creating the style. David Bryant argued in 1981 from the basilica's own records that the singers were not habitually split across the building, and that the split-choir psalm was a liturgical and textual practice that Venice inherited rather than invented. The music survives the deflation; the founding legend does not.","Take one psalm, not the whole set, and listen for the handover points where one choir takes over from the other; then wait for the doxology at the end, where they combine. Follow it with Gabrieli's In ecclesiis, two generations later in the same building, to hear what happened once instruments joined in. Recordings made in St Mark's itself are worth seeking out."],"willaert-vecchie-letrose":["Two minutes of comic abuse aimed at spiteful old women who are good for nothing except sharpening pins. Four voices in a Neapolitan street idiom: fast, repetitive, entirely chordal, with the rhythm of speech rather than of song, and a refrain that comes back like a taunt. Compared with the same composer's Vesper psalms it sounds like a different person, and it is over before the joke wears out.","The villanesca alla napolitana was the rough popular part-song of Naples, and by the 1540s it had become fashionable in Venice, where sophisticated composers arranged street material for private entertainment among literary and musical academies. Willaert published a book of them in 1545. His household was the centre of Venetian musical life, and the audience for these pieces was the same one that admired his most learned work.","The music deliberately breaks the rules. Parallel fifths, forbidden in serious counterpoint and codified as forbidden by Willaert's own pupil Gioseffo Zarlino, are used openly and repeatedly, because in this idiom they signal rusticity and low comedy. The most technically strict composer in Italy is writing wrong notes on purpose, and every trained listener in the room would have caught it.","Pieces like this were long treated as trifles beside the motets and madrigals, and editions ignored them. Attitudes changed once historians began taking sixteenth-century popular culture seriously and noticed how much of it survives only where a learned composer bothered to arrange it. There is a running argument about whether Willaert's versions preserve real Neapolitan songs or parody them from a distance.","Listen once for the sheer speed, then again for the rude parallel harmonies, which sound like a village band and are meant to. Follow it with Lassus's Matona mia cara for another learned composer clowning, and then with Willaert's own psalms to feel how wide the same man's range was. Small ensembles that let the singers act it rather than blend do it best."],"rore-da-le-belle-contrade":["A five-voice madrigal on a sonnet about lovers parting at dawn. It opens calmly, describing the morning star rising, and then the woman speaks, and the music changes completely: the metre shifts, the voices lock into chords and move together, and her words come out at a different speed from everything around them. When her speech ends, the earlier manner returns, altered. Five minutes, and the whole design turns on that one intrusion of a voice.","Rore had been maestro di cappella to Ercole II d'Este at Ferrara, then at Parma, and briefly succeeded Willaert at St Mark's in Venice before dying at Parma in 1565. This madrigal was published in his fifth book for five voices in 1566, after his death. The Italian madrigal by then was a genre for connoisseurs, sung one to a part in private rooms by people who could read the poetry as closely as the music.","The trick is that the poem contains direct speech and the music marks it as such. Everything outside the quotation marks is narration; everything inside is set differently, so that the listener can hear where the woman's voice begins and ends. Nothing in the previous century had needed music to do that. Once a composer can indicate who is speaking, opera becomes conceivable.","In the argument that broke out around 1600 over whether words should govern harmony, Monteverdi's defenders named Rore as the man who started the new practice, placing him at the head of the line that leads to opera. That claim shaped his reputation permanently and is still the frame in which he is discussed. Whether the honour belongs to him alone, or to a Ferrarese circle, is debated.","Read a translation of the sonnet and mark where the woman starts speaking; then listen and catch the moment the music changes to signal it. Play it twice. Then hear Monteverdi's Cruda Amarilli, forty years later, where the same principle has become an argument, and Gesualdo, where it becomes an obsession. One voice to a part is how it was meant to sound."],"rore-missa-praeter-rerum-seriem":["A Mass for seven voices, thirty-five minutes, built out of a six-voice motet by Josquin about the miracle of the Virgin birth. Rore does not merely quote the older piece: he takes its phrases apart and rebuilds each movement from them, adding a seventh voice that Josquin never wrote. The sound is broad and low-lying, with long spans between cadences and moments where the borrowed chant phrase surfaces plainly through the texture.","Rore spent over a decade at Ferrara serving Ercole II d'Este, in a chapel with a deep institutional memory of Josquin, who had worked for Ercole's grandfather and had written a Mass on his name. Choosing a Josquin motet as the basis for a Mass in that court was a statement about lineage: the new maestro placing himself in a line that ran back through the building he worked in.","Josquin's motet is itself unusual, a six-voice setting whose repeated chant phrase gives it an almost incantatory quality. By expanding it to seven parts, Rore both honours and outbids his model. Listen for the recurring chant figure, which Josquin used as a refrain and which Rore redistributes across a Mass lasting seven times as long, so the same shape holds together a far bigger structure.","Parody Masses on famous motets were routine, but choosing Josquin at a point when his music was thirty years out of fashion was a deliberate act of homage, and scholars read this Mass as evidence of how quickly Josquin became a classic rather than a contemporary. Its scale has kept it out of the repertory: seven demanding parts for half an hour is a considerable undertaking for any choir.","Listen to Josquin's motet first; it takes eight minutes and its repeating phrase is unforgettable. Then take the Credo of the Mass and try to catch that phrase returning inside a much longer argument. A choir of twenty-five to thirty, in a resonant building, keeps the seven parts audible without turning them to soup."],"monte-super-flumina-babylonis":["Eight voices divided into two groups, six minutes, on the psalm about the exiles who sat down by the rivers of Babylon and wept. The two choirs trade phrases and combine, but the writing is closer and more continuous than Venetian double-choir music, with a lot of overlapping. The emotional weight falls on the question at the centre of the text: how shall we sing the Lord's song in a strange land.","Monte was Kapellmeister to the Habsburg emperors and by 1583 was based in Prague under Rudolf II. Thirty years earlier, as a young singer, he had spent a year in England in the chapel of Philip of Spain, husband of Queen Mary. In 1583 he sent this motet from Prague to William Byrd in London, at a moment when Byrd, an unconcealed Catholic, was living under a Protestant government that was tightening the law against his religion.","Monte chose his verses carefully, arranging the psalm so that the exiles' question lands with maximum force, and sent it to a man whose situation the verses described. Byrd answered the next year with an eight-voice setting of the reply, how shall we sing, taking up the psalm where Monte left it. Two composers who had met once, half a lifetime earlier, conducted an argument about religious exile across Europe using nothing but scripture.","The exchange survives in English manuscript partbooks and is one of the best-documented instances of music used as coded correspondence. Some scholars caution that the psalm was a commonplace text and that the political reading may be ours rather than theirs; most accept that the pairing of pieces, the dates and Byrd's specific choice of continuation are too pointed to be accidental.","Hear this first and Byrd's reply straight afterwards, in that order, as a single fifteen-minute event; nothing else in the century works quite like it. Listen for the moment the two choirs come together on the central question. Palestrina's setting of the same psalm makes a useful third, showing the text handled as devotion rather than as message."],"fayrfax-missa-albanus":["Four movements — Gloria, Credo, Sanctus, Agnus Dei — for five unaccompanied voices; English practice left the Kyrie to plainchant. Each movement begins with two or three voices alone before the full choir enters, and each ends with a long spinning treble line riding high above a slow bass. The sound is florid and unhurried: twenty or thirty notes flow past on a single syllable, cadences arrive later than you expect, and the top part asks for boys with unusually secure high notes.","Fayrfax was the leading composer at the courts of Henry VII and Henry VIII, a Gentleman of the Chapel Royal, paid well for presenting decorated manuscripts to the king and travelling with the royal chapel to the Field of the Cloth of Gold in 1520. He took a Cambridge doctorate in music in 1504 and an Oxford one in 1511, and this mass was probably the exercise submitted for the first. Large collegiate chapels then kept boys and men endowed to sing daily.","The whole mass grows out of a short figure said to have been sung by a vision of Saint Alban. Fayrfax runs it backwards, turns it upside down and stretches it into long notes as the work proceeds, so one shape underlies music that never sounds like repetition. Working out where the theme is hiding, and in which direction, is exactly the kind of puzzle a doctoral examiner would have been asked to admire.","Fayrfax's Latin music went out of use at the Reformation and stayed unsung for four centuries; the Tudor Church Music volumes of the 1920s and a run of recordings from the 1990s brought it back. The live argument now is about pitch and voice type: whether those high lines were sung by boys at written pitch or transposed upward, and how far a modern mixed choir can reproduce the bright, slightly wiry sound of a Tudor chapel.","Start with the Agnus Dei and let the long treble lines wash past without trying to follow the words; the music works by span, not by detail. Then put it beside Taverner's Missa Gloria tibi Trinitas from a generation later to hear how the style tightened. Small English ensembles using boy trebles catch the brightness that big mixed choirs smooth away."],"ludford-missa-benedicta-et-venerabilis":["A festal mass in four movements for six unaccompanied voices, forty minutes long, built over a Marian chant that one voice sings in very long notes while the others move freely around it. Ludford's method is architectural: pairs and trios of voices alternate for minutes at a time, the full six-part sound held back so that its returns land like doors opening. The final Agnus Dei is the longest single stretch and the richest.","Ludford spent his working life at St Stephen's Chapel in the Palace of Westminster, the royal free chapel a few steps from the king's own rooms, as a singing-man and later verger. He also wrote seven short Lady Masses, one for each day of the week, meant to alternate with organ verses — the only English cycle of its kind to survive. Henry VIII's dissolution of the free chapels in 1548 ended both the institution and the repertory.","The borrowed chant is nearly inaudible for long stretches because the voice carrying it moves so slowly; you notice it only when the texture thins and a single sustained note is left exposed. Ludford also enjoys writing two voices in near-identical rhythm a step apart, producing a shimmer that sounds like a mistake and is not. The six-part sections are scored so that no two entries begin on the same word.","Ludford was effectively unknown until the later twentieth century: the partbooks survived incomplete, the music needed editorial reconstruction, and only the editions of the 1960s onward and recordings from the 1990s put it in front of listeners. Reviewers reached for the word rediscovery so often that a counter-argument grew up, holding that he is a fine craftsman of a shared style rather than an isolated figure. Both positions are still defended.","Give it the Agnus Dei first — the most concentrated fifteen minutes — and listen for the moment when reduced voices give way to all six. Then hear Fayrfax's Missa Albanus, written a generation earlier, and notice how similar the ambitions are. English chamber choirs recording in real chapel acoustics serve this music better than studio balance does."],"tye-missa-euge-bone":["Six voices, four movements, no Kyrie, twenty-eight minutes. Tye writes a fuller and more solid sound than his predecessors: fewer decorative flourishes, more moments where all six parts move together and the harmony becomes the event. The Sanctus opens with a rising figure passed down through the voices; the Agnus Dei builds to a final section in which the lower parts hold long notes while the trebles climb over them, and then the piece simply stops.","Tye was master of the choristers at Ely Cathedral from 1543, took a Cambridge doctorate in 1545 and an Oxford one in 1548, and later took holy orders and left music for a country parish. The mass sits on a knife edge in English religious history: Latin polyphony of this scale was in retreat under Edward VI, briefly restored under Mary, and gone again under Elizabeth. Nobody knows for which of those moments it was written.","No one has found the music the title points to. Masses of this kind are usually named after a chant or song they borrow, but the source of Euge bone — the phrase means well done, good servant — has never been identified, and the head-motif may be Tye's own invention, or a lost antiphon, or a private motto. The mass may therefore be one of the first English masses built on nothing but itself.","Tye's reputation long rested on an anecdote of doubtful truth in which Elizabeth I complained that his organ playing was out of tune and he replied that her ears were. The music itself was recovered by twentieth-century editors, and this mass is now the piece by which he is judged. Scholars argue about its date, which changes its meaning entirely: an Edwardian survival, a Marian celebration, or a private act of nostalgia.","Begin with the Sanctus, the most immediately gripping movement, and listen for how often all six voices land on a chord together — this is the point where English polyphony starts to sound harmonic rather than linear. Compare Tye's consort In Nomines afterwards for the same mind working instrumentally. Cathedral choirs with a strong bass line suit it."],"robert-white-lamentations-a-5":["Fifteen minutes for five low voices on verses from the Lamentations of Jeremiah. Each section opens with the name of a Hebrew letter — Heth, Caph, Lamed — set as a long wordless meditation before the sentence itself arrives. The scoring stays dark, with two tenor parts and little treble brightness, and the harmony leans on suspensions: one voice holds while the others move under it, so that the sound is repeatedly sour before it settles. It closes on Jerusalem, turn again to the Lord.","White succeeded Tye at Ely, married Tye's daughter, and moved on to Chester Cathedral and then Westminster Abbey. He died of plague in 1574, in his mid-thirties, and his wife and children died with him. Under Elizabeth I, Latin polyphony survived in a few chapels and private houses; Lamentations texts, mourning a fallen holy city, were set by Catholic and conforming composers alike and could carry whatever grief the singers brought to them.","The Hebrew letter-names are structural markers copied out of the Bible, not part of the sense — they are the equivalent of chapter numbers. English composers turned them into the most elaborate music in the piece, so the listener spends minutes on the word Heth before reaching a single word of meaning. The effect is a deliberate delay: the grief is announced long before it is spoken.","Thomas Morley named White among the best English composers in his 1597 treatise; a century and a half later Charles Burney dismissed him with the rest of Tudor polyphony. The music returned through the Tudor Church Music edition and postwar recordings. Performing groups now argue over pitch, since singing at written pitch makes the piece darker and heavier than the upward transposition most choirs use.","Take the sections one at a time rather than as a continuous work, and let the long wordless letter-settings do their job. Play it against Tallis's Lamentations to hear two very different temperaments on the same text, then against Victoria's O vos omnes for the Spanish equivalent. Seek a men's ensemble singing low and slow."],"sheppard-media-vita":["One movement lasting over twenty minutes, among the longest single spans in Tudor music. It is a Lenten respond: plainchant verses for a small group alternate with enormous six-part polyphonic paragraphs, and the sentence In the midst of life we are in death returns three times in full, each time exactly as before. The treble line sits extraordinarily high and stays there, so the texture has a permanent bright ceiling above a slow-moving floor.","Sheppard trained choristers at Magdalen College, Oxford, then joined the Chapel Royal. This music belongs to the short Catholic restoration under Mary I, from 1553 to 1558, when the Sarum rite came back and English composers were suddenly asked for large-scale Latin music again after Edward VI's plain English services. Sheppard died in 1558, the year the queen did, and the style he was writing in died within months of him.","Because the refrain returns unchanged three times, what changes is you: the same twelve minutes of music lands differently after each intervening verse. Listen also for the clashes English composers loved, where one voice sings a note a hair away from another's and neither gives way — Sheppard uses them at moments of maximum sweetness, so the piece keeps sounding slightly out of focus on purpose.","Sheppard was almost unknown until the twentieth century and is still under-recorded. The central performance question is pitch: sung as written, the treble part is punishing, and most choirs transpose upward on the argument that Tudor notation stood lower than modern pitch. That decision changes the piece from a dark work with a strained top to a radiant one, and reviewers have taken sides accordingly.","Clear twenty-two minutes and do not treat it as background; the effect is entirely cumulative, and the plainchant verses exist to reset your ear before each return. Compare it with Tallis's Spem in alium for sheer scale, though Sheppard's is scale in time rather than in voices. English cathedral-trained ensembles in a resonant building are the right sound."],"morley-now-is-the-month-of-maying":["A ballett for five voices lasting two minutes: three verses to identical music, each half of each verse ending in a fa-la-la refrain that everyone sings together. The verses are almost chordal, the words arriving in step so you can hear every one; the refrains break into scampering imitation, the parts chasing each other through the same figure. The rhythm is a dance step, light and springing, and the piece never once slows down.","Published in Morley's First Booke of Balletts to Five Voyces in 1595. A pupil of Byrd, organist of St Paul's and then a Gentleman of the Chapel Royal, Morley later held the royal patent for printing music in England. London had caught madrigal fever after Nicholas Yonge's 1588 anthology of Italian pieces with English words, and Morley's business instinct was to supply the same pleasure in a form that ordinary households could sing at sight.","The tune and the whole shape come from Orazio Vecchi's Italian canzonetta So ben mi ch'a bon tempo of 1590; Morley was an anthologist of Italian music as well as a composer and borrowed without embarrassment. The words are also less innocent than four centuries of school choirs have assumed: barley-break was a chasing game played by couples, and the invitation in the last verse is not about the weather.","Victorian glee clubs and twentieth-century schools made this the most sung English madrigal and flattened it into rustic decoration. Edmund Fellowes and later scholars restored the Italian model and the sexual reading of the text. Performance splits between one voice per part, which keeps the refrains transparent, and full choirs, which make them sound like a crowd; the fashion has moved decisively toward the smaller version.","Sing it rather than listen if you have four friends. Failing that, pick one inner voice and follow it through both fa-la refrains — it takes a different route each time, which is the whole joke. Then hear Vecchi's original, and afterwards Weelkes, to feel how strange the same language becomes in other hands."],"morley-april-is-in-my-mistress-face":["Ninety seconds, four voices, four lines of verse, and every line given its own weather. April in her face and July in her eyes are set quick, high and bright, the voices overlapping in short imitative runs. September in her breast slows the motion. The last line, December in her heart, drops the parts to their lowest register, halves the speed and closes on long, cold chords that are held until the sound dies.","From Madrigalls to Foure Voyces, Morley's first full madrigal book, published in 1594 while he was organist of St Paul's Cathedral. The text is an English version of a conceit that had circulated in Italian verse for decades — the beloved whose beauty is spring and whose feelings are winter. Morley's market was the amateur household with four singers and an evening to fill, and pieces of this length were designed to be sung several times running.","The piece is a clock. Each named month is slower and lower than the one before, so the music itself performs a year passing, and the listener registers the cooling before understanding the joke. Notice too that the imitation, busy in the first two lines, thins out as the seasons advance until the voices are simply moving together — warmth is counterpoint, coldness is chords.","It has become the standard specimen of English word-painting in textbooks and competitions, which has attracted the objection that it is a demonstration rather than a madrigal — too short and too neat to have any inner life. Defenders point out that the brevity is the point. It remains an audition piece for madrigal groups precisely because nothing in it can be hidden.","Hear it three times in a row; it is shorter than the explanation. First time, follow the words. Second time, listen only to the bass and hear it sink. Third time, ignore the words entirely and notice how the piece cools. Then compare Wilbye's Draw on, sweet night, which does the same trick over five minutes."],"weelkes-as-vesta-was-from-latmos-hill":["Six voices, four minutes, and a poem in which Vesta's attendants come down a hillside while Diana's nymphs climb it, the two groups meeting around the figure of Queen Elizabeth as Oriana. Weelkes draws every image literally: falling scales on descending, rising ones on ascending, tumbling runs on running down amain, two voices for two by two, three for three by three, one voice alone on all alone, and a long-held sound on long live.","The piece closes The Triumphs of Oriana, an anthology Morley assembled in 1601 in which twenty-odd composers each wrote a madrigal ending with the same couplet, modelled on an Italian collection made for a Venetian lady a decade earlier. Weelkes was in his mid-twenties and organist at Winchester College. Whether the book really honoured the ageing Elizabeth, who died two years later, or paid coded tribute to someone else has never been settled.","After all the counting games, the last third abandons illustration and becomes a straight fugue on the refrain, so the queen's name overlaps with itself continuously until it sounds less like a chorus than a crowd. And Latmos is not a neutral hill: it is where the moon goddess found Endymion asleep and kissed him, which makes the compliment considerably warmer than the schoolroom reading allows.","Every textbook of the madrigal uses this as its example, which has provoked the standing objection that the word-painting is mechanical, a party trick rather than expression. The counter-case is that the joke is the expression: the piece is a courtly game played in public. Modern performance has moved from choral societies to consorts of six solo voices, which makes the counting audible.","Read the poem first, then listen with it in front of you and tick off the illustrations; there is no shame in it, since the composer expected exactly that. Play it beside Morley's balletts to hear how much further Weelkes pushes. One voice per part, English consorts, and do not accept a slow tempo."],"weelkes-thule-the-period-of-cosmography":["Two linked madrigals for six voices, five minutes together, which take the form of a geography lesson that turns out to be a love poem. Iceland's volcano burning under ice, Etna's flames, flying fish, a Peruvian mine of gold — each marvel gets its own harmonic colour, and after each one the same shrugging refrain returns: all this is nothing beside my love's strangeness. The chords slide sideways into regions no other English music of the time visits.","From Madrigals of 5 and 6 Parts, apt for the Viols and Voices, published in 1600 when Weelkes was a young organist about to move to Chichester Cathedral. London was reading travel writing as popular entertainment: Hakluyt's collection of English voyages had been reissued two years earlier, the East India Company received its charter the same year, and exotic geography was a fashionable subject for verse in the way space travel later became.","The second madrigal is the first one again in disguise — the Andalusian merchant's wonders answer Thule's marvel for marvel, in the same order and at the same length, so the second time round you already know where the harmony will lurch. The lurch to listen for comes on sulphureous fire, where the music steps to a chord that has no business following the one before it.","This is routinely called the most harmonically adventurous English madrigal, and comparisons with Gesualdo have been made since the nineteenth century — usually to conclude that Weelkes's strangeness is pictorial where Gesualdo's is temperamental. Editors disagree over accidentals, which in this repertory were often left to the singer, so recordings differ audibly in exactly the passages that make the piece famous.","Read the poem before you listen; the music is a commentary on it and makes little sense otherwise. Then take just the phrase about the burning mountain under ice and play it twice. Compare Gesualdo's Moro, lasso for chromatic writing that comes from a different motive entirely. Six solo voices, closely recorded."],"weelkes-hosanna-to-the-son-of-david":["A full anthem — chorus throughout, no soloists — for six voices in three minutes. The word Hosanna arrives as a short rhythmic tag rather than a tune, which lets Weelkes stack it in any order he likes, entries piling in from all six parts until the texture is crowded. A quieter middle on Blessed be the King thins to a few voices, then the opening acclamation returns and the anthem ends at full stretch with the trebles high and exposed.","Weelkes became organist and master of the choristers at Chichester Cathedral around 1602 and stayed until his death. The English cathedral tradition after the 1559 settlement needed anthems in English, and the full anthem was the older and plainer of the two types then in use. Weelkes's later years went badly: the chapter recorded complaints about his drunkenness and blasphemy, and he lost his standing at court while keeping his cathedral post.","Setting Hosanna as a rhythm rather than a melody is the structural trick of the piece, and it is why the entries can overlap so densely without the sound turning to mud — the ear tracks the pattern even when it cannot follow a line. Listen for the bass, which states the tag least often and therefore anchors everything the upper voices do with it.","Much of Weelkes's church music survives in incomplete sets of partbooks and has had to be reconstructed by editors, so different editions genuinely sound different and choirs sometimes sing lines that are informed guesswork. His anthems were long treated as the lesser half of his output beside the madrigals; the argument since the mid-twentieth century has been that the church music is where his ambition actually lay.","Hear it in a resonant building or a recording made in one, since the overlapping entries are designed to blur slightly. Listen once for the top line, once for the bass. Then put it next to Gibbons's O clap your hands, written a decade later, to hear the same festivity organised more strictly."],"wilbye-draw-on-sweet-night":["Six voices, five minutes: the slowest and longest of the great English madrigals, and the least eventful on the surface. Night is invited in as a friend to a melancholy mind. The same phrases keep returning brighter or darker by turns, the harmony shifting between two colourings of one chord within a single line. The last section repeats a phrase about a life ill-served for want of comfort again and again, each return sinking lower, and then simply stops.","From the Second Set of Madrigals of 1609, the second and last book Wilbye published. He spent his career in one household: musician to the Kytson family at Hengrave Hall in Suffolk from 1598 into the 1620s, growing prosperous on a leased sheep farm and retiring to Colchester. Music like this was made for a room with a chest of viols in it and half a dozen educated amateurs, not for a hall or a church.","The melancholy is a fashion as much as a mood. Elizabethan and Jacobean England ran a cult of scholarly gloom, with its own costume, posture and vocabulary, and the word sable in the text nods toward Dowland's black-clad public persona. Structurally, notice that the two top voices are equal and cross each other constantly, so what sounds like a single soaring melody is actually shared between two singers.","Edmund Fellowes called it the finest English madrigal and the judgement has largely stuck, though it says as much about Edwardian taste for slow sadness as about Wilbye. The performance argument is whether these are chamber pieces for six people round a table or concert works for a choir; consorts of solo voices now dominate recordings, and the piece loses its intimacy at choral scale.","Listen late and quietly, and do not follow the words closely on a first hearing — let the harmony's constant re-shading do the work. Then play Dowland's Flow My Tears immediately after, and notice that the two pieces are the same emotion approached from opposite directions, one solitary, one shared."],"wilbye-sweet-honey-sucking-bees":["A madrigal in two halves for five voices, four minutes in all. The first half sends bees away from the flowers to gather from the beloved's lips instead, and the upper voices buzz through quick repeated figures that pass from part to part. The second half, beginning Yet, sweet, take heed, warns that those lips hide a sting; the motion slows, the register drops, and the word sting is set to a dissonance that hangs before it resolves.","Also from the Second Set of 1609, published from Hengrave Hall in Suffolk. The bee-and-sting conceit is ancient — it comes down from Greek epigram through Italian verse and was everywhere in Elizabethan poetry — and English madrigalists took their texts from a common pool of translations rather than commissioning poets. The Kytson household inventories list viols and lutes, and title pages of the period routinely offered such pieces for voices or instruments interchangeably.","The two halves are almost exactly the same length, and the second answers the first phrase for phrase, so the warning arrives as a formal mirror of the invitation. The buzzing figure of the first half never disappears in the second — it is slowed down until you no longer recognise it as buzzing, which is how the pleasure turns into the threat without any new material.","Wilbye's reputation rests on a small output of about sixty madrigals, and critics since Fellowes have divided them between the ambitious and the merely charming; this piece is usually placed with the ambitious because of its two-part structure. Recordings by consorts one to a part have become standard, and the argument for choral performance has quietly collapsed over the last forty years.","Listen to the first half twice, then the second, and try to catch the moment the buzzing has become something else. Compare Morley's April is in my Mistress' Face for a compressed version of the same warm-to-cold move. Small consorts with a clear top line, sung at a speed brisk enough for the bees to be bees."],"gibbons-the-silver-swan":["Under two minutes: five parts, one short poem, no repeats and almost no imitation. The top voice carries what is effectively a tune while the four beneath it move as accompaniment, which makes the piece feel more like a song than a madrigal. The swan, silent all its life, sings once as it dies; the music turns downward at the moment of death, and the final line — more geese than swans now live, more fools than wise — is delivered flatly, without emphasis.","From The First Set of Madrigals and Motets of 5 Parts, apt for Viols and Voices, published in 1612, Gibbons's only secular collection. He was twenty-nine and already a Gentleman and organist of the Chapel Royal, and would become organist of Westminster Abbey before dying suddenly at Canterbury in 1625. The title page means what it says: a household with four viols and one good singer could perform these pieces as readily as five singers could.","The last line is a complaint about the trade. By 1612 the madrigal fashion in England was already finished — Gibbons's book is effectively its obituary — and the geese crowding out the swans are plausibly the amateurs and rivals filling a market that had briefly been lucrative. Notice too that the piece repeats nothing at all, which for a madrigal is close to a formal rebuke.","The most anthologised English madrigal, and regularly attacked for not being one: it barely imitates, its parts are unequal, and it behaves like a part-song. Performance is genuinely split, and the two readings sound like different pieces — five voices make it a communal statement, a countertenor with viols makes it a private one. Recent recordings increasingly take the title page at its word.","Two minutes each way: hear the five-voice version, then the solo-voice-and-viols version, and decide which poem you have just heard. Then place it beside Dowland's Flow My Tears as the other great English study in resigned sadness. Afterwards, Weelkes's madrigals will sound like a different century."],"gibbons-this-is-the-record-of-john":["A verse anthem of about six minutes. A solo countertenor tells the story from the opening of John's Gospel — the priests and Levites sent to ask the Baptist who he is, and his three denials — in three long stanzas, accompanied by viols or organ. After each stanza the full choir repeats the soloist's closing words in five parts. The soloist has all the narrative; the choir only ever confirms what has already been said.","The verse anthem was the English contribution to church music of the period: a solo voice given the words and the choir reduced to a responding body, a structure that suited a liturgy newly focused on preaching and on hearing scripture in English. Gibbons wrote it for the Chapel Royal, probably for William Laud during his presidency of St John's College, Oxford, whose patron saint the text celebrates.","The three denials are set as three distinct refusals rather than one repeated one: each is firmer, lower and more deliberate than the last, so the Baptist's I am not becomes a position rather than an evasion. And the chorus, which never introduces a word of its own, functions like a congregation assenting to a sermon — the form itself is a piece of Jacobean theology.","The anthem survives with viol accompaniment and with organ, and since the early-music revival of the 1980s the viol version has become standard, its five independent lines making the accompaniment a character rather than a support. Countertenor performance raises the other question: the part sits in a range that a high tenor can also take, and the two choices produce very different narrators.","Choose a recording with viols and follow a text; the words carry the piece and the music exists to deliver them. Listen for the three denials and hear how each one differs. Then compare Gibbons's O clap your hands, which is the same composer doing the opposite — no soloist, eight voices, all celebration."],"gibbons-o-clap-your-hands":["Psalm 47 set for eight voices divided into two equal four-part choirs, about six minutes. The choirs answer each other in blocks before combining, and the eight-part sound when it arrives is dense rather than spacious — Gibbons keeps the parts close together so the effect is weight, not distance. A Gloria Patri follows the psalm, and the work ends with an Amen extended into the longest continuous stretch of counterpoint in his church music.","Written in 1622 as the exercise for the Oxford doctorate of William Heather, Gibbons's friend, who later endowed the university's music professorship; Gibbons took his own doctorate on the same occasion. He was organist of the Chapel Royal, would move to Westminster Abbey the following year, and died at Canterbury in 1625 while the court awaited Charles I's bride. Academic exercises of this kind were expected to demonstrate technique in public.","The two choirs are not high against low, as Venetian double-choir writing usually arranges them, but identically scored, which means neither can dominate and the exchanges are a conversation between equals rather than an echo. The Amen is where the exercise shows: eight real parts, none doubling another, sustained far beyond what the text needs, because that was the point of the submission.","It became a festival and coronation favourite and has stayed in cathedral repertory continuously, which is unusual for music of its date. The sources differ in places and editions vary accordingly. Some critics find the eight-part writing thicker than Gibbons's five-part music and less characteristic; others take it as evidence of what he could have done had he lived past forty-one.","Find a recording made in a large building, or better, sit between the two choirs if you ever get the chance — the piece is designed for a listener in the middle. Wait for the Amen and then stop listening for words. Compare Tallis's Spem in alium for the English taste for many voices at once, and Weelkes's Hosanna for the looser version."],"tomkins-when-david-heard":["Five minutes of music on twenty-two words. David learns of his son Absalom's death and goes up to his chamber to weep, and the setting simply refuses to move on: the phrases O my son Absalom and would God I had died for thee are repeated by voice after voice, overlapping so that no statement is ever the last. Full silences separate the paragraphs — rare in this music — and the piece never rises above a controlled level of sound.","Published in Tomkins's Songs of 3, 4, 5 and 6 Parts in 1622, a book in which every piece carries a dedication to a friend or patron. Around 1610 to 1615 this text became briefly a national subject: Weelkes, Thomas Ramsey and others set it, and the vogue is usually connected with the death of Prince Henry, heir to James I, in 1612, which produced a wave of public mourning England had not seen for a generation.","Tomkins never uses volume as an expressive device here. At the point where any later composer would build a climax, he instead brings the five parts closer together in range until they are almost on top of one another, so the intensity comes from crowding rather than from force. The silences do the rest: after each paragraph the music stops entirely, and grief is left with nowhere to go.","Tomkins's setting and Weelkes's are permanently compared, usually to the conclusion that Weelkes is more dramatic and Tomkins more relentless. By 1622 Tomkins was writing in a manner three decades out of date and knew it — he outlived the style by a lifetime. His revival came through mid-twentieth-century editions and then through the professional consorts of the 1980s onward.","Hear Weelkes's setting and Tomkins's back to back, in that order, and notice which one you are still thinking about. Then listen to Tomkins alone with the text in front of you and count how many times the word son is sung. Small groups with real bass depth, in a dry acoustic that lets the silences sound."],"tomkins-sad-pavan-for-these-distracted-times":["A pavan for keyboard in three sections, each played and then repeated with the writing thickened and quickened, about five minutes in all. The dance step is there but slowed until it barely functions as a dance; the left hand moves in slow tread while the right circles above it, and the ornamented repeats decorate a melody that keeps arriving at the same downward turn. The final section falls further than the others and stops without a flourish.","Tomkins dated the manuscript 14 February 1649, a fortnight after the execution of Charles I. He was in his late seventies, organist of Worcester Cathedral for half a century, a pupil of Byrd who had written for the coronation of the king now beheaded. Parliamentary troops had taken Worcester and destroyed the cathedral organ three years earlier; the choir was disbanded, the liturgy abolished, and the professional world he had worked in no longer existed.","The style is deliberately antique. In 1649 this music was fifty years out of date — it is written as Byrd would have written it in the 1590s — and the archaism is the statement: an old man answering a revolution in the language of the world it destroyed. The title is his own, written on the page, one of the few pieces of Tudor and Stuart keyboard music to carry its occasion in its name.","The piece is usually treated as a document as much as a composition, and the temptation to hear the axe in every cadence has been noted and resisted by scholars in roughly equal measure. Performance choice matters unusually much: on harpsichord it sounds ceremonial, on virginal domestic, on chamber organ liturgical, and each reading changes what the pavan is mourning.","Listen once knowing nothing but the title, then once knowing the date. Follow the left hand rather than the tune — the slow tread underneath is where the piece lives. Then hear Byrd's keyboard pavans from fifty years earlier and register that almost nothing has changed, which is the point."],"morales-emendemus-in-melius":["Four minutes for five voices, and two texts sung at once. Four of the parts sing a penitential prayer — let us amend what we have done amiss, lest death overtake us — in flowing, overlapping lines. The fifth does something else entirely: at long intervals it delivers one sentence in slow, plain notes, remember, man, that thou art dust and to dust shalt return, and then falls silent again. The prayer keeps going; the sentence keeps interrupting.","Morales sang in the papal chapel in Rome from 1535 to 1545, the only Spaniard of the first rank there, and published two books of motets in the city, this one among them. Spanish printing and Spanish empire together carried his music further than any of his contemporaries': it reached Mexico, Guatemala and Peru, where cathedral choirs were still copying his masses a century after his death.","The interrupting sentence is not decoration. It is what the priest says while marking a forehead with ash on Ash Wednesday, so the motet stages the ritual it accompanies — the congregation praying, the priest cutting across the prayer with the same words to each person in turn. Because the ash voice sings in long notes and long gaps, it arrives like a bell rather than a singer.","Morales was called the light of Spain in his own century and then eclipsed for four hundred years by Victoria, whose emotional directness suited later taste better. Spanish musicology from the 1930s onward, and recordings from the 1980s, restored him. This motet is the piece most often used to argue his case, though some hear the two-text device as a scholastic trick rather than an expressive one.","Listen once for the four-part prayer and once for the fifth voice alone, which is easy to miss on a first hearing and impossible to unhear afterwards. Then compare Victoria's O vos omnes, which achieves the same weight by opposite means, and Josquin's Miserere, which uses the same interrupting-voice device a generation earlier."],"morales-missa-mille-regretz":["A parody mass: all five movements are built from the phrases of a famous chanson about a thousand regrets, taken apart and reassembled. Six voices, thirty-five minutes. The chanson's falling opening sigh is the first thing you hear in the Kyrie and it keeps returning throughout, sometimes complete, more often as three or four notes buried in an inner part. The last Agnus Dei thickens the texture and slows the motion for a broad close.","Published in Rome in 1544 in Morales's second book of masses. The chanson, usually attributed to Josquin, was known in Spain as the Emperor's Song because Charles V was said to love it above all others, and Luis de Narváez had already arranged it for vihuela under that name. Morales, established in Rome but angling for a return to Spain and for imperial favour, chose a tune that functioned as the emperor's personal signature.","Morales rarely quotes more than a fragment at a time, so the chanson is present as a habit of shape rather than as a tune you could sing along with. The transformation is also a joke with a straight face: a secular song of a thousand regrets, complete with its sighing descent, becomes without altering a note the Kyrie's plea for mercy. The words change; the gesture does not.","Parody masses of this kind were later attacked by reformers for smuggling love songs into the liturgy, and the Council of Trent legislated against secular models within twenty years of this publication. Modern scholarship has also unsettled the foundation: the attribution of Mille regretz to Josquin is now questioned, which leaves Morales building a monument on an author who may not be there.","Hear the chanson first — two minutes — then the Kyrie, and the borrowing will be obvious. After that stop hunting for quotations and let the mass be a mass. Compare Josquin's Missa Pange lingua for the older technique of building on chant rather than on a song. Spanish or English early-music choirs, one voice or two to a part."],"guerrero-ave-virgo-sanctissima":["Five voices, four minutes, a Marian greeting sung almost entirely in smooth stepwise motion with very little dissonance. The two soprano parts sing exactly the same music one after the other from beginning to end — a strict canon — while three lower voices move around them. The opening notes are borrowed from the Salve Regina chant, so the piece announces itself before a word registers, and the whole motet floats at a nearly unvarying speed.","Guerrero learned from Morales, sang and then presided as chapel master at Seville Cathedral for four decades, and served a city that was the single legal port for trade with the Americas. His printed books travelled in the same ships: his motets were sung in Mexico City, Puebla, Bogotá and Cuzco long after his death. He also sailed to Jerusalem in 1588 and published a bestselling account of the pilgrimage.","Almost nobody hears the canon. The second soprano follows the first closely enough that the ear merges them into one line with a shadow, and the effect is a slight blurring of the top of the texture rather than an audible imitation. That is the technical joke: the most rigorous device in the piece is the one designed to be inaudible, hidden inside music that sounds effortless.","This became the most widely sung motet of the Spanish empire, copied and imitated across the Americas and used by later composers as the basis of their own masses. Northern European scholarship long filed Guerrero as a milder Victoria; recordings from the 1990s, particularly by Spanish and English ensembles, have made a case for him as the more consistently beautiful composer of the two.","Listen once for the two top voices only, trying to separate them; once you can hear the delay you will never lose it. Then compare Victoria's Ave Maria for eight voices, which chooses splendour where Guerrero chooses sweetness. Ensembles that keep the tempo steady and refuse to swell serve this music best."],"guerrero-missa-de-la-batalla-escoutez":["A mass built on Clément Janequin's chanson about the battle of Marignano, the most famous piece of noise-painting in sixteenth-century music. Thirty minutes in which fanfare figures, hammering repeated notes and drum rhythms run underneath the ordinary words of the liturgy. The Gloria opens like a cavalry charge; the Credo keeps returning to the same military tread; the Agnus Dei, asking for peace, is built from the material of a battle piece and does not disguise it.","Published in Rome in 1582 in Guerrero's second book of masses. The battle mass was a Spanish speciality, and it suited a monarchy that presented itself as the church militant: Philip II's armies were fighting in the Netherlands, Lepanto was within living memory, and the imagery of holy war was official. Cathedral choirs in Mexico and Guatemala kept battle masses for the feast of Santiago, Spain's warrior apostle.","Janequin's chanson imitates gunfire with strings of nonsense syllables — the voices literally go bang. Guerrero keeps every rhythm and throws the syllables away, so what survives inside the mass is the pattern of the shooting without the sound of it. The military figure then reappears under the most solemn words in the text, which is either a very dry joke or a theological argument about which battle is being fought.","Battle masses embarrassed later taste and were long dismissed as vulgar; the Council of Trent's suspicion of secular models applied to them more obviously than to any other repertory. They have returned through the early-music movement's appetite for loud, instrumentally doubled performance, which raises its own question, since the prints specify voices only and the brass is an inference from cathedral payment records.","Listen to Janequin's La guerre first — it is unmistakable and funny — then go straight to Guerrero's Gloria. Once you have the figure in your ear you will hear it everywhere in the mass. Choose a recording with cornetts and sackbuts for the full ceremonial effect, then an unaccompanied one to hear what the notes actually do."],"cabezon-diferencias-canto-del-caballero":["Four minutes of variations on a popular Spanish song. The tune appears first almost unadorned in the top voice; then each variation keeps its harmony and its exact length while everything around it grows busier — running figures beneath, then broken chords, then rapid scales passing between the hands. Nothing is dramatic and nothing accelerates: the piece works by steady increase of activity over a foundation that never moves from where it started.","Cabezón was blind from early childhood, became keyboard player to the imperial household and then to Philip II, and travelled with the court through Italy, Germany, the Netherlands and England, where he spent 1554 to 1556 during Philip's marriage to Mary Tudor. He published nothing himself; his son Hernando printed the surviving works in Madrid in 1578 in a volume offering them for keyboard, harp or vihuela as though the three were interchangeable.","The variation set as a form was Spanish before it was anything else, and pieces like this are among its founding examples — the idea of keeping a harmonic frame fixed and varying everything else began here rather than in Italy or England. The 1578 print uses number tablature rather than staves, which suited a blind composer's household, where music was made and taught by ear and by touch.","Byrd and the English virginalists probably heard Cabezón play during the two years the Spanish court was in England, and the resemblance between Spanish diferencias and English grounds and variations has been argued over ever since. Some hold the influence direct and documented by dates; others think both traditions grew independently out of the same improvising habits. The question is unresolved and probably unresolvable.","Hear it on a chamber organ or a clavichord rather than a modern piano — the quiet instruments show the inner parts. Keep hold of the tune in your head and notice that it never actually leaves. Then play Byrd's The Bells or John come kiss me now and hear the same idea in an English accent thirty years later."],"cabezon-tiento-primer-tono":["Five minutes in a single unbroken span. A tiento is the Spanish equivalent of the ricercar: no dance rhythm, no tune to follow, just a handful of short figures taken up in turn by four voice-parts that the keyboard player has to sustain alone. The texture begins sparse, thickens as the entries accumulate, and thins again before a new figure starts the process over. It ends without a cadential flourish, as if the argument simply finished.","Cabezón was chamber organist to Philip II and paid better than most courtiers. Spanish organs of the period were built for this music: a short keyboard compass, divided stops that let one hand play a solo against the other, and a tuning in which some chords are pure and others unusable. Tientos were played during the mass in alternation with the choir, so the congregation heard them in place of sung verses.","The word tiento means a touch or an attempt, and the genre began as a way of testing an instrument's tuning before playing in earnest — the opening bars still behave like someone feeling their way across a keyboard. The four voices are a fiction on a single instrument, but Cabezón maintains them so strictly that the whole piece can be sung by four singers without altering a note.","Cabezón's counterpoint has been compared with Bach's for rigour since the nineteenth century, usually by writers wanting to rescue him from the category of curiosity. The change in how the music sounds came from instruments: recordings on surviving Iberian organs tuned in mean-tone temperament, from the 1970s onward, made the harmony sharply characterful in a way modern equal-tempered instruments flatten out.","Seek a recording made on a historic Spanish organ and listen for the tuning as much as the notes — some chords will sound startlingly pure, others deliberately harsh. Track one entry of the opening figure and follow it from voice to voice. Then hear Cabezón's variations for the same mind working with a tune instead of an idea."],"milan-el-maestro-pavana-1":["Three minutes: the first of six pavanas in Milán's book, a stately processional dance in short sections, each repeated. Full chords are struck and left to ring, and between them single-line runs dart upward and fall back. The pattern alternates all the way through — weight, then flight, then weight again — and the piece stays close to one harmony throughout, so its interest is entirely in the change of texture rather than of key or of tune.","El Maestro, printed in Valencia in 1536, is the first published book of vihuela music and the first Spanish instrumental print of any kind. The vihuela was the aristocratic Spanish cousin of the lute, guitar-shaped and gut-strung. Milán served the Valencian court of Germaine de Foix, wrote books on court manners and games as well as music, and organised the volume as a graded teaching course, easy pieces first.","This is the earliest printed music to give tempo instructions in words. Milán tells the player to take the chords slowly and the runs quickly within the same piece — a direction about internal flexibility rather than overall speed, and one that written notation had until then had no way of conveying. He also notes that these pavanas imitate the Italian ones then fashionable in Spain, so the model is foreign and admitted to be so.","Milán's rhythmic notation is ambiguous and editors have never agreed on how to bar it, which produces recordings that differ in basic pulse. Guitarists tend to play it in strict tempo, vihuelists more freely, following the composer's own advice. The entire vihuela repertory was recovered in the twentieth century largely by classical guitarists, which is why most people first meet this music on the wrong instrument.","Hear it on a real vihuela with gut strings first — quieter, drier, more silvery than a guitar — and then on guitar, and decide what the modern instrument adds and costs. Compare Dowland's Frog Galliard for the English lute equivalent a lifetime later. Three minutes; play it twice."],"lobo-versa-est-in-luctum":["Six voices, five minutes, on two lines from Job: my harp is turned to mourning, and my music into the voice of those who weep. The parts move almost together and mostly by step, so the piece registers as harmony rather than as counterpoint — there is very little imitation and no display of any kind. Its whole expressive weight rests on suspensions: one voice holds while the others shift beneath it, producing a clash that sinks downward, again and again.","Lobo was chapel master at Toledo Cathedral when Philip II died in September 1598, and this motet belongs to the vast obsequies staged for him in Spanish cathedrals, where carpenters built catafalques the size of buildings and choirs sang for days. Victoria set the same text at almost the same moment for the funeral rites of the Empress María. The motet was printed in Lobo's book of masses in Madrid in 1602.","The text is about instruments falling silent, and Lobo sets it for voices alone in music that never once imitates an instrument — no fanfare, no dance rhythm, nothing plucked or struck. The silencing has already happened before the first note. Listen also for how consistently the harmony resolves downward: almost every tension in the piece is released by sinking, never by rising.","Lobo's name survived for three centuries mainly through a reported remark of Victoria's ranking him as an equal, which is the kind of testimony scholarship treats warily. The motet re-entered the repertory through Spanish editions and English cathedral recordings from the 1980s and is now among the most performed pieces of Spanish polyphony, sometimes to the point of overshadowing the rest of his output.","Play Victoria's setting of the same words and Lobo's one after the other; they are close in date, close in temperament, and audibly different in method. Listen for the very end, where the word for weeping is drawn out over a long descent. Cathedral choirs with a deep bass section suit this better than light consorts."],"encina-hoy-comamos-y-bebamos":["A villancico for four voices, two minutes: refrain, verse, refrain again. The parts move together on the same syllables almost throughout, so the words come across like shouted speech rather than sung line, and the rhythm has the stamp of a dance. Two shepherds work through the last day before Lent — today we eat, drink and sing, tomorrow we fast — and the music gets rowdier with each return of the refrain rather than more elegant.","Encina was a poet and playwright before he was a composer, in the household of the Duke of Alba at Alba de Tormes. This song ends his Égloga de Antruejo, one of the short plays staged in the ducal hall at Carnival, so it is theatre music: the last thing the audience hears before Lent begins. Some sixty of his songs survive in the Cancionero de Palacio, the great song manuscript of Ferdinand and Isabella's court.","The shepherds do not speak ordinary Castilian. Encina writes them in sayagués, a rustic stage dialect that educated audiences found funny in the way stage yokels have always been funny, and the flat, blocky setting is part of the joke — these characters cannot manage counterpoint. The piece is therefore a comic impersonation of unrefined music, written by a man who could write refined music perfectly well.","Encina is claimed both as the founder of Spanish secular drama and as the first named composer of the villancico, and the two claims prop each other up more than the evidence strictly allows. Performance is the live argument: the manuscript gives four vocal lines and nothing else, while most modern recordings add shawms, percussion and vihuelas on the reasonable but unprovable grounds that court theatre had a band.","Find a version with instruments and percussion and let it be as raucous as it wants; then find one with four voices alone and hear how much of the noise was the performers' idea. Follow a translation, since the whole point is the words. Then play Encina's Triste España for the same form turned inside out."],"encina-triste-espana-sin-ventura":["Three minutes, four voices, refrain and verses, the parts moving together in a low register with almost no ornament and no imitation at all. The address is to the country itself — sad Spain, without fortune — treated as a person capable of grief. The music is slow, plain and very short of incident: the same few chords keep returning, and the refrain comes back after each verse without variation, so the piece works by dull repetition of a single feeling.","Prince Juan, the only son of Ferdinand and Isabella and heir to the newly joined crowns of Castile and Aragon, died at Salamanca in October 1497, aged nineteen and married six months. The succession eventually passed out of the Spanish line to the Habsburgs. Encina, in the Duke of Alba's service and looking for royal favour, wrote both the words and the music, and the song functions as a piece of public mourning made to order.","It is a state commission written in the vocabulary of a love song. The form, the low register, the sighing repetitions and the address to an absent beloved all come straight from Encina's songs about unhappy lovers; only the addressee has changed. The effect is that a dynastic catastrophe is mourned in the language of private disappointment, which may be tact, or may be all the language available.","The song is now read as much as a political document as a musical one — the moment Spain's native dynasty lost its heir, recorded by a court poet within weeks. Its bareness has divided listeners: some hear restraint appropriate to public grief, others hear a competent professional producing what the occasion required. Recordings vary from stark unaccompanied voices to versions with viols that soften it considerably.","Play it directly after Encina's Carnival song: same composer, same form, same four voices, opposite world. Then compare Josquin's lament for Ockeghem, written the same year, for how a musician mourned a musician while a poet mourned a prince. Unaccompanied male voices, slow, no swelling."],"flecha-la-bomba":["An ensalada — literally a salad — for four voices and whatever instruments are available, running about nine minutes without a break. A ship is going down: the sailors shout for the pump, bail, panic, pray, sight land and are saved, at which point the storm is revealed as an image of the soul rescued by Christ and the piece turns into a dance and then a Christmas carol. The languages change without warning between Castilian, Catalan, Latin and Italian.","Flecha was a Catalan who served in the Spanish royal household, and the ensaladas were made for court Christmas entertainments, where devotion and slapstick were expected in the same evening. They were not printed in Spain at all: his nephew, Mateo Flecha the Younger, who worked for Emperor Rudolf II, published them in Prague in 1581, decades after his uncle's death, which is the only reason this music survives.","The piece is a collage of borrowed tunes. Popular songs of the day are dropped whole into the middle of prayers, so a sixteenth-century listener heard a running commentary of half-remembered street music under the shipwreck — an effect largely lost now that the sources are unfamiliar. The shouted word bomba is both the ship's pump and the rhythm the whole first section is built on, so the emergency and the beat are the same thing.","Ensaladas were long treated as curiosities of a provincial court rather than as compositions. Recordings by Jordi Savall's ensembles from the 1970s onward turned them into repertory and set an entire performing style — loud, instrument-heavy, theatrical, close to street theatre. Purists object that the surviving print specifies voices and nothing else, and that the whole exuberant sound of modern performance is an invention.","Follow a translation from the first bar; this is a story with a plot and a punchline, and hearing it without the words is like watching a play in a language you do not speak. Give it the full nine minutes. Then compare Guerrero's battle mass for the same taste for noise in a sacred frame."],"tromboncino-ostinato-vo-seguire":["A frottola: a short strophic song in four parts where the top line carries the tune and the lower three were as often played on lute or viols as sung. Three minutes at most. The phrases are clipped and squarely cadenced, the harmony built from plain triads, the words declaimed at close to speaking speed. The title line — stubbornly I mean to follow on — returns like a refrain, and it is what a listener remembers after a single hearing.","Tromboncino spent his career in the northern Italian courts, above all Mantua, where Isabella d'Este collected frottole the way she collected paintings. Between 1504 and 1514 Ottaviano Petrucci printed eleven books of them in Venice, the first mass-market secular songbooks in Europe, and Tromboncino appears in them more often than anyone else. The buyers were amateurs: a nobleman with a lute, a lady with a printed part-book, an evening to fill. Nothing in the music assumes professional singers.","Listen for what the printed page does not say. The lower parts carry words in the books, but players of the time treated them as accompaniment, so a modern performance for one singer and lute is following period habit rather than taking a liberty. Notice too how the lowest line behaves: it already works as a foundation holding up a tune, the arrangement that basso continuo would formalise a century later.","The frottola was long written off as a jingle the madrigal soon improved upon, a verdict fixed by nineteenth-century historians who prized counterpoint above everything. Scholars now treat it as a repertory with its own aims — clarity, wit, and words a listener can actually catch — rather than a failed experiment. Performances divide between four singers and the solo-with-lute reading, and both have period authority.","Follow the words rather than the parts; the piece is built so that you can. Hear it once with four voices and once with a singer and lute, and notice how much lighter and more personal the second becomes. Then put on Josquin's El grillo, written for the same Italian courts, and hear the same appetite for a good tune plainly set."],"cara-io-non-compro-piu-speranza":["Cara's best-known frottola turns love into a commercial transaction: I'll buy no more hope, I have paid too much for it already. Four parts, but plainer than most — the top voice carries a tune of almost folk simplicity over slow chords, with a lute filling in below. Three minutes, strophic, each verse landing on the same lilting cadence. Cara's manner is gentler and more coaxing than the clipped declamation of his Mantuan colleague Tromboncino.","Cara ran the household music for the Gonzaga at Mantua for some thirty years, singing to his own lute for Francesco Gonzaga and Isabella d'Este: a performer at least as much as a composer. Petrucci printed his songs in Venice from 1504 onward. Isabella's letters show her ordering settings of particular poems the way one orders a dish, and a hard-headed lover's complaint of this kind suited a court that liked its sentiment salted with irony.","The bass hardly moves for whole phrases, which leaves the singer free to stretch and lean on the words; contemporary praise of Cara singles out exactly that, a way of singing that persuaded by phrasing rather than volume. The poem runs its bargaining metaphor to the end, and the setting saves its firmest cadences for the close of those lines, so the argument lands rather than the melody.","Some contemporaries rated Cara above Tromboncino — Castiglione's circle admired his mild, persuasive delivery — and then he vanished for four centuries. Twentieth-century editions of the Petrucci prints brought the songs back, and early-music groups now programme them as the missing link between the fifteenth-century chanson and the madrigal. How many parts were meant to be sung, and how much was improvised, remains unsettled.","Take it as a song with a punchline. Listen to the ends of the verses, where the music settles and the joke lands, then compare a Tromboncino frottola to hear the difference between a coaxing singer and a plain speaker. Voice-and-lute recordings suit it better than four-part choirs, which make it sound more solemn than it is."],"arcadelt-il-bianco-e-dolce-cigno":["Four voices, three minutes, and the most quoted piece of the early madrigal. The swan dies weeping, the poet dies laughing — a pun on morire that everybody in 1539 understood. Arcadelt keeps the texture clear and largely chordal, the four parts moving together so the words arrive whole, then breaks the pattern at the last line: a thousand deaths a day comes back over and over in overlapping entries, each voice a beat behind the one before, until the piece simply has to stop.","Arcadelt was a northerner from the Low Countries working in Florence and then in the papal chapel in Rome, and the madrigal was an Italian fashion barely a decade old when his first book of four-voice madrigals appeared in Venice in 1539. The poem is attributed to Alfonso d'Avalos, a Spanish nobleman and general in Italy. Printing made the book's fortune: Venetian presses could ship part-books to amateur singers across Europe, and this one stayed in the catalogues for over a century.","The final line is a small piece of arithmetic. Each voice enters with the same short figure at a different moment, so that the phrase about a thousand deaths a day is literally multiplied by the counterpoint; the effect lies in the number of entries rather than in any one of them. Notice how deliberately little the harmony does before that point, banking its capital for the last twenty seconds.","The collection ran to dozens of editions and was still being reprinted in the mid-seventeenth century, making it commercially the most successful madrigal book ever issued. Later critics found Arcadelt tame beside Rore and Marenzio, and he settled into the conservatoire as a model of correct four-part writing. Recent scholarship has restored the erotic reading that nineteenth-century editors quietly translated away, and with it the piece's wit.","One voice per part, and read the eight-line poem first. Follow the top line, then wait for the closing phrase and count the entries as they pile up. Afterwards put on Gesualdo's Moro, lasso, built on the same conceit sixty years later, and hear how far the madrigal travelled between a joke and a wound."],"arcadelt-o-felic-occhi-miei":["Two minutes for four voices, and about as simple as a madrigal gets: happy eyes of mine, happy because you are dear to the eyes you love. The parts move almost entirely together in warm thirds and sixths, phrase by short phrase, with the opening exclamation echoed at a new pitch before the piece has properly begun. There is no word-painting to speak of and no dark corner anywhere — the charm is the evenness of it.","The piece stands in the same 1539 Venetian book that carried Arcadelt's swan madrigal, sold to households where four people could read music and enjoy an hour of singing after dinner. Arcadelt was writing for that market as much as for his Medici and papal employers. Italian poetry set in Italian, in a texture a competent amateur could manage at sight, was a commercial proposition new to the 1530s, and Venetian printers had worked out how to supply it.","The Ave Maria attributed to Arcadelt and sung in churches everywhere is not his: it was assembled in Paris in the 1840s by Pierre-Louis Dietsch from one of his three-voice French chansons, with a new Latin text laid over the top. Pieces like this one are what the real Arcadelt sounds like — secular, Italian, four-square, and sung by friends rather than by a choir.","Its very plainness kept it in print and then in anthologies, and later made it suspect: to nineteenth-century critics raised on Palestrina it seemed pretty rather than serious. Modern editions and one-to-a-part recordings have shifted the argument to how such music was performed — tuning in pure thirds, flexible speed, sung ornament added by the singers — rather than whether it is deep enough.","Listen to the tuning. With one voice per part and no vibrato the chords lock and ring, and that resonance is the whole point of the piece. Try it beside a lute-song by Dowland to hear two different answers to the same question of how to set a short love poem for people singing at home."],"rore-ancor-che-col-partire":["A four-voice madrigal on a conceit of parting: every leave-taking is a small death, so the lover would gladly part a thousand times a day for the pleasure of each return. Rore writes in long singable arcs rather than the short chordal blocks of the 1530s, giving every voice a line worth decorating. Four minutes, with a closing section that quickens into short repeated phrases circling the words a thousand times a day without ever quite settling.","Rore was a Fleming who made his name in Venice in the 1540s, in Willaert's orbit, before taking charge of music at the Este court in Ferrara. This madrigal comes from the beginning of that ascent. Where Arcadelt wrote for amateurs, Rore increasingly wrote for the professional singers courts were now hiring, and Ferrara in particular collected virtuosos the way it collected paintings and horses.","This piece became the standard exercise for instrumental ornamentation. Manual after manual — for cornetto, recorder, viol and voice — printed worked-out divisions on it across more than half a century, spinning sixteen notes where Rore wrote one. Hearing a decorated version against the plain one is the closest we can get to what sixteenth-century performance actually sounded like, and it changes the piece from a lament into a display.","Monteverdi named Rore as the founder of the seconda pratica, the way of composing in which the words command the harmony rather than the reverse, and used him as a shield in his quarrel with the theorist Artusi. Rore's contemporaries treated him as the composer other composers studied. Modern debate turns mainly on how heavily his madrigals should be ornamented, and on how far the diminution manuals reflect ordinary practice.","Hear the plain four-voice version first so the shape is clear, then find a recording where a cornetto or a viol player takes the top line and improvises over the rest. Notice how the fast decoration rides on a harmony that barely changes. It is the same relationship a jazz solo has to its changes."],"rore-o-sonno":["An invocation to Sleep, son of the quiet, damp, shadowy night, from a sonnet by Giovanni della Casa. Rore sets it low and slow: the voices sit in their darkest register, the harmony shifts to the flat side and stays there, and whole phrases move in near-stillness before a sudden crowding of parts at the poet's complaint that sleep will not come. Five minutes, and almost nothing in it hurries.","By 1557 Rore had been maestro di cappella at Ferrara for years and was writing for a court that expected difficulty. Della Casa, an archbishop and the author of a famous manual of manners, wrote a hard, knotted kind of sonnet that deliberately avoided Petrarch's smoothness, and Italian composers of the 1550s took to such verse precisely because it resisted easy setting. Insomnia as a subject for art music was itself new.","The trick is register. By keeping all four voices low for minutes at a time, Rore leaves himself one unused resource, and when the poem turns from invocation to grievance the parts open upward and the sound seems to gain light without getting louder. Listen also to the cadences that refuse to close properly: sleep is asked for and withheld by the harmony as much as by the words.","Nineteenth-century historians filed Rore under experimental chromaticism and moved on. Alfred Einstein's history of the Italian madrigal made the case for this piece as psychological writing rather than effect-hunting, and it has been recorded steadily since. Ensembles argue about pitch standard: sung at modern pitch the low writing can sound merely muddy, and many groups now transpose upward, which changes the colour considerably.","Play it late, quietly, and follow the bass. Nothing announces itself, so listen for the moment the voices lift out of the murk. Compare Dowland's Flow My Tears, another study in the dark, to hear how differently an English lute-song and an Italian madrigal handle the same weather."],"marenzio-solo-e-pensoso":["Petrarch's sonnet about walking alone through empty fields, measuring them with slow and heavy steps. Marenzio gives the top voice a line that climbs by semitones, one long note at a time, right through an octave and beyond, then walks back down the same way — a single unbroken ascent lasting the best part of a minute, while the four lower voices go about their ordinary madrigal business underneath. Five voices, five minutes, and one unforgettable image.","It opens the ninth and last book of five-voice madrigals, published in Venice in 1599, the year Marenzio died. He had spent his career in Rome in cardinals' households, with spells at Ferrara and a season at the Polish court, and had been the most widely printed madrigal composer in Europe. The late books turn away from the brilliance that made his name toward something harder, more Petrarchan and more severe.","The rising line is not merely chromatic decoration; it is a measuring device. Each step is the same size, so the ear counts paces, which is exactly what the poem describes. Underneath, the other voices keep the piece in a normal harmonic world, so that every new semitone of the top line lands in a different chord — the singer stays constant and the ground shifts, which is the point.","For three centuries Marenzio was remembered for lighter, tuneful madrigals, and the late books lay unperformed. Their rediscovery in the twentieth century recast him as the peer of Gesualdo, though the two use chromaticism oppositely — one across a slow single line, the other in sudden harmonic swerves. Singers still argue about the tuning of that ascent, which does not fit comfortably in any single sixteenth-century temperament.","Listen only to the top voice on a first hearing and track how high it has climbed by the end of the opening section. Then listen again to the harmony underneath. Follow it with Gesualdo's Moro, lasso for the contrast between a long walk uphill and a series of harmonic ambushes."],"marenzio-tirsi-morir-volea":["Guarini's little erotic narrative in three linked sections, seven minutes in all: Tirsi wants to die, his nymph delays him, they die together and are reborn to die again. Marenzio sets it as a scene rather than a poem, changing texture at every turn of the story — sighing pairs of voices for the lovers, quick chattering all five for the delay, and a broad slow ending as the pair come back to life. The dying, of course, is not dying.","This closes the first book of five-voice madrigals of 1580, the collection that made the twenty-six-year-old Marenzio famous across Europe within a year. He was then in the Roman household of Cardinal Luigi d'Este. Guarini's verse, written for the Ferrarese court, supplied composers with polished erotic miniatures at precisely the moment courts began employing small groups of professional singers who could handle rapid changes of texture.","The two lovers are almost always given separate voice-pairs, so the five-part group behaves like two duets plus a referee; when they finally die together the pairs fuse into a single slow chord progression. Listen for the moment the busy surface stops. Marenzio also keeps the highest voice out of the lowest textures, so the nymph's entries always arrive as fresh light.","The 1580 book was reprinted for decades and pirated in England, where Nicholas Yonge's Musica transalpina put Marenzio into English hands and helped start the English madrigal school. Later critics used the piece as evidence that the Italian madrigal was decorative rather than deep. Modern performers, taking the erotic subject at face value, have made it funnier and franker than the Victorian editions allowed.","Read a translation first: the piece is a small comedy and every joke is in the words. Then follow the voice-pairing rather than any single line, and wait for the point where the texture stops moving. English listeners can go straight from here to the Elizabethan madrigalists, who learned this manner from prints like this one."],"marenzio-liquide-perle-amor":["Love pours liquid pearls from my eyes: the opening madrigal of Marenzio's first book, and for a long while the most reprinted piece he wrote. Three minutes of light, quick imitation — a short figure passed from voice to voice so that the falling tears keep arriving from different directions — with a clean bright harmony and a dancing lift at the cadences. Nothing is dark about it, which is precisely why singers loved it.","Published in Venice in 1580 by a composer barely known outside Rome, it opened a book that ran through edition after edition. Marenzio was then in service to Cardinal Luigi d'Este, writing for the cardinal's private musicians. The market had changed since Arcadelt: publishers now wanted five voices rather than four, professional-sounding textures that skilled amateurs could still attempt, and a first item in a book that would sell the rest.","The tears are built into the shape of the little imitative figure, which always falls and always overlaps with itself, so the ear hears dripping without any obvious picture-painting. Notice that Marenzio ends phrases on the brightest available harmony rather than the saddest: the poem's grief is a courtly pose, and the music knows it. The wit lies in the mismatch.","Its very popularity told against it later. When the late books were rediscovered, critics contrasted the serious Marenzio with the caterer of pleasant novelties, and this piece stood for the second. That division has softened; the 1580 book is now heard as a young composer establishing a manner of enormous technical polish, and the piece is a standard test of an ensemble's lightness.","Count the entries of the opening figure in the first thirty seconds and you will hear the tears multiply. Keep the volume moderate — this music dies under choral weight. A one-to-a-part group at a brisk speed shows why every publisher in Europe wanted the book, and it makes a good pairing with the late Solo e pensoso."],"wert-giunto-alla-tomba":["Tancredi comes to the tomb of Clorinda, the woman he has killed, and stands there frozen before he can speak. Wert sets Tasso's stanzas in two long sections, some eight minutes together, and does something the madrigal had barely tried: the voices declaim on repeated notes, in speech rhythm, low and almost without melody, over harmony that shifts underneath them. Then the line breaks upward for the cry, and the effect is shocking because so little has moved before it.","Wert was a Fleming brought to Italy as a boy chorister who spent thirty years as director of music at the Gonzaga court in Mantua. Tasso's Gerusalemme liberata was circulating in 1581, the literary event of the decade, and Wert set the tomb episode in his seventh book of five-voice madrigals that same year. Mantua and neighbouring Ferrara were then in open competition over singers, poets and the prestige of setting Tasso first.","Count the repeated notes. Whole lines sit on a single pitch, which is a deliberate refusal of the madrigal's usual imitation and word-painting, and it forces the listener to hear the text as a speaking voice. That refusal is the direct ancestor of operatic recitative — and the young Monteverdi, working under Wert at Mantua, took the manner into his own Tasso settings.","Contemporaries knew Wert as the leading madrigalist of the north Italian courts; posterity remembered him mostly as Monteverdi's employer. Scholarly interest revived with the twentieth-century argument about the origins of recitative, which showed monody growing out of madrigals like this rather than arriving from Florence fully formed. Ensembles disagree on whether the declamation should be sung strictly in time or freely, as speech.","Read the episode from Tasso first — knowing that Tancredi killed Clorinda without recognising her makes everything audible. Then listen for how long the music stays on one note, and for the moment it finally leaves. Follow it with Monteverdi's Cruda Amarilli to hear the pupil pushing the same idea further."],"wert-vezzosi-augelli":["Tasso again, but the opposite world: the enchanted garden of Armida, where charming birds sing among the leaves and the breeze answers them. Wert writes fast, high, decorated lines for the upper voices — trills, turns, quick repeated notes that imitate birdsong without ever becoming a novelty effect — over a light, almost dance-like tread below. Four minutes of bright surface, with a sudden darkening at the reminder that the rose which blooms today will be gone tomorrow.","The eighth book of five-voice madrigals, 1586. By then the Duke of Ferrara's concerto delle donne — three professional women singers, extravagantly paid and famous across Italy — had changed what composers dared write for high voices, and Mantua answered with its own ensemble. Wert wrote for singers whose ornaments were the court's chief entertainment, and this madrigal is designed to show them off in public.","The birdsong is scored, not improvised. Wert writes out ornaments that singers would normally have added themselves, which fixes the effect in the print and tells us what the Ferrarese women actually did. Listen for the moment the flourishes stop: at the line about the rose the decorated writing vanishes and the voices move together in plain chords, and the garden is suddenly revealed as an illusion.","Nineteenth-century writers dismissed such pieces as gilded trifles. Twentieth-century research into the concerto delle donne turned them into primary evidence about virtuoso singing before opera, and about the sudden prominence of professional women musicians at Italian courts. Performances now divide between one-to-a-part groups with period ornament and larger choirs, and only the first makes the writing sound like the display it was.","Listen for the pause. The whole piece is a bright surface with one trapdoor in it, and the change from decorated singing to plain chords at the rose is the moment to wait for. Then play Wert's Tancredi madrigal from five years earlier to hear the same composer at his darkest and plainest."],"luzzaschi-aura-soave":["Three sopranos and a harpsichord, four minutes, and a texture that sounds a generation ahead of its date: the voices trade elaborate curling ornaments back and forth, in thirds and then in echo, while the keyboard supplies a full written-out accompaniment underneath rather than doubling anybody. A soft breeze of secret words, says the poem, and the music keeps circling the word soave with runs so fast they blur into a single shimmer.","Luzzaschi was organist and music director at Ferrara, and these pieces were written in the 1580s and 1590s for the concerto delle donne, the three virtuoso women singers whose private concerts for the Duke were the most exclusive entertainment in Italy. They were printed in Rome only in 1601, after the Este court had collapsed and the singers dispersed. Publication was in part an act of memory: this is what the secret music had been.","It is the accompaniment that matters historically. Most surviving music of the period leaves the keyboard player to improvise, but here the harpsichord part is written out in full, which makes this collection our clearest evidence of how the famous Ferrarese performances were actually put together. Notice that the voices' ornaments are written out too, so nothing is left to invention — an unusually complete photograph of a lost practice.","The 1601 print survives in very few copies and was long a bibliographical curiosity rather than living repertory. Once modern editions appeared, the pieces became central to the argument that monody emerged from courtly virtuoso singing rather than from Florentine theorising about ancient Greece. Performers now debate how strictly to follow the written ornaments, given that they were transcriptions of improvisation in the first place.","Listen with the balance in mind: the harpsichord is a real part, not wallpaper, so try to follow it against the voices. Two-soprano recordings show the echo writing best. Then play Caccini's Amarilli, published a year later for one voice, and hear the same ornament vocabulary shrunk to a single line."],"luzzaschi-o-primavera":["Spring, youth of the year — Mirtillo's speech from Guarini's pastoral play Il pastor fido, set for a single soprano over a written keyboard part. Three minutes. The voice declaims freely, hanging on the opening exclamation, then breaking into rapid decorated runs at the returning flowers and slowing almost to speech at the turn, where the speaker realises that spring has come back but his happiness has not.","Il pastor fido was the most set text in Italy around 1600: Guarini wrote it for Ferrara, where Luzzaschi worked, and composers everywhere mined its speeches for madrigals. This version belongs to the Roman print of 1601 that preserved Luzzaschi's music for the duke's private singers. The play's mixture of pastoral sweetness and real complaint suited a court style built on solo singers who could turn from ornament to plain address in a bar.","The piece is a solo song in everything but name, a year before Caccini's book announced solo song as a new invention. What separates them is a claim, not a sound: Caccini published a theory, Luzzaschi published a practice. Listen for the point where the ornament stops and the voice simply speaks — that gear-change is the technique the next generation would build entire operas on.","Because the collection appeared just as Florence was announcing the birth of monody, Luzzaschi's music has been used as the leading evidence that the Florentines overstated their originality. Scholarship now describes a north Italian court practice running in parallel, of which this print is the fragmentary survivor. As so few copies exist, questions about how representative it is remain open.","Compare it directly with Monteverdi's Cruda Amarilli, another Pastor fido setting from four years later, one for solo voice and one for five: the same poet, the same emotional turn, two completely different solutions. Listen here for the moment the runs cease. A light harpsichord and a singer who treats the words as speech serve it best."],"caccini-le-nuove-musiche":["A book of solo songs for one voice and a plucked or keyboard bass: a dozen or so through-composed madrigals, where the music follows the poem line by line, and a similar number of strophic arias that repeat their tune for each verse. Complete, it runs over an hour, though it was made to be dipped into. The sound is a single singer speaking in tune over slow chords, with ornament used sparingly and always at the words that deserve it.","Caccini was a Medici court singer in Florence and had spent the 1580s among the amateurs and scholars around Giovanni de' Bardi, arguing from Girolamo Mei's research that ancient Greek music had been sung by one voice so that words could be understood. The book, printed in Florence at the turn of 1601 into 1602, was his claim to have got there first — a claim aimed squarely at his rival Jacopo Peri.","The preface is the real invention. It teaches, in words, how to do things no notation had captured: sprezzatura, a noble carelessness in which the singer leans across the beat; the esclamazione, swelling or slackening a held note; and the trillo, a single pitch repeated faster and faster. It also insists the singer must never let decoration obscure the sense, which is why the ornaments are printed rather than left to chance.","Caccini was a relentless self-promoter, and posterity long took his word that he invented monody. Twentieth-century scholarship dismantled that story, pointing to Ferrara and to solo singing from written madrigals decades earlier. What survives the deflation is the preface, still the single most useful source on how early Baroque singers actually made a sound, and consulted by every performer who takes up this repertory.","Do not play it end to end. Take three or four songs, and listen for the swell and fade on long notes rather than for tune. A chitarrone accompaniment gives the sparest, most speech-like result; harpsichord makes it more formal. Then hear Monteverdi's Possente spirto to see what a composer of greater imagination did with the same equipment."],"caccini-amarilli-mia-bella":["Three minutes, one singer, a few chords: the most famous song of the early seventeenth century. The lover tells Amaryllis to believe he loves her, and if she doubts it, to open his heart and read her name written there. The line is nearly all stepwise, close to speech, with a gentle rocking bass beneath. Then the last phrase returns three times, decorated a little more each time, and the song simply lets go.","The words are by Battista Guarini, whose pastoral verse supplied half of Italy with texts, and the song circulated in Florentine court circles for years before it was printed in Caccini's collection. Florence around 1600 was full of solo singers accompanying themselves on the chitarrone at private gatherings; this is that music, written down. Caccini's own daughters were among the singers who made such pieces famous.","The repetitions at the end are not padding. Caccini writes each return slightly differently, printing the ornament he wanted rather than trusting the singer, and the effect is a lover repeating himself with rising urgency and diminishing certainty. Notice also how little the bass moves: the harmony walks the same short path over and over, so all the interest sits in the vocal line and the words.","The song had a long second life. In the 1880s Alessandro Parisotti anthologised it among the arie antiche with a romantic piano accompaniment, and generations of singers learned it that way, in a style with almost nothing to do with 1602. Period-instrument performance has restored the plucked bass and the free timing, and the argument between the two versions is still audible in any voice teacher's studio.","Hear it twice: once with piano from an old-fashioned recital album, once with chitarrone. The second sounds like someone talking, the first like an aria, and the difference is four centuries of habit. Listen for how the singer bends the beat against the accompaniment — that stretch is the sprezzatura Caccini wrote about."],"peri-euridice":["The earliest opera that survives complete: about ninety minutes in a single act, Orpheus loses Eurydice, goes to the underworld, and — since this was a wedding entertainment — brings her back. Almost all of it is sung in a new manner halfway between speech and song, one voice at a time over a handful of instruments hidden behind the scenery, broken up by short choruses and dances. There are no arias in the later sense and very little repetition of any kind.","It was staged at the Palazzo Pitti in Florence in October 1600, part of the celebrations for the marriage of Maria de' Medici to Henri IV of France. Ottavio Rinuccini wrote the libretto; Peri, a court singer with famously long hair, sang Orpheus himself. Caccini insisted that singers under his authority perform his own settings of their lines, so the first performance was a patchwork, and both men rushed rival scores into print.","Listen to what the bass does under the recitative. Peri explained in his preface that he made the accompaniment hold still through dissonances that speech would rush past, moving only where the sense turns — the harmony waits for the voice rather than leading it. That single decision is what makes dramatic recitative possible, and everything from Monteverdi to Mozart's dry recitative descends from it.","Its historical priority is undisputed and its popularity was never large; the tragic ending was avoided, the invention was quickly outclassed by Monteverdi's Orfeo seven years later, and the piece slept for three centuries. Modern revivals have argued that it is more than a museum object, though performers disagree sharply on how much instrumental colour to add to a score that specifies almost none.","Come to it after Monteverdi's Orfeo, not before, and the point becomes clear: this is the same story told with a fraction of the resources, so all the weight falls on the singer's delivery of the words. Listen for the messenger's speech announcing the death. Choose a recording with a small plucked continuo and singers who treat Italian as speech."],"cavalieri-rappresentatione-anima-et-corpo":["A sacred play set to music from beginning to end, ninety minutes in three acts, in which the Soul and the Body argue about pleasure, time and death, with Counsel, Intellect, Worldly Life and a chorus of the blessed and the damned joining in. The singing runs continuously between declamation over a bass and short, catchy choral numbers, several of them in dance rhythms with instruments doubling. It ends with an invitation to the audience to dance.","It was staged in February 1600 at the oratory attached to the Chiesa Nuova in Rome, the church of Filippo Neri's congregation, whose devotional meetings had long used simple sung laude to hold a lay audience. Cavalieri, a Roman nobleman, had been organising Medici court entertainments in Florence through the 1590s and knew the new declamatory singing from the inside. The score was printed in Rome that same year.","The print is a technical landmark: it carries numbers over the bass line to tell the keyboard player which harmonies to fill in — among the earliest figured basses in existence — plus a preface with practical instructions on staging, on how to place the instruments out of sight, and on how much ornament to allow. The rules for running a continuo group start here, in a book about the fate of the soul.","Historians have quarrelled for a century about what to call it: the first oratorio, because of its subject and its venue, or a sacred opera, because it was costumed, acted and danced. Cavalieri himself complained bitterly that the Florentines were stealing credit for the new style. The work now sits awkwardly between the two genres it helped begin, and stagings remain rare and expensive.","Start with the choruses rather than the arguments; they are short, tuneful and rhythmically springy, and they show how a devotional audience was kept awake. Then listen to a dialogue between Soul and Body for the new declamation. A staged video makes far better sense of it than an audio recording, because the piece was designed to be seen."],"frescobaldi-fiori-musicali":["Organ music for the Mass: three sets of pieces to be played at the points where the liturgy leaves room — before the service, between the verses of the Kyrie, after the Epistle, at the Elevation of the Host, at the end. Complete it runs over an hour. The writing alternates strict imitative pieces built on short subjects with free, wandering toccatas, and the Elevation toccatas are the strangest music Frescobaldi wrote: slow, dissonant, drifting, deliberately without a pulse.","Frescobaldi had been organist at St Peter's in Rome since 1608, the most conspicuous keyboard post in Italy, and published this collection in Venice in 1635 near the end of his life. Its purpose is practical: church organists needed music of the right length and character for fixed moments in the service, and this was a model book by the acknowledged authority, meant to be studied as much as played.","Bach owned this book and copied it out by hand in 1714, and the fingerprints are everywhere in his organ writing a generation later. Frescobaldi also plants puzzles: one ricercar carries an instruction about an obligation the player must observe, and the Bergamasca at the end is prefaced with a wry note that whoever plays it will learn no small thing. It is a teaching book disguised as a service book.","Uniquely for seventeenth-century keyboard music, it never fell out of use: organists and composers passed it down through Froberger to the German tradition and on to Bach, and later to nineteenth-century historians looking for the roots of counterpoint. Modern argument is about instruments and tuning — meantone temperament makes the Elevation dissonances bite in a way that equal temperament flattens out completely.","Do not attempt the whole book. Play one Elevation toccata alone, in a quiet room, and let its refusal to settle work on you; it sounds far closer to twentieth-century music than to 1635. Choose an Italian organ in meantone tuning. Then hear a Bach organ chorale and listen for the family resemblance."],"frescobaldi-cento-partite-sopra-passacagli":["A hundred variations, roughly a quarter of an hour, over the short repeating bass patterns that Italian guitarists strummed in the street: the passacaglia and its cousin the ciaccona. Frescobaldi keeps moving between them, and between speeds, metres and keys, so the piece drifts rather than builds — sometimes dancing, sometimes stalling into a slow chromatic descent, then picking up again somewhere unexpected. There is no grand climax, only continuous transformation.","It appeared in the expanded 1637 reprint of his first book of toccatas and partitas, published in Rome, by which time Frescobaldi was the most imitated keyboard player in Europe and his prints were reissued repeatedly. The passacaglia had risen from popular guitar practice — a strummed formula between the verses of a song — into art music within a generation, and this piece is the point where the street pattern becomes a long argument.","The joins are the substance. Frescobaldi shifts between passacaglia and ciaccona sections and between keys without preparation, so a listener who tries to follow the harmony as a plan gets lost, which is deliberate: his prefaces tell players to take these pieces in flexible time, as if singing a modern madrigal, and to stop wherever a section makes sense. The hundred variations are less a structure than a supply.","Nineteenth-century editors, wanting shapely forms, cut and reordered it. Its restless key changes were long treated as evidence of an unfinished or corrupt text before scholars accepted that the wandering is the design. It remains a test case in the argument about rhythmic freedom in Frescobaldi: how far a modern player should stretch the beat when the composer explicitly asked for it.","Do not listen for structure; listen for the bass pattern and count how many times it goes round before something changes. Harpsichord recordings make the dance origins clearer, organ ones the chromatic gloom. Then hear Bach's Passacaglia in C minor to feel the difference between an Italian drift and a German building."],"frescobaldi-toccata-nona-libro-secondo":["Six minutes of keyboard writing that behaves like improvisation caught in the act: a chord held while the fingers scatter figures above it, then a passage of imitation that promises to become a fugue and abandons the idea, then knotty cross-rhythms where the two hands seem to disagree about the beat. Nothing lasts more than half a minute before the argument turns. It is technically the hardest piece in the book, and it ends in a thicket of syncopation.","Printed in Rome in 1627 in his second book of toccatas, written for the harpsichords and small organs of Roman palaces rather than for church, and for players good enough to buy an expensive engraved score. Frescobaldi's toccatas were the equivalent of published jazz solos: written-down versions of what he did at the keyboard, sold to musicians across Europe who wanted to know how the famous man actually played.","At the end of this toccata Frescobaldi printed a remark to the performer: one does not reach the finish without effort. It is a joke about the difficulty, but it also tells you the piece was expected to defeat people. More important is the instruction in his prefaces that the beat should not be kept strictly, but taken as singers take it in modern madrigals — the score is a suggestion about timing, not a command.","The toccatas were reprinted throughout the century and copied by German organists, Froberger foremost, who carried the style north. Later they were treated as formless and unplayable, a reputation that lasted until harpsichordists in the twentieth century relearned the flexible rhythm the prefaces describe. Today the arguments are about temperament, ornamentation and how far the written rhythm should be bent.","Listen for the seams: every time the music seems about to settle into a pattern, note how long it takes to break. Try both a harpsichord and an organ recording of the same piece — the instrument changes the character completely. Then hear Bach's Toccata and Fugue in D minor for the same rhetorical gestures put to work in a fixed design."],"frescobaldi-se-l-aura-spira":["A short strophic song, three minutes, from the one book of vocal music by a composer known almost entirely for keyboards. If the breeze blows softly, dance, nymphs, among the flowers — and the music dances too, in a lilting triple time over a bass that steps down in a repeating pattern, with a light refrain returning after each verse. The voice stays high, tuneful and mostly unadorned, which is unusual for its date.","Frescobaldi spent 1628 to 1634 in Florence, in Medici service, away from St Peter's, and published two books of arie musicali there in 1630. Florence had been the capital of solo song for thirty years by then, and he was writing for its market — pieces short enough for an evening's entertainment, printed for singers who accompanied themselves. The poem is a pastoral invitation of the sort Florentine drawing rooms consumed by the yard.","Listen to the bass: it walks down the same few steps, over and over, in the manner of the strummed dance patterns Frescobaldi spent his life varying at the keyboard. The song is the same craft in miniature, with a tune on top. Notice also how little ornament is written in — after thirty years of monody, a composer could trust the pattern to carry the piece without decoration.","The vocal books were long treated as a sideline by scholars fixed on the keyboard music, and they are still under-recorded. Guitarists and lutenists adopted this song early because the repeating bass suits plucked instruments, and it is now one of the few early Baroque songs to appear regularly on recital programmes. Whether the arias were meant for professional or amateur singers remains unclear.","Take it as a dance and let the bass carry you; try tapping the repeating pattern and see how the tune floats free of it. A guitar or theorbo accompaniment suits it better than harpsichord. Follow it with Caccini's Amarilli for the contrast between a song that speaks and a song that moves."],"dindia-piangono-al-pianger-mio":["Four minutes for one voice and a bass, and among the harshest music of its decade. The mountains weep at my weeping, says the poem, and d'India sets it by moving the bass through intervals that have no business following one another, so the singer's held notes keep landing in harmonies that have shifted underneath them. The vocal line itself is mostly plain declamation; the strangeness is entirely in what supports it, and it never resolves comfortably.","D'India was Sicilian, from a noble Palermo family, and published this first book of solo songs in Milan in 1609 while looking for a court post; he found one soon after at the Savoy court in Turin. Solo song with continuo was seven years old in print and already crowded with imitators of Caccini. To stand out, a composer needed either a famous name or an unmistakable manner, and d'India chose the second.","In his preface d'India claimed to move through intervals unusual in modern practice, and he means it literally: the harmony steps into remote territory and steps back by a different route, so the ear cannot reconstruct the path. He had known Gesualdo's music, and this is what Gesualdo's chromatic shocks sound like when there is only one voice to carry them and nothing to hide behind.","D'India was well regarded in his lifetime and then almost completely forgotten, kept alive only in dictionaries as a follower of Caccini. Twentieth-century editions and a run of recordings by early-music singers reversed the judgement, and he is now heard as the most harmonically adventurous monodist of the generation after Caccini. The main practical debate concerns how freely the continuo should be realised under such unstable harmony.","Listen twice: first to the voice alone, which sounds almost ordinary, then to the bass, which does not. The gap between them is the piece. A singer who resists sounding operatic and a lutenist willing to let chords sit bare will serve it best. Then try Gesualdo's Moro, lasso for the five-voice version of the same instinct."],"dindia-lamento-d-olimpia":["Olimpia wakes on a deserted island to find that her lover has sailed away in the night. Eleven minutes for one voice and continuo, without arias, choruses or any other relief: the singer calls, listens, calls again, argues with the absent man, breaks down, collects herself, and breaks down once more. The music follows every swerve, moving between flat declamation, sudden high cries and passages where the beat almost stops. Nothing repeats.","The subject comes from Ariosto's Orlando furioso, the epic every literate Italian knew, and the piece belongs among the extended laments d'India published in his later books of monodies in the early 1620s, when he was moving between Turin, Modena and Rome. Monteverdi's Lamento d'Arianna had made the solo lament the most admired thing a composer could write, and every serious monodist attempted one.","The formal problem is silence. With no orchestra and no chorus, d'India has only the singer's stopping and starting to build ten minutes of structure, so he uses returns of a single interval — a falling cry that comes back transformed at each new stage of the collapse — as the thread. Listen for that shape recurring; it is what keeps a monologue from becoming a ramble.","The Arianna lament survives only as a fragment, which makes the extended laments of d'India and his contemporaries our best evidence of what the genre was actually like. They were neglected until modern singers, chiefly sopranos with a theatrical bent, made them concert repertory in the late twentieth century. Dating remains uncertain, as the laments appeared across several of his books.","Treat it as a one-woman play and follow a translation line by line; the piece is unlistenable as background and gripping as drama. Wait for the moments where the accompaniment drops out and the voice is alone. Compare Purcell's Dido, seventy years later, to see how much a ground bass changes the same situation."],"strozzi-lagrime-mie":["Eight minutes for soprano and continuo, and it opens with the singer holding the word tears while the line sags downward by semitones, unaccompanied except by a bare chord. My tears, why do you hold back? The piece then alternates: free, halting recitative for the grief, and short passages that gather into something like an aria before falling apart again. The opening cry returns at the end, unchanged, having settled nothing.","It appeared in Diporti di Euterpe, published in Venice in 1659. Strozzi was the adopted — probably natural — daughter of the poet Giulio Strozzi, who founded an academy in Venice partly as a platform for her singing, and she published eight volumes of her own music, more secular vocal music in print than any composer of the century. Excluded from church and opera-house employment, she made her career in print and in private rooms.","The construction is a trap that never springs. Twice the music starts to organise itself into a regular aria with a walking bass, and twice it disintegrates back into recitative, so the listener is repeatedly denied the release a seventeenth-century audience expected. The returning opening phrase confirms it: the piece is circular by design, a complaint that cannot get anywhere.","Strozzi's music was praised and then forgotten for three centuries, and early modern scholarship spent more time on whether she worked as a courtesan than on the songs. That has been reversed: she is now recognised as the leading composer of the Venetian chamber cantata and one of the most published composers, of either sex, in her century. This piece has become the standard recital entry point.","Listen to the first thirty seconds several times, and notice how long the singer holds the opening word before anything moves. Then follow the piece for the two false starts toward an aria. Sopranos differ enormously here — some sing it as opera, others as speech, and the second approach is closer to what the score suggests."],"strozzi-l-eraclito-amoroso":["Hear me, lovers: the singer begins by asking for attention, then explains that she has been betrayed and intends to spend the rest of her life weeping. Five minutes, soprano and continuo. The music sets the complaint over a bass that keeps stepping downward in the pattern seventeenth-century Italy used for grief, and the vocal line above it alternates between plain, almost conversational phrases and long sighing descents that come back like a refrain.","From Cantate, ariette e duetti, published in Venice in 1651 and dedicated, like several of her books, to a powerful patron whose protection a woman publishing music badly needed. The title casts the singer as Heraclitus, the philosopher who wept — a conceit typical of the Venetian academies where Strozzi performed. Venice in the 1650s had public opera houses, a busy music-printing trade and an appetite for chamber pieces of exactly this length.","The falling bass is the whole argument. It is the same descending pattern Monteverdi used for his weeping nymph, by 1651 so well established that any Venetian listener would recognise grief before a word was sung. Strozzi's contribution is what she does against it: the voice repeatedly refuses to align with the pattern, entering late or holding over, so the singer seems to resist the very form she is trapped in.","Her earlier books were long dismissed as elegant salon pieces beside the laments of the later collections. Recent study of the Venetian cantata has instead put them at the centre of the genre's formation, and noted how consistently Strozzi wrote for her own voice, with a technical demand quite unlike the music men wrote for their patrons' singers. Recordings have multiplied since the 1990s.","Find the falling bass in the first minute, then listen for how often the voice enters out of step with it. Compare Monteverdi's Lamento della ninfa, built on the same descent, to hear a public complaint against a private one. A small continuo group with theorbo suits it better than a full harpsichord and cello."],"strozzi-che-si-puo-fare":["What can one do, when the stars have no pity? Seven minutes of alternating recitative and aria for soprano and continuo, beginning with a long, drooping question and moving through passages of resignation and flashes of anger — the singer at one point resolving to sing anyway, and setting off into a lilting triple-time section that never quite becomes cheerful. The bass under the opening moves in slow, heavy steps, as though the question were being asked against resistance.","From Arie, published in Venice in 1664, the last of her collections to appear and issued when she was in her forties and providing for children of her own. The Venetian chamber cantata by this date was a recognised form with its own market: sold in print, performed in academies and private houses, and written for the professional soprano voice. Strozzi wrote almost nothing else — no operas, no church music, no instrumental works.","Listen to how she treats the word for pity. It arrives on the highest note of a phrase and is then repeated lower each time, so the appeal audibly loses force with every attempt. The piece as a whole works by that kind of erosion: each new section starts with more energy than it can sustain, and the recitative returns to reclaim it.","Because she published nothing but chamber songs, older histories treated Strozzi as a minor figure, a woman confined to the drawing room. The counter-argument, now widely accepted, is that the chamber cantata was where seventeenth-century Venice did its most concentrated vocal writing, and that publishing eight books was an extraordinary act of professional self-assertion in a city where women could not hold musical posts.","Follow the alternation: whenever the beat becomes regular, expect it to be taken away. Listen for the repeated appeals at falling pitches. Sopranos with a light, flexible voice and clear Italian do most for the piece; heavier, later-Baroque styles of singing bury it. Pair it with one of her laments to hear the range within a single book."],"francesca-caccini-liberazione-di-ruggiero":["A court entertainment of about seventy-five minutes: the knight Ruggiero is held captive by the enchantress Alcina, and the good sorceress Melissa frees him, whereupon Alcina's island of illusions is destroyed and the piece ends with a ballet on horseback. Two rival sorceresses drive the whole drama. The music runs continuously between declamation over continuo and short, memorable set pieces, with choruses of enchanted plants and a spectacular scene of collapsing magic.","Staged at the Villa di Poggio Imperiale near Florence in February 1625 for the visit of Prince Władysław Sigismund of Poland, and commissioned by the Archduchess Maria Magdalena, then ruling Tuscany as regent. Two powerful women commissioned and composed a drama in which two women hold all the power and the male hero is passed between them; nothing about that arrangement was accidental at a court governed by a regent.","The two sorceresses are separated by musical means as much as by plot: Alcina's music is the sensuous, ornamented, chromatic language of court song, while Melissa's is plainer, firmer and more declamatory, and Melissa first appears disguised in another voice's manner. Listen for Alcina's final rage, which abandons the seductive style entirely — the illusion drops out of the music before it drops out of the scenery.","Long cited only as the first opera composed by a woman, and reportedly performed in Poland a few years later, it went unheard for three centuries. Modern editions and staged revivals since the 1980s have shifted the discussion to the score itself and to what a Medici regency wanted from a spectacle about female power. Whether to call it an opera or a court ballet with music is still debated.","Listen for the difference between the two sorceresses; once you hear it, the plot needs no explanation. Alcina's lament near the end is the emotional centre. Look for a recording with a small band and a light, speech-based singing style, and follow it with Peri's Euridice from the same city a generation earlier."],"francesca-caccini-primo-libro-delle-musiche":["A large collection of songs for one or two voices with continuo, sacred and secular side by side: Latin motets, spiritual songs in Italian, madrigals that follow the poem line by line, strophic arias, and a few pieces with written-out variations for each verse. An hour if taken whole, though it was designed for browsing. The writing is compact and speech-led, with ornament placed sparingly and always where the words turn.","Printed in Florence in 1618, when Francesca Caccini was the highest-paid musician at the Medici court — singer, teacher, lutenist and composer, daughter of Giulio Caccini and trained in the house where solo song was invented. The mixture of sacred and secular reflects the court's actual use of music, in chapel one hour and chamber the next, and the two-voice pieces were probably meant for her to sing with a pupil.","Several arias print a different set of ornaments for each stanza, which is rare and revealing: instead of leaving decoration to the singer, she writes out how a strophic song should be varied as it goes, so the last verse is the most elaborate. That is a teacher's book as well as a performer's, and it preserves the improvising habits of the Florentine school in a form we can actually hear.","The collection went unstudied for centuries and is still incompletely recorded, in part because its mixed contents suit neither a sacred nor a secular programme. Recent scholarship treats it as the largest surviving single-composer collection of early solo song and as evidence of a professional woman's career in print. Performers argue about which pieces were written for her own voice and which for teaching.","Pick one strophic aria and follow it verse by verse, listening for how much more decorated the last stanza is than the first — that is the whole art of this style in three minutes. Then hear her father's Amarilli and note how the daughter writes more tightly and gives the singer less room to wander."],"praetorius-terpsichore":["More than three hundred short dances — bransles, courantes, voltas, gaillardes, ballets — set in four, five or six instrumental parts. Praetorius names no instruments at all, so each performance invents its own colour: recorders, shawms, crumhorns, sackbuts, violins, viols, guitars, tambourine and drum. The textures are plain and chordal, the tune almost always on top, the bass stamping the step. Modern players string a dozen dances into suites of five to ten minutes, running one straight into the next.","Praetorius spent most of his career as Kapellmeister to the Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg at Wolfenbüttel, where French dancing masters were employed to teach the court its steps. He collected their repertory, had a French violinist, Pierre-Francisque Caroubel, help arrange much of it, and printed the result in 1612 as the fifth volume of a projected series of secular collections. The rest never appeared; he died in 1621, having devoted the remaining years to sacred music and to writing about instruments.","Because no instrumentation is given, the book is unplayable without its companion: the second volume of Praetorius's own treatise Syntagma musicum, which lists every instrument then in use, gives its range, and prints engravings of them to scale. It is the reason we can dress these dances at all. Individual pieces carry small attributions — some to Caroubel, many marked as by an unknown hand — so the collection is part anthology, part arrangement, part composition.","The dances went unheard for three centuries and returned only with the early-music revival, when consorts led by David Munrow and others found in them the most immediately likeable music of the German Renaissance. Their popularity created its own argument: the prints contain no percussion, no dynamics and no instruments, and critics divide between performances that add drums and jingles freely and those that keep the sound sober and consort-like.","Put on any recording that runs several dances together and let the volta arrive — a fast triple-time dance in which the man lifted his partner off the ground, and you can hear the lift in the music. Compare a percussion-heavy account with a quiet one played on viols; both are defensible. Then try Dowland's galliards to hear the same steps taken more seriously."],"praetorius-es-ist-ein-ros-entsprungen":["Two and a half minutes of four-part singing, all voices moving together on the same syllables, the melody in the top part and nothing underneath it but warm consonance. There is no imitation, no counterpoint to follow, no instrumental support: just a hymn tune harmonised so that the third and fourth phrases lift gently and then settle. Most performances take one or two of the original German stanzas, sometimes adding a soft final verse in a later, richer setting.","Praetorius printed it in 1609 in one of the volumes of his vast Musae Sioniae, a project to supply Lutheran Germany with settings of its hymns in every conceivable format, from twelve-part polychoral splendour down to the plainest congregational harmony. The melody and its Marian text were older, from a Cologne hymnbook of 1599, and had roots in Catholic devotion; Praetorius, a Lutheran court musician, kept the tune and let the theology sit quietly.","The original hymn is about Mary — the rose springing from the root of Jesse — and Protestant hymnals gradually rewrote the second stanza to make the rose Christ instead. Singers today often perform a Marian first verse and a reworded second without noticing the seam. Listen also to how little Praetorius does: one chromatic inflection near the end of the fourth phrase is almost the only moment where the harmony steps outside plain diatonic motion, and it lands exactly on the word about the flower blooming.","For most of its life this was a functional hymn harmonisation among hundreds. Its modern fame is a byproduct of English cathedral Christmas services, where Theodore Baker's translation, \"Lo, how a rose e'er blooming\", and Hugo Distler's twentieth-century variations on the same tune made it a fixture. Scholars are careful to note that Praetorius composed the setting, not the melody, a distinction that concert programmes routinely blur.","Listen for the moment all four parts land together on the word \"Ros\" and the chord opens out. Hear it once by a small unaccompanied choir at conversational volume, then again in a big cathedral acoustic, and notice how much the reverberation does to music this simple. It pairs well with the plain hymn settings that Bach would write a century later on the same principle."],"hassler-mein-gmut-ist-mir-verwirret":["A love song for five voices, three minutes long, in the crisp dance-like manner of the Italian canzonetta: short phrases, clear rhythm, all voices moving more or less together so the words stay audible. The tune sits in the top part and falls in a shape so memorable that it survives being sung four times slower. The lower voices supply a lightly skipping accompaniment rather than independent counterpoint.","Hassler published it in 1601 in his Lustgarten neuer teutscher Gesäng, a collection of German secular songs and instrumental dances issued while he was working in Nuremberg for the city and, earlier, in Augsburg for the Fugger banking family. He had studied in Venice with Andrea Gabrieli, and the book is an attempt to give German words the lightness of the Italian songs he had absorbed there. The text complains, conventionally, of a young man distracted by a girl.","The melody had a second life its composer never saw. Within a decade it was fitted with a sacred text, and by 1656 Paul Gerhardt's \"O Haupt voll Blut und Wunden\" had claimed it permanently. Bach then used it five times in the St Matthew Passion, lowering it a step each time it returns in the closing stretch so that the tune sinks as Christ dies. The flirtatious original is buried inside one of the most solemn things in Western music.","Nineteenth-century writers were faintly embarrassed by the pedigree and sometimes suppressed it; twentieth-century scholarship put it back, and the borrowing is now a standard illustration of how freely Lutheran hymnody raided secular song. Performance splits between choirs who sing it briskly as the dance it is and those who slow it into a proto-chorale, which reverses the historical direction of travel.","Play the Hassler first, at its own quick tempo, and then any recording of the Passion chorale, and let the shock of recognition do the work. Look for a performance by a small vocal ensemble of one voice per part with a lute or viols doubling; the counterpoint is thin enough that a full choir can make it heavy."],"hassler-dixit-maria":["A short Latin motet for four unaccompanied voices on Mary's answer to the angel: \"Behold the handmaid of the Lord; be it unto me according to thy word.\" It opens with the voices entering one after another on the same rising figure, then relaxes into smooth, unhurried polyphony. The whole thing lasts under three minutes and never raises its voice; the only real event is the gentle brightening at the final cadence.","Hassler wrote it while employed in Augsburg by Octavian II Fugger, one of the richest men in Europe, in a household that expected Catholic music of Italian polish. He was a Lutheran serving Catholic and Protestant patrons in turn, which was ordinary enough in the free imperial cities and required him to write fluently in both idioms. The motet appeared in his Cantiones sacrae of 1591 and was reissued in expanded form in 1599, the version usually sung.","Hassler later built a whole mass on this motet — a parody mass, in the technical sense, recycling its opening imitation and its cadences as raw material for the Kyrie, Gloria and the rest. Hearing the motet and then the mass, you can watch a three-minute piece being stretched to half an hour. Notice too that the imitative opening enters at the fifth in a strict order, the schoolbook procedure, which is exactly why the piece is used to teach Renaissance counterpoint.","It has become one of the most-sung Renaissance motets in choral training precisely because it is so orthodox: nothing in it is difficult or strange. That has cost it critical attention, and writers on Hassler tend to point instead to his madrigals and his German songs. Its Italian manner has also raised a fair question about how much of Hassler is Andrea Gabrieli's teaching and how much is his own.","Follow one inner voice — the alto is easiest — from the first entry and hear how it keeps handing the same shape onward. Then listen for the single moment where all four voices arrive together, which is Hassler's way of underlining the text. Palestrina's motets make the obvious comparison; put \"Sicut cervus\" beside it and the family resemblance is unmistakable."],"schein-israelsbrunnlein":["Twenty-six settings of German scripture for five voices with a supporting bass line, each lasting three or four minutes. Schein sets the words the way Italian madrigalists set love poetry: a phrase about weeping gets falling lines and clashing suspensions, a phrase about running gets fast repeated notes, silence is used as punctuation. The complete set runs about seventy minutes, but the pieces were designed to be taken one at a time, at funerals and devotional gatherings.","Schein was Thomaskantor in Leipzig, in the post Bach would hold a century later, and the collection appeared in 1623, five years into the Thirty Years' War. His subtitle promised the texts set \"in a particular Italian madrigal manner\", which was both an advertisement and a warning: this was new. His own life supplied the tone. Four of his children died before he did, and several of the pieces were written for specific Leipzig funerals.","The German title means \"little fountains of Israel\", and Schein numbers the pieces as if they were springs. Follow the bass line: it is figured continuo, a device barely a generation old in Germany, and it lets the five voices thin out to two or three without the harmony collapsing — which is how Schein gets those sudden hollow moments on words about death. The most quoted is \"Die mit Tränen säen\", where the sowing figure and the reaping figure are the same motif at different speeds.","Contemporaries found the dissonance startling for church use, and the collection did not travel far outside Saxony. Its reputation was rebuilt in the twentieth century by German choral scholarship, which placed Schein beside Schütz and Scheidt as one of the \"three great S's\" of early Baroque Germany. Debate now centres on scale: whether these are choral pieces or, as the writing often suggests, music for five soloists with an organ.","Start with \"Da Jakob vollendet hatte\" or \"Die mit Tränen säen\" rather than the whole book. Listen for the moment the texture drops from five voices to two — Schein uses it as a stage direction. A one-voice-per-part ensemble with chamber organ shows the word-painting most clearly; a full choir turns it into something grander and blurrier."],"schein-banchetto-musicale":["Twenty dance suites for a five-part string consort, each opening with a stately paduana, following with a gagliarda, a courante and an allemande, and ending with a quick variation of that allemande called a tripla. One suite lasts about ten minutes; concerts usually take two or three. The sound is dark and even, all instruments in the same family, with the top line singing over three inner parts and a bass that keeps the dance honest.","Printed in Leipzig in 1617, the year after Schein took up the Thomaskantor post, the collection was aimed at the town's amateur music societies and student consorts as much as at any court — a musical banquet, as the title says, to be served in courses. German towns had strong traditions of collegia musica, and a set of pieces playable on whatever five string instruments were in the room was a commercial proposition as well as an artistic one.","Schein's innovation is buried in the ordering. The five dances of each suite are not independent pieces bundled together but variations on shared melodic and harmonic material, so the galliard is the pavan in triple time, and the tripla is the allemande sped up. He says so himself in the preface, and asks that the dances of one suite be kept together and not mixed with others — an early, explicit argument that a suite is a single work.","The book was reprinted in Schein's lifetime, which for German instrumental music of the period is a mark of real success, and it stands as the fullest German answer to the variation-suite idea before the century turned toward Italy. Modern performances argue over instruments: the title page allows viols or violins, and the two choices produce startlingly different music, the viols reflective, the violins sharply articulated.","Take one whole suite and follow the tune from the slow pavan into the galliard; the pleasure is entirely in recognising the same idea wearing different clothes. Try a viol consort first, then a violin band, and see which you prefer. Praetorius's Terpsichore, from five years earlier, is the natural companion, though it is a collection of dances rather than an argument about them."],"scheidt-tabulatura-nova":["Three volumes of organ music: variation sets on chorales and on secular songs, fantasias, echo pieces exploiting two contrasting manuals, and a third book of liturgical music for the Lutheran service. A single variation set runs eight to fifteen minutes and works by keeping one melody constant while the surrounding texture is rebuilt again and again — first in long notes, then in running figures, then broken up between the hands and the pedals.","Scheidt spent his life in Halle, where he was court organist and then Kapellmeister to the Margrave of Brandenburg, and he had gone as a young man to Amsterdam to study with Jan Pieterszoon Sweelinck, the teacher of half of north Germany's organists. The 1624 publication came just as the Thirty Years' War reached Saxony; within a few years his court chapel was disbanded, plague took four of his children, and he was reduced to teaching and to writing small sacred concertos.","The title means \"new tablature\", and it is a claim about notation, not music. German organists wrote on a cramped system of letters; Scheidt printed his music on five separate staves, one per voice, in Italian open score, so that every strand of the counterpoint could be seen and studied. He explains the pedal markings and the use of two manuals in a preface. The book is thus both repertory and instruction manual, and it teaches the eye as much as the hands.","Scheidt's contemporaries treated the collection as a standard, and its variation technique runs straight through Buxtehude and Pachelbel to Bach. Later criticism has been cooler, finding the sheer number of variations mechanical, and there is a fair argument that the pieces were meant to be excerpted rather than played whole. Registration is the live performance question: Scheidt specifies almost nothing, and the north German organs he knew are not the ones most recordings use.","Start with a set of variations on a tune you know, such as \"Vater unser im Himmelreich\", and count the ways the melody is disguised while never actually changing. Listen for the echo pieces, where a phrase is answered quietly on the other manual — a party trick in 1624 and still an effective one. A north German organ with bright, separated stops suits this music better than a smooth Romantic instrument."],"scheidt-ludi-musici":["Instrumental pieces in four and five parts: paduanas, galliards, courantes, allemandes, canzonas, and several sets of variations on popular tunes, all with a continuo bass. Individual movements run two to five minutes. The writing is more contrapuntal than most dance music of the time — the inner parts have real melodic work — and the canzonas move by contrasting fast, chattering sections with slow, chordal ones, in the Italian manner Scheidt knew from prints rather than travel.","The first book appeared in Hamburg in 1621, with further parts following through the decade, aimed at the town waits, court bands and student consorts of Protestant Germany. Scheidt was still Kapellmeister at Halle and had an ensemble to write for; within a few years the war had removed it. Some of the later parts survive only incompletely, individual part-books having been lost, which is why performers often play reconstructions.","Several sets take their tune from popular song, including a much-copied set on the melody known as \"Est-ce Mars\", which Sweelinck had also varied at the keyboard. Scheidt's version is the same pupil's exercise translated to a consort of instruments, and comparing the two shows what he learned in Amsterdam. Note how often the bass line, rather than the top part, gets the melody in the middle of a variation — a habit he carried from the organ loft.","The collection has never had the standing of the Tabulatura nova, partly because of those missing part-books and partly because dance music by a famous organist tends to be treated as a sideline. Recent playing has argued the opposite case: that this is where the German violin band first sounds like an ensemble with a personality rather than a group of players doubling a tune.","Listen for a variation set rather than a single dance, and follow the tune as it migrates from the top voice down into the bass and back. A period band of violins, violas and violone with organ or harpsichord gives the right bite. Schein's Banchetto musicale of four years earlier is the obvious companion; Scheidt is the rougher and more contrapuntal of the two."],"froberger-tombeau-blancrocher":["Six minutes for solo harpsichord in the free, unmeasured French lute manner: chords spread from the bottom upward, phrases that stop and restart, no steady pulse at all. The piece drifts through a series of sighing suspensions and then, in its last bars, releases a slow scale that falls the length of the keyboard to the lowest note and stays there. Nothing is repeated; nothing comes back. It simply descends and stops.","Froberger, imperial court organist in Vienna, was travelling in Paris in 1652 when the lutenist Charles Fleury, Sieur de Blancrocher, died after a fall down a flight of stairs. Froberger is said to have been present, or at least close by. Blancrocher was well liked in Parisian musical circles, and at least four composers wrote tombeaux for him, that being the French custom: an instrumental memorial in the style the dead man himself had played.","The falling scale at the end is generally heard as the fall down the staircase, which would make this one of the earliest pieces of keyboard music to narrate a specific accident. Froberger did this elsewhere too — he wrote pieces about being robbed on the Rhine and about his own future death — so the reading is in character rather than fanciful. Note also that the whole piece is notated without bar lines in some sources, meaning the performer, not the composer, decides where the beats fall.","Froberger published almost nothing; his music circulated in manuscript and in the autograph volumes he presented to the emperor, and two of those volumes only resurfaced in the early 2000s, which reopened questions of text and attribution across his output. Performers argue continually about how free the unmeasured style should be. Louis Couperin's tombeau for the same lutenist makes the comparison unavoidable, and opinion divides on which is the finer piece.","Give it your full attention for six minutes with nothing else playing; it is too quiet to survive as background. Wait for the final descent and let the sound die away completely before moving. Then hear the same music on a fretted clavichord if you can find a recording — the instrument's tiny dynamic range makes the piece even more private."],"froberger-lamentation-ferdinand-iv":["This stands in place of the opening allemande of a harpsichord suite, and it behaves like the tombeau: slow, unmeasured, built from broken chords and drooping figures, with the rhythm left largely to the player. What sets it apart is the ending. After five minutes of sinking gestures, a single line climbs steadily upward through three octaves to the very top of the keyboard and holds there, alone, before the piece stops.","Ferdinand IV, King of the Romans and heir to the Habsburg throne, died of smallpox in July 1654 at twenty-one, an event that destabilised the imperial succession and shocked the Vienna court. Froberger had been court organist there since 1637, with long absences for travel, and knew the young king personally; Ferdinand was himself musically trained. The piece belongs to a small group of Froberger's occasional laments written for members of the imperial family.","The rising scale at the close is not decoration but a picture: the soul ascending. In one surviving source the ascent is drawn out and marked so that the final note is unmistakably a destination, and copies of the piece transmit an annotation about the ascent to heaven. Set it against the Blancrocher tombeau, which ends by falling to the bottom of the instrument, and you have the same composer using the same device in opposite directions two years apart.","Froberger's laments were once treated as curiosities beside his suites and toccatas; the rediscovery of autograph sources has made them central to arguments about how far seventeenth-century instrumental music could carry a specific programme. The unmeasured notation remains contested, and modern editions differ so much in barring that two recordings can sound like different pieces.","Listen for the last minute above all, and follow the single line as it climbs; the effect only works if you have accepted the slowness of everything before it. Hear it played on a small Flemish-style harpsichord rather than a large one. Then play Froberger's Blancrocher tombeau immediately afterwards and hear the mirror image."],"rosenmuller-sonate-da-camera":["Eleven works for five-part strings with continuo, each opening with a sinfonia and continuing through a chain of dances — allemande, courante, ballo, sarabande. The sinfonias are the interesting part: substantial, contrapuntal, often slow and grave before turning fast, and closer to the Italian church sonata than to anything a dancer could use. A single work runs six or seven minutes; the complete set makes an hour of unusually varied listening.","Rosenmüller was a Leipzig man, assistant at the Thomasschule and heir apparent to the Thomaskantor post, until his arrest in 1655 on charges involving choirboys. He escaped custody and fled to Venice, where he spent some twenty-five years as a trombonist at St Mark's and composer to the Ospedale della Pietà, the orphanage-conservatory that would later employ Vivaldi. This collection was printed in Venice in 1667, by a German writing Italian music for Italian players.","The book is a hybrid nobody else was writing: German suite order with Italian sinfonias bolted on the front, five real parts where Italians were reducing to trio texture, and dances too contrapuntally busy to dance to. Listen to the viola parts, which carry independent lines rather than filling in chords — a habit from the German consort tradition that Italian composers had already abandoned. Rosenmüller finally returned home in 1682 to Wolfenbüttel, bringing the Venetian style with him.","Contemporaries in Germany knew and copied the collection, and it is one of the routes by which Italian sonata style reached the north before Corelli's prints did. Modern attention has been slow and partly biographical, since the scandal makes a better story than the music; the corrective view treats him as the most Italian of the German instrumental composers and the missing link between Schein and Muffat.","Begin with any sinfonia and stop there if the dances feel slight; that is where the ambition is. Listen for the moment the slow opening breaks into imitation and the five parts start chasing one another. A one-per-part period ensemble with organ continuo suits it; the music thins badly with a large string body."],"rosenmuller-welt-ade-ich-bin-dein-mude":["A short funeral piece for five voices with continuo on a text that says goodbye to the world — \"World, farewell, I am weary of you\" — in the resigned, unhurried tone German Lutherans reserved for good deaths. The melody is Rosenmüller's own and moves in even, stepwise phrases with a gentle rise at the start of each line. The five parts stay close together, more harmonised hymn than counterpoint, and the whole thing is over in four minutes.","Rosenmüller wrote it in Leipzig around 1649, in his twenties, while he was making his name as an assistant at the Thomasschule; funeral music was steady, paid work in a city where prominent burials came with printed music. Germany was one year out of the Thirty Years' War, with a third of the population dead in some regions, and hymns about being tired of the world were not metaphors for most of the people singing them.","Seventy-five years later Bach reached for this exact setting. The closing chorale of his cantata \"Wer weiß, wie nahe mir mein Ende\" is Rosenmüller's five-part harmonisation, taken over essentially unaltered rather than reworked in Bach's usual manner — a rare case of Bach conceding that someone else had already got it right. Whether Leipzig congregations recognised the older Kantor-in-waiting's music is unknown, but the tune had stayed in local hymnals throughout.","The piece survives mainly through Bach's borrowing, which is both its good fortune and the reason it is rarely discussed on its own terms. Dating rests on hymnal appearances rather than an autograph, so \"about 1649\" is the honest formulation. Performances divide between choirs who take it as a chorale, one note to a syllable and slow, and ensembles who find a subtle dance rhythm in it.","Hear it first in a Bach cantata recording, where it arrives as the last two minutes after half an hour of music, then find it standing alone and notice how differently the same notes land. A small ensemble with organ is right. Compare it with Schütz's funeral settings, which are more elaborate and less consoling."],"biber-mystery-sonatas":["Fifteen sonatas for violin and continuo, grouped in threes to follow the Joyful, Sorrowful and Glorious mysteries of the Rosary, and a sixteenth piece, a passacaglia for unaccompanied violin, standing outside the scheme. Each sonata is a loose sequence of movements — a slow prelude, a set of variations over a repeating bass, a dance, an aria. Complete performances run two hours. The sound changes constantly and unnervingly, because the violin is retuned between almost every sonata.","Biber was in Salzburg in the service of the Prince-Archbishop, and the manuscript is dedicated to him. Salzburg's Jesuits ran a Rosary confraternity whose devotions involved processing between fifteen altars, one per mystery, meditating at each; music of this kind fits that practice. The set was never printed and survives in a single manuscript in Munich, without a title page, so even the name by which we call it is a modern convenience.","Fourteen of the sixteen pieces require scordatura: the strings are tuned to something other than the normal intervals, which changes the chords the hand can reach and gives each sonata its own acoustic personality. The eleventh, on the Resurrection, goes furthest — the middle two strings are crossed over each other between the pegbox and the bridge, physically forming a cross on the instrument, and the retuning lets the violin play the Easter hymn in octaves. Every sonata in the manuscript is headed by a small engraving of its mystery.","Unknown outside Salzburg for two centuries, the cycle was published in 1905 and only became widely played after 1970, when violinists willing to keep several instruments in different tunings on stage began recording it complete. It is now among the most recorded Baroque works. Arguments continue over whether it was devotional music for actual use, a presentation gift, or a private exercise, and over how literally the pictures should shape the playing.","Do not start at the beginning. Try the eleventh sonata, the Resurrection, for the strange doubled sound of the crossed strings, then the closing passacaglia, four descending notes repeated sixty-five times under a single violin. Choose a recording that uses several instruments rather than one retuned repeatedly; the tuning is audibly more secure."],"biber-battalia":["Ten minutes of battle music in eight short movements: a bustling opening, a scene of drunken soldiers, a march, a night watch, the battle itself, and a closing lament for wounded musketeers. The effects are physical. Players strike the strings with the wood of the bow, a sheet of paper is woven between the strings of the bass to make it buzz like a snare drum, and one movement stacks eight different folk songs on top of one another in eight different keys at once.","Written in 1673, when Biber was newly established in Salzburg, and inscribed to Bacchus. Central Europe had been at war for most of the century and would face the Ottoman advance on Vienna within ten years; battle pieces were a well-worn genre, going back to Renaissance chansons full of trumpet imitations. What is unusual here is the tone, which begins as comedy and ends without any victory at all, only the sound of wounded men.","The movement titled \"the dissolute company of all sorts of humours\" is the one people remember: Biber writes in the score that the soldiers are bawling different songs, and the resulting collision of keys was not attempted again in European art music for well over two centuries. His instructions in the manuscript are unusually chatty, explaining the paper trick and the bow-wood striking as things the players will not otherwise believe. The final lament is in a different world entirely, slow and quiet and completely serious.","Twentieth-century writers seized on the polytonal movement as a prophecy of Ives or Stravinsky, which is anachronistic but understandable; the more careful reading is that Biber is depicting noise, not exploring harmony. The piece is now a recital favourite and risks being played for laughs, and the best performances treat the joke seriously enough to make the ending hurt.","Ten minutes is nothing; play it whole. Wait for the drunken chorus — you will hear it collapse — and then hold on for the final lament, which is the point of the whole piece. Compare it with Schmelzer's Viennese ballet suites for the courtly version of the same world, and with any conventional Renaissance battle piece to hear what Biber refused to do."],"biber-missa-salisburgensis":["A setting of the mass in fifty-three separate parts: two full choirs of eight voices, two string groups, oboes and cornetts, two sets of trumpets and drums, and continuo, plus two extra brass ensembles placed apart from the rest. Forty-five minutes long. The effect is not density but space — blocks of sound answer each other from opposite sides of a building, and the full ensemble is saved for a handful of moments that arrive like doors opening.","Almost certainly written for Salzburg Cathedral, whose galleries and side balconies were designed for exactly this kind of distributed music-making, and probably for the celebrations of 1682 marking eleven hundred years since the founding of the archbishopric. Biber was Kapellmeister there and had the whole cathedral establishment plus visiting musicians at his disposal. The Ottoman army would besiege Vienna the following year; this was the last confident moment of the old Habsburg church spectacle.","The scoring exploits architecture as an instrument. Groups are stationed at different heights and distances, so the same chord reaches the listener from several directions with slightly different delays, and the piece is essentially unperformable in a concert hall as intended. Note how sparingly Biber uses everyone at once — most of the mass is thin, two or three groups conversing, which is what makes the tutti passages register at all.","For a long time the work was credited to the Roman composer Orazio Benevoli and dated to 1628, which made it a monument of Roman polychoral writing decades earlier than it is. Examination of the manuscript paper and watermarks in the 1970s moved the attribution to Biber and the date to around 1682, rewriting a chapter of textbooks. A small number of scholars still hedge, but the Biber attribution is now standard.","Seek a recording made in Salzburg Cathedral itself, where the separations are real rather than simulated by microphones; headphones help enormously. Listen first for the trumpet groups entering from different directions in the Gloria. Tallis's forty-part motet is the obvious comparison, and the difference is instructive: Tallis weaves, Biber builds walls."],"schmelzer-sonatae-unarum-fidium":["Six sonatas for a single violin and continuo, each running eight to twelve minutes in one continuous span rather than separate movements. The music moves by changing gear: an improvisatory opening gives way to a set of variations over a short repeating bass, then to a fast passage in running notes, then back to something slow. Several sonatas are built almost entirely on ground basses, so the same few chords cycle underneath while the violin invents at increasing speed above.","Schmelzer was the leading violinist of the Vienna imperial court, working for Ferdinand III and then Leopold I, an emperor who composed himself and spent lavishly on music. The 1664 print — its title means \"sonatas for single strings\", that is, for one violin — was probably the first published collection of solo violin sonatas by a German-speaking composer, and it advertised a level of playing that Italy had assumed was its own property.","Listen to the bass lines. Half the collection sits on ostinatos of four or eight bars, which lets Schmelzer write increasingly wild violin figuration without losing the listener, because the harmony never moves anywhere new. This is the technique Biber would inherit and use for the great passacaglia that ends the Rosary Sonatas. Schmelzer also writes multiple-stopped chords freely, treating the violin as capable of its own accompaniment.","The collection was known and copied across Austria and southern Germany and stands behind the whole Austro-Bohemian violin school, but its composer was overshadowed within a generation by his own successor in the tradition, Biber. Schmelzer's reputation has recovered slowly since the 1970s. Ornamentation is the practical dispute: the printed line is spare, and the performer is expected to add a great deal that is not written down.","Pick a ground-bass sonata and set yourself to hear the bass pattern come round; once you have it, the violin's escalating invention above becomes far more gripping. Then hear Biber's passacaglia and note that the trick is the same. Look for a recording with a varied continuo group — organ, harpsichord, theorbo and viol trading off — since the bass repeats a great deal."],"schmelzer-die-fechtschule":["A ballet suite of short character movements, twelve minutes end to end, depicting a fencing school: an entry, the bouts themselves with clipped exchanges of phrases between the upper strings, a movement for the barber-surgeon who patches up the losers, and a closing dance. The scoring is a five-part string band with continuo. Everything is brief, sharply rhythmic, and pictorial in a deadpan way — the fencers' passes really do sound like blade meeting blade.","Schmelzer supplied the Vienna court with ballet music by the cartload; well over a hundred such suites survive, written for the entertainments that filled the Habsburg calendar between operas, tournaments and equestrian displays. Fencing schools were public spectacle in seventeenth-century Vienna, with prizes and crowds, and putting one on stage was a joke the audience would get immediately. Court ballet at Vienna was less codified than at Versailles and more willing to be funny.","The comedy is structural: the fencing movements are built from short phrases that answer each other at close range, so the ensemble is literally trading blows, and the barber's movement limps in a deliberately awkward rhythm. Schmelzer's ballet suites also preserve a detail the operas do not — the dance music that framed the sung drama, most of which was thrown away after use. That so many of his survive is largely luck of one archive.","This repertory has been dismissed as functional court filler for most of its history, and the manuscripts sat unexamined in Kroměříž and Vienna. Period-instrument bands rediscovered it in the 1980s and found music of real character. The honest caveat is that we do not know the choreography, so pacing, repeats and the order of movements are largely modern reconstruction.","Listen for the exchanges in the fencing movement and try to hear them as two players, not one ensemble. It works well straight after Biber's Battalia, which is the same world with the joke pushed much harder. A small period band that plays the dance rhythms crisply, with a percussion player kept on a short leash, gives the best account."],"johann-christoph-bach-es-erhub-sich-ein-streit":["Eight minutes of war in heaven, scored in twenty-two parts for two five-voice choirs, four trumpets, drums, strings and continuo. The text is from Revelation: Michael and his angels fought against the dragon. The piece opens with the choirs hurling the word \"Streit\" — battle — at each other over blazing trumpets, drops to a hushed passage as the dragon is cast down, and ends in a shout of victory. There is almost no counterpoint; it works by mass and impact.","Johann Christoph Bach was town organist and harpsichordist at Eisenach, in the same Thuringian musical dynasty as his younger relative Johann Sebastian, who was born there. Michaelmas, on 29 September, was a major civic feast in Lutheran Germany and the occasion for the loudest music of the year, when town trumpeters could be hired legitimately for church use. The scale here suggests a joint effort of church, court and town musicians.","Johann Sebastian Bach copied out this music, performed it in Leipzig, and in the family genealogy he compiled called this cousin \"the profound composer\" — a rare piece of direct testimony from Bach about anyone's music. The piece survived in the Altbachisches Archiv, the family's own collection of ancestral scores, which Bach maintained. That archive vanished at the end of the Second World War and only resurfaced in Kyiv in the 1990s, so the work was known for decades chiefly from old editions.","Nineteenth-century enthusiasts sometimes credited the piece to Johann Sebastian, and the confusion between the many musical Bachs has dogged the older generation's music generally. Scholarship now treats Johann Christoph as a substantial figure in his own right rather than a footnote to his cousin's family tree, though performances remain uncommon because assembling twenty-two parts for eight minutes is an awkward proposition.","Listen for the first entry of the trumpets and then for the sudden drop in volume when the dragon falls — the contrast is the whole design. Play it beside Schütz's dramatic dialogues to hear what a generation added in sheer noise. A period band with natural trumpets is essential; valved instruments make it sound merely loud."],"johann-christoph-bach-ach-dass-ich-wassers-gnug-hatte":["A lament for one alto voice, a solo violin and three violas over continuo — an unusually dark string sound, with nothing above the violin and everything else clustered in the middle register. The text, from Jeremiah, wishes for enough water to weep day and night. The violin sighs above the voice throughout, the violas move in slow, thick harmony beneath, and the piece never quickens or resolves into anything consoling. It lasts about eight minutes.","Sacred solo pieces of this kind, for one voice with strings, were the intimate end of Lutheran church music in the later seventeenth century, performed at funerals and in private devotions rather than at the main Sunday service. Johann Christoph worked at Eisenach in a post that never paid well; he complained about money for most of his life. The dark viola-heavy scoring belongs to a German tradition of funeral music that deliberately removed the bright upper instruments.","The violin and the voice imitate each other continually, but never quite in agreement — the instrument enters a beat late or a step apart, so the two are always slightly out of phase, which is what produces the sensation of inconsolable circling. Bach's alto lament \"Erbarme dich\", violin and voice entwined the same way, is close enough that the resemblance has been noticed for a century, though there is no evidence of direct modelling.","The attribution is not secure. The piece comes down in sources naming a Johann Christoph Bach without specifying which of several relatives, and scholars have variously assigned it to the Eisenach organist and to others in the family; the Eisenach attribution is the majority view but is argued rather than proved. Its modern fame rests largely on recordings by countertenors, which has drawn the objection that the original was probably sung by a boy or a falsettist of a different sort.","Follow the violin rather than the voice for the first two minutes and hear how it refuses to line up with the singer. Then hear Bach's \"Erbarme dich\" straight afterwards. Look for a recording with real violas rather than modernised scoring, and with an organ continuo; harpsichord makes the texture too bright for what the piece is doing."],"kerll-capriccio-cucu":["Five minutes built from two notes. A cuckoo call — a falling third, always the same two pitches — enters and then simply will not go away: it becomes a fugue subject, an accompaniment figure, a bass line, an interruption in the middle of other people's phrases. Around it Kerll writes brisk running counterpoint that keeps trying to develop into something else and keeps being cut off by the bird. The piece ends with the call alone, unanswered.","Kerll spent his career between Munich and Vienna as court organist and Kapellmeister to Bavarian and Habsburg employers, having trained in Vienna and, probably, in Rome with Carissimi. Keyboard capriccios on birdcalls and animal noises were an Italian tradition he would have met there; Frescobaldi had written one on the cuckoo. The piece circulated in manuscript rather than print, which was normal for organ music intended as a party trick as much as a service voluntary.","The joke has a technical point. Because the cuckoo call is fixed at the same two pitches and never transposes, every harmony Kerll writes must accommodate those notes, and the piece becomes a demonstration of how many different chords can contain the same interval. Listen for the passages where the surrounding music modulates and the bird suddenly sounds dissonant without having changed at all. Handel later borrowed from Kerll's keyboard canzonas in Israel in Egypt, so this repertory travelled further than its manuscript circulation suggests.","Kerll's reputation has always been squeezed between Froberger before him and Pachelbel and Buxtehude after, and his output is scattered across manuscripts of varying reliability, some of them attributing the same pieces to different composers. The Capriccio is his best-known piece by a wide margin, which slightly misrepresents a composer whose masses and organ versets are more substantial. Performers disagree about whether it belongs on organ or harpsichord; the sources do not say.","Count the cuckoo calls if you like — the running total is part of the fun — and notice how Kerll times the ones that arrive too early. Try it on a bright organ and then on a harpsichord and pick a side. Then hear Rameau's hen and Couperin's nightingale for the French version of the same idea, which is prettier and less obsessive."],"muffat-armonico-tributo":["Five multi-movement sonatas for strings, each about twelve minutes, mixing Italian sonata movements with French dances and ending, in the fifth, with a long passacaglia. The point of the collection is written into it: Muffat marks where a small group of soloists should play and where the full body should join, so the same piece can be a chamber sonata for five players or an orchestral work for twenty. That alternation is the concerto grosso in embryo.","Muffat, born in Savoy of Scottish descent, had studied in Paris in the 1660s among Lully's musicians and then gone to Rome, where in 1682 he heard Corelli's concertos played in Corelli's own house. He was organist at Salzburg under the Prince-Archbishop, alongside Biber, and published this collection there that same year. It is the work of a man who had personally sat in the two rooms where the French and Italian styles were being invented.","Muffat's prefaces are as valuable as his notes. He explains, in several languages, exactly how the loud-soft alternation should be managed and how many players to use, making this one of the earliest documents to describe orchestral practice as a deliberate choice rather than an accident of who turned up. Nineteen years later he reworked the same music into a set of full concerti grossi, so the two versions can be compared directly to see what he thought the orchestra had become.","Because Corelli's Opus 6 concertos were published only in 1714, long after both men had heard each other's music, priority arguments are unresolvable and mostly beside the point; Muffat himself credited Corelli generously and repeatedly. The collection was long treated as a curiosity of style-mixing. It is now read as a key document in how a specifically international Baroque idiom formed, and as the ancestor of the German orchestral suite tradition.","Listen for the moments where the sound suddenly thickens as the full band joins the soloists, then thins again; that switch is the whole invention. The fifth sonata's passacaglia is the best single movement to start with. Compare a performance played one to a part with a full string-orchestra version — Muffat sanctioned both, and they are barely the same piece."],"muffat-florilegium-secundum":["Eight orchestral suites, each opening with a French overture — slow dotted rhythms, then a fast fugal section — and continuing through a string of dances with picturesque titles. Five string parts, thicker in the middle than Italian scoring, played by an orchestra rather than soloists. A single suite runs eight to twelve minutes. The style is Lully's, transplanted: crisp, rhythmically inflected, dependent on a bowing discipline that makes a whole band phrase as one player.","By 1698 Muffat was Kapellmeister to the Prince-Bishop of Passau, having left Salzburg, and Germany was full of musicians trying to write French music without ever having been to France. He had been there. The Florilegium volumes were published as a service to those musicians: not just tunes, but the operating instructions for a style that German players were producing badly because nobody had told them how it worked.","The preface is the famous part, printed in four languages and running to many pages. It sets out how the French bow their strings — the rule that the strong beat always takes a down-bow, which is why the music breathes as it does — how they play unequal notes in passages written evenly, how they ornament, and how French and Italian taste differ. It is the single most detailed account of Baroque performance practice by anyone who had watched both traditions from inside.","The music has been overshadowed by its own preface, which is quoted in every book on Baroque performance while the suites go unplayed. Period-instrument orchestras have begun to correct that, and the works reveal themselves as more inventive than their model in the inner parts. The prefaces have also raised a scholarly caution: Muffat is describing French practice in the 1660s, decades before publication, so his rules may already have been out of date.","Start with the overture of any suite and listen to the dotted rhythms — whether they are played sharply, dragging behind the beat, or evenly tells you everything about the performers' convictions. Follow with Bach's orchestral suites, which are the same French genre a generation on. A period band that observes Muffat's bowing rules sounds noticeably different from one that does not."],"muffat-apparatus-musico-organisticus":["Twelve toccatas for organ, plus a chaconne, a passacaglia and a set of variations, about eighty-five minutes complete. Each toccata is a chain of contrasting sections rather than a single argument: grand opening chords, a fugue, a passage of free running figuration, another fugue at a different speed, a closing flourish. Nothing lasts long enough to settle. The organ's full range is used, with pedal entries that arrive like an extra person joining the argument.","Published in Salzburg in 1690 and dedicated to Emperor Leopold I, in the years when Muffat was seeking a better post and using publication as a calling card; he moved to Passau soon afterwards. He had studied in Rome with Bernardo Pasquini and absorbed the Italian toccata tradition that ran back to Frescobaldi, then brought it to instruments and an audience formed by the very different north German organ culture.","The sectional design is a rhetorical one, learned in Rome: each toccata is built like a speech, with an exordium to seize attention, a body of argument in the fugues, digressions and a peroration. Muffat's own preface talks about affect and about the freedom of tempo the style requires, which means that a performance following the note values exactly is playing the piece wrong. The chaconne and passacaglia at the end sit apart, strict variation forms placed after all that freedom.","The collection was widely known in German-speaking lands and is a plausible link between the Italian toccata and the great preludes of Buxtehude's and Bach's generation, though direct influence is hard to prove and easy to overstate. Modern organists find the writing occasionally awkward, since Muffat wrote for Italian-influenced instruments with different pedal expectations, and registration for these pieces is largely a matter of informed guesswork.","Take one toccata and mark the seams — the moment each new section starts is usually unmistakable, and the piece becomes far more coherent once you hear it as a sequence of episodes. The closing passacaglia is the easiest way in. Compare with Buxtehude's toccatas for the northern equivalent, which is wilder and less Roman."],"fux-costanza-e-fortezza":["A festa teatrale in three acts on a Roman subject — the constancy of the besieged city, an allegory of Habsburg steadfastness — running well over three hours. The music alternates formal da capo arias with unusually weighty choruses and instrumental movements, and the counterpoint is much denser than Italian opera of the period generally allowed. Trumpets and drums dominate the loud numbers; the writing assumes an orchestra several times the size of a normal theatre band.","Composed for the coronation of Emperor Charles VI as King of Bohemia and staged in Prague in the late summer of 1723, in a wooden open-air theatre built for the occasion in the castle grounds. The performing forces were enormous, drawing on musicians summoned from across the empire. Fux, Hofkapellmeister in Vienna and by then in his sixties and badly afflicted by gout, had to be carried to Prague to supervise it.","The audience was as remarkable as the show. Musicians who travelled to Prague for it included the flautist and writer Johann Joachim Quantz, who left an account of the performance, and a generation of German composers who took the experience home; the occasion functioned as an unofficial convention of central European musical life. Listen for how Fux writes fugues into an opera, a decision contemporaries found either magnificent or perverse depending on nationality.","German observers regarded it as the summit of the serious style; Italian visitors thought it stiff and church-like, and that split — learned counterpoint against vocal fluency — defines Fux's reputation to this day. The work was effectively unrepeatable, since nothing outside a coronation could fund it, and it has been staged only rarely in modern times. It survives more as a documented event than as living repertory.","Do not attempt the whole thing first. Find a recording of the overture and one or two of the choruses, and listen for the moment the trumpets and drums open out — this is music designed for the open air and a crowd. Set it beside Handel's coronation anthems for the same job done more directly, and beside Pergolesi's comic intermezzo for the world that was about to replace it."],"fux-missa-canonica":["A setting of the mass ordinary for four voices in which almost every section is a canon: one voice sings a line and another follows it exactly, at a fixed distance in time and pitch, all the way through. Half an hour of music written under that constraint, in the deliberately old-fashioned unaccompanied style of the sixteenth century rather than the noisy idiom of Fux's own Vienna. The surface is calm and even; the machinery underneath is relentless.","Fux was Hofkapellmeister to three Habsburg emperors and the chief guardian of the Vienna court chapel's musical standards, an institution that consciously preserved the old church style for solemn occasions alongside the modern orchestral masses. Writing a mass entirely in canon was a display of qualification as much as an act of devotion — the sort of thing a Kapellmeister produced to prove he had earned the position and could out-write anyone who wanted it.","The canons are not audible as canons unless you are listening for them; Fux hides the machinery so successfully that the piece sounds merely smooth. Follow the two upper voices from any entry and you will hear one shadowing the other a bar or two behind. The composer set out the whole method five years later in his teaching manual Gradus ad Parnassum, which used a dialogue between master and pupil to explain what this mass demonstrates.","Fux's fame rests almost entirely on that textbook, which Haydn worked through as a young man, Mozart's father used, and Beethoven copied out exercises from — an influence on music history out of all proportion to how often Fux is performed. Critics have long asked whether his own compositions are the proof of the method or merely its exercise. Recordings are few, and most choirs meet Fux only through the rules.","Listen once without trying to hear the canons, for the sound alone, and then again following the second soprano against the first; the piece changes completely between the two hearings. Ockeghem's canonic mass makes the ideal comparison, two hundred and fifty years earlier and even stricter. Prefer a small unaccompanied choir with a light organ underneath."],"zelenka-lamentationes-jeremiae":["Six lessons from the book of Lamentations, one voice at a time with a small instrumental group, distributed across the three days of Holy Week. Each lesson is a chain of sections separated by the Hebrew letters that head the verses in scripture, set as long wordless melismas on a single syllable. About seventy-five minutes complete. The tone is uniformly dark, the harmony full of unexpected swerves, the bassoon and oboes used as expressive soloists rather than filler.","Zelenka was a Bohemian double-bass player who rose to become the working church composer of the Catholic court chapel in Dresden — a Catholic island in Lutheran Saxony, established after the Elector converted to win the Polish crown. That chapel needed a full Roman liturgical repertory in a hurry, and Zelenka supplied it. The Lamentations, written in 1722, belong to the Tenebrae services of Holy Week, sung in a church where candles were extinguished one by one.","The Hebrew letters — Aleph, Beth, Ghimel — are what to listen for. Every other composer sets them as brief punctuation; Zelenka turns them into extended vocal arias on a single syllable, so the most elaborate music in the piece is attached to a letter of the alphabet rather than to any word with meaning. His harmony is the other oddity: chords arrive from directions the period's rules do not lead to, and eighteenth-century copyists occasionally corrected them, assuming errors.","Zelenka was passed over for the Dresden Kapellmeister post in favour of Johann Adolph Hasse, and after his death his manuscripts were locked away by the Saxon court, which effectively removed him from history for two centuries. Bach admired him and requested copies of his music. Serious revival began only in the later twentieth century, and he is now routinely described as the most harmonically adventurous composer of his generation — a claim that is easier to hear in this work than to prove.","Start with a single lesson rather than the whole cycle, and listen through the opening melisma on the Hebrew letter before the words arrive; that is Zelenka at his strangest. Follow the bassoon line, which is unusually independent. Tallis's Lamentations make an instructive comparison — the same text, one utterly calm, the other restless."],"zelenka-trio-sonatas":["Six sonatas of four movements each, running fifteen to twenty minutes apiece — enormous for chamber music of the 1720s. Two oboes lead, but the bassoon is a third soloist rather than a bass instrument, with lines as fast and as high as the oboes'. The counterpoint is genuinely three-voiced and unusually dense, the slow movements wander harmonically, and the fast movements demand breath control and finger speed at a level that keeps the set out of most players' hands.","Probably written around 1721, either during or shortly after Zelenka's period of study in Vienna with Fux, and intended for the Dresden court orchestra, which by then was arguably the finest in Europe and employed wind players of exceptional skill. The Elector's establishment could afford specialists; a composer writing for it could assume that anything technically conceivable would be executed. The sonatas were never published in his lifetime and stayed in the Dresden archive.","Listen to what the bassoon is asked to do. In the fast movements it plays running passages in the same register and at the same speed as the oboes, on an instrument with far clumsier fingering, and in the slow movements it carries melody rather than harmony. This is chamber music written to embarrass a rival court. Note also the harmony in the slow movements, which sets off in directions that make the eventual cadence sound like a lucky escape.","Unknown until the twentieth century, the sonatas have become the works that made Zelenka's reputation among performers, precisely because the technical demands are a talking point. Some critics find them overlong and argue that Zelenka's contrapuntal ambition outruns his sense of proportion; others regard them as the finest wind chamber music before Mozart. Both positions concede the strangeness of the harmony.","Choose the second or the sixth sonata, and follow the bassoon exclusively for one fast movement — it turns the piece from pretty into astonishing. Then compare with Bach's fifth Brandenburg for the same trick of promoting a background instrument to soloist. Period oboes and a Baroque bassoon make the difficulty audible, which is part of the pleasure."],"zelenka-hipocondrie":["A single movement of about seven minutes in the shape of a French overture: a slow, heavily dotted opening, a fast fugal middle, a return to the opening manner. Seven concertante parts — two oboes, bassoon and strings — trade material constantly, so the texture never settles into soloist-and-accompaniment. The harmony keeps sliding sideways into unrelated chords and then correcting itself, which is presumably the joke the title makes.","Written in 1723, the busiest year of Zelenka's life, when he was also preparing a large Latin melodrama for the Prague festivities surrounding the coronation of Charles VI as King of Bohemia — the same occasion that produced Fux's coronation opera. Hypochondria was a recognised medical diagnosis in the period, a disorder of the spleen thought to produce melancholy and restlessness, and naming an instrumental piece after an illness was a fashionable conceit.","The title is a structural instruction. The music is deliberately unsettled: phrases end where they should not, the harmony reaches for a resolution and takes a different one, and the fugal section keeps interrupting itself. Whether Zelenka meant it as self-portraiture is unknowable, though he was a solitary figure who never married and complained of being overlooked. The French overture form makes the joke sharper, since that form is normally the most confident and ceremonial in the repertory.","The piece is now a favourite of period orchestras looking for a short, strange opener, and its title guarantees it a paragraph in any programme note. Scholarship has been more interested in what it reveals about Zelenka's relationship to the French style he encountered at Dresden, where an entire French-trained contingent worked alongside the Italians. Its brevity has kept it from the serious analysis his sacred works receive.","Seven minutes, so play it twice: once for the sound, once listening only for the moments where the harmony goes somewhere unexpected. Follow it with any orthodox French overture — Lully or Muffat — to hear how far off-centre Zelenka has pushed the form. A small period band with characterful oboes gives the best account."],"lully-atys":["A prologue and five acts, running close to three hours. The goddess Cybèle loves the mortal Atys, who loves Sangaride; the goddess sends a madness that makes him kill the woman he loves, and he is changed into a pine tree. The sound is a five-part string band with thick inner voices, sung declamation that follows the swing of French verse, and long dance divertissements. The Act III sleep scene, with recorders and muted strings, is the passage everyone remembers.","First given at Saint-Germain-en-Laye on 10 January 1676, during the court's carnival season. Lully had held a royal monopoly on sung drama in France since 1672 and could shut down any rival. Philippe Quinault supplied the libretto, as he did for eleven of these works. Louis XIV took a personal interest, reportedly choosing airs himself, and the piece became known at court as the King's opera. Paris saw it later that year.","Listen closer to the sleep scene. Lully more or less invented the operatic sommeil here: three recorders over hushed strings, then the Songes funestes, the bad dreams, breaking in with jagged rhythms. Notice too how the recitative changes metre signature almost bar by bar, bending the beat to the accents of the spoken alexandrine. Singers of the period were coached from the Comédie-Française stage.","Revived at court and in Paris until 1753, then unheard for over two hundred years. William Christie's 1987 production with Les Arts Florissants, staged by Jean-Marie Villégier, turned it into the emblem of the French Baroque revival and was brought back in 2011. Performers still argue about the low French pitch, about how far to over-dot the overture, and about how much rhythmic inequality the dances want.","Begin with the Act III sommeil, then jump to Atys's final scene. If the recitative feels flat at first, follow a text and let it work as heightened speech rather than melody. Compare it with Purcell's Dido, written a decade later on a smaller scale. Christie's recording is the natural first choice."],"lully-armide":["A prologue and five acts drawn from Tasso's crusader epic. The sorceress Armide captures the knight Renaud, and in the scene that made the work famous stands over him asleep with a raised dagger, unable to strike, talking herself out of murder and into love. The music slips between recitative and measured air within a single sentence. Act V ends with a long passacaille over a repeating bass, then Armide destroys her enchanted palace and is left alone on stage.","Given at the Académie Royale de Musique in Paris on 15 February 1686, without a court première. It was the last of Lully's collaborations with Quinault, who retired from the theatre afterwards. Louis XIV had chosen the subject himself but, by then in his devout later years, apparently never saw the staging. Lully died the following year of an infected foot, struck with the long staff he used to beat time.","Listen closer to the monologue \"Enfin il est en ma puissance\". Two generations argued over these forty bars. Rameau published a bar-by-bar analysis to prove the harmony obeyed natural law; Rousseau attacked the same passage in his Lettre sur la musique françoise of 1753 as arbitrary noise dressed as declamation. The dagger falls at the moment the bass line stops moving.","It became the most admired French opera of the century, revived at intervals into the 1760s. Gluck set the identical Quinault text in 1777, inviting direct comparison and mostly winning it for a while. Modern performance turns on how freely to ornament, how much inequality to give the dances, and whether the passacaille is a dance or a meditation.","Go straight to the monologue, twice: once for the words, once for the way the bass moves under them. Then the Act V passacaille, which builds without ever raising its voice. Compare Gluck's setting of the same lines. Look for a period band at low pitch with a large continuo group."],"chambonnieres-pieces-de-clavessin-1670":["Two engraved books of about sixty short pieces, gathered into suites by key: an allemande, two or three courantes, a sarabande, sometimes a gigue, plus chaconnes, pavanes and character pieces with nicknames. Nothing lasts more than four minutes. The texture is the point — chords are never struck whole but spread and staggered, so the sound is always arriving rather than landing, and the melody surfaces out of the middle of the hand.","Chambonnières played the spinet for Louis XIII and then held the harpsichordist's post in Louis XIV's chamber. In 1641 he founded a subscription concert series in Paris, one of the earliest anywhere. He lost his court place around 1662; the story told later was that he could not, or would not, realise a figured bass in Lully's band, and he sold the post to d'Anglebert. The two books appeared two years before his death, in reduced circumstances.","Listen closer for what the printed page had to add. The books carry a table of ornament signs, one of the first serious attempts to fix the French agréments in notation rather than leave them to a teacher's hands. The broken-chord manner itself is borrowed wholesale from the Paris lutenists, so a plucked instrument with seven courses is being imitated by one with two keyboards.","For a long time he was filed as an ancestor rather than a composer. The manuscript sources, above all the Bauyn collection, hold far more of his music than the print, and editors disagree about which readings he authorised. Performers argue over how much to unsettle the beat: the courantes hover between two pulses, and a metrically strict reading kills them.","Take one suite whole rather than sampling. Listen for the courantes refusing to settle into a count of three or a count of six. Then set a Louis Couperin pavane beside it to hear what the next generation did with the same materials. A French double-manual instrument in unequal tuning suits this better than a bright Italian one."],"louis-couperin-pavane-f-sharp-minor":["A slow processional dance in a chain of repeated strains, each darker than the last, running about seven minutes. The writing is dense, often in five real voices, with lines that slide downward by half-steps and rub against each other before resolving. There is no display, no fast passage, nothing to admire in the fingers. The whole effect comes from suspensions held a beat longer than comfortable and then let go.","Louis Couperin came from Chaumes-en-Brie, a village east of Paris. Around 1650 his family played for Chambonnières at a local feast; the court harpsichordist took the young man back to Paris with him. Couperin became organist of Saint-Gervais in 1653, founding a family tenure at that loft that lasted more than a century and a half. He died at about thirty-five, in 1661, with nothing published.","Listen closer to the tuning. In the meantone temperaments of the 1650s, F-sharp minor was close to unplayable: the note the music needs as D-sharp was tuned as E-flat, producing a snarling interval every time it appears. Part of what sounds like grief here is probably a tuning wound, and the piece has been used as evidence that Couperin's instrument was set in some modified temperament.","None of his music was printed in his lifetime, and it survives chiefly in the Bauyn manuscript copied decades after his death. Modern editions therefore differ over notes and ornaments, and some sources give the piece bare of the signs a French player expected. Recordings split between those that keep it strictly in tempo and those that treat every strain as free recitation.","Follow the bass. It walks downward, again and again, like a lament figure that never quite becomes a ground bass. Ravel's Pavane pour une infante défunte makes an instructive comparison two and a half centuries later, and the same slow-dance-as-elegy idea sits behind Dowland's Lachrimae. Choose a recording on a harpsichord in unequal tuning; equal temperament flattens the whole argument."],"louis-couperin-preludes-non-mesures":["Around sixteen preludes, each two to six minutes, written down entirely in whole notes with no bar lines, no time signature and no indication of duration. Long curved lines drawn across the page group notes into harmonies and show which are held and which pass through. The player invents the rhythm. Some of the larger preludes open out, in the middle, into a strictly notated fugal section before dissolving back into free time.","Improvised preluding was how a harpsichordist warmed up, tested the tuning and set the key of a suite. What was new was writing it down at all. Froberger passed through Paris in 1652 and Couperin knew his work well enough to head one prelude an imitation of Monsieur Froberger. The notation appears to have been a Paris speciality; it survived for two generations and then died out entirely.","Listen closer and you are hearing a performer's decision, not a composer's. The slurs are the whole grammar, and modern editors disagree about where several of them start and stop, which changes which notes ring on and which are dropped. Two recordings of the same prelude can differ in length by half. The player is finishing the piece in front of you.","Unknown outside specialist circles until the twentieth century, when Davitt Moroney and others produced usable editions and made the case that this is Couperin's most original work. The argument now is whether the notation records a fixed shape that has merely lost its rhythms, or is a set of instructions for improvising afresh each time. Recorded readings range from stately to almost formless.","Do not look for a tune or a beat; listen as though to someone thinking aloud at the keyboard, testing a chord and then abandoning it. Satie's barless Gnossiennes give a distant modern parallel. Set one of these next to the Well-Tempered Clavier's first prelude to hear how differently two masters treated the same act of preluding."],"louis-couperin-tombeau-de-blancrocher":["A memorial piece of about six minutes, moving at the pace of a slow allemande. The chords are never struck together: notes enter one after another from the bottom, so each harmony blooms and fades rather than sounding. There is no repeat scheme, no dance form to fill; the music simply proceeds, pausing on unresolved harmonies, and ends without a cadence of the emphatic kind.","Charles Fleury, sieur de Blancrocher, was a Paris lutenist and a well-liked figure among musicians. He died in November 1652 after falling down a staircase, reportedly in Froberger's company. Four composers wrote tombeaux for him — Froberger, Denis Gaultier, Dufaut and Couperin — which is one reason we know how central he was to that circle. The tombeau itself was a lutenists' genre, borrowed here for the harpsichord.","Listen closer to what Couperin does not do. Froberger's tombeau for the same man ends with a scale tumbling the length of the keyboard, a plain picture of the fall. Couperin refuses the picture. He keeps the lute's broken chords, the dead man's own idiom, and lets the bass sink by degrees instead. The homage is in the manner of playing rather than in any event.","Like all his music it comes down through manuscript copies, and its attribution has never been seriously doubted, though the date rests on Blancrocher's death rather than on any source. Performers argue about pace: taken strictly it is a stiff allemande, taken freely it becomes an unmeasured prelude with a pulse. Most modern players lean toward freedom.","Listen once for the spread chords alone, ignoring the melody, and you will hear the lute inside the harpsichord. Then play Froberger's tombeau for the same lutenist beside it — the contrast between depicting a death and refusing to is the whole lesson. Dowland's Flow My Tears is the English cousin of this mood."],"sainte-colombe-le-tombeau-les-regrets":["One of sixty-seven concerts for two equal bass viols, laid out as a chain of short movements with French titles — among them Les Pleurs and Les Regrets. Nothing accompanies anything: the two instruments are the same size and tuning, they cross constantly, and the ear cannot reliably tell which is above. The sound is low, dry, closely spaced, with bow strokes shaped like breath and long silences between phrases.","Sainte-Colombe was a Paris viol player and teacher who kept clear of the court, taught Marin Marais and, according to eighteenth-century writers, added a seventh low string to the bass viol and had the lowest strings overspun with silver wire for a fuller tone. Almost nothing else is documented — not his first name with certainty, not his dates. The concerts were never printed and survive in a single manuscript.","Listen closer to the balance. Because the instruments are identical, the writing can hand a phrase from one to the other in the middle of a bar without any change of colour, so a line seems to move without anyone moving it. This is chamber music for two players and no listener; the parts are written for people sitting knee to knee, and the effect thins out in a concert hall.","The music was essentially unknown until Jordi Savall's recordings and Alain Corneau's 1991 film Tous les matins du monde, which used it throughout and briefly made the bass viol fashionable. The film's story of a severe master and an ambitious pupil is invention built on a single sentence in Titon du Tillet. Scholars have since argued over the composer's identity and even his dates.","Listen late and quietly, with nothing else running. Follow one instrument for a whole movement, then start again and follow the other. Marais's Le Labyrinthe shows what the pupil did with the same instrument. Savall's recordings, with Wieland Kuijken as the second viol, remain the point of reference."],"anglebert-pieces-de-clavecin-1689":["A single engraved volume of about seventy-five minutes' music: four large suites in G major, G minor, D minor and D major, a set of variations on the folia bass, arrangements of Lully overtures and dance numbers, five fugues on one subject with a four-part quatuor, and a written treatise on accompaniment. The keyboard writing is the thickest of the century — five voices are common, and the ornament signs sometimes outnumber the notes on a stave.","D'Anglebert bought Chambonnières's court post around 1662 and served as harpsichordist of the King's chamber, also working for the Duke of Orléans. He published nothing until this book, engraved in Paris two years before his death and dedicated to the Princesse de Conti, one of his pupils. His son inherited the post. Publishing was expensive and mostly a way of advertising a teacher; this volume reads as a summation rather than a sales pitch.","Listen closer to the ornaments — then look at the table that explains them. D'Anglebert lists twenty-nine signs, the fullest such table of the period, and Bach copied it almost entire into the little keyboard book he made for his eldest son in 1720. The transcriptions of Lully matter too: they show how a French orchestra's sound was reduced to two hands, which is how most of the country heard opera at all.","Reputed in his lifetime as the hardest French harpsichord music to play, and it has stayed that way. Editors disagree about how literally to take the ornament table when a sign appears on an already-full chord. The Lully arrangements were long dismissed as commercial filler and are now valued as evidence of French orchestral practice, which no score of the period writes out.","Start with the G minor suite, then hear one Lully arrangement against a recording of the opera. Expect density rather than brilliance: the pleasure is in how much detail one pair of hands sustains. A large French double-manual instrument, closely recorded, does it more justice than a concert-hall acoustic."],"anglebert-folies-d-espagne":["Twenty-two variations, called couplets, over the sixteen-bar harmonic frame that Europe knew as the folia. The theme is stated plainly, then reappears in every conceivable disguise: as spread lute-like chords, as running scales split between the hands, as a gigue, as a dense chordal march. The bass pattern never alters and never modulates, so each couplet has to find its interest in texture and touch alone. Around twelve minutes.","The folia had circulated for a century as a stock pattern for dancing, improvising and showing off — Iberian in origin, common property by the 1680s. D'Anglebert's set appeared inside his 1689 volume. Corelli's far more famous violin variations on the same bass came eleven years later, in the Op. 5 sonatas of 1700, which suggests how widely the frame was already in the air.","Listen closer to how the couplets are ordered. They are grouped so that each takes up a different item from the composer's own ornament table — one couplet lives on the mordent, another on the port de voix, another on the tremblement — which turns the set into a demonstration lesson as much as a piece. The harmony carries no surprises at all; everything is decoration.","Usually treated as an appendix to the big suites, and it is the piece from the volume most likely to be extracted for recital. Some players cut couplets, arguing that twenty-two in a row defeat any audience; others insist the cumulative flattening is the point. Comparison with Corelli tends to be unkind, since the violin set is more dramatic and much better known.","Do not try to follow the theme; feel the sixteen-bar cycle turning over instead, and notice what changes each time round. Then hear Corelli's folia variations, and afterwards the Goldberg aria, for three different answers to the question of what a fixed bass can carry. A single sitting, all the way through, works better than sampling."],"lalande-de-profundis":["A grand motet on the psalm that begins \"Out of the depths I have cried\": half an hour built as a chain of movements that alternate solo récits, ensembles for a small group of picked voices, and the full five-part chorus doubled by the orchestra. The opening is low, slow and thickly scored. After the psalm ends, Lalande appends the Requiem aeternam, so the whole turns from supplication into a service for the dead.","Lalande won one of the four sous-maître posts of the Chapelle Royale in the competition of 1683, and by 1714 held all four quarters of the year himself. At Versailles the King heard a low Mass daily, accompanied throughout not by liturgical singing but by a grand motet — a concert in church, with the court watching the King as much as the altar. He wrote some seventy of them.","Listen closer for the orchestra's independence, which grew over decades. Lalande revised his motets all his life, and this one survives in several states; in the later readings the strings and winds have their own material rather than shadowing the voices, and the symphonie sections expand. Performing this piece therefore means choosing a decade, not just a text, and recordings that sound quite different are all authentic.","Lalande's motets dominated the Concert Spirituel from its founding in 1725, keeping his name alive for fifty years after his death and then vanishing with the Revolution. The modern revival came through Philippe Herreweghe and Hervé Niquet in the 1980s and 1990s. The main scholarly argument is over versions: some editors reconstruct the earliest state, others print the composer's last thoughts.","Wait for the first entry of the full chorus after a solo — that swell from one voice to a wall of sound is what the whole genre exists to deliver. Then compare Charpentier's sacred music, written in Paris for a different, less official audience. Niquet's Le Concert Spirituel or Herreweghe's Collegium Vocale are the obvious starting points."],"lalande-symphonies-pour-les-soupers-du-roi":["A large collection of orchestral dance music, grouped into numbered suites by key and normally played in selections of an hour or so. Fanfares, chaconnes and passacailles sit beside minuets, gavottes, sarabandes and bourrées, and among them are movements Lalande simply called caprices, which follow no dance pattern at all. The scoring is the French five-part string band, brightened by oboes and flutes, with drums where a fanfare wants them.","Louis XIV dined in public. At the grand couvert the court stood and watched him eat while the band played from an adjoining room — music as a component of ceremony rather than something anyone sat down for. Lalande directed this repertoire from the 1680s until his death, and the collection grew for roughly forty years, absorbing numbers lifted from his own ballets and court divertissements.","Listen closer to the middle of the texture and you can date the movements by ear. The older numbers use the five-part French layout with three inner viola lines filling the middle; the later ones thin out to the four-part Italian arrangement with a single viola. Nobody composed this as a work: the suites are a librarian's grouping, and no two manuscript sources order them the same way.","Dismissed for two centuries as background music, which is precisely what it was commissioned to be. Recordings by Jordi Savall and Hugo Reyne have argued that the best movements stand comparison with Handel's outdoor music, and that the caprices in particular are ambitious pieces smuggled into a dinner service. Which movements belong in which suite remains an editorial choice rather than a fact.","Do not listen straight through; pick a suite and treat it as a dance sequence. Listen for the caprices, which break the pattern and are where Lalande stops being a functionary. Handel's Water Music makes the natural comparison — royal music outdoors versus royal music indoors. Savall or Reyne for the sound of a real French band."],"marais-sonnerie-de-sainte-genevieve":["Nine minutes built on three notes. A short bell figure enters at the start and repeats, unaltered, well over two hundred times to the end, while a violin and a bass viol spin a continuous chain of variations above and around it — first plain, then in running scales, then in dialogue, then in long held lines that ignore the bells altogether. The piece has no sections and no cadence until the last bar.","Published in 1723 in a collection of instrumental pieces, near the end of Marais's career; he retired from the Opéra orchestra about two years later. The abbey church of Sainte-Geneviève stood on the hill where the Panthéon now is, and its bells were an ordinary daily sound of the Left Bank. Taking a street noise as the foundation of a composed piece was an unusual, faintly domestic gesture for a royal chamber musician.","Listen closer to what the bells prevent. The figure never moves to another key, so every harmonic surprise has to happen above a bass that will not cooperate; twice the upper parts imply a different key while the bells go on insisting, and the friction is the whole event. It is a chaconne with a ground borrowed from the street rather than from the dance floor.","Now by far his best-known piece, which would have surprised his contemporaries, for whom he was the viol player. Performers argue about tempo — bells do not accelerate, so any push in the upper parts has to be made against a fixed pulse — and about who should play the ostinato: continuo alone, or doubled by the viol, which changes the balance completely.","Count the bell figure for the first minute, then let go of it and follow the violin. What you are testing is how long a fixed pattern can hold attention. Boléro is the extreme modern answer to the same question; Debussy's Sunken Cathedral is the other kind of bell piece. Look for a period-instrument group with a light, dry continuo."],"marais-le-labyrinthe":["Nine minutes of music that refuses to stay in one key. It sets off confidently, turns a corner into an unrelated harmony, hesitates, doubles back, stops mid-phrase, tries again, and only near the end finds an exit into a bright C major and a steady dance. Marais printed a description of the journey alongside the notes, so the wandering is declared rather than merely felt. The viol plays chords as often as lines.","From the fourth book of Pièces de viole, published in Paris in 1717, when Marais was sixty-one. Within that book sits a group he called a suite in a foreign taste, a set of experiments in effects and manners outside the ordinary French dances. He had been a viol player at the Opéra under Lully and a pupil of Sainte-Colombe, and by this date was a senior figure writing for connoisseurs rather than for a court.","Listen closer to how the escapes are made. The turns out of one key into another are enharmonic sleights — a note is written as one thing and treated as another, so the ear is somewhere new before it notices leaving. Very little music before the nineteenth century deliberately withholds a home key for minutes at a time. The maze is a technical demonstration wearing a story.","Long read as a curiosity, and the printed description encouraged that. It is now taken more seriously as evidence that programmatic and harmonically adventurous writing existed in France well before Rameau's operas. Performers argue over the pauses: played strictly the piece merely modulates a lot, played with real hesitation it becomes theatre, and recordings differ by several minutes.","Read Marais's description first, then listen without it and see whether you would have guessed. Notice the moment the harmony finally settles and stays settled — the relief is engineered. Set it beside a Bach cello suite prelude to hear the difference between music that explores a key and music that loses one. Savall or Pandolfo."],"marais-tableau-de-l-operation-de-la-taille":["Three or four minutes of music with a running commentary printed between the staves, describing an operation to cut a stone from the bladder, performed without anaesthetic. The captions arrive in order: the sight of the instruments, the shiver at seeing them, the patient bound with silken cords, the incision, the introduction of the forceps, the drawing out of the stone, the loss of voice, the flow of blood, the untying, the carrying to bed. A gentle dance, the convalescence, follows.","From the fifth and last book of Pièces de viole, published in 1725, three years before Marais died at seventy-two. Lithotomy was a Paris speciality and a public fascination; itinerant operators performed thousands of them, and survivors such as Samuel Pepys kept the extracted stone as a trophy and marked the anniversary. Roughly half of those cut lived. Everyone reading the score would have known someone who had faced it.","Listen closer to where the words sit. The captions are engraved inside the music, so the piece cannot be played as pure sound: the performer is reading a text while bowing, and printed editions still carry it. The shivering is a written-out tremble of the bow rather than a mood marking, and the incision is a single scraped stroke — sound effects specified in notation, not left to the imagination.","Treated for two centuries as a joke or a curiosity, and it is genuinely funny in performance if played straight. Recent writing reads it as an unusually literal piece of programme music, with the programme fixed in the score rather than in a preface, and as documentation of what surgery sounded like from the table. Whether Marais underwent the operation himself is not known.","Read the captions in time with the music the first time; listen without them the second, to check whether the music carries the story alone. Then hear the convalescence that follows, which is the point of the pairing. Berlioz's March to the Scaffold makes a good century-later comparison. Savall or Pandolfo again, on a seven-string viol."],"marais-alcione-tempete":["The storm from the fourth act of the tragédie en musique Alcione, five minutes long and detachable. Rushing scale figures pass upward through the strings while a low rumble runs beneath the whole texture; the chorus of sailors and onlookers cries out over it; the wind rises in waves rather than in one crescendo. Ceyx drowns within sight of home, and the noise simply stops rather than resolving.","First performed at the Académie Royale de Musique in Paris on 18 February 1706, on a libretto by Antoine Houdar de La Motte after Ovid's story of Ceyx and Alcyone. Marais was fifty and had spent his life in the Opéra orchestra, so he knew exactly what that band could be made to do. Contemporary accounts say he reinforced the bass line with extra instruments and a large drum, and the effect was talked about for decades.","Listen closer to the construction. The storm is built from one rising figure restated in ever shorter note values, so the sense of acceleration comes from note density rather than from any change of tempo — the beat stays where it started. The drum part, if the accounts are right, is among the earliest specified in French opera, and it sits under the orchestra rather than in it.","Alcione was revived repeatedly until the 1770s, and eighteenth-century writers were frank that the tempest was the reason. It then disappeared with the rest of the repertoire. Marc Minkowski's 1990 recording and Jordi Savall's later performances brought it back; modern productions argue over how much percussion the accounts actually license, since no surviving score writes the drum out in full.","Listen for the silence at the end rather than the noise in the middle — the abruptness is the dramatic stroke. Then hear the storm in Vivaldi's Summer, written a few years later for four string players instead of a theatre orchestra. Minkowski or Savall; a big modern orchestra smooths away the rawness."],"campra-l-europe-galante":["A prologue and four entrées, about an hour and three-quarters. Each entrée is a short love intrigue set in a different country — France, Spain, Italy, Turkey — with its own dances and its own manner: a courtly forest fête, a jealous serenade beneath a balcony, a Venetian carnival with masks, a seraglio. There is no continuing plot and no tragedy. The dances carry more weight than the singing, and every entrée ends in a chaconne or a passacaille.","Given at the Académie Royale de Musique on 24 October 1697, with a text by Antoine Houdar de La Motte. Campra was at that moment maître de musique at Notre-Dame de Paris and could not admit to writing for the theatre; the score went out under his brother Joseph's name, and the joke went round Paris within weeks. Lully had been dead ten years, the tragédie was flagging, and audiences wanted something lighter and more Italian.","Listen closer to the Italian and Turkish entrées. The Italian one is written in genuine Italianate coloratura, so the piece is quietly arguing a position in the taste wars rather than just painting a picture. The Turkish music leans on flat repetition and percussion — the first sustained French essay in musical exoticism, and the direct ancestor of Rameau's Indes galantes forty years later.","The success was immediate and durable; it held the stage for decades and effectively created the opéra-ballet, a form in which dance and spectacle replace dramatic argument. Campra left Notre-Dame for the theatre in 1700. Some contemporaries complained that the national portraits were caricature, an objection modern productions have to negotiate rather than dissolve.","Skip the prologue and start with the Spanish entrée, then the Turkish. Listen for how quickly the national colour is established — a rhythm, a scoring, two bars. Then hear Rameau's Danse des Sauvages to see where the idea went. William Christie or Jean-Claude Malgoire; the dances need a band that can actually dance."],"campra-requiem":["About forty minutes in seven movements: Introit, Kyrie, Graduel, Offertoire, Sanctus, Agnus Dei and a Post-communion. There is no Dies irae — no day of wrath, no terror, no trumpet. The writing is warm and unhurried, with long choral lines over walking basses, and it leans heavily on the high male voice the French called the haute-contre, which floats above the tenors and gives the whole work a pale, upward colour.","Campra had come from Aix-en-Provence by way of Toulouse and Notre-Dame, spent two decades writing for the Opéra, and in 1723 became one of the sous-maîtres of the Chapelle Royale at Versailles. The Requiem probably belongs to those years; the occasion is not documented. French usage habitually left the Dies irae sequence out of the funeral Mass, a custom Fauré and Duruflé were still following two hundred years later.","Listen closer to the last movement. The Post-communion brings back the words of the opening Introit, so the work closes on the phrase it began with and the whole shape turns into a circle rather than a progression. Notice also how rarely the chorus sings loudly: the piece is scored for consolation, and the loudest moments are reserved for the Sanctus.","For most of the eighteenth century the French requiem people knew was Jean Gilles's, sung at Rameau's funeral among others; Campra's was recovered later. Its date has never been fixed, and the surviving sources leave room for argument about the scoring. Recordings by Herreweghe and Christie established it as the French Baroque requiem most listeners now meet first.","Start with the Offertoire, the longest and most varied movement, then go back to the Introit. Listen for the haute-contre line and how it changes the balance of the chorus. Fauré's Requiem is the obvious comparison — same omissions, same refusal of terror. Verdi's Dies irae is the argument on the other side."],"jacquet-de-la-guerre-cephale-et-procris":["A tragédie en musique in a prologue and five acts, following the Lullian pattern exactly: French overture, sung declamation shaped to the verse, choruses, and a divertissement of dances in every act. Céphale loves his wife Procris; the dawn goddess Aurore wants him and plants jealousy between them; he kills Procris by accident with a javelin that cannot miss. The orchestral writing is light, the dances plentiful, the recitative supple.","Staged at the Académie Royale de Musique in Paris on 15 March 1694 — the first opera by a woman to be produced there. The libretto was by Joseph-François Duché de Vancy. Jacquet de la Guerre had been a court prodigy since about the age of five, protected by Madame de Montespan, and was around twenty-nine when it was mounted. The season was crowded and the theatre in financial trouble.","Listen closer and notice that the prologue is about Louis XIV, not about the story: praise of the King was a required frame, and the mythology only begins in Act I. The score was engraved by Ballard the same year and survives complete, which is the only reason the opera can be staged at all; most Paris failures of the 1690s left nothing behind but a printed libretto.","It ran a handful of performances and was never revived in her lifetime. Contemporaries, including the critic Le Cerf de la Viéville, blamed the libretto rather than the composer, and later writers such as Titon du Tillet praised her warmly while treating the opera as an anomaly. Modern scholarship reads the failure as a matter of weak text and bad timing; performances and recordings date from the 1990s onward.","Start with the overture and the first divertissement, where the dances show what she could do quickly. Then hear a scene of Lully's Atys beside it and listen for how closely she works to his model and where she loosens it. A period band at low pitch; the piece dies in a modern opera-house acoustic."],"jacquet-de-la-guerre-pieces-de-clavecin-1707":["Her second harpsichord collection: suites that open with a free prelude and run through allemande, courantes, sarabande, gigue, a chaconne and a few character pieces, about forty-five minutes in all. The title page offers the violin as an option, and the printed violin line mostly doubles and decorates the harpsichord's top voice rather than accompanying it. The keyboard writing is full but never as crowded as d'Anglebert's, and the chaconnes are the largest movements.","Published in Paris in 1707, twenty years after her first harpsichord book. She was forty-two, widowed since 1704, and had lost her only son; she supported herself by teaching and by giving concerts in her own rooms, which was unusual and which several visitors recorded. Printing music was expensive and rarely profitable, so a book like this functioned as a calling card for a private teaching practice.","Listen closer to the preludes. In her 1687 book they were written in the old barless manner, all whole notes and slurs, with the player inventing the rhythm; here they are notated in a halfway state, part free and part measured. The tradition was dying, and this collection catches it mid-collapse. The violin option is a commercial instinct too — two markets, one plate.","Titon du Tillet, writing a generation later, ranked her among the finest keyboard players France had produced, and then she disappeared from view for two hundred and fifty years. The revival began in the 1970s. Editors argue about whether the violin part is genuinely optional or a later addition to the plates, and about how much of the older unmeasured freedom the preludes still want.","Begin with a prelude and then the chaconne from the same suite, so you hear her at her freest and her most structured in succession. Compare François Couperin's ordres from a few years later, which take the character piece much further. A French double-manual harpsichord; the violin version is worth hearing once for the contrast."],"jacquet-de-la-guerre-le-sommeil-d-ulisse":["A French cantata of about a quarter of an hour, alternating recitatives that carry the story with airs that stop it. Ulysses falls asleep within sight of Ithaca; his crew, suspecting treasure, open the bag of winds Aeolus gave him, and the ship is blown back out to sea. There is a sleep air over a murmuring flute, a short storm, and a closing air that draws the moral. One voice does all of it.","From her book of secular cantatas published in Paris in 1715, the last year of Louis XIV's reign. The cantate françoise was a recent import: Italian in origin, cut down for the salon, a mythological episode compressed into twenty minutes for one singer and one or two instruments. It suited exactly the private concert circuit she had built her living around, and it needed no theatre, no chorus and no licence.","Listen closer to the sleep air and you are hearing an opera scene shrunk to fit a drawing room. The recipe is Lully's sommeil from Atys — flutes, murmuring accompaniment, a voice half-dissolving — reduced to three players. Notice the ending too: the moral tacked on at the close was a formal requirement of the genre, not an afterthought, and every cantata of the period has one.","The cantatas were admired in her lifetime and named by contemporaries as the equal of her keyboard music, then vanished with the rest of her output. Recordings and editions have appeared steadily since the 1990s. The main performance question is the continuo: harpsichord alone keeps it intimate, adding a bass viol makes it sound more like an opera excerpt than a chamber piece.","Listen for the flute at the moment sleep arrives, then for how quickly the winds break in — the whole point of the form is compression. Compare Lully's sleep scene in Atys for the theatrical original, and Monteverdi's Lamento della ninfa for a much earlier one-voice drama. A small period group in a dry acoustic."],"rameau-hippolyte-et-aricie":["A prologue and five acts after Racine's Phèdre, running close to three hours. Phèdre desires her stepson; Thésée goes down to the underworld and returns; a sea monster destroys Hippolyte, who is then restored by Diane. The orchestra is large by French standards and constantly busy, the choruses of Fates and infernal spirits are massed and loud, and the harmony lurches sideways into distant keys at moments of shock rather than easing between them.","Given at the Académie Royale de Musique on 1 October 1733. Rameau was fifty and had never written for the stage; Paris knew him as the author of the Traité de l'harmonie of 1722 and of two books of harpsichord pieces. The Abbé Simon-Joseph Pellegrin wrote the libretto. The reaction split the city into lullistes and ramistes, the first of the century's two great French musical quarrels, and Campra reportedly said the score held enough music for ten operas.","Listen closer to the second Trio des Parques, \"Quelle soudaine horreur\". Rameau moves the three Fates through a series of enharmonic shifts — notes respelled and reinterpreted — that leave singers with no reliable point of reference. They could not hold the pitch in rehearsal and the passage was cut before the first night, printed in the score with a note explaining why. It is now routinely restored.","Admired and attacked in equal measure at once: the older party called it noisy and overloaded with harmony, the younger heard a new language. Rameau revised it for revivals in 1742 and 1757, cutting and rewriting, and modern editions must choose between states. After the 1760s it went unheard for a century and a half; Christie and Minkowski returned it to the stage in the 1980s and 1990s.","Start with the second Trio des Parques — it is short and it is the moment the argument was about. Then take Act II whole, in the underworld, for the choral writing. Set the overture beside Lully's Atys to hear fifty years of change. Christie or Minkowski; the choruses need a real period band."],"rameau-platee":["A comic ballet in a prologue and three acts. Jupiter pretends to court Platée, an ugly marsh nymph who believes herself irresistible, so that Juno will catch him and be cured of jealousy for good. Platée is sung by a man — a high tenor in a dress. The orchestra croaks and cackles; there are choruses of frogs and cuckoos on nonsense syllables, a mad scene for the character La Folie, and a wedding that collapses when the veil comes off.","First staged at Versailles on 31 March 1745, in the temporary theatre of the Grande Écurie, as part of the festivities for the marriage of the Dauphin to the Spanish Infanta María Teresa Rafaela — a comedy about a bride nobody wants, performed at a royal wedding. Rameau was sixty-one and by then the dominant figure at the Opéra. The Paris production of 1749 was where it became a genuine success.","Listen closer to La Folie. She arrives claiming to have stolen Apollo's lyre, and Rameau has the orchestra pluck in imitation while she sings ariettes in an unmistakably Italian manner — deliberate stylistic quotation, years before the Querelle des Bouffons made French-versus-Italian a public fight. When that fight came, both parties claimed this piece. The frog syllables mimic the cadences of spoken French.","How the Versailles audience reacted is disputed and the evidence is thin. From 1749 it held the stage. Later critics found the humour cruel, and the twentieth century largely avoided it; Minkowski and Laurent Pelly's 1999 production reset it as satire on the crowd that laughs rather than on the woman laughed at, and that reading now dominates.","Go to La Folie's ariettes first, then the frog chorus, then the final scene where the joke turns. Notice how the orchestral writing is funnier than anything the singers do. Compare Rameau's own Hippolyte for the tragic voice of the same composer. Minkowski's recording; the piece needs a company willing to play it straight-faced."],"rameau-pieces-de-clavecin-en-concerts":["Five suites, called concerts, of three or four short movements each, about an hour altogether. Most movements are portraits with names — La Livri, La Timide, La Cupis, La Forqueray, La Marais, L'Indiscrète — and end with a pair of Tambourins. The harpsichord part is written out in full and frequently leads; the violin or flute and the viol are partners with their own material, sometimes accompanying the keyboard rather than the other way round.","Published in Paris in 1741, between two of Rameau's operatic seasons. The Italian trio sonata had taken over Parisian chamber playing, with the keyboard reduced to filling in a figured bass. Rameau's answer keeps the harpsichord in charge and treats the strings as its equals. Mondonville had tried a version of the idea seven years earlier. Rameau added a preface on balancing the instruments and arranged five movements for harpsichord alone.","Listen closer to the titles, which name real people. La Forqueray and La Marais salute the two great viol players of the age, and La Rameau is a self-portrait — a composer putting himself into his own suite alongside his rivals. La Cupis probably names the dancing and violin-playing family of that name. The dedications are encoded in music rather than printed on a dedication page.","Widely reprinted in the eighteenth century and then neglected until the early-music revival. The main practical argument is instrumentation: flute or violin, viol or second violin, and whether the harpsichord-alone arrangements are concessions to the market or genuine alternatives. Scholars now credit Mondonville with priority for the format while giving Rameau the better music.","Take one concert whole. Listen specifically to the harpsichord's right hand and treat the strings as commentary — the reversal is the point. Then hear Brandenburg No. 5, where the harpsichord steps out of the accompaniment in a different way and in the same decade. Period instruments; a modern piano loses the balance entirely."],"leclair-violin-concerto-op-7-no-3":["Three movements, fast, slow, fast, about eighteen minutes. The outer movements use the Italian ritornello plan — a full-band refrain returning between solo episodes — but the refrains are dotted and squared off in the French manner, and the solo writing is stiff with double stops and long trills. The slow movement is a single long-breathed melodic line over a walking bass. The finale dances in triple time and gets progressively harder for the soloist.","Published in Paris in 1737 as one of six concertos making up Op. 7. Leclair had been a dancer and ballet master in Turin before studying the violin there with Somis, a pupil of Corelli, so he arrived at the instrument by an unusual route. He served Louis XV as an ordinaire de la musique du roi from 1733 until 1737, resigning after a dispute over precedence with the violinist Guignon. The Concert Spirituel had made the solo concerto a Paris fashion.","Listen closer to the ornaments — they are all written out. Italian composers left decoration to the player; Leclair engraves his trills, appoggiaturas and slides into the text, applying a French habit to an Italian form. The result is a concerto in which the soloist's freedom is largely removed, and the phrase \"les goûts réunis\", the reunited tastes, becomes something you can point at on the page.","Contemporaries treated him as the head of the French violin school, a judgement later writers repeated without much examining the music. The concertos went unplayed for two centuries. Modern performance turns on the bow: a Baroque bow with gut strings gives the dotted rhythms their bite, while a modern setup smooths the writing into something more like early Mozart.","Start with the slow movement, which is where the French melodic instinct shows most plainly, then go back for the finale and listen to how much the soloist is asked to do. Vivaldi's L'estro armonico is the Italian model this is answering; Bach's A minor concerto is the German answer to the same problem. A period-instrument soloist."],"leclair-scylla-et-glaucus":["His only opera: a tragédie en musique in a prologue and five acts, close to three hours. The sea god Glaucus loves Scylla; the sorceress Circé loves Glaucus and, refused, poisons the pool where Scylla bathes and turns her into a monster. The score is unusually string-driven — busy, high, with the kind of figuration a violinist writes — and the big set pieces are Circé's incantation and the choral scenes around it.","Given at the Académie Royale de Musique on 4 October 1746, on a libretto by d'Albaret. Leclair was forty-nine, employed by the Duke of Gramont at Puteaux, and had written nothing for the stage in a career built entirely on sonatas and concertos. Rameau dominated the Opéra and had been doing so for thirteen years. The work ran about eighteen performances and was not revived in Leclair's lifetime.","Listen closer to the number on the title page. Leclair published the opera as his Op. 11, slotting a three-hour stage work into the numbered sequence of his violin books as though it were another collection of sonatas — a habit of mind that tells you how he thought of his own output. The orchestral writing gives the same thing away: the violins are asked for things opera composers did not ask.","Politely received and quickly gone, in a season dominated by Rameau. Rediscovered by John Eliot Gardiner, who staged it in Lyon in 1986 and recorded it soon after, and it is now generally regarded as the strongest French tragédie of its decade by anyone other than Rameau. His death is a separate mystery: he was stabbed in Paris in 1764 and the killer was never identified.","Go to Circé's incantation scene first — it is the most theatrical thing he wrote. Then listen to any chorus for the string writing underneath the voices. Compare Rameau's Hippolyte et Aricie, the model and the competition. Gardiner's recording remains the only real point of entry."],"leclair-sonata-op-5-no-6-le-tombeau":["Four movements, about fourteen minutes, for violin with harpsichord and bass. The first three follow the Italian sonata's expectations — a weighty opening, a fugal allegro, a dance — with Leclair's usual double stops and written-out ornaments. Then the finale, where a brilliant quick movement should be, is headed Tombeau: slow, in C minor, tread-like, built over a bass that keeps returning to the same descent. The sonata simply darkens and stops.","Op. 5 was published in Paris in 1734, twelve sonatas for violin and continuo, and became his most reprinted collection. He was then in royal service at Versailles. The tombeau was an old French genre belonging to lutenists and viol players — a memorial piece, usually slow, usually in a dance rhythm — and bringing one into an Italian sonata was a deliberate crossing of categories. Whom, if anyone, it commemorates is unknown.","Listen closer to the last movement's bass, which walks downward by half-steps in the manner every seventeenth-century listener recognised as a lament. Leclair borrows the figure from a French tradition a century old and sets it where Corelli's grammar demands a fast finale. Ending a sonata slowly was close to a formal error at the time, and he does it once, at the end of the book.","Op. 5 was reprinted repeatedly in France, the Netherlands and England, and the Tombeau was the movement anthologists picked. Modern performers argue over the continuo — harpsichord alone keeps it dry, adding a cello or viol thickens it toward orchestral weight — and over whether the earlier movements should be played with the same gravity or as a deliberate contrast.","Go straight to the Tombeau, then start the sonata from the beginning so you hear what it interrupts. Louis Couperin's Tombeau de M. de Blancrocher, eighty years earlier, is the French ancestor of the gesture. Gut strings and a Baroque bow; the descending bass needs to be audible under the violin rather than balanced with it."],"mondonville-pieces-de-clavecin-en-sonates-op-3":["Six sonatas of three movements each, roughly fifty-five minutes together, for harpsichord with violin. Both parts are written out in full: the harpsichord has a composed right hand rather than a figured bass to fill in, and it frequently carries the tune while the violin sustains, doubles or answers. The textures are bright and busy, the movements short, and the violin writing shows a player who knew the instrument's upper reaches well.","Published in Paris in 1734. Mondonville was a violinist from Narbonne who had made his name in the north and then at the Concert Spirituel, which he went on to direct from 1755 to 1762; he later became a sous-maître of the Chapelle Royale. Parisian chamber music at this date meant Italian trio sonatas with the keyboard reduced to accompaniment, and the title of this book — harpsichord pieces in the form of sonatas — announces a reversal.","Listen closer to the violin's upper register. Mondonville was an experimenter: four years later he published Les Sons harmoniques, the first collection to use violin harmonics systematically, and the same restless curiosity about what an instrument can be made to do is already audible here in the harpsichord's independence. The book's importance is a matter of engraving as much as invention — writing the keyboard out changed who could play it.","Overshadowed almost at once by Rameau's Pièces de clavecin en concerts of 1741, which does the same thing with better material. Scholarship now credits Mondonville with the priority and lets Rameau keep the music. He was a Concert Spirituel favourite for decades, and during the Querelle des Bouffons his pastorale Titon et l'Aurore of 1753 was made the banner of the French party against the Italians.","Follow the harpsichord and treat the violin as the second voice — the point of the collection is the reversal of the usual roles. Then hear Rameau's 1741 concerts to judge how much better the same idea can be executed. Bach's sonatas for violin and obbligato harpsichord are the German parallel. Period instruments, closely balanced."],"corrette-le-phenix":["A concerto with no treble instrument in it at all: four bass players and a continuo, about ten minutes in three movements. The four soloists take turns with material that would normally live an octave or two higher, so melodies arrive in a register the ear expects to be holding the floor. The effect is thick, warm and slightly comic, and the fast outer movements sound busier than they are because everything is happening low down.","Published in Paris around 1738, into a market Corrette understood better than almost anyone. He was an organist who spent sixty years writing instruction books — tutors for the flute, the violin, the cello, the harp, the hurdy-gurdy, nearly everything with strings or holes — for the growing crowd of amateur players in a city with money and leisure. A novelty piece for four bass instruments was aimed straight at them.","Listen closer and notice that the instrumentation is not fixed. The parts specify only basses, so bassoons, cellos, viols or any mixture can take them, and no two performances match. Corrette's magpie habits ran further: in 1765 he turned Vivaldi's Spring into a Latin motet, Laudate Dominum de coelis, by fitting sacred words to a concerto everybody already knew.","He has always been treated as a hack with good instincts, and the sheer volume of his output invites that. What has changed is the value placed on his tutors, which are now among the most quoted sources for how eighteenth-century instruments were actually held, fingered and tuned. The concert pieces are still played mainly for their oddity, which is a fair reading of their intent.","Hear a version on four bassoons and then one on four cellos: the piece changes character completely, which tells you something about how flexibly eighteenth-century ensembles were assembled. Vivaldi's Concerto for Two Cellos is the nearest relative in the catalogue. Ten minutes, no preparation needed; play it for someone who thinks Baroque music is solemn."],"cavalli-giasone":["Three acts and a prologue, well over three hours, for a dozen solo voices, a small band of strings and a continuo group of harpsichords, theorbos and cello. Jason has spent a year of anonymous nights with a masked lover, and the abandoned Hypsipyle arrives to reclaim him. The music runs mostly in flexible recitative that tips without warning into short, catchy arias over a walking bass, and drops into farce whenever the stammering servant Demo appears.","Venice had opened the first ticket-selling opera houses in 1637, and by 1649 Cavalli was their leading supplier. Giasone was written for the Teatro San Cassiano during carnival, to a libretto by the Florentine lawyer Giacinto Andrea Cicognini. Cavalli was also an organist at St Mark's, and his theatre scores are lean by court standards: strings and continuo only, no chorus, because impresarios were paying singers rather than orchestras.","Demo the servant stammers in the music itself, his line breaking into repeated broken syllables before each word arrives, one of the earliest musical jokes written into a vocal part rather than left to the actor. Listen too for Medea's incantation, where the vocal line abandons melody for a slow chanting monotone over shifting bass notes, the moment the opera turns from comedy to sorcery.","Giasone survives in more manuscript copies than any other seventeenth-century opera, which is the clearest measure of how far it travelled. Purists disapproved, complaining that Cicognini had ruined tragedy by threading farce through it. The work then vanished for nearly three centuries and returned only in the 1980s; producers still argue over how much to cut from a score that plays uncut for over four hours.","Go straight to Medea's incantation at the end of the first act and hear how the singing stops sounding like speech and starts sounding like a spell. Then track Hypsipyle, who gets the serious music while everyone else jokes. Choose a period-instrument recording with a large continuo team; the harpsichords, lutes and guitars supply most of the colour here."],"cavalli-la-calisto":["A prologue and three acts, about two and a half hours, scored for solo voices, strings and continuo. Jupiter wants the nymph Calisto, who has sworn chastity to Diana, so he disguises himself as Diana, and the singer shifts into a high register for the impersonation. Around this run Diana's own forbidden love for the shepherd Endymion, Juno's jealousy, and the satyrs' crude comedy. Calisto ends as a bear, and then as a constellation.","Written for the Teatro Sant'Aponal in the carnival of 1651, to a libretto by Giovanni Faustini, Cavalli's regular collaborator, who died young during the season. The plot comes from Ovid's Metamorphoses, filtered through the Venetian appetite for gods behaving like Venetians. Public opera in the city was a commercial business by then, with boxes rented by the season and audiences who ate, talked and gambled through the recitative.","The transformations are written into the vocal writing rather than the scenery. When Jupiter poses as Diana he sings up in falsetto, and the join between his own voice and the borrowed one is audible every time he forgets himself. At the close, Calisto's music turns simple and slow as she is set among the stars, the only passage in the opera where nobody is deceiving anybody.","The 1651 season gave it a handful of performances and then it disappeared for three hundred years. Raymond Leppard's Glyndebourne production of 1970 made it famous again, but his edition rewrote the orchestration, reassigned voices and reordered numbers, and scholars objected loudly. Later conductors have returned to Cavalli's spare original, and the two approaches sound like different operas.","Listen first to the final scene, four or five minutes that turn a bawdy comedy into something quiet and strange. Then go back to the first act for the disguise, and notice how the audience is told the trick in sound before it is shown on stage. A period recording with light strings and a busy continuo suits Cavalli far better than a full modern orchestra."],"cesti-orontea":["Three acts, close to three hours, for solo voices with strings and continuo. Orontea, queen of Egypt, has sworn never to marry; a wounded painter called Alidoro is carried into her court and the vow collapses. The score is built from short, shapely arias, many over a repeating bass, separated by quick recitative and interrupted by the drunken servant Gelone. Its best-known number is the queen's lullaby to her sleeping beloved, Intorno all'idol mio.","Cesti was a Franciscan friar and a celebrated tenor, a combination his order tolerated with difficulty and from which he was eventually released. Orontea was written for the court of Archduke Ferdinand Karl at Innsbruck, where the abdicated Queen Christina of Sweden had lately arrived on her way to Rome. The libretto, by Cicognini, had already been set in Venice, and Cesti's version rapidly displaced it.","Intorno all'idol mio is a lullaby sung over a sleeping man, and the accompaniment does the sleeping: the strings breathe in slow paired chords while the voice moves above them in long, unhurried phrases. The word for breezes is set to a rising figure repeated at three different pitches, so the air seems to circle the bed rather than blow past it.","Documented productions in at least seventeen Italian cities over the following thirty years make Orontea one of the most travelled operas of its century, and the surviving scores differ so much that no two revivals agree on what the piece is. Nineteenth-century historians ignored it entirely. Modern editors have had to choose between versions rather than reconstruct a single original.","Start with Intorno all'idol mio, which works perfectly well alone and is often sung in recital. Then hear the whole thing and discover that the opera around it is a comedy, which usually surprises people. Compare Cesti's smooth melodic manner with Cavalli's rougher, faster-moving style; Cesti is the easier writer, and that is why the next generation copied him."],"cesti-il-pomo-doro":["A festa teatrale in a prologue and five acts, planned to run across two evenings, with sixty-six scenes and around two dozen complete stage sets. The Judgment of Paris frames a procession of gods, battles, storms and an underworld. The orchestra is far larger than any Venetian theatre could afford: strings, cornetts, trombones, trumpets, recorders, bassoons and a rasping regal organ reserved for hell. Music for two of the five acts is lost.","Vienna staged it in 1668 to celebrate the marriage of Emperor Leopold I to the Spanish Infanta Margarita Teresa, in a wooden theatre built on the Cortina specifically for the show and taken down afterwards. Ludovico Ottavio Burnacini designed the machinery and the scenery, which were engraved and published so that the rest of Europe could see what Habsburg money bought.","The surviving engravings are the real record of the piece: a stage deeper than the auditorium, clouds that carried singers aloft, a collapsing tower, a moving sea. Cesti wrote much of his music as short interludes fitted between these effects, so the score reads oddly on the page, all beginnings and endings. Two acts are missing entirely, and modern performances patch them with music from elsewhere.","The production is usually cited as the most expensive theatrical event of the seventeenth century, with figures quoted that nobody can now verify. Historians have long treated it as the point where opera stopped being drama and became display, a charge that says as much about later taste as about 1668. The music, when performed, turns out to be leaner and better than that reputation suggests.","This one rewards reading and listening together: find the Burnacini engravings, then play the surviving music alongside them. Listen for the changes of instrument, which do the work that stage lighting does now, brass for the gods, recorders for the pastoral scenes, the regal's buzz for the underworld. Complete recordings are rare; excerpt discs are the practical way in."],"legrenzi-la-cetra-op-10":["Eighteen sonatas published in Venice in 1673, scored for two, three or four string instruments over a continuo of organ or harpsichord with cello. Each runs six to nine minutes and falls into short contrasted sections rather than separate movements: a grave opening, a fugal fast passage, a spell of dancing triple time, a return to gravity. Some are written for four violas, a dark scoring almost nobody else attempted.","Legrenzi came from Clusone in the Bergamo hills, worked in Ferrara, and settled in Venice, where he ran music at the Ospedale dei Mendicanti and finally became maestro di cappella at St Mark's in 1685. There he enlarged the basilica's permanent instrumental group to more than thirty players. Printed sonata collections like this one were how an Italian composer advertised himself to buyers across the Alps.","One of Legrenzi's fugue subjects reached Leipzig. Bach wrote an organ fugue on a theme taken from Legrenzi, and the shape of that subject, a long leap followed by a stepwise climb down, is exactly the kind of idea these sonatas are built from. Legrenzi taught Antonio Lotti, and his fingerprints are all over the next Venetian generation.","For two centuries Legrenzi was a name in histories rather than a sound in concerts, remembered chiefly because Bach and Handel had borrowed from him. Modern editions and period ensembles have reversed that. The sonatas are now heard as the crucial link between the sectional canzona of the early century and the four-movement church sonata Corelli standardised twenty years later.","Pick a single sonata and follow the bass line rather than the top part; Legrenzi's basses walk, leap and imitate the violins in a way later Baroque basses rarely do. Then listen for the joins, the sudden slowings between sections, which are the real architecture. Ensembles using organ continuo give these a churchier, weightier sound than harpsichord versions."],"stradella-san-giovanni-battista":["An oratorio in two parts, about eighty minutes, on the death of John the Baptist. Five soloists carry the whole drama, the Baptist, Herod, Herodias, her daughter and a counsellor, with a small chorus. Stradella splits his strings into a solo group and a larger body, so the accompaniment thickens and thins from bar to bar. There is no narrator: the story is acted in sound as an opera would be, but without staging.","Rome had banned public opera repeatedly, and the oratorio, sung in a church without costumes or scenery, was the legal substitute. This one was written for 1675, a Holy Year that filled the city with pilgrims, for the church of San Giovanni dei Fiorentini and its confraternity. Stradella was then living on commissions and on his wits, having already been caught trying to divert church money.","The final number puts Herod and the girl together and lets them sing at the same time in opposite moods: her triumph in bright, quick, dancing phrases, his horror in slow chromatic descent, the two lines colliding without resolving. It is one of the earliest places in music where two irreconcilable emotions are made to sound simultaneously rather than in turn.","Handel knew Stradella's music and lifted from it, and passages from his serenatas turn up rewritten in Israel in Egypt. That borrowing kept the name alive while the works went unheard. Stradella's murder in Genoa in 1682, after an affair with a nobleman's mistress, later produced a novel and an opera by Flotow, and for a long time the legend obscured the music entirely.","Begin at the end, with the closing duet, and play it twice, once following Herod, once following his stepdaughter. Then go back to the opening and notice how the string sound keeps changing size, three players, then twelve, then three again. Look for a recording with a small, sharply characterised solo team; this is chamber drama, not choral spectacle."],"stradella-sonata-di-viole":["About twelve minutes of short contrasted sections for two bodies of strings: a trio of two violins and cello set against a fuller group, each answering the other. The trio plays fast, decorated, exposed music; the larger band replies with slower, heavier blocks of sound. The effect is closer to two choirs trading phrases across a church than to a modern soloist standing in front of an orchestra.","Stradella wrote for Roman patrons who could hire large numbers of string players for a single feast day, and Roman churches were built for exactly this kind of spatial contrast. He had trained in Bologna and worked in Rome for the abdicated Queen Christina of Sweden among others, before scandal drove him north. Printing was not the point: pieces like this circulated in manuscript among the households that paid for them.","The score labels the small group concertino and the large one concerto grosso, and it does so years before Corelli's famous set of concerti grossi was written down. Whether Stradella invented the layout or simply wrote down what Roman musicians were already doing is unresolved, but the terms are here in his hand, which makes this the earliest firm evidence of the idea.","Nineteenth-century histories gave Corelli sole credit for the concerto grosso, and Stradella's instrumental music was barely known outside archives. The rediscovery of these sinfonias moved the date of the form back by a generation and complicated a tidy story. Scholars still disagree about dating individual pieces, since most survive in undated manuscript copies rather than prints.","Listen for the size of the sound rather than the tune: this is a conversation between three players and twelve, and every section change is a change of scale. Play it directly before a Corelli concerto grosso and the family resemblance is immediate. Recordings that place the two groups apart in the stereo image, or in a resonant church, make the design far clearer."],"steffani-niobe-regina-di-tebe":["Three acts, well over three hours, for a Munich court orchestra with recorders, oboes and a large continuo group. Niobe, queen of Thebes, boasts of her children and is punished; her husband Anfione abdicates to contemplate the heavens and dies. The centrepiece is Anfione's vision of the harmony of the spheres, where the strings turn slow rotating figures under a voice that barely moves. Niobe's final scene freezes into stone.","Munich under the Elector Max Emanuel wanted Italian opera on the Habsburg scale, and Steffani, then court director of chamber music, supplied it to a libretto by Luigi Orlandi after Ovid. He had studied in Rome and was already moving between music and diplomacy. Court opera of this kind came out of the treasury, played to invited guests, and mattered politically as evidence of the Elector's standing.","Steffani published some of his operas under the name Gregorio Piva, his secretary, because a man in holy orders and on diplomatic business could not be seen writing for the stage. Then there is the orchestration: recorders and hushed strings carry the sphere music, and trumpets are kept out of it entirely, so the loudest scene in the opera is not the divine one.","Niobe went unheard for over three centuries. Staged again at Schwetzingen in 2008 and in London soon after, it startled reviewers who had expected a minor curiosity and found a long, strange, slow-burning piece with two enormous title roles. Debate now centres on cuts, since the uncut score tests modern audiences and every production has trimmed it differently.","Find Anfione's sphere aria and start there; three or four minutes will tell you whether the rest is for you. Notice how little the vocal line moves and how much the accompaniment does. Then listen to a Handel opera aria from twenty years later and hear how much faster the harmony has begun to turn. Countertenor recordings suit the role better than transposed baritone versions."],"steffani-stabat-mater":["Six voices, strings and organ, about half an hour, setting the medieval poem on Mary at the cross. Steffani writes six real parts rather than the usual four, so the texture is unusually thick and slow-moving, and he alternates full choral sections with duets that wind two lines around each other in close imitation. The opening is a chain of leaning dissonances over a stepwise falling bass, and that descent returns at the end.","Steffani was a composer, a Catholic bishop and a working diplomat who negotiated on behalf of German courts, an unusual combination even then. He wrote this in Hanover near the end of his life, after the Academy of Vocal Music in London elected him its honorary president in 1727; it was his gift in return. He had spent decades writing opera for Munich, Hanover and Düsseldorf, and this is his farewell.","Steffani's speciality was the chamber duet, two voices treated as equals in continuous imitation, and he wrote well over a hundred of them. This is those duets scaled up: listen for the passages where the six voices divide into pairs and the writing suddenly turns intimate and conversational inside a large sacred work. His duet manner passed to Handel, who took over his old Hanover post in 1710.","The Academy treated the score as a relic of a vanishing tradition even in 1727, and Steffani's music then dropped out of sight for two hundred years. Interest revived sharply in the 2010s, when singers began building recital programmes around his operas, and the Stabat Mater has followed them back into concerts. Editors still work largely from manuscript, since little of it was ever printed.","Play the first two minutes twice. The bass walks slowly downward while the voices lean into each other and pull apart, and once you hear that pattern you will hear it recur to the last bar. Then compare Pergolesi's setting of the same poem, written a few years later and in almost every respect the opposite: two voices, thin textures, opera manners."],"lotti-crucifixus-8-voci":["Four minutes, drawn from the Credo of a larger mass setting. Eight voice parts enter one after another from the bottom upwards, each new arrival adding a note that grates against what is already sounding, so the chord grows steadily more painful before it resolves. Once all eight are in, the texture stays dense to the end, and the final cadence arrives as a long, slow widening out. Nothing repeats.","Lotti spent his life at St Mark's in Venice, rising from singer to organist to maestro di cappella, with a break in Dresden from 1717 writing opera for the Saxon court. He was a pupil of Legrenzi and taught Galuppi. The Venetian basilica's long tradition of dividing singers around the building lies behind writing this thick, even in a piece meant to be heard from one place.","The effect depends entirely on the order of entries. Because each voice comes in a step below or above a note already sounding, every arrival makes a dissonance, and the listener hears not a chord but a wound opening. Choirs often sing it unaccompanied, though an organ bass was probably intended; it works either way, and the unaccompanied version is the one that made it famous.","In 1731 the Academy of Ancient Music in London discovered that Giovanni Bononcini had presented a madrigal of Lotti's as his own. Lotti's letters settled the case, Bononcini's London career collapsed, and the affair became the best-known episode in an otherwise quiet life. The Crucifixus itself reached choirs late, and is now among the most-sung Baroque pieces in the amateur repertoire.","Follow one inner voice all the way through if you can, which is hard and worth attempting, since the piece is designed to make individual lines disappear. Then listen only to the bass, which moves least and grounds everything. Compare it with Caldara's sixteen-voice setting, which does the same thing with twice the forces and half the sting."],"torelli-trumpet-sinfonia-d":["Nine minutes in three or four short movements for a valveless trumpet in D, strings and continuo. The trumpet can play only the notes of one harmonic series, so Torelli builds the fast movements from fanfare shapes, repeated notes and rapid scale fragments high in the instrument's range, and drops it out entirely for the slow movement, where the strings play alone. The key never strays far from D, because it cannot.","San Petronio in Bologna kept trumpeters on its payroll and used them on the patron saint's October feast, when the basilica's galleries allowed brass and strings to be placed far apart. Torelli wrote more than thirty pieces of this kind for the building. The players used long natural trumpets without valves or vent holes, and the reputation of Bolognese trumpeters spread across Europe on the strength of this repertoire.","Listen for what the trumpet does not do: it never plays a phrase in the minor and never fills in a stepwise line low down, because those notes physically do not exist on the instrument. Torelli turns the limitation into a style, giving the strings all the shadowed harmony and reserving the trumpet for blazing outlines. Modern players on valved instruments must work hard to sound this constrained.","The Bolognese trumpet repertoire vanished with the practice that produced it, and its revival waited for players willing to relearn the natural instrument in the 1970s. Recordings still divide: modern piccolo trumpet is accurate and easy, period trumpet is riskier, rougher and closer to what the notes were designed to do. Attributions within the San Petronio archive remain unsettled, since several composers wrote near-identical pieces.","Play a period-instrument recording and a modern piccolo-trumpet one back to back; the difference in colour is the whole argument. Wait for the slow movement, where the trumpet falls silent and the strings suddenly sound intimate in what has been a public piece. Then try Haydn's Trumpet Concerto to hear what happened once the instrument acquired keys."],"torelli-christmas-concerto-op-8-no-6":["Eleven minutes for two solo violins against a string band, headed in the print as a concerto for the most holy Christmas. Four short movements alternate slow and fast, and the whole thing ends with a pastorale: a lilting rocking rhythm in swaying triple time over a bass that holds a single note for bars at a stretch, imitating shepherds' bagpipes. The two soloists move as a pair rather than competing.","Torelli spent his working life in the orchestra of San Petronio in Bologna, a basilica with a vast interior and money for a large instrumental band on feast days. The chapel was disbanded for economy in 1696 and he took work in Ansbach and Vienna before returning in 1701. This concerto appeared in a collection published in Bologna the year he died, seen through the press by his brother, the painter Felice Torelli.","The bagpipe drone in the finale is not decoration but doctrine. Italian churches at Christmas filled with real pipers down from the mountains, and composers imitated them so that the congregation would recognise the sound at once. Corelli, Vivaldi, Handel and Bach all wrote the same swaying figure into their Nativity music, and Torelli's is among the earliest to sit inside a concerto.","Torelli's reputation has always been that of a pioneer rather than a favourite: histories credit him with helping fix the three-movement concerto and with founding the repertoire for trumpet and strings, then move on to Vivaldi. This piece is the exception, kept in circulation by seasonal programmes that pair it with Corelli's. Bolognese scholarship since the 1960s has argued for a wider hearing.","Skip to the last movement first. Once the drone starts, count how long the bass sits still, then hear the two violins turn slowly above it like a music box. Play it next to Corelli's Christmas Concerto and Vivaldi's pastoral writing and the shared vocabulary is obvious. Small period bands of eight or ten catch the chamber scale better than full modern string orchestras."],"bonporti-invenzioni-op-10":["Ten pieces for violin and continuo, each in several short movements, running five or six minutes apiece. Bonporti calls them inventions rather than sonatas because they refuse the church-sonata pattern: a movement may be a fantasia, a recitative, an aria or a set of variations, and the sequence differs every time. The violin line is lyrical rather than athletic, closer to singing than to display, and the bass is unusually active.","Bonporti was a priest in Trento, in the prince-bishopric on the Italian side of the Alps, and spent forty years petitioning without success for a canonry at the cathedral. Composing was his second career and perhaps his campaign for advancement; he dedicated volumes to emperors and cardinals. He published mainly in Venice and Bologna, retired to Padua in old age and died there, still a simple chaplain.","Four of these inventions spent two centuries inside Bach's collected works. Copies made in Bach's circle lost their title page, and the great nineteenth-century Bach edition printed them as his. The error was corrected in the early twentieth century, which was both a demotion and the highest possible compliment: for two hundred years nobody who heard them doubted they were Bach's.","That misattribution is still how most people arrive at Bonporti, and it has coloured judgment in both directions, first inflating him and then, on correction, making him look like an impostor. Recent Italian scholarship and a handful of recordings argue for him on his own terms, as an inventive miniaturist working outside the Venetian and Roman mainstreams. Little of his output has been properly edited.","Find the four that were once Bach's and listen without knowing which is which, then look them up. The point is not that they fooled anyone but that the distance between a very good provincial composer and a great one is smaller than histories suggest. A recording with organ continuo suits the churchier pieces; harpsichord suits the dances."],"a-marcello-oboe-concerto-d":["Three movements, about eleven minutes: a brisk opening in which the oboe answers the strings phrase for phrase, a slow middle movement of two or three minutes that is the reason the piece is famous, and a quick finale. In the slow movement the strings repeat a plain rocking accompaniment while the oboe spins one long line above it, and the player is expected to decorate that line personally.","Alessandro Marcello was a Venetian nobleman who treated music as one accomplishment among several, alongside mathematics, poetry and painting, and published under the Arcadian pen-name Eterio Stinfalico. The concerto appeared in an Amsterdam collection around 1717, in the years when Dutch printers were selling Italian instrumental music across northern Europe. He held no musical post and answered to no employer, which is unusual and audible.","Bach copied the concerto out and arranged it for solo harpsichord, and his written-out ornaments for the slow movement are the best surviving evidence of how an oboist of the time would have decorated it. The attribution has been a mess for two centuries: the piece has been printed under Vivaldi's name and under that of Alessandro's younger brother Benedetto, who wrote no such thing.","The slow movement escaped the concerto entirely, turning up in films and compilations and often played on instruments it was never written for. Scholars settled the authorship in Alessandro's favour on the evidence of the Amsterdam print, but older editions and record sleeves still say Benedetto. Period players argue about how heavily to ornament, since Bach's version suggests a great deal.","Listen to the slow movement, then immediately to Bach's harpsichord arrangement of it, and hear the ornaments a good oboist would improvise. Then decide whether you prefer the plain or the decorated line. Baroque oboes are quieter, reedier and more vocal than modern ones; on a period recording the instrument sounds like a singer rather than a soloist."],"b-marcello-estro-poetico-armonico":["Eight volumes setting the first fifty psalms, published over three years, in Italian paraphrase rather than Latin. Each psalm is a chain of short movements for one, two, three or four voices with continuo, sometimes with viols, ranging from bare recitative through strict fugue to plain hymn-like song. Marcello changes manner constantly to fit the words, and the whole collection runs to several hours of music.","Benedetto Marcello was a Venetian patrician who sat as a magistrate and treated composing as the occupation of a gentleman rather than a trade. The texts are Girolamo Ascanio Giustiniani's Italian versions of the psalms, and both men presented the project as a rescue of sacred music from operatic frivolity. Four years earlier Marcello had published a savage anonymous satire on the opera business.","Some settings are built on melodies Marcello notated from Venice's synagogues, both the Ashkenazi and the Sephardi congregations, and printed with an explanatory note; others use chants he believed to be ancient Greek. It is among the earliest attempts by a European composer to treat non-Christian liturgical music as usable material rather than curiosity, and the borrowed tunes are identified in the score itself.","The collection made his European reputation almost immediately: translated, reprinted in England and Germany, admired by musicians who cared for nothing else Venetian. Later opinion has cooled, finding the piety earnest and the invention uneven across fifty psalms. The Hebrew melodies, meanwhile, have become important to scholars of Jewish music, who read them as a rare early transcription.","Nobody hears this whole; choose a single psalm from an anthology recording and take it as a small cantata. Listen for the change of texture between movements, which is where the interest lies, and for the settings that suddenly sound modal rather than major or minor, since those are often the borrowed tunes. Small vocal ensembles with organ suit it better than choirs."],"geminiani-concerto-grosso-la-follia":["Twelve minutes of variations on the Folia, the old Iberian dance bass that walks slowly through the same sixteen chords again and again. Geminiani took Corelli's violin-and-continuo set and rewrote it for a small solo group against a full string band, so each variation acquires a different colour: solo violin alone, two violins in thirds, the whole orchestra hammering. The last variations pile on speed until the bass is nearly buried.","Geminiani studied with Corelli in Rome, moved to London in 1714 and stayed in Britain and Ireland for the rest of his life, teaching, dealing in paintings and publishing his own music. English amateurs revered Corelli, so arranging his sonatas as concertos was both homage and shrewd business; this one appeared in the second instalment. Geminiani died in Dublin, where he had been working for years.","Compare the two versions bar for bar and you can watch an arranger think. Geminiani keeps Corelli's notes almost intact but redistributes them, moving a running passage from the soloist to the cellos, doubling a melody two octaves apart, letting the orchestra drop out for a whole variation so the return lands harder. Nothing is added; everything is re-lit.","Contemporaries nicknamed him Il Furibondo for the rhythmic waywardness of his playing, and Charles Burney complained that his tempo changes made him impossible to accompany. Later opinion treated the Corelli arrangements as parasitic. That has softened: they are now heard as the first substantial body of orchestral transcription in Western music, and as evidence of how Corelli was actually played.","Listen to Corelli's original first, then this, keeping count of the variations so you can hear where Geminiani intervenes. The moment to wait for is the sudden thinning to a single violin after a full-orchestra variation. Recordings with a small band and a strong harpsichord bring out the dance origin; large modern string orchestras make it grand and lose the stamp of the feet."],"caldara-crucifixus-16-voci":["Five minutes for sixteen independent vocal parts arranged in four choirs, sung unaccompanied in most performances. The voices enter a few at a time on the word crucifixus, and as more join, the harmony thickens into clusters where neighbouring parts rub against each other and resolve only to rub again. There is no melody to follow, only a slowly accumulating and slowly clearing mass of sound; the ending simply thins back out.","Caldara was a Venetian who worked for the Gonzagas in Mantua and for Prince Ruspoli in Rome before becoming vice-Kapellmeister to Emperor Charles VI in Vienna in 1716, reportedly on a salary larger than that of Fux, his nominal superior. Vienna's Holy Week required enormous quantities of new sacred music every year, and Caldara supplied it at speed, leaving several thousand works behind him.","Sixteen parts is not showing off; it is a machine for making a particular sound. With that many voices Caldara can move one part at a time, so the chord is never quite still and never quite changes, and a single word can be stretched over more bars than any four-part setting could sustain. Try counting the entries at the start; most listeners lose track after six.","Caldara's reputation suffered from his own productivity, since histories that admire concentration have little use for a man who wrote three thousand works. Most of his output still sits unedited in Viennese archives. This one piece escaped, taken up by choirs wanting a short unaccompanied showpiece, and it is now far better known than anything else he wrote, which distorts the picture badly.","Five minutes, so listen three times: once for the whole cloud of sound, once trying to track a single low voice through it, once for the point of greatest density, roughly two-thirds through, where the most parts are sounding at once. Then hear Lotti's eight-voice setting of the same word beside it and decide which texture serves the word better."],"locatelli-arte-del-violino-op-3-no-12":["A three-movement violin concerto with two enormous unaccompanied solos built into it, printed as capricci. The one in the finale is headed the harmonic labyrinth and carries a motto saying that entry is easy and exit difficult. It runs for several minutes alone, climbing into positions almost nobody played in 1733, leaping across strings, and modulating so far from home that returning becomes the whole drama. The orchestra simply waits.","Locatelli was born in Bergamo, trained in Rome and settled in Amsterdam in 1729, where he taught wealthy amateurs, dealt in strings and had his music engraved to a high standard. Amsterdam was the printing capital of European music, and this collection was aimed at a buying public as much as a playing one. He is reported to have refused to let professional violinists hear him practise.","The twenty-four capricci scattered through this set are the direct ancestors of Paganini's, and Paganini owned the volume. Look at what is asked for: stretches of a tenth and more, passages high above the fingerboard's normal working range, bowing patterns crossing three strings in a bar. Much of it was considered unplayable for a century, and some editions quietly simplified the hardest lines.","Eighteenth-century critics divided sharply. Some heard a charlatan chasing effects, and Burney thought the music had more difficulty than sense. Nineteenth-century violinists largely ignored him. The modern reassessment began when players started treating the capricci as studies again, and the argument now is about tempo and ornamentation: how much of the written difficulty was meant to be taken literally at speed.","Go to the last movement and listen to the caprice alone, without worrying about the concerto around it. Follow how far the harmony travels, then catch the moment it starts finding its way home; that is the labyrinth. Afterwards play Paganini's Twenty-fourth Caprice and hear the family line. Gut strings and a lighter bow make the strain audible in a way modern setups smooth away."],"porpora-polifemo-alto-giove":["Eight minutes for a single high voice, strings and continuo, from the third act of a three-act opera. Aci has been crushed under a rock by the jealous Cyclops and returned to life as an immortal, and this is his thanks to Jupiter. The aria opens with one note held for an extraordinary length before the line moves at all, then unfolds in long, slow phrases over a barely shifting accompaniment before a contrasting middle section and a decorated return.","In 1733 a group of London aristocrats set up the Opera of the Nobility to compete with Handel, hired Porpora as composer and, the following season, secured Farinelli as their star. Polifemo was written for that company at the King's Theatre in the Haymarket. The star fees and the duplicated seasons ruined both companies within three years, and Porpora left England soon afterwards.","The famous opening note exists to display a specific skill. Castrato training built a breath capacity no unmodified adult voice has, and the whole aria is engineered around it, with phrases long enough that the audience is meant to wonder whether the singer will make it. Everything else is deliberately plain, because ornament was supposed to be added live and differently each night.","Porpora was celebrated in his lifetime chiefly as a teacher: Farinelli, Caffarelli and, later in Vienna, the young Haydn as his accompanist all passed through his hands. His operas disappeared with the castrati who sang them. This aria returned through a 1994 film whose soundtrack blended a countertenor and a soprano electronically, and then through recital discs. Whether any modern voice can do it as written remains disputed.","Time the first note. Then listen to two different singers, a countertenor and a mezzo-soprano, and compare what each adds on the repeat, since the written line is only a frame. Notice how little the strings do; this is nearly a solo. If it works on you, follow it with Handel's Ombra mai fù, written for the same kind of voice and the same kind of stillness."],"veracini-sonate-accademiche-op-2":["Twelve sonatas for violin and bass published together in London, each running ten to fifteen minutes and most in four or five movements. Veracini mixes strict fugal writing with dance movements and with passages of pure display, and several sonatas end in variation sets. The violin writing is angular, full of wide leaps and abrupt stops, and the bass frequently argues with it rather than politely supporting.","Veracini worked in Venice, then at the Dresden court from 1717, then in London, where he wrote operas in competition with Handel's company. He published this set at his own risk, dedicating it to the Saxon-Polish king he had once served. Selling a large violin collection by subscription in London in the 1740s meant courting amateurs who wanted music they could half play and fully admire.","The title claims academic status: these are sonatas for the concert academy, deliberately more learned than the fashionable galant sonatas filling London shops, and Veracini crams them with counterpoint to prove the point. He owned two prized violins that he named after saints and lost them at sea. He had also fallen or jumped from a Dresden window in 1722, and walked with difficulty afterwards.","Burney found him wild and thought his ambition exceeded his judgment, a verdict that stuck. The publication seems not to have sold well, and Veracini ended his life back in Florence in obscurity. Interest revived only with the period-instrument movement, which found the awkwardness deliberate rather than incompetent; the sonatas are now heard as a conscious protest against easy listening.","Do not attempt all twelve. Take one, and listen for the way phrases stop dead in the middle rather than rounding off, which is Veracini's signature and can sound like a fault until you have heard it happen thirty times. Compare it with a Corelli sonata for the contrast between smoothness and grit. Gut strings and a harpsichord suit the sharp edges."],"tartini-devils-trill-sonata":["Four movements, about a quarter of an hour, for violin with a bass line so plain that the violin carries everything. It opens with a slow swaying siciliana, then two fast movements are repeatedly interrupted by slow passages, and the finale demands a long trill sustained by two fingers while two others pick out a melody on a neighbouring string at the same time. Most performances end with a written-out cadenza added much later.","Tartini ran a violin school in Padua from 1727 that drew students from across Europe and was known as the school of nations, and he served the Basilica of Sant'Antonio for decades. He also worked as an acoustician, describing the faint third tone that sounds when two notes are played precisely in tune. The sonata was printed only in 1798, long after his death, in a Paris violin anthology.","Tartini told the astronomer Jérôme Lalande that he had dreamed of selling his soul to the devil, handed him the violin, and heard him play something so far beyond anything Tartini could write down that the sonata is only a shadow of it. The story travelled better than the piece. The trill itself is the mechanical residue of the legend: an effect that sounds impossible and is merely very hard.","Nineteenth-century editors treated the sonata as raw material, and the cadenza most players use is Fritz Kreisler's, written around 1900 and unmistakably later in style. Period specialists now play it with a light bow, a plain continuo and no cadenza at all, which halves its size and changes its character completely. Dating remains guesswork, since no autograph survives and the print is posthumous.","Hear it twice, once in a big romantic performance with piano and Kreisler's cadenza, once on gut strings with harpsichord and cello. They are barely the same piece, and the disagreement is the interesting part. In either version, wait for the finale and listen for the moment the trill begins underneath a melody, then keep listening for how long the player has to hold it."],"sammartini-symphony-f-j-c-32":["Three movements, about eleven minutes, for strings and continuo alone: a bustling fast opening, a short slow movement, a quick finale in dance rhythm. The first movement already works the way a classical symphony does, with a busy opening idea and a lighter contrasting one, and the bass moves in clean repeated patterns rather than the continuously spinning line of a Baroque allegro. Nothing lasts long; everything is stated and moved past.","Sammartini spent his whole career in Milan, holding posts at several churches at once and directing music for aristocratic academies. Milan was Austrian territory, on the road between Italy and Vienna, and visiting musicians carried his symphonies north with them. He wrote around seventy of them across four decades, mostly for private concerts rather than public halls, and rarely bothered to have them printed.","Gluck studied with him in Milan around 1737 to 1741, and Sammartini's manner, short phrases, clear textures, a strong purposeful bass, is audible in Gluck's later reforms. The symphonies survive scattered across European libraries in copies made by travellers, which is why the catalogue took until the twentieth century to assemble and why dates for individual works remain approximate.","Nineteenth-century histories awarded the invention of the symphony to Mannheim or to Vienna. Sammartini's rediscovery, largely the work of scholars piecing together manuscript copies from the 1950s onwards, shifted the earliest layer to Milan and to the 1730s. Some now argue he has been over-credited in turn, and that the sinfonia grew simultaneously in several cities out of the opera overture.","Eleven minutes, and the point is how early it sounds like something much later. Listen to the first movement, then to an early Haydn symphony, and try to date them by ear; most people put Sammartini decades too late. Small period bands of eight or ten strings suit him. Pay attention to the bass line, which is where the Baroque has already been abandoned."],"galuppi-filosofo-di-campagna":["A comic opera in three acts, about two and a half hours, for seven singers and a small orchestra. A wealthy father has promised his daughter to Nardo, a farmer with pretensions to philosophy; she loves someone else, and a quick-witted maid rearranges everybody. The music is fast, tuneful and short-breathed, and each act ends with a long chain finale in which the ensemble grows singer by singer while the tempo keeps rising.","Galuppi and the playwright Carlo Goldoni worked together on around twenty comic operas in Venice in the 1750s, at the moment when opera buffa was overtaking serious opera in popularity. This one opened at the Teatro San Samuele in 1754 and travelled further than any of them, reaching dozens of theatres within twenty years. Galuppi later became maestro di cappella at St Mark's.","The chain finale is the innovation. Instead of ending an act with a solo aria, Goldoni and Galuppi keep the plot moving through a continuous musical stretch of ten minutes or more, adding characters and changing tempo as the confusion mounts. Mozart and Da Ponte inherited the device intact thirty years later; the second-act finale of Figaro is built on exactly this plan.","Galuppi was among the most performed composers in Europe in his lifetime and among the least remembered afterwards, because comic opera in Venetian dialect and topical humour travels badly across centuries. Revivals since the 1950s have found the music sharper than the reputation. The scores exist in many divergent copies, since travelling companies rewrote freely for whatever singers they had.","Skip to an act finale, which is where the piece does something historically new, and count the singers as they arrive. Then hear the end of the second act of The Marriage of Figaro and the debt is obvious. Recordings are few; look for a period-instrument one with a light orchestra, since heavy scoring buries the patter."],"galuppi-keyboard-sonata-c":["Ten minutes in three short movements, the middle one a slow andante far better known than the rest. The writing is thin by Baroque standards, a singing right hand over a simple broken accompaniment, with none of the continuous counterpoint of Scarlatti or Bach; the interest lies in the turn of the melody and in the silences between phrases. The outer movements are quick, light and cheerfully repetitive.","Galuppi wrote around ninety keyboard sonatas, mostly for teaching and for the amateur market, and few were printed while he lived; a set appeared in London around 1756, which is how much of his keyboard music reached players outside Italy. He spent three years in St Petersburg from 1765 working for Catherine the Great, where he taught Bortniansky and wrote for the Orthodox liturgy.","This is transitional music caught in the act. The instrument it was written for is a harpsichord, which cannot swell a note, yet the melody is shaped as though it expects to. Play the andante on a piano and it sounds like early Mozart; play it on a harpsichord and the phrasing has to be created by timing alone. The sonata sits exactly on the hinge between the two instruments.","Robert Browning built a poem around a piece of Galuppi's keyboard music, heard as a memento mori for a vanished Venice, and that poem has done more for the composer's English reputation than any performance. Which piece Browning had in mind, if any, is unknown. Modern editions of the sonatas remain incomplete, and attributions among the manuscript copies are unreliable.","Play the middle movement first, and listen for how much space Galuppi leaves; the pauses carry as much as the notes. Then hear a Domenico Scarlatti sonata immediately afterwards and notice how much busier and more physical Scarlatti is. Try both a harpsichord and a piano recording, since this music genuinely works on either and sounds like two different decades."],"cimarosa-oboe-concerto-c":["Four short movements, twelve minutes in all, for oboe and strings: a solemn introduction, a quick allegro, a swaying siciliana in the minor, and a brisk finale. The oboe plays almost continuously, mostly in long singing lines rather than rapid figuration, and the strings accompany with light repeated chords. It is the most played oboe concerto of its apparent period and the friendliest piece in the instrument's repertoire.","Cimarosa wrote no oboe concerto. The Australian composer Arthur Benjamin assembled this one in 1942 from four of Cimarosa's short keyboard sonatas, choosing movements that suited the oboe, adding a string accompaniment and joining them into a concerto shape. Benjamin was living in Britain during the war, and arrangements of eighteenth-century Italian music were then both fashionable and quick to produce.","The joins are audible once you listen for them. Each movement is a self-contained miniature, and the keys were chosen by the arranger to make a sequence Cimarosa never planned, so there is no thematic connection between movements at all. Compare the concerto with the original keyboard sonatas, several of which are recorded, and Benjamin's contributions stand out: the accompanying figures, the transitions and the cadences are his.","Purists have objected since it appeared, on the grounds that it presents as an eighteenth-century concerto something written in 1942, and older record sleeves credited Cimarosa alone. It survives anyway because oboists need repertoire from a period that produced very little for them. The argument over whether such arrangements are fraud or homage now attaches to a whole shelf of similar wartime confections.","Take it as what it is, a twentieth-century affectionate forgery, and it becomes more interesting rather than less. Listen to the siciliana, the slow third movement, for the most convincingly Cimarosa-like music, then find a recording of the original keyboard sonatas and hear how much thinner and stranger they are. Modern oboe suits it; the piece was written for one."],"paisiello-barbiere-di-siviglia":["Four short acts, a little over two hours, for a handful of singers and a modest orchestra. Count Almaviva, disguised, courts Rosina under the nose of her guardian Bartolo, with Figaro the barber arranging everything. The music is light, quick and small-scaled, with brief arias and a good deal of rapid patter over simple accompaniment; there is a storm interlude before the last act and very little that could be called grand.","Paisiello wrote it in St Petersburg, where he had been Catherine the Great's court composer since 1776, and it was first given at the imperial court theatre in 1782. Beaumarchais's play was only seven years old and still faintly scandalous. Paisiello returned to Naples in 1784 and later served Napoleon in Paris; his career maps the last decades in which an Italian composer could work for any court in Europe.","Paisiello's Figaro is a small part. The barber sets the plot going and then leaves most of the singing to the lovers, which is the reverse of Rossini's arrangement thirty-four years later, where Figaro's entrance aria is the most famous number in the opera. Comparing the two casts shows how much the nineteenth century wanted a charismatic servant at the centre of its comedies.","When Rossini set the same play in 1816 he gave his version a different title to avoid the comparison, and Paisiello's partisans disrupted the first night in Rome anyway. Rossini's opera then drove Paisiello's off the stage for a century and a half. Revivals since the 1950s argue that the two are different in kind rather than in quality: Paisiello's is a chamber comedy, Rossini's a machine.","Listen to Paisiello's setting of a scene you already know from Rossini, the music lesson for instance, and hear how much quieter and faster it is. The whole opera is shorter than Rossini's by half an hour and lighter by an entire orchestra. Look for a small period band; modern-orchestra recordings inflate a piece whose charm is its modesty."],"paisiello-nel-cor-piu-non-mi-sento":["Four minutes, a duet from the comic opera L'amor contrastato, usually called La molinara, though it is almost always heard now as a solo song. The tune is short, symmetrical and immediately memorable: a phrase up, an answering phrase down, over a plain accompaniment that never draws attention to itself. The whole design is transparency, nothing to distract from a melody a listener can sing back after one hearing.","Paisiello wrote the opera for Naples in 1788, in the years between his Russian and his French appointments, for a public theatre rather than a court. Publishers of the period made their money on detachable numbers, and this one was printed, arranged and sold across Europe within a few years, for voice and keyboard, for guitar, for flute duet, for anything that could carry a tune.","Beethoven wrote six piano variations on it in 1795, one of several sets he produced on popular theatre tunes as calling cards for Viennese salons. Paganini wrote his own variations for solo violin. Neither piece would exist without a melody plain enough to be decorated: the tune's very lack of character is exactly what made it useful raw material for two virtuosos.","The opera it comes from vanished; the tune did not, and for a century it was among the best-known melodies in Europe, familiar to people who had never heard of Paisiello. That fate is common for eighteenth-century comic opera, and has led scholars to argue that judging these works by their surviving hits misrepresents them badly. Complete recordings of La molinara remain rare.","Hear it sung plainly first, in the duet version if you can find one, so the tune registers as a tune. Then play Beethoven's variations and count how quickly the melody stops being recognisable. Then Paganini's, where it disappears altogether under the decoration. Three pieces, twenty minutes, and a clear lesson in what variation actually does to a theme."],"cimarosa-matrimonio-segreto":["Two acts, under three hours, for six singers with no chorus at all, which is unusually lean for a full-length comedy. Carolina has secretly married her father's clerk; the father wants to marry both daughters upward; a visiting count picks the wrong sister. The overture is a bustling piece still played on its own, and the act finales run for a quarter of an hour each, building ensembles out of characters talking past one another.","Cimarosa had spent four years in St Petersburg working for Catherine the Great and then moved to Vienna as court composer to Leopold II, arriving in the city just as Mozart's career there ended. The libretto by Giovanni Bertati adapts an English comedy about a clandestine marriage. The premiere was at the Burgtheater in February 1792, the same theatre and much the same audience that had received Figaro and Così.","Leopold II is said to have given the cast supper after the first performance and then had the entire opera performed again the same night, which if true is the longest encore on record. The score's real subtlety is quieter: with only six characters and no chorus, Cimarosa must make every ensemble from the same voices, and he does it by changing who is lying to whom.","For most of the nineteenth century this was the comic opera, performed more often than Figaro and treated as the model of the form; Stendhal preferred it to anything of Mozart's. The twentieth century reversed the verdict almost completely, and it now survives at the edge of the repertoire while the overture stays in concerts. The reversal says more about changing taste for depth than about the piece.","Start with the overture, three minutes, then jump to the first-act finale to hear the same energy sustained for fifteen. Listen for how Cimarosa keeps six voices distinguishable in ensemble, which is the craft the nineteenth century admired. Then play the second-act finale of Figaro and judge the comparison yourself; it is closer than the current reputations suggest."],"clara-schumann-piano-trio-g":["Four movements for violin, cello and piano, about half an hour. A restless G-minor opening with the violin singing over the piano's churning figures; a light scherzo in the pace of a minuet rather than a headlong dash; a slow movement whose long-breathed tune the cello takes over from the violin; and a finale that stops being lyrical and turns contrapuntal, the three players chasing one fugue subject through every key before a brusque close.","Written in Dresden in 1846, when she was twenty-seven, running a household with four small children, recovering from a miscarriage and managing Robert's collapsing health. She had spent 1845 working through counterpoint exercises with him, and the trio is the first large work in which that study shows. Robert wrote his own D-minor Piano Trio the following year; the two pieces were composed in the same rooms and clearly listened to each other.","The finale's fugue is the point of the piece. Trios of the 1840s were expected to end in a good tune and a rush; hers ends by proving she can build. Listen also to how rarely the piano takes the melody outright — she was the finest pianist in Europe and gives herself accompaniment, letting the strings lead. The scherzo's odd, courtly gait is a deliberate archaism.","She thought well of it at first and then talked herself out of it, writing that a woman's work must always lack force and invention. Publishers kept it in print but concert life dropped it, and it went essentially unheard between the 1890s and the 1970s. The current view is that it is the strongest chamber work by any woman before Fanny Mendelssohn's trio, and that her verdict on it was borrowed, not felt.","Start with the third movement, the slow one, and note the moment the cello inherits the violin's tune. Then jump to the finale and wait for the fugue to lock in. Play it back to back with Robert Schumann's D-minor Piano Trio of 1847; the family resemblance is startling and the traffic runs both ways."],"clara-schumann-three-romances-violin-piano":["Three short pieces, about eleven minutes together, each built on a single melody rather than an argument. The first is soft and hesitant, the violin entering mid-phrase as though the music were already under way. The second is quicker and more anxious, with the piano pushing against the violin's line. The third returns to calm, the violin high and quiet over rippling chords, and the set ends almost inaudibly.","The summer and autumn of 1853 were the happiest months of her life: Joseph Joachim was visiting, the twenty-year-old Brahms arrived at the door in September, and Robert published his article announcing him to the world. She wrote the Romances for Joachim, who played them with her; they were published in 1855 with a dedication to him. Within three years Robert was dead and she had all but stopped composing.","The violin almost never has the tune alone. She keeps the melody in the piano's right hand and lets the violin weave a second voice around it, so the two instruments are always slightly out of step — a pianist's way of hearing a duo. Notice too that none of the three pieces has a real climax; each rises and then deliberately declines to arrive.","Joachim programmed them and reported that audiences liked them, but they went out of circulation for most of a century along with the rest of her catalogue. Violinists rediscovered them in the 1970s, and they are now recital regulars — sometimes criticised as slight, more often defended as the most concentrated writing she left. Editions differ on tempo markings, which changes the character of the second piece considerably.","Listen with your attention on the piano rather than the violin and the pieces reorganise themselves. Eleven minutes is short enough to hear twice in a row; do that. Then set them beside Robert Schumann's short piano pieces of the 1830s and hear how much further she is willing to go in leaving a phrase unfinished."],"clara-schumann-piano-concerto-finale":["The last of three movements played without a break, and by far the longest. A solo cello has been in quiet conversation with the piano; the orchestra cuts in and the finale erupts in A minor with a stamping polonaise rhythm. The piano runs in octaves and cascades, the orchestra punches back, a slower lyrical episode opens a window, and the movement ends by wrenching itself into A major with the soloist blazing.","She began this movement at fourteen as a free-standing concert piece, then wrote two more movements in front of it. Robert Schumann, then her father's lodger, helped with the orchestration. The premiere came at the Leipzig Gewandhaus on 9 November 1835, Mendelssohn conducting, the composer sixteen and already a touring celebrity managed — and exhibited — by her father Friedrich Wieck. Concertos by women were not published; hers was, as Op. 7.","The three movements run together with no applause gaps, an idea she took from Weber and Mendelssohn and used before either Schumann or Liszt made it standard. The slow movement is not a slow movement at all but a duet for piano and a single solo cello, no other instruments — an unheard-of texture in 1835, and the reason the finale's orchestral entry lands like a door slamming.","Reviewers treated it as a prodigy's calling card rather than a composition, and it vanished with her performing career. Recordings from the 1970s onward have made the case that the finale's polonaise energy and the cello duet are genuinely original, not merely precocious. The counter-argument, still heard, is that the orchestration is thin — which is fair, and partly Robert's responsibility.","Begin one movement early, at the cello duet, so you hear the finale break in. Then listen for how the piano writing avoids empty passagework: nearly every run has a tune inside it. Compare with Chopin's concertos of five years earlier, which she played constantly, and with Robert Schumann's A-minor concerto of a decade later."],"clara-schumann-liebst-du-um-schonheit":["Two minutes for voice and piano on a Rückert poem in four stanzas. Three of them begin with a refusal — if you love for beauty, do not love me; if for youth, do not love me; if for treasure, do not love me — and the music keeps returning to the same gently rocking chords, barely changed. Only the last stanza, where love is loved for its own sake, lifts the vocal line and lets the piano settle.","Written in 1841, the first year of the marriage. Robert had spent 1840 writing nothing but songs; for the following New Year the two of them made a joint gift of settings from Rückert's Liebesfrühling, published together as his Op. 37 and her Op. 12, with no indication of who had written which. The plan was that a listener should not be able to tell.","Nobody was told the authorship, and reviewers guessed wrong. The music itself is her argument: instead of building to a declaration, she keeps three stanzas almost identical and lets the fourth simply widen, so the change of heart registers as relief rather than triumph. Mahler set the same poem sixty years later, for Alma, and does the opposite — he swells it.","Because the songs were unattributed, hers were absorbed into Robert's reputation for over a century and printed in his collected editions. Restoring the attributions was slow archival work, and recital programmes only began separating the two names in the 1980s. The current argument is not whether her songs are good but why she wrote so few — she stopped composing almost entirely after 1856.","Hear it beside Mahler's setting of the same words and notice which one trusts the poem. Follow the piano's left hand, which barely moves for three stanzas. Any good lieder singer with a real pianist will do; avoid orchestrated arrangements, which fill in exactly the space the song needs to leave empty."],"clara-schumann-scherzo-no-2-c":["About six minutes of solo piano in fast triple time. A hard, dry C-minor idea in the right hand over a left hand that has to leap across the keyboard to keep up; the phrases come in irregular lengths, so the dance keeps tripping. A contrasting middle section slows the harmony and lets a warmer tune surface, then the opening returns and the piece ends by dying away rather than crashing home.","Written in 1841, the same year as the Rückert songs and the first year of her marriage, in Leipzig. She was playing Chopin's scherzos in public at a time when few pianists would, and the debt is open. The concert scherzo — a dance movement blown up into a stand-alone showpiece — was barely a decade old, and this is one of the earliest by anyone other than Chopin.","The left hand does the real work: it crosses over the right repeatedly, so a listener hears the melody arriving from underneath. The phrase lengths are the second trick — she cuts a bar out where you expect one, which is why the piece feels breathless without ever getting faster. And the ending refuses the big chord that every scherzo of the period ends on.","It was one of her own recital staples for decades, which is why it stayed in print, but critics filed it under 'salon music' and it disappeared with her. Pianists returned to it in the 1970s and now programme it as a Chopin companion. The recurring complaint is that it is derivative; the answer is that it was written while the model was still fresh, not after it had become a genre.","Watch the hands if you can — the left-hand crossings are the piece. Listen for the bar that goes missing near the end of each phrase. Then play Chopin's Second Scherzo immediately afterwards and hear how much bigger and more theatrical his is, and how much drier and stranger hers."],"franck-violin-sonata-fourth-movement":["Six minutes built on one of the plainest devices in music: a canon. The violin sings a broad, walking tune in A major and the piano repeats it exactly, one bar behind, all the way through — the two instruments locked a step apart. Between the canonic statements, themes from the three earlier movements come back, darker and unresolved, before the canon reassembles and drives the sonata to a blazing A-major finish.","Franck was sixty-three, organist at Sainte-Clotilde and a Conservatoire professor with a circle of disciples. The sonata was a wedding present for the Belgian violinist Eugène Ysaÿe, delivered on the morning of the ceremony in September 1886; Ysaÿe read it through and played it that day. The public premiere came in Brussels that December, in a museum gallery where the light failed and the last movements were played from memory in the dark.","A hesitant three-note rising figure opens the first movement and quietly generates everything afterwards, including the canon's tune — Franck's 'cyclic' method, which his pupils turned into doctrine. The canon is at the octave and strictly maintained, which means the piano is always playing what the violin has just finished; the effect is of a memory arriving one beat too late. Proust's readers have hunted the fictional Vinteuil Sonata here ever since.","It was a success from the start and became the most played French chamber work of its century, transcribed for cello, flute, viola and almost anything else. The story of the darkened Brussels premiere comes from Vincent d'Indy and may have been improved in the telling. Sceptics argue the cyclic technique is a formula that lets a movement end without earning it; performers answer by taking the canon slower than the marking suggests.","Put your ear on the piano and follow it echoing one bar behind — once you hear the gap you cannot unhear it. Then go back to the first movement's opening three notes and track them through all four movements. A cellist's version is worth hearing for how much the transposition changes the light."],"franck-symphony-d":["Three movements rather than the customary four, about forty minutes. It opens low and slow with a three-note question in the strings, answered and re-answered until the music finally moves. The middle movement does the work of both slow movement and scherzo: plucked strings and harp set up a hushed floor, and a cor anglais plays the tune everyone remembers. The finale gathers all the earlier themes and stacks them in the brass.","Franck was in his mid-sixties, an organist and teacher rather than a public composer, surrounded by devoted pupils. France had almost no symphonic tradition to speak of — opera absorbed everything — and the Société Nationale de Musique, founded after the defeat of 1870, was trying to build one. The Conservatoire premiere in February 1889 was chilly; the establishment reportedly regarded the cor anglais and harp as instruments no symphony should contain.","The opening three-note figure is the whole symphony in miniature: it returns transformed as the main allegro theme, hides inside the cor anglais melody's accompaniment, and reappears in the brass at the close. The second movement is also a structural trick — the scherzo material is not a separate section but a faster string figure woven around the slow tune, so two movements happen simultaneously.","The premiere was frosty and Gounod is said to have called it the affirmation of incompetence pushed to dogmatic lengths. It then became a repertoire staple through Monteux, Furtwängler and Bernstein, and slipped again after mid-century, criticised for organ-loft scoring and brass that doubles everything. Recent French-orchestra recordings, with brighter reeds and lighter brass, have made a persuasive case for it.","Start with the second movement and its cor anglais over pizzicato and harp — it is one of the great tunes and it arrives within a minute. Then go back to the beginning and count how many times the opening three notes come back in disguise. Seek a French orchestra: the nasal reeds change the piece completely."],"franck-panis-angelicus":["Four minutes of almost nothing but one melody. A solo tenor sings a rising phrase, falls back, and rises again a step higher each time; underneath, harp and low strings keep a gently pulsing tread that never changes character, with the organ holding the harmony steady. The words are two stanzas of Aquinas: the bread of angels becomes the bread of men. It is now sung by sopranos, choirs and massed orchestras as readily as by tenors.","Franck had been organist at Sainte-Clotilde in Paris since 1858, with a new Cavaillé-Coll instrument to improvise on and a congregation rather than a public. He had written a three-voice Mass in 1860; twelve years later, in the Catholic revival that followed the Commune, he wrote this movement and slipped it into the existing score. It is a devotional interpolation, not part of the Mass ordinary at all.","The whole piece is one phrase repeated at rising pitch levels, which is why it feels like it is climbing a staircase; there is no second theme and no development. The scoring is the giveaway that it was written for a specific building — harp and pizzicato bass against organ is a Sainte-Clotilde sound, and most performances substitute a string orchestra that smooths the edges away.","It has been dismissed for a century as pious and saccharine, and it is completely unrepresentative of the austere Franck of the late chamber and organ works. It has also been recorded by nearly every famous tenor since Caruso, sung at weddings, funerals and stadiums, and outsold everything else he wrote. Scholarship now treats it as an example of how a liturgical fragment escapes its liturgy.","Find a version with organ and harp rather than full orchestra, and with one voice rather than a hundred. Listen for the pulse underneath, which never varies — the melody's rise is the only event. Then hear Fauré's Cantique de Jean Racine for the same French church sound handled with less sugar."],"franck-prelude-choral-et-fugue":["Eighteen minutes of solo piano in three linked sections with no breaks. The Prelude ripples in broken chords, restless and unsettled. The Choral answers with a slow hymn-like idea punctuated by tolling, bell-like descending figures low in the instrument. The Fugue then uncoils a chromatic subject that keeps sliding downwards, builds through dense counterpoint, and at the end all three sections' material sounds at once as the music turns from B minor to B major.","Franck was sixty-two and had spent his life improvising at the organ of Sainte-Clotilde; this is piano music written by a man who thought in pipes and pedals. He conceived it first as a prelude and fugue in homage to Bach — part of the French Bach revival of the 1870s and 80s — and inserted the chorale between them afterwards. Marie Poitevin gave the first performance at the Société Nationale in January 1885.","The fugue subject is smuggled into the Prelude before you ever hear it announced, buried in the arpeggios. And the bells of the Choral are not a separate idea: their falling chromatic line is the same descent that the fugue subject spells out slowly. So the three movements are one shape at three speeds — which is why the ending, stacking all of them together, sounds inevitable rather than clever.","Vincent d'Indy hailed it as a modern equivalent of Bach and read a spiritual progression into the three sections, which later writers have called special pleading. Cortot and Rubinstein championed it; other pianists find it stubbornly organ-bound, with textures that need a pedalboard and a building. The technical demands — huge stretches, Franck had enormous hands — keep it off many recital programmes.","Listen for the tolling bells in the middle section, then go back and find the fugue subject hiding inside the opening ripples. Give the last three minutes your full attention: everything you have heard returns at once. Cortot's old recording and a modern one make almost different pieces; try both."],"franck-symphonic-variations":["Fifteen minutes for piano and orchestra in one unbroken span. It opens as an argument: the strings throw down a stern dotted phrase and the piano answers softly, twice, three times, neither giving way. Out of the piano's reply grows a quiet theme, which is then varied — six times, growing warmer and more decorated. Finally the music tips into F sharp major and a dancing finale, the piano skipping over pizzicato strings.","Franck wrote it as a thank-you to the pianist Louis Diémer, who had played an earlier orchestral piece of his. He was sixty-three and had never written a concerto: in the Paris of the 1880s the form was associated with vulgar display, so he called the work variations instead and refused the soloist a cadenza. Diémer gave the premiere at the Société Nationale on 1 May 1886.","The two opening ideas are the same idea. The orchestra's dotted challenge, turned upside down and slowed, is the piano's meek reply — and the variation theme grows out of that reply, so the entire fifteen minutes derives from one gesture heard two ways. The dancing finale tune is the challenge again, in the major and skipping. Nothing new is introduced after the first minute.","Long considered the best-balanced of his orchestral scores, and a favourite of pianists who dislike concerto bombast — Cortot, Casadesus and Curzon all recorded it. The complaint is that the ending is too easily won, the minor-key tension dissolved rather than resolved. Its refusal to give the soloist a display cadenza remains unusual enough that programmers still bill it as a concerto anyway.","Listen to the first ninety seconds twice: everything else comes from there. Then track how the theme grows more ornate with each variation while the harmony stays put. Pair it with a Liszt showpiece to feel exactly what Franck was refusing to write."],"smetana-vltava-moldau":["Twelve minutes tracing a river. Two flutes trickle as the cold and warm springs, joined by clarinets, until the streams merge into the broad, slightly melancholy E-minor tune carried by violins and oboe. The river then passes a hunt (horns in the distance), a village wedding (a stamping polka), a moonlit scene where nymphs dance to muted strings and harp, and the crashing St John's Rapids, before broadening into E major past the Vyšehrad rock and fading downstream.","The second of the six symphonic poems of Má vlast, written in under three weeks in late 1874. Smetana had gone completely deaf that October and had resigned as conductor of Prague's Provisional Theatre; he composed the piece hearing none of it. Bohemia was a Habsburg province with a Czech-language cultural movement building a national theatre and a national repertoire more or less from scratch, and he was its designated composer.","The river tune is not Czech. It is a wandering European melody — traceable through an Italian song and later adopted as the Israeli national anthem — which Smetana knew and used anyway, so the most patriotic tune in Czech music is a borrowed one. Note also that the river stays in the minor even at its most contented, and that the four-note Vyšehrad motif from the cycle's first poem returns in the brass at the very end.","Má vlast opens the Prague Spring festival every 12 May, the anniversary of Smetana's death, and Kubelík's return from exile to conduct it in 1990 is part of Czech national memory. Vltava is played worldwide as a concert opener, which purists resent: the cycle is designed as a single arch and the Vyšehrad quotation makes no sense in isolation. Tempo in the rapids remains a running argument.","Follow the two flutes for the first minute and hear the second stream join. Then keep a list of the scenes as they pass — hunt, wedding, moonlight, rapids — and notice how briefly each is sketched. Once you know it, hear the whole cycle in order; Vltava sounds different when Vyšehrad has come first."],"smetana-bartered-bride-overture":["Seven minutes that start with one full-orchestra whack and then hand everything to the strings, which take off in a whispering fugal scramble — second violins first, then firsts, then violas, then cellos, each entry piling on until the whole section is racing. Woodwind interrupt with a broad singing tune; the scramble returns, brass and timpani join, and the piece finishes in a stamping rush without ever quite catching its breath.","Written in Prague between 1863 and 1866 for the Provisional Theatre, the Czech-language house that had opened in 1862. Smetana was under attack from critics who thought him a Wagnerian and doubted Czech could be sung at all, and he set out to write a comedy that answered both charges. The premiere, on 30 May 1866, took place with Prussian armies advancing on Bohemia; the house was half empty and the run collapsed.","The overture was composed before most of the opera and its scrambling fugue subject never appears in the drama proper — it is essentially a free-standing concert piece welded on. And for all its village-square sound, the score quotes no folk tunes whatever: every 'Czech' melody in the opera is Smetana's invention, built from the rhythms of the spoken language rather than from collected song.","The 1866 flop became the most-performed Czech opera in history after Smetana revised it into three acts with dances by 1870; the Provisional Theatre had given it over a hundred performances within fifteen years. Abroad it has often been patronised as operetta, a reading Mahler and Beecham both fought. Conductors still disagree about whether the fugue should be whispered at speed or merely played fast.","Listen to the strings alone in the first minute and count the entries as each section joins the chase. Then hear the Dance of the Comedians from the same opera for the other side of the piece. A recording by a Czech orchestra is worth seeking: the accents fall differently."],"smetana-string-quartet-my-life-first-movement":["Eight minutes, and it begins not with a theme but with a warning: the viola cries out a stabbing descending figure over trembling strings, and the other three instruments react to it rather than accompany it. A second, tender idea in the major offers relief and is repeatedly cut off. The movement is compact sonata form, unusually terse for 1876, and it ends quietly and unresolved rather than with the expected drive to a cadence.","Composed at Jabkenice in the autumn of 1876, two years into total deafness, living in his daughter's forester's cottage outside Prague with his public career over. He wrote out a private programme for a friend: the first movement is the call of fate, his youth, his longing for art and the warning he did not understand at the time. Chamber music in Bohemia was amateur domestic music, and publishers found the writing unplayable.","The viola's four-note cry is the whole quartet's spine — it returns in the finale, immediately after the piercing sustained high note that Smetana said was the tinnitus that preceded his deafness. The programme itself is hidden: it survives only in a private letter and was never printed with the score, so audiences for decades heard the piece as absolute music.","Joseph Hellmesberger rejected it as unplayable, and the first performance in 1878 was private, with Dvořák on viola. Schoenberg later praised its construction; Szell made an orchestral version that is still occasionally heard and that most quartets regard as a betrayal. The live argument is whether the programme should be printed in programmes at all, or whether telling an audience about the tinnitus turns a quartet into a case history.","Memorise the viola's first four notes, then listen for them at the very end of the last movement. Play the finale afterwards for the high sustained note that is the reason the quartet exists. Compare with Janáček's second quartet, another Czech composer writing his private life into four instruments."],"smetana-bartered-bride-dance-comedians":["Five minutes of accelerating whirl. It is a skočná, a fast Czech 'hop' dance in brisk duple time: a chattering woodwind figure, a stamping tune handed round the orchestra, and a series of gear changes that keep raising the speed until brass and percussion take over for a headlong finish. On stage it accompanies a travelling circus troupe tumbling into the village square in the third act.","Not part of the original 1866 opera. Smetana expanded The Bartered Bride over four years into the three-act version of 1869–70, adding a full set of dances because Prague audiences expected them and because the Provisional Theatre, where he was chief conductor, needed hits. The circus scene gave him an excuse for a virtuoso orchestral number in a comic opera about a village marriage contract.","The opera parades three distinct Czech dance types — polka, furiant and this skočná — as a kind of national catalogue, and each has a different foot pattern; the skočná is the one that lands off the beat you expect, which is why it feels like tumbling. The number was an afterthought, and abroad it has outlived almost everything else in the opera except the overture.","The dances are usually extracted as a concert suite, which strips the circus context and turns character music into showpiece. Conductors compete on speed, sometimes past the point where the woodwind articulation survives. Czech performers tend to argue that the rhythmic snap matters more than the tempo, and that non-Czech orchestras smooth out exactly the accents that make it a dance.","Try to feel where the beat actually lands — it is rarely where you first guess. Listen for the woodwind chatter under the tune. Then hear it staged, with acrobats, and see how much of the orchestration is describing bodies in the air rather than music in the abstract."],"bruckner-symphony-no-7-adagio":["Twenty minutes built in slow terraces. A quartet of Wagner tubas — dark, soft-edged instruments between horn and trombone — intones a descending chorale in C sharp minor, and the strings answer with a rising line that keeps trying to lift the music out of it. A gentler second theme in F sharp major offers relief. Everything then climbs, in patient blocks, to an enormous C major climax, crowned in most performances by cymbals and triangle; the tubas return for a hushed funeral coda.","Bruckner was fifty-eight, still barely performed, teaching harmony in Vienna and regarded there as a provincial organist with delusions. He began this movement early in 1883 telling pupils he was thinking of Wagner's approaching death; the news came on 13 February, and he wrote the coda as funeral music for the Master. The Wagner tubas were the instruments Wagner had had built for the Ring. Nikisch's Leipzig premiere in December 1884 was Bruckner's first real triumph.","The opening tuba theme is closely related to the 'Non confundar' of his own Te Deum, so the movement carries a text it never sings. The famous crux is the climax: the manuscript's cymbal and triangle carry a pencil note reading 'invalid', and nobody can prove whether Bruckner wrote it or one of his interfering pupils. Every conductor therefore makes a decision you can hear.","The Leipzig and Munich performances of 1884–85 finally made his name at sixty. The cymbal question split editors — Haas removed the stroke, Nowak restored it — and remains the standard test case for the whole 'Bruckner problem'. German radio played this Adagio when Hitler's death was announced in 1945, an association later conductors have had to play through. Tempo ranges from Karajan's flow to Celibidache's near-stasis.","Listen for the four Wagner tubas in the opening bars — that dark, slightly clouded sound is the whole movement's colour. Then wait for the climax and decide whether you want the cymbal. Compare two recordings that differ on it; the same twenty minutes become two different pieces."],"bruckner-symphony-no-4-romantic-first-movement":["Eighteen minutes that begin with almost nothing: a whispering E flat tremolo in the strings, over which a solo horn calls a falling fifth, as if from across a valley. The call spreads through the orchestra and grows into a full brass proclamation. The second group is lighter — chirping violin figures over a walking bass — and the whole movement proceeds in great blocks of sound separated by silences, ending with the horn call blazing in the full brass.","Written in 1874 while Bruckner was scraping a living in Vienna as a Conservatory teacher, mocked by Hanslick's circle for his Wagner enthusiasm and his rural manners. The catastrophic failure of his Third Symphony in 1877 sent him back to this score: he rewrote it wholesale, replacing the scherzo in 1878 with the 'hunt' movement and the finale again in 1880. Hans Richter's Vienna premiere in February 1881 was his first Viennese success.","The chirping figure in the second group is birdsong — Bruckner told pupils it was the call of a great tit, and it is written out literally. The movement is also stitched together by the 'Bruckner rhythm', two beats followed by a triplet, which appears in almost every transition once you know to listen for it. The opening horn call comes back to end the finale, an hour later.","The version wars are worse here than anywhere: the 1874 original is a different piece, the 1878–80 score is the standard, and the heavily cut and re-orchestrated 1888 edition published by his pupils was for decades the only one available. Haas and Nowak editions differ again. The 'Romantic' nickname was Bruckner's own, but he gave friends contradictory programmes involving medieval cities, knights and dawn.","Hold on to the very first horn call and wait for it to return at full blast — it is the simplest way into a composer who otherwise seems formless. Listen for the birdsong in the quieter second theme. Then hear the 1874 original version to grasp how much he was willing to throw away."],"bruckner-symphony-no-8-adagio":["The longest slow movement in the nineteenth-century symphony, around twenty-five minutes. Strings rock in a syncopated pattern while the violins reach upward for a theme that repeatedly stretches higher and falls back. Harps enter — the only harps in any Bruckner symphony — and Wagner tubas lay down a solemn chorale beneath them. The music builds across three enormous waves to a shattering climax with cymbals and triangle, then dissolves into quiet tuba chords.","Bruckner finished the first version in 1887 and sent it, confident, to the conductor Hermann Levi, who could not follow it and declined. The rejection broke him: he began revising not only this symphony but most of his back catalogue, which is the origin of the editorial chaos surrounding his music. The revised score was dedicated to Emperor Franz Joseph, and Hans Richter's Vienna premiere in December 1892 was a genuine triumph.","The upward-reaching interval that opens the movement's melody is the same interval that opens the whole symphony, so the Adagio is the symphony's first gesture slowed to a crawl. The harps are the tell that Bruckner heard this as sacred music rather than symphonic argument. And the great climax was scaled back in the 1890 revision — the 1887 version goes further and resolves differently.","Levi's rejection, and Bruckner's willingness to accept it, is the founding episode of the 'Bruckner problem'. Haas conflated the 1887 and 1890 versions into a hybrid; Nowak kept them separate; conductors still choose sides, and the choice changes several minutes of music. Furtwängler, Karajan and Celibidache built reputations on this movement, Celibidache stretching it towards half an hour on its own.","Give it the full twenty-five minutes with nothing else running; the piece depends entirely on accumulated time. Watch for the harps, and notice how the music never speeds up — the intensity comes from harmony and volume alone. Then hear the 1887 version's different climax and pick your side."],"bruckner-locus-iste":["Three minutes for unaccompanied four-part choir, in a simple arch. The voices state the words 'Locus iste a Deo factus est' — this place was made by God — in warm, block C major harmony, all four parts moving together. The middle turns unsettled and chromatic on 'inaestimabile sacramentum, irreprehensibilis est', the voices drifting apart into suspensions. Then the opening returns unaltered and the piece fades on the same chord it began with.","Written in 1869 for the dedication of the votive chapel of the new cathedral in Linz, where Bruckner had been cathedral organist. He had just returned from triumphs as an improviser in Nancy and Paris, where French organists had been astonished by him, and he was about to move to Vienna. The Cecilian reform movement was then demanding that church music be purged of orchestras, opera and display, and return to Palestrina.","The most harmonically unstable bar in the piece falls on 'irreprehensibilis' — blameless — which is either a joke or a theological point about the gap between the word and the world. The design is exactly symmetrical: the opening returns note for note, so the whole motet is one arch with the doubt placed in the middle. And the terraced dynamics are the same technique as his symphonies, compressed into three minutes.","For most choral singers this is the first Bruckner they ever meet, and it is sung in cathedrals and school halls alike. A strand of criticism argues that the motets, not the symphonies, are his purest music, because the Cecilian rules stopped him revising. Performance debates concern vibrato, whether to use boys' voices, and how much the tempo should breathe.","Sing along with the opening phrase — it is designed to be memorable in one hearing — and then listen for where the harmony goes strange in the middle. Follow it with Os justi to hear the same composer working under even tighter self-imposed rules. A cathedral acoustic helps; a dry studio recording does not."],"bruckner-os-justi":["Five minutes for unaccompanied choir, expanding from four voices to eight and back. It opens with hushed block chords, the mouth of the just man meditating wisdom. The middle sets the second clause as a slow fugue, the voices entering one by one from the bottom and rising to a full-throated climax. The opening texture returns, and the motet closes with a single line of plainchant, sung in unison, that seems to step outside the piece entirely.","Written in 1879, while Bruckner was in Vienna working on his String Quintet, for Ignaz Traumihler, choirmaster at the abbey of St Florian — where Bruckner had been a choirboy, where he had played the organ, and where he would eventually be buried beneath it. Traumihler was a Cecilian hardliner who wanted church music stripped back to Palestrina, and Bruckner set out to satisfy him completely.","He restricted himself to the white notes: the whole motet is in the Lydian mode with not a single sharp or flat, and no seventh chords anywhere — he said so in the covering letter, half boasting. The unison line at the end is not his tune at all but plainchant, dropped in as a signature. Working under those restrictions is what produces the strange sideways harmonic slides in the fugue.","Widely held to be the finest of his motets and the strongest evidence for the argument that Bruckner was fundamentally a church composer who happened to write symphonies. The eight-part writing raises balance problems that large choirs rarely solve; chamber consorts of sixteen or twenty singers now often do it better. The plainchant ending is sometimes omitted, which most scholars consider a mistake.","Wait for the fugue and count the voices entering from the bass upward — that rising stack is the emotional event. Then listen to the last twenty seconds of unison chant and hear how much older it sounds. Compare a large cathedral choir with a small consort; the eight parts come out completely differently."],"johann-strauss-blue-danube":["Ten minutes, far longer than a ballroom dance needs to be. It begins with a shimmering string tremolo and a rising horn call that hangs without a clear beat, so the waltz seems to condense out of mist rather than start. Then five linked waltzes follow, each with a pair of tunes, growing brighter and faster; a coda races back through them all and finishes with drum roll and flourish.","Vienna in 1867 was still absorbing the military catastrophe at Königgrätz the previous summer, and the Vienna Men's Choral Association asked Strauss for something cheerful for its carnival concert. He supplied a choral waltz to a mocking text by Josef Weyl; the February premiere was only a modest success. The orchestral version, taken to the Paris World Exhibition that summer, made it the most famous piece of dance music ever written.","The tune everyone hums is nothing but a broken D major chord, rising — the simplest possible material, which is why it survives every arrangement. The opening tremolo deliberately withholds the downbeat for nearly a minute. The words now sung, 'Donau so blau', were written only in 1890, more than twenty years after the music. And the Danube at Vienna is grey-brown.","The serious press filed it under light music; Brahms and Wagner both admired it, and Brahms once autographed a fan with its opening bars and the note that it was, alas, not by him. It has since become Austria's unofficial anthem, closes the Vienna Philharmonic's New Year concert every year, and acquired a second life turning spacecraft in Kubrick's 2001. Scholars note how symphonic its proportions are for a dance piece.","Listen to the first minute for how long the waltz refuses to arrive. Then hear the original choral version, which almost nobody plays, and notice how much sharper the piece is with words. A New Year's Concert recording gives you the Viennese lilt, where the second beat comes slightly early."],"johann-strauss-fledermaus-overture":["Eight minutes assembled from the operetta's best tunes, in the frank manner of a medley. A chattering opening, a chiming bell figure, a sighing woodwind phrase that will later be sung by a jailed husband, a giggling clarinet, the great second-act waltz laid out at full length, and a headlong accelerando to finish. The joins are theatrical rather than symphonic: Strauss stops the music dead before the waltz so the audience can lean forward.","Written in about six weeks in 1874 for the Theater an der Wien, a year after the Vienna stock market crash had ruined much of the audience and the World's Fair had emptied the city's pockets. The libretto came from a French farce by way of Meilhac and Halévy. Vienna's response was warm but not immediate; the operetta's real conquest began in Berlin, and its Viennese canonisation came later.","This is a genuine potpourri, not an overture that argues anything — and that is a deliberate commercial decision, an eight-minute advertisement for the show. The silence before the waltz's final statement is the only structural device in the piece and does more work than any modulation. The sighing figure returns in the third act as a drunk jailer's lament, which is funnier if you have heard it here first.","Operetta was long treated as beneath serious criticism until Mahler programmed Die Fledermaus at the Vienna Hofoper in 1894 and gave it the standing of an opera. It is now a New Year's Eve fixture across the German-speaking world. The running arguments concern the spoken dialogue, how heavily to cut it, and the tradition of stuffing the second-act party with celebrity guest turns.","Count the tunes as they go past — there are more than you expect in eight minutes. Notice the silence before the big waltz returns. Then watch the whole operetta, ideally staged rather than recorded, and enjoy recognising where each fragment came from."],"johann-strauss-tales-vienna-woods":["Twelve minutes, the longest of his waltzes, and the only one with a folk instrument at its centre. A slow introduction — horns over rustling strings, evoking the wooded hills outside Vienna — leads not to a waltz but to a solo zither playing a Ländler, the heavy peasant dance the waltz descended from. The orchestra then takes over for five waltzes, and the zither returns alone before the coda closes the circle.","Written in 1868, at the height of Strauss's dominance of Viennese dance music, when the family firm ran several orchestras a night. The Wienerwald was where the city walked on Sundays, and the zither belonged to the Alpine countryside, not the ballroom; putting a peasant instrument into a formal orchestral waltz was a calculated piece of nostalgia for a Vienna that was rapidly rebuilding itself along the new Ringstrasse.","You can hear the waltz evolving inside a single piece: the Ländler is slower, heavier, and lands squarely on the beat, while the waltzes that follow are lighter and lean forward. The zither part needs a specialist and is often replaced by a solo violin or quietly dropped — an omission that removes the entire point of the composition.","Ödön von Horváth took the title for a bitter 1931 play about Viennese cruelty and sentimentality, and the piece has never quite escaped that reading since. It remains a New Year's Concert regular, where the introduction is sometimes cut for time. Purists insist on a real zither player; orchestras without one usually programme a different waltz.","Wait for the zither — about two minutes in — and notice how differently it makes the room feel. Compare its Ländler with the waltzes that follow to hear a dance being modernised in real time. Then set the piece beside the Blue Danube's introduction to see how Strauss varied the same trick."],"johann-strauss-emperor-waltz":["Eleven minutes that open in the wrong metre. Instead of a ballroom flourish there is a quiet march in duple time, over two minutes long, then a solo cello sings a bridge, and only then does the first waltz arrive. Four waltzes follow, several shaded towards the minor, and a long coda reviews them before a restrained close. The mood throughout is processional and valedictory rather than giddy.","Written in 1889 for the opening of a Berlin ballroom, at a moment of official Austro-German toasting after Franz Joseph's visit to Wilhelm II. Strauss's original title was 'Hand in Hand'; the publisher Fritz Simrock suggested 'Kaiser-Walzer' precisely because it names no emperor and could flatter either. Strauss was sixty-four; Vienna that January had absorbed the suicide of Crown Prince Rudolf at Mayerling, and the imperial mood was not festive.","The march introduction is the real subject: over two minutes of duple time before a waltz is allowed, which is a structural joke about ceremony delaying pleasure. The cello solo that bridges into the first waltz is the emotional centre of the piece and is easy to miss. Schoenberg arranged the whole waltz for a chamber group in 1925 — his way of arguing that this was serious composition.","Generally reckoned the most symphonic of his waltzes and the one that has best survived being taken seriously. Schoenberg's arrangement, made for his Society for Private Musical Performances, is the standard evidence in that argument and is now recorded almost as often as the orchestral original. The usual complaint is that the introduction outstays its welcome; conductors who cut it prove otherwise.","Give the introduction its full two minutes without impatience, and listen for the cello that ushers in the waltz. Then hear Schoenberg's chamber version, where you can follow every inner line. Play it directly after the Blue Danube to hear twenty-two years of difference in the same composer."],"johann-strauss-tritsch-tratsch-polka":["Under three minutes and over almost before it registers. A scampering tune of repeated notes is tossed between piccolo, violins and brass; a stamping middle section arrives with drum and cymbal; the opening returns and the whole thing bolts for the finish. There is no development and no contrast beyond changes of colour — the piece is a single idea played at increasing brightness until it stops.","Written in 1858, when Strauss in his early thirties was running the family dance business across Vienna, sending brothers to conduct at venues he could not reach himself. The polka had arrived from Bohemia in the 1830s and swept every ballroom in Europe. The title means chit-chat or gossip, and probably refers to a Viennese satirical paper of that name; the later story about his wife's chattering appears to be invention.","It is a 'polka schnell', the fastest of the three polka types the Strauss firm distinguished and marketed separately, and the tempo marking is the joke. The repeated-note figure literally imitates gossiping voices interrupting each other — the tune never lets a phrase finish. Listen for the piccolo, which carries the chatter above everything and is what makes the piece sound faster than it is.","Nobody has ever claimed it is important, and it is one of the most played orchestral pieces in the world — an encore, a cartoon soundtrack, a figure-skating standard. Musicologists use the Strauss polkas as evidence of how the family industrialised dance music into a publishing business. Conductors compete to take it faster than the woodwind can articulate, which defeats the point.","Follow the piccolo. Then notice that no phrase in the piece is ever allowed to complete itself. Three minutes is exactly right for an encore; hear it in a live New Year's Concert recording where the audience clapping along becomes part of the orchestration."],"johann-strauss-voices-spring":["Seven minutes with no introduction at all: the waltz simply launches, and the tune leaps and trills as though it were a bird rather than a dancer. Four waltz sections follow, the melodic lines full of rapid runs and wide jumps that are far more vocal than orchestral, and a whirling coda gathers them up. In its original form a coloratura soprano sings the entire first waltz.","Written in 1882 for the soprano Bianca Bianchi, who sang it at the Theater an der Wien the following spring, with words by Richard Genée. Vienna was then obsessed with vocal display, and Strauss — by now a composer of operettas as well as dances — was writing for singers rather than dancers. The piece flopped at home and became a hit in Russia, where he spent summers conducting at Pavlovsk.","It is the only one of his great waltzes conceived for a solo voice, and the absence of the customary slow introduction is a break with his own formula — a singer needs to enter while the audience is still settling. The leaps in the melody are written like a violin part, which is why coloraturas find it awkward and orchestras find it easy.","A Viennese failure and a Russian success, which says something about both cities in 1883. It is now sung by every coloratura and played every New Year, and there is an unresolved argument about whether the orchestral version, which is much more common, loses the joke entirely — the piece is a soprano imitating a bird imitating a waltz, and without the voice the imitation collapses into charm.","Hear the sung version first, with a singer willing to sound slightly ridiculous, then the orchestral one. Notice that the waltz starts immediately, with no throat-clearing. Compare with Delibes's Coppélia waltz for the same period's dance-hall lyricism handled by a theatre composer."],"brahms-symphony-no-4-first-movement":["Thirteen minutes that begin with no introduction whatever: the violins simply start sighing, a falling two-note figure repeated in a chain that keeps stepping downward. A fanfare-like second group brings horns and woodwind; the development turns the opening sigh into a whisper in the horns; and the recapitulation is disguised, the same notes stretched out over drum rolls so slowly that you are inside it before you notice. A hard, driving coda.","Composed over the summers of 1884 and 1885 at Mürzzuschlag in the Styrian Alps, where Brahms was fifty-two, reading Greek tragedy and copying out Bach cantatas. He feared the work was too austere for anyone and played it through on two pianos for his Viennese friends, who were baffled; Hanslick said it felt like being beaten by two extremely clever people. Brahms conducted the premiere with Bülow's Meiningen orchestra in October 1885.","Almost the entire movement is generated by the interval of a third. The opening sigh is a chain of falling thirds; invert it and you get the rising fanfare of the second group; compress it and you get the accompaniment figures. So the 'contrasting' theme is the first theme wearing a different coat, which is why the movement feels both endlessly varied and completely unified.","The two-piano run-through alarmed his own circle, and Wagnerians dismissed the symphony as academic. Its reputation was transformed by Schoenberg's essay 'Brahms the Progressive', which used exactly this thematic economy to claim him as a modernist ancestor. Live arguments concern the Meiningen tradition of flexible tempo, which Bülow taught and which most modern conductors decline, and whether to take the exposition repeat.","Hum the first two notes — that falling step is all you need to hold — and then count how many times the shape comes back, upside down, sped up, or hidden in the accompaniment. Listen for the recapitulation, which sneaks in slowed almost to a standstill. Then hear the passacaglia finale, which does the same trick with a bass line."],"brahms-hungarian-dance-no-5":["Under three minutes of swagger. A minor-key tune with a snapped, dotted rhythm struts in, stops dead, and starts again; a faster wheeling section follows; and the tempo lurches without warning throughout, so a conductor has to shape the piece by instinct rather than by the beat. Written for piano duet and best known in orchestral dress, it is all attitude and abrupt gear changes, with no development at all.","Brahms toured northern Germany in 1853 as accompanist to the Hungarian violinist Ede Reményi, a political refugee from the failed revolution of 1848, and absorbed the café repertoire of Romani bands. Sixteen years later he published two books of these dances without an opus number, calling himself the arranger rather than the composer — a caution that turned out to be prudent. They earned him more money than anything else he wrote.","Number five is not a folk tune. It is a published csárdás by the Hungarian bandmaster Kéler Béla, which Brahms took for anonymous folk material; the mistake produced a plagiarism row that his 'arranger' billing survived. The orchestral version most people know is by Albert Parlow, not Brahms — he orchestrated only three of the twenty-one — and it shifts the dance up a semitone from the original.","The plagiarism dispute did no lasting damage but has shaped how the dances are catalogued. The deeper argument is stylistic: this is urban café music played by professional Romani bands, not Magyar peasant song, as Bartók and Kodály demonstrated forty years later with phonographs and field notebooks. It remains one of the most performed pieces of classical music in the world, in every conceivable arrangement.","Hear the piano-duet original first — it is spikier and the sudden stops are more shocking with two players than with sixty. Then listen to a Hungarian band play a real csárdás and hear both the resemblance and the distance. Then Bartók's Romanian Folk Dances, for what happened when someone went and collected the actual songs."],"brahms-german-requiem-wie-lieblich":["Five minutes at the still centre of a seventy-minute work. Strings and woodwind set up a gently swaying triple pulse, and the sopranos enter with a rising phrase on 'Wie lieblich sind deine Wohnungen' — how lovely are thy dwelling places. Two short bursts of faster fugal writing lift the texture on 'Meine Seele verlanget', and both times the music sinks back to the opening sway. It ends where it began, without a climax.","Brahms assembled his own text from Luther's German Bible rather than using the Latin Mass for the Dead: no Day of Judgement, no mention of Christ, nothing about the fate of souls. The work grew across the years around his mother's death in 1865 and the long shadow of Schumann's collapse and death. A six-movement version performed in Bremen Cathedral on Good Friday 1868 made his reputation at thirty-four.","This movement sits at the exact centre of a symmetrical arch of seven, and it is the only one from which the trombones and timpani — which dominate the great outer movements — are entirely absent, so the orchestral colour itself marks the still point. The whole Requiem also begins and ends with the same word, 'selig', blessed: it is addressed to the living, not the dead.","The Bremen performance turned him from a promising pianist-composer into a national figure. Clergy objected to a requiem with no redeemer in it; Brahms replied that he would gladly have called it 'A Human Requiem'. Performance debates now centre on whether to sing it in English translation, and on chorus size — the nineteenth-century massed choirs against modern forces of sixty.","Start here rather than at the beginning: it is the easiest door into a long work. Feel the gentle three-beat rocking that never stops, and notice the two moments the voices try to break out of it. Then hear the whole Requiem in one sitting, with a large chorus and a good bass section."],"brahms-intermezzo-op-118-no-2":["Just under six minutes of quiet A major. The melody is not on top: the right hand plays a top voice and an inner voice at once, and the tune keeps slipping into the middle of the texture, so the piece sounds folded in on itself. A middle section in F sharp minor grows more urgent, with a chorale-like phrase in the bass, before the opening returns and the music closes almost inaudibly.","Written at Bad Ischl in 1893, when Brahms was sixty and had already announced that he had finished composing. He wrote four sets of short piano pieces instead, of which this is the second of six. The set was sent to Clara Schumann, by then in her seventies, ill, and barely able to play. These are pieces for a domestic room and a private listener, not a concert hall.","In several passages the left hand echoes the right a beat later, so the melody is quietly a canon with itself. The opening rising third is the same interval that generates his Fourth Symphony's argument — the late Brahms tends to build everything from thirds. And the middle section's bass chorale is marked to be played almost without pedal, which is why it sounds like a different instrument.","The most recorded of all his piano pieces, and heavily used in film, which has given it a reputation for gentleness it does not entirely deserve. Scholars read Opp. 116 to 119 as a late private diary. Pianists argue about the middle section: how much rubato it can take, and whether it may ever become loud — Brahms's dynamic markings say no, and most players disagree.","Listen twice. Once following only the top line, once hunting the inner voice underneath it; the piece changes shape entirely. Then hear the F sharp minor middle section for the chorale hidden in the bass. Any thoughtful pianist will do — this is not music that rewards a big tone."],"brahms-symphony-no-1-finale":["Seventeen minutes that begin in the dark. A slow C minor introduction gathers weight, the strings plucking faster and faster into a crisis — and then a horn rings out a long, open call over shimmering strings, answered by flute, as if a window had opened. Trombones, silent for three whole movements, intone a chorale. The Allegro then breaks out with a broad striding C major tune, and the coda brings the trombone chorale back at full blast.","Twenty-one years separated the first sketches from the premiere. Schumann had announced the twenty-year-old Brahms in print as the coming man in 1853, and the weight of that, plus Beethoven's Ninth, paralysed him for two decades — he told a friend one had no idea how it felt to hear a giant's footsteps behind one. He finished it in 1876 and it was first played at Karlsruhe that November.","The horn call is a real alphorn tune he heard in the Swiss mountains: he wrote it out on a birthday card for Clara Schumann in 1868, with words about a greeting sent across the valley, eight years before the symphony was finished. The trombones do not play a single note until this movement, which is why their chorale registers as an arrival rather than a colour.","Bülow's quip that it was 'Beethoven's Tenth' stuck, flattered nobody and irritated Brahms, who snapped that any fool could hear the resemblance between his big tune and the Ode to Joy. Wagnerians called the symphony academic; the Brahms camp called it the rescue of absolute music. Current disputes are about tempo relations in the introduction and whether period instruments thin the chorale too much.","Start about four minutes in, at the horn call: it is one of the great openings-out in music and needs no preparation. Then go back for the dark introduction, which now makes sense. Compare it with the finale of Beethoven's Ninth and judge the family resemblance yourself."],"brahms-symphony-no-3-poco-allegretto":["Six minutes where a scherzo should be. The cellos sing a curling, falling melody in C minor over a gently rocking accompaniment; the violins take it up; a lighter major-key middle section offers a brief lift. Then the horn plays the tune back, and later the oboe — the same notes, three different colours, each version sadder for being familiar. Nothing is developed and nothing is resolved; the movement simply stops.","Written in a single summer at Wiesbaden in 1883, when Brahms was fifty and in the company of the young contralto Hermine Spies. Richter dubbed the symphony Brahms's 'Eroica', which the composer thought absurd. Wagner had died that February, and at the Vienna premiere in December the Wagner faction hissed — a partisan war conducted in a concert hall while both leaders were dead or dying.","The melody keeps essentially the same outline in every appearance but is reharmonised underneath each time, so what changes is not the tune but the ground it stands on. When the horn takes it over, the strings play a different rhythm from the one the cellos had, which is why the return feels unstable. And the movement is in C minor inside an F major symphony — a key it has no business being in.","Extracted, arranged and sentimentalised: Sinatra sang it as 'Take My Love', and it carried the film of Sagan's Aimez-vous Brahms?, associations that some scholars think have coated the movement in sugar. The symphony's quiet ending was thought anticlimactic in 1883 and is now considered its finest stroke. Period-instrument readings take the movement faster than the tradition and make it less of a lament.","Follow the cellos on the first statement, then track the tune to the violins, then to the horn, and notice how it darkens each time. Six minutes is enough to hear twice. Then play the symphony's last two minutes, which fade out rather than finish, and see the two ideas as one."],"brahms-violin-concerto-adagio":["Nine minutes that start without the soloist. A wind band — oboe carrying the tune over bassoons, horns and clarinets — plays a complete, self-sufficient melody, more like an open-air serenade than a symphonic slow movement. The violin finally enters, but not with that tune: it decorates and meditates around it. A darker central section clouds over, and only late does the soloist sing the oboe's melody outright, ornamented and an octave up.","Composed in the summer of 1878 at Pörtschach on the Wörthersee, in close consultation with Joseph Joachim, the greatest violinist of the age and Brahms's friend since 1853; Joachim advised on the writing and supplied the first-movement cadenza still most often played. The premiere was at the Leipzig Gewandhaus on New Year's Day 1879, Joachim playing, Brahms conducting. The friendship later ruptured when Brahms took the side of Joachim's wife in their divorce.","The soloist is denied the main theme for most of the movement, which is the structural joke and the source of the famous complaint about it. The opening scoring is deliberately Harmoniemusik — the wind-band music of eighteenth-century Austrian gardens — dropped into a Romantic concerto. And Brahms wrote no cadenza of his own for the first movement, which is why different violinists still play different music at that point.","Sarasate refused to perform a concerto in which he had to stand on stage holding his violin while the oboe played the only decent tune. Bülow called it a concerto written against the violin rather than for it. Both jibes stuck and both were wrong: it is now a cornerstone of the repertoire. The choice of cadenza — Joachim's, Kreisler's, Busoni's or a modern one — remains a real interpretive decision.","Let the oboe finish its whole melody before the violin arrives, and hear how complete it already is. Then wait for the moment the soloist finally takes it. Compare a recording in the Joachim line of descent with a modern one; the amount of portamento tells you a century of history."],"brahms-piano-concerto-no-2-andante":["Twelve minutes in which the soloist is not the soloist. The orchestra's principal cello sings the long opening melody alone; the piano enters underneath, as accompanist and commentator, and spends much of the movement playing inner figuration rather than tunes. A hushed middle section moves to a remote key, two clarinets murmuring over trembling strings while the piano answers in far-off high notes. The cello returns to finish what it started.","Brahms was forty-eight and had just come back from Italy. This is a concerto in four movements — he added a scherzo, which no one had done — running nearly fifty minutes, and he described it to Elisabet von Herzogenberg as a tiny little piano concerto with a tiny little wisp of a scherzo. It is dedicated to Eduard Marxsen, his first teacher in Hamburg, and he gave the premiere himself in Budapest in November 1881.","The cello melody reappears four years later as the tune of his song 'Immer leiser wird mein Schlummer' — the concerto's most famous moment was, or became, a song about dying. The two clarinets over shivering strings in the middle section is a texture he returns to obsessively in old age, and it turns up again in the Clarinet Quintet a decade later.","Early pianists found it unplayable and unrewarding — enormous difficulty with no display in return. Orchestral principal cellists now treat the solo as a career test, and there is a genuine argument about whose movement it is; some recordings credit the cellist on the sleeve. Brahms's own performances of the piece, by contemporary accounts, were powerful and technically rough.","Listen to the cello's opening melody without waiting for the piano, then hear the song it later became. Track how seldom the pianist gets the tune. Then compare a recording where the cellist plays it as chamber music with one where the conductor treats it as an orchestral solo."],"brahms-wiegenlied-lullaby":["Two minutes, two verses, voice and piano. The piano rocks in a steady gentle pattern and the voice enters slightly off the beat, so the melody always floats just behind the cradle's motion and never quite lands with it. The words are folk texts — the first verse from Des Knaben Wunderhorn, the second from a later collection — and the second verse repeats the music of the first almost exactly.","Written in 1868 for Bertha Faber, an old friend from the women's choir Brahms had directed in Hamburg, to congratulate her on the birth of her second son. He sent it with instructions that she should sing it to the baby. It was published as the last of five songs, and became, almost immediately and against his intentions, the most widely known melody he ever wrote.","There is a private joke inside it. The piano's inner voice quotes a Viennese Ländler that Bertha used to sing to Brahms years earlier, when they were both young and he was interested in her — so the lullaby for her child is built over a love song she once sang to him. The rhythmic disagreement between voice and accompaniment is deliberate and disappears in most arrangements.","Nursery culture, music boxes and greetings cards have detached it from everything that makes it interesting, including the words, which in the second verse concern being carried away to paradise. Recital singers restoring the original scoring and the full text tend to surprise audiences. Scholars treat it as a case study in how a domestic gift becomes global property with no royalties attached.","Hear a real singer with a real pianist, not a music box, and listen for the accompaniment's inner line — the borrowed Ländler. Notice how the voice keeps arriving late. Two minutes; play it three times. Then read the second verse's words and hear the song differently."],"brahms-clarinet-quintet-adagio":["Eleven minutes for clarinet and string quartet. The strings put on mutes and the clarinet glides above them in a slow B major song, everything veiled. Then the mutes come off, the strings begin to shake with tremolo, and the clarinet breaks into a free, wailing, improvisatory lament full of swoops and turns, like the leader of a Hungarian band. The veiled song returns, quieter than before, and the movement ends almost without weight.","Brahms had made a will in 1890 and told friends he was finished. In March 1891 he heard Richard Mühlfeld, principal clarinet of the Meiningen orchestra, play Weber and Mozart, and came out of retirement to write four clarinet works for him — he called Mühlfeld 'Fräulein Klarinette' for his soft tone. The quintet was written that summer at Bad Ischl and premiered in Berlin in December 1891.","The middle section's wailing is Hungarian-Romani style, written out in full rather than left to the player's invention — the same music he had loved since touring with Reményi in 1853, now notated bar by bar. The mutes are the movement's structural device: they stay on for the outer sections and come off for the middle, which is why the tremolo sounds raw rather than merely loud.","Recognised at the premiere as farewell music, and the origin of the entire 'autumnal Brahms' vocabulary that critics have used ever since. Performance arguments concern vibrato — Mühlfeld's generation used little — and whether a German-system clarinet's darker tone is essential. Some players make the middle section a gypsy showpiece; others keep it inside the movement's grief, and the difference is substantial.","Wait for the strings' tremolo about four minutes in; that is the moment the piece opens up. Listen for the mutes going on and off. Then compare a German clarinet with a French one on the same passage — the darker instrument makes it a lament, the brighter one a song."],"brahms-academic-festival-overture":["Ten minutes that begin conspiratorially — a quiet, marching C minor idea, muttered rather than announced — and then unpack a chain of German student drinking songs fitted into sonata shape. Brass intone one as a solemn hymn; bassoons snort another, the song sung at freshmen; strings carry a third. Finally the whole orchestra, with cymbals, triangle and bass drum that appear in no other Brahms overture, blazes out 'Gaudeamus igitur' in C major.","The University of Breslau awarded Brahms an honorary doctorate in 1879. He sent a thank-you postcard, was informed that a musical thank-you was expected, and produced this the following summer at Bad Ischl — alongside the grim Tragic Overture, remarking that one of them weeps and the other laughs. He conducted the premiere himself in Breslau in January 1881, in front of the faculty that had honoured him.","All four tunes are genuine student songs with real histories: 'Wir hatten gebauet ein stattliches Haus' belonged to the nationalist student fraternities that the German states had banned, so the overture smuggles suppressed political songs into an academic ceremony. The bassoons' theme is the hazing song sung at first-year students. And the enormous percussion battery is Brahms deliberately overdressing for the occasion.","The Breslau faculty seem to have expected something more dignified than a beer-hall medley. Critics have called it a potpourri, which it is; conductors love it as a concert opener, which it also is. The most useful modern practice is programming it with the Tragic Overture, its twin from the same summer, so that the joke and the grief explain each other.","Wait for 'Gaudeamus igitur' at the end, which everyone recognises, and then go back and hunt the earlier tunes, each of which returns. Listen for the bassoons about halfway through. Then play the Tragic Overture straight after and hear what Brahms was doing with his other hand that summer."],"borodin-polovtsian-dances":["Thirteen minutes of linked dances played without a break. First the captive maidens: a soft, swaying chorus over oboe and cor anglais, the melody curling back on itself. Then a savage men's dance with hammering timpani, blaring brass and stamping accents; a lighter, skittering dance for boys; and a final whirl that stacks all the earlier tunes on top of one another over relentless drums. There is a version without chorus; it loses a great deal.","Borodin was a full-time professor of chemistry at the Medico-Surgical Academy in St Petersburg and could compose only when illness excused him from teaching. He worked on his opera Prince Igor for eighteen years, drawn to the medieval Igor Tale because it set Russians against Central Asian 'Polovtsians' and licensed the orientalism the Mighty Five prized. He died in 1887 with the opera unfinished; it reached the stage in 1890.","The languorous maidens' tune became the Broadway song 'Stranger in Paradise', which is how most of the English-speaking world first heard it. The orchestration is substantially Rimsky-Korsakov's, completed after Borodin's death, so the brilliance you hear is not certainly the composer's. And the 'Polovtsian' idiom is invented — the scales and drum patterns are constructed exoticism, not transcription.","A hit from 1890 onward, and the concert suite quickly eclipsed the opera that contains it. Musicologists now read the dances through the history of Russian imperial expansion into Central Asia, which complicates their appeal without diminishing it. Editions differ over how much material is Borodin's and how much Rimsky-Korsakov's and Glazunov's; no performance is free of that question.","Insist on a version with chorus — the maidens' opening is written for voices and the orchestral arrangement flattens it. Listen for how each dance keeps its own drum pattern, then hear all of them collide at the end. Then find the Kismet number for the shock of recognition."],"borodin-steppes-central-asia":["Seven minutes and essentially two tunes. It opens on a single high violin note held over silence — the empty air of the steppe. A clarinet sings a plain Russian melody; plucked strings walk in underneath like hooves approaching; a cor anglais answers with a curling Eastern tune. The two melodies circle each other, and the climax is not a loud one: it is the moment they are finally played together, fitting exactly. Then the held note returns and the music evaporates.","Written in 1880 for a series of tableaux vivants planned to mark the silver jubilee of Alexander II's reign. The tableaux never happened, but the piece survived. Russia had recently conquered the Central Asian khanates, and the programme Borodin printed in the score — a Russian caravan crossing the desert under Russian protection, natives and settlers travelling in peace — is imperial self-congratulation as much as landscape painting. He dedicated the score to Liszt.","The whole piece hangs from one sustained high violin note that opens and closes it, so the music appears out of nothing and returns there. The caravan is depicted by plucked strings alone, nothing else. And the counterpoint at the centre is the composer's actual argument: he was a chemist, and he wanted to show two substances combining rather than one conquering the other.","An immediate success, the most played of his concert works in his lifetime, and Liszt accepted the dedication warmly. Modern commentary treats the 'peaceful empire' programme sceptically and reads the piece as a document of Russian colonial ideology. Musically it is sometimes dismissed as a picture postcard, which underrates how much is achieved with two themes and one held note.","Listen for the high violin note at the very beginning and the very end — the piece is framed by it. Then wait for the moment the Russian and Eastern tunes overlap; that is the whole design. Compare it with the Polovtsian Dances to hear the same composer's exoticism at opposite volumes."],"borodin-string-quartet-no-2-nocturne":["Eight minutes for four strings. The cello, playing high where it sounds like a tenor voice, sings a long curving melody over gently rocking inner parts; the first violin answers it a register above; then the two chase each other in strict canon, finishing each other's phrases. A more agitated central passage darkens things briefly. At the end cello and violin sing the tune together, and the movement fades out on plucked chords.","Written in a single summer at Zhitovo in 1881, unusually fast for a man who normally took years — chemistry lectures, laboratory work and his campaign for women's medical education left him almost no time. Family tradition holds that the quartet was a present for his wife Ekaterina, marking twenty years since they met in Heidelberg, where he had gone as a young chemist and she as a pianist convalescing.","Borodin played the cello himself, and gives the cello the melody up in a register where the instrument stops sounding like a bass and starts sounding like a singer. The canon between violin and cello is strict for several bars, which is why the lines seem to complete each other's sentences rather than merely alternate. The tune later became the song 'And This Is My Beloved'.","The most played movement of any Russian quartet, and often arranged for string orchestra, which thickens what was written for four players and loses the intimacy of the canon. The anniversary story rests on family testimony rather than documents, and scholars treat it cautiously. Quartets disagree over how much portamento the cello's opening line can bear before it becomes salon music.","Follow the cello's opening phrase all the way to its end, then hear the violin repeat it. Once the canon starts, try to hold both lines at once. Then listen to a string-orchestra arrangement to hear exactly what four players give you that forty cannot."],"borodin-prince-igor-overture":["Eleven minutes assembled from the opera's principal themes. A slow, brooding introduction with a rising phrase in the low strings; then a fast section built from the material of Igor's music, with a lyrical horn tune lifted from his great second-act aria, and darker Polovtsian colours cutting across it. A broad climax gathers the themes together and a hurtling coda closes it. It sounds like an overture in the German manner, which the opera behind it is not.","Borodin collapsed and died at a fancy-dress ball in February 1887, leaving Prince Igor in pieces — some numbers finished, others sketched, much unorchestrated. Rimsky-Korsakov and his pupil Alexander Glazunov assembled a performable score over the following three years. Glazunov's account is that he wrote this overture down from memory of hearing Borodin play it through at the piano, supplying whatever he could not recall.","No autograph of the overture in Borodin's hand is known, so the proportion that is his and the proportion that is Glazunov's twenty-five-year-old memory cannot be established. The horn's love theme comes from Igor's Act II aria, which is why it feels like a recollection rather than an announcement. Some recent productions drop the overture altogether and begin with the prologue.","Audiences have always liked it and orchestras programme it as a curtain-raiser. Scholars treat it as a document of Glazunov's memory rather than of Borodin's intentions, and productions since the 1990s that return to Borodin's surviving manuscripts have reopened the question of whether it belongs to the opera at all. Nobody disputes that it is effective; the dispute is over whose effectiveness it is.","Listen for the horn's broad love theme in the middle, then find Igor's second-act aria and hear where it came from. Notice how German the construction is compared with the rest of the opera. A Russian orchestra's brass changes the character of the climax considerably."],"locke-the-tempest":["Locke's score for the 1674 operatic remake of Shakespeare's play is a suite of short instrumental numbers for strings and continuo, about fifteen minutes end to end: an Introduction, a Galliard, a Gavot, a Saraband, a jaunty Lilk, and the celebrated Curtain Tune. That last piece is a storm in miniature, the same music played louder, then softer, then violent, with the dynamics written into the parts, so the band swells and recedes like weather crossing a stage.","The Restoration theatre had reopened after the Commonwealth ban and wanted spectacle: flying machines, painted waves, singing devils. Thomas Shadwell's Tempest, adapted from an earlier Dryden and Davenant version, was the hit of 1674 at the Dorset Garden playhouse. Locke, composer in ordinary to Charles II and the senior English theatre musician of his generation, supplied the instrumental music while others wrote the songs. He was in his early fifties and died three years later.","The Curtain Tune carries instructions written into the parts, among them soft, lowder by degrees and violent, some of the earliest graded dynamics in English music, at a time when loudness was normally left to a player's instinct. The 1674 production was a shared job: Locke wrote the instrumental music, Pelham Humfrey and John Banister the vocal numbers, and Giovanni Battista Draghi the dances, so the printed Tempest music is really a committee.","Locke's theatre music was overshadowed within twenty years by Purcell's semi-operas and then forgotten for two centuries; nineteenth-century historians treated him as a rough provincial who came before the real thing. Twentieth-century editors and early-music groups restored him as the sharpest English composer between the viol consort and Purcell. Scholars still argue over exactly which numbers in the surviving print are his, since the show's music was pooled.","Start with the Curtain Tune and play it twice: once for the storm, once to follow the written swells. Then hear how much of Purcell's theatre manner is already here, the abrupt harmonic sidesteps, the dance rhythms that lurch. A one-to-a-part string band on gut strings gives the grit; large modern-orchestra recordings smooth away the roughness that is the point."],"locke-consort-of-fower-parts":["Six four-movement suites for a consort of viols, each running Fantazia, Courante, Ayre, Saraband, and each in a different key; the whole set lasts about three quarters of an hour. The fantazias are the substance: dense imitative writing for four equal lines, with sudden harsh dissonances that scrape and then resolve. The dances that follow are short, plain and consoling by comparison, the argument settled, the room quiet again.","By 1660 the viol consort was the music of an England that had just ended. Chamber fantasias belonged to private houses and to the Commonwealth years, when the theatres and cathedral choirs were shut and serious music retreated indoors. Locke wrote his set as Charles II returned with French tastes, a twenty-four-violin band and a preference for tunes you could dance to. These suites are among the last great English consort music, written for a private circle rather than a court.","Listen for the false relations, one player holding a note a semitone away from another's, a sour English speciality that Locke uses more aggressively than anyone before him. He also shapes each suite so that ideas from the fantazia return, transformed, in the dances, which is why the sets feel argued rather than assembled. The autograph survives in Locke's own hand, a rarity for English chamber music of the period.","Charles Burney, writing a century later, found Locke crude and unmelodic, and that verdict held into the twentieth century. The revival of the viol consort after 1950 turned the same roughness into a virtue: what looked like incompetence is now heard as deliberate friction. Performers still debate the instrumentation, since the parts suit violins and viols equally well, and whether an organ should double the lines.","Go straight to the fantazias and ignore the dances the first time through. Listen for the moment a line enters against a note it clashes with; Locke never softens it. Compare with Purcell's viol fantazias of 1680, written by a young man looking back at exactly this tradition. Seek a one-to-a-part viol group; the sound should be reedy and slightly astringent, not plush."],"locke-sagbutts-and-cornetts":["A handful of short movements in five parts for the old loud consort: cornetts, wooden treble instruments with finger holes played through a small cup mouthpiece, and sackbuts, the ancestor of the trombone. It runs under ten minutes, mostly in a solemn processional tread with two brisker sections. The sound is unlike anything else in English music of the day, bright and buzzing and faintly rough, closer to a cathedral organ than to a modern brass quintet.","Charles II was crowned in Westminster Abbey in April 1661, and the procession through London the day before needed music audible outdoors over crowds. The King's wind players, the sagbutts and cornetts, were an ensemble already going out of fashion, and this was effectively their last great occasion. Locke, newly appointed composer in ordinary, wrote for them knowing that the King's twenty-four violins were about to take over ceremonial duty at court.","This is close to the last music written in England for cornett and sackbut before the instruments fell out of use for two centuries. The writing keeps the five parts tightly spaced rather than spread out, so the chords sound as one thick column of tone, which carries in the open air where clarity of individual line would have been lost anyway. Nothing in it is decorative; every note is there to be heard from a distance.","The music was effectively unknown until the twentieth century, when brass players looking for repertoire beyond Gabrieli found it. It now appears constantly on early brass programmes, sometimes on modern trombones, which flattens the character considerably. Historians point out that calling it coronation music is a modern label of convenience; the source tells us who played it, not what occasion it served.","Under ten minutes, so hear it whole, then put a Gabrieli canzona beside it: the Venetian sound is bright and antiphonal, Locke's darker and more compact. Look for a recording on period cornetts, an instrument played like a trumpet but sounding almost like a voice. Modern brass versions are grander and much less strange, which loses the reason to hear it."],"humfrey-by-the-waters-of-babylon":["A verse anthem of about eight minutes on Psalm 137, the song of the Jewish exiles in Babylon, for three low solo voices over a continuo of organ and bass viol. There is no chorus and almost no display. The voices move in close, aching suspensions, one entering underneath another on the word wept; the line about hanging harps upon the willows slows almost to a standstill before the final plea.","Humfrey was a chorister of the restored Chapel Royal, sent abroad in his teens at Charles II's expense, probably to France and Italy, and back at twenty with continental manners and, according to Pepys, an unbearable opinion of himself. Restoration church music had to be built again from nothing: the old cathedral repertory was scattered, the King wanted string bands and beat time with his hand, and young men like Humfrey supplied a devotional style shaped by French recitative.","The word-setting is French in method and English in feeling. Humfrey stretches single syllables across falling lines so the sentence sounds spoken rather than sung, a trick learned from Lully's recitative. Listen for how often two of the three voices land a semitone apart and hold there before one gives way; that friction, not melody, is what carries the grief. Purcell, a boy chorister under Humfrey, took the whole procedure over.","Humfrey died at twenty-six, and posterity has mostly filed him as Purcell's teacher rather than a composer in his own right. Burney thought the music thin; modern scholars hear instead the first English composer to absorb French declamation whole. Performance questions stay open, notably whether the continuo should be organ alone or with a bass viol, and how freely singers may bend the rhythm, since the notation implies speech.","Eight minutes, three men and an organ: hear it once for the sound of the words, then again following one voice only. Compare it with Purcell's Hear my prayer, O Lord to see what the pupil did with the teacher's dissonance. Look for a small English cathedral-trained group; big choirs and heavy reverberation blur the very suspensions that make the piece work."],"blow-venus-and-adonis":["A masque in a prologue and three short acts, under an hour, sung throughout with no spoken dialogue. Venus and Adonis are lovers; Cupid drills a chorus of little Cupids in spelling; Adonis goes hunting and is gored by a boar; he dies in Venus's arms and the chorus mourns. The forces are small, strings and continuo with a handful of soloists, and the manner is conversational, closer to a play sung at every moment than to grand opera.","Blow wrote it for Charles II's private entertainment, probably around 1683. Venus was sung by Mary Davis, one of the King's mistresses, and Cupid by her daughter by him. England had no opera tradition: the public theatre preferred plays with songs, and continental opera was thought foreign and ruinously expensive. A court masque, performed indoors before an audience who knew the singers personally, was where an English through-composed drama could first happen at all.","The Cupids' spelling lesson is comic on the surface, small voices chanting letters, but the word they spell is mercenary, a joke aimed squarely at the court watching. Listen also to the closing chorus: it mourns over a short bass line repeated again and again, the ground-bass lament Purcell would take up for Dido six years later. And Venus gets no grand death scene; the piece simply stops with the chorus, which is the boldest thing in it.","Venus and Adonis vanished with the court that watched it and was not staged again until 1920. It is now routinely called the first English opera, which annoys scholars who point out that it calls itself a masque and lasts less than an hour. The livelier argument is about Purcell: how much of Dido and Aeneas is modelled on it, and whether Blow's flatter, plainer manner is a limitation or a deliberate refusal of Italian display.","Under an hour, so hear the whole thing, then go immediately to Dido's Lament and listen for what Purcell learned. Notice how little the music decorates: the vocal lines follow English speech rhythm so closely that you can follow every word without a text. A period-instrument recording with light, small-scale voices suits it; grand operatic singing makes the piece sound like a fragment of something bigger."],"blow-ode-on-the-death-of-purcell":["Dryden's ode Mark how the lark and linnet sing, set for two countertenors, two recorders and continuo, running about a quarter of an hour. The two high male voices twine round each other in long chains of suspensions, imitating a beat apart, while the recorders answer in the gaps. There is no chorus and no orchestra: the scale is chamber-sized, which makes the grief sound private rather than ceremonial.","Purcell died in November 1695, aged thirty-six. Blow had taught him as a chorister, given up the Westminster Abbey organ post to him in 1679, and took it back after his death. Dryden, England's greatest living poet and Purcell's collaborator in the theatre, wrote the memorial verses; Blow published the setting in 1696. London's musical world was small enough that the loss was personal to everyone in it, and the printed ode was a public act of mourning.","Blow writes in Purcell's own manner and does it deliberately: the falling chromatic bass, the harsh clashes between the two voices, the sudden literal painting of single words are all things his pupil had made famous. Listen for the passages where the two countertenors cross, so the lower voice rises above the higher, an old madrigal device that here sounds like two people talking over each other in distress.","Eighteenth-century writers admired the ode as an act of piety and rarely performed it; the two-countertenor scoring made it awkward for choirs. The countertenor revival after Alfred Deller changed that, and it is now among Blow's most recorded works. Critics divide over the ending, heard by some as resigned consolation and by others as abrupt, and over how much rhythmic freedom singers should take in the recitative-like stretches.","Follow one voice all the way through, then listen again for the other. The recorders are the piece's colour: they enter where the words stop, as if finishing sentences. Compare Purcell's own funeral music for Queen Mary, written the year before his death and played at his own funeral. Two well-matched countertenors with a single theorbo or organ is the ideal; larger forces make it a ceremony instead of a conversation."],"purcell-dido-and-aeneas":["Three short acts, about an hour: Dido, Queen of Carthage, loves the Trojan Aeneas; a coven of witches conjures a false messenger who orders him to leave; he goes, and she dies. Purcell writes almost entirely in short numbers, dances, choruses and arioso, with the chorus commenting after nearly every scene. Everything converges on the last three minutes, When I am laid in earth, sung over a bass line that sinks by semitones and repeats while the voice refuses to follow it down.","The first documented performance was at Josias Priest's boarding school for girls in Chelsea, probably in 1689, the year William and Mary took the throne. Nahum Tate's libretto compresses Virgil's Aeneid into something a school could stage in an evening. Whether Purcell wrote it for the school or for the court first is unresolved; the earliest surviving score dates from decades after his death, and a prologue Tate wrote is lost, music and all.","The witches are the giveaway that this is English theatre and not Italian opera: they cackle in choruses out of the London stage's masque tradition rather than anything continental. The libretto also lists dances, among them a guitar ground, whose music does not survive, so performances borrow or improvise them. And the lament is prepared far in advance: descending chromatic lines shadow Dido from her first aria, so the ending is the shape she has been trapped in throughout.","Neglected for two centuries and revived in 1895 at the Royal College of Music, Dido became the founding exhibit for the idea of a continuous English musical tradition. Scholars now argue that the main surviving manuscript is a later, cut adaptation, and that the opera we hear is missing music. Performance practice swings between grand contralto Didos in the Janet Baker manner and light period-instrument casts; the piece survives both treatments.","Start at the end, with When I am laid in earth, then go back and hear how the whole hour leans towards it. Notice that the chorus behaves like a Greek chorus, closing each scene with a short comment. Two very different recordings repay hearing side by side: a large-voiced Dido with modern strings, and a period band at a brisk hour. The second makes it a play, the first a tragedy."],"purcell-fairy-queen":["A semi-opera: Shakespeare's A Midsummer Night's Dream is spoken by actors, and after each act the music takes over for a self-contained masque on drunkenness, night and sleep, the four seasons, and a mock-Chinese garden. Purcell wrote about two and a half hours of it, none of it sung by the play's characters. Among the high points are a stammering drunken poet, the Plaint O let me weep for soprano and solo violin, and a finale with trumpets and drums.","The Dorset Garden Theatre in London staged it in 1692 with machinery, dancing monkeys and a garden that rose through the stage floor; it reportedly cost so much that the company struggled to profit despite full houses. English audiences would not accept characters who sang their dialogue, so the compromise was the semi-opera: a spoken play plus lavish musical interludes. Purcell was thirty-two, at his peak, three years from death, and writing for the best band in London.","Nobody in Shakespeare's play sings a note; the singers are spirits, allegorical figures and hired entertainers, so the music runs beside the drama rather than through it. The Night masque is the finest stretch, with muted violins and the strings marked to play as softly as possible for characters named Night, Mystery, Secrecy and Sleep. The score was lost after Purcell's death and rediscovered only in 1901, which makes the work a modern recovery rather than a survival.","The Fairy Queen is the problem piece of English theatre music: nobody quite knows how to stage it now, since the Shakespeare takes three hours and the music another two and a half. Most performances cut heavily, and some drop the play entirely and present the masques as a concert sequence. Purists object that this destroys the design; audiences generally prefer it. Since the 1901 rediscovery the music itself has never been in doubt.","Do not start at the beginning. Go to the Act II night masque, from See, even Night herself is here through to Hush, no more, twenty minutes of the quietest music Purcell wrote. Then the Plaint, a soprano over a solo violin and a repeating bass. Period-instrument recordings that take the dances fast serve it best, and a staged video helps make sense of the structure."],"jeremiah-clarke-prince-of-denmarks-march":["A rondeau in D major lasting about three minutes: a bright eight-bar tune returns three times with contrasting episodes between. Clarke wrote it for harpsichord, where it is a modest keyboard trifle, and almost nobody hears it that way. In its universal form a solo trumpet takes the tune over full organ, the notes fitting the natural trumpet's open harmonics so exactly that the instrument seems to have written the piece itself.","Clarke was organist of St Paul's Cathedral and worked in a London where a good tune circulated in print within weeks of being played. The march probably honours Prince George of Denmark, Queen Anne's consort, and Clarke reused it in other arrangements of his own. He shot himself in 1707, reportedly over a hopeless love affair, and was buried in the crypt of the cathedral where he had played.","For most of the twentieth century this was printed as Purcell's Trumpet Voluntary, an attribution that came from a nineteenth-century edition and stuck fast; the correction to Clarke was only widely accepted after the 1950s. The tune works on a valveless trumpet because every note lies within the natural harmonic series of the tube, with no valves and no cross-fingering, which is exactly why it sounds so open and unforced.","Being a wedding standard has cost the piece its critical standing: it is heard as furniture rather than as music. Scholars who have examined Clarke's fuller output, the anthems, harpsichord suites and an ode on Purcell's death, argue that he was a genuinely inventive minor master flattened by one famous tune. Performance debate is mostly practical: natural trumpet or modern, organ or full orchestra, and whether the tempo is a walk or a stride.","Hear it once on harpsichord alone, as written, to find out how slight it is, then once with natural trumpet and organ to hear what arrangement can do to three minutes of music. Notice the episodes between returns of the tune; they are the only place anything unexpected happens. Compare Purcell's own Trumpet Tune and Air, still often bundled with it on the same recordings."],"croft-st-anne":["A hymn tune of four short phrases, sixteen bars in all, that takes about a minute to sing through once and is normally repeated for several verses. The first line rises and falls in even steps with no ornament whatever; the second answers it lower; the last climbs to the highest note on the strongest word. Sung to Isaac Watts's paraphrase of Psalm 90, O God, our help in ages past, it is the tune English crowds reach for at public mourning.","Croft was organist of St Anne's Church in Soho and later of Westminster Abbey and the Chapel Royal, the leading English church musician of the generation after Purcell. The tune first appeared anonymously in a psalm supplement of 1708 and is named after his church. English congregational singing was then being standardised: printed tune books were spreading metrical psalms to parishes with no choir, so a tune had to be memorable at first hearing and singable by people who could not read music.","The opening four notes are among the most reused shapes in music. Bach's great E flat organ fugue opens with the same figure, almost certainly by coincidence, and the English nickname St Anne stuck to that fugue because of the resemblance. Croft's own harmonisation was never fixed as definitive; the version congregations sing today is a later arrangement, not his, which is common for hymn tunes of the period.","Hymn tunes rarely attract criticism, and this one has been studied mostly by hymnologists. Attribution was uncertain for a century, since the 1708 print carried no composer's name, and the case for Croft rests on later ascriptions. What is not disputed is its function: since the eighteenth century it has been the default English tune for grief in public, sung at royal funerals and remembrance services, which has made it hard to hear as a composition at all.","Sing it, or at least follow the shape of the first line, up, up, down, up. Then hear an unaccompanied choir take it slowly, where the harmony matters more than the tune. Compare it with Bach's fugue on the same four notes, a quarter of an hour of counterpoint built from what a congregation gets through in ten seconds. English cathedral choirs with organ carry the right weight."],"croft-burial-service":["The Book of Common Prayer's funeral sentences set for unaccompanied four-part choir: I am the resurrection and the life, I know that my Redeemer liveth, We brought nothing into this world, Man that is born of a woman, and the rest, about a quarter of an hour when sung complete. The style is slow and plain, mostly chordal, with the words falling in speech rhythm and almost no imitation. One of the movements is not by Croft at all.","Croft published the set in Musica sacra in 1724, one of the first English music books engraved in full score rather than as separate parts. As organist of Westminster Abbey he buried monarchs and statesmen for two decades and needed music that a choir could sing while walking, indoors and out. The Church of England had no fixed funeral music before this; his settings became the standard and have been used at English state funerals ever since.","Croft left the sentence Thou knowest, Lord, the secrets of our hearts exactly as Purcell had set it thirty years earlier, and said so in his preface: he could not, he wrote, improve on it. The join is audible, because Purcell's harmony leans and stings where Croft's stands still. That one deferred movement is the most performed thing in the whole collection, which Croft would probably have expected.","Because it is functional music the Burial Service has rarely been reviewed, only used. Musicologists note its deliberate plainness as a rejection of the elaborate verse anthem Croft himself wrote elsewhere. The live arguments are liturgical and practical: which sentences to include, whether to sing the Purcell movement in his original scoring with brass, and how slowly a choir can move outdoors before the harmony comes apart.","Listen for the moment the Purcell sentence arrives; the harmony suddenly aches where everything around it is steady. Fifteen minutes complete, though the individual sentences work alone. Compare it with Purcell's Music for the Funeral of Queen Mary, where the same words are surrounded by drums and flat trumpets. An English cathedral choir in a large resonant building is the sound this music was written into."],"eccles-semele":["A full-length all-sung English opera in three acts on Congreve's libretto: Semele, loved by Jupiter, is tricked by his jealous wife Juno into demanding that he appear in his true divine form, and is burned to ashes by the sight. Eccles writes in the English declamatory manner, with vocal lines that follow the poetry's stresses closely, short airs rather than long da capo arias, and a busy chorus. It runs about two and a half hours.","Eccles was Master of the King's Musick and the leading composer for the London theatres. The Queen's Theatre in the Haymarket commissioned an English opera around 1706, and Congreve, already a famous playwright, provided a libretto that treats the myth with dry comic distance. It was rehearsed and never performed: Italian opera arrived in London in the same seasons, fashion turned almost overnight, and English through-composed opera was abandoned for a generation.","Congreve's text is the very one Handel set in 1744, which makes the pair a controlled experiment: identical words, one composer writing English declamation, the other Italian aria. Eccles keeps the numbers short and the words clear; Handel stops the action so that someone can sing. Hear Endless pleasure or Myself I shall adore in both settings and the difference in what an aria is for becomes plain within a minute.","The score sat unperformed for nearly three centuries, and a first complete recording appeared only in recent years, after a modern edition was prepared. Reviews treated it as a curiosity that turned out better than expected, dramatically alert if without Handel's melodic reach. The historical argument it supports is larger than the music: English opera did not fail for want of talent, it was displaced commercially by imported Italian singers.","Come to it after Handel's Semele rather than before, and treat it as the same play staged by a different director. Start with Act I and notice how fast the story moves when nobody sings a da capo. Listen for the chorus, which Eccles uses as a crowd rather than a commentator. The Academy of Ancient Music's period recording is the way in."],"pepusch-beggars-opera":["A play with songs, about two and a quarter hours: Macheath the highwayman, Peachum the fence who informs on his own men, and two women who each believe they have married Macheath, in a London of thieves, gin and gaol. There are sixty-nine short numbers, nearly all of them existing ballads, folk tunes and popular airs fitted with new words. Pepusch supplied the overture and the bass lines. The dialogue is spoken, so the songs land like punchlines.","John Gay wrote it in 1727 and John Rich staged it at Lincoln's Inn Fields in January 1728, where it ran for sixty-two nights, an unheard-of figure. Handel's Royal Academy was importing Italian castrati at enormous cost for an aristocratic subscription audience; Gay's show cost almost nothing, used English words everyone could follow, and mocked the prime minister Robert Walpole in the person of a criminal boss. The joke that it made Gay rich and Rich gay circulated at once.","The tunes are chosen for irony as much as for melody. The march from Handel's Rinaldo, the grandest Italian opera London had seen, returns as Let us take the road, sung by highwaymen setting out to rob, so the audience heard the fashionable theatre being pickpocketed in real time. Pepusch, a learned German theorist who taught counterpoint for a living, wrote the overture and the basses: he supplies the craft, Gay the aim.","It was the biggest theatrical success of the century and the most imitated; ballad operas flooded the London stage, and the Licensing Act of 1737, which introduced stage censorship, was provoked partly by satire of this kind. Later ages rewrote it constantly, from Brecht and Weill's Threepenny Opera in 1928 to Britten's realisation of 1948. Scholars argue over how much of the surviving arrangement is Pepusch's and how much later editors'.","Listen for how short the numbers are, most under a minute and several under thirty seconds, and how the spoken lines set each one up. Start with Let us take the road, then find the Rinaldo march to hear the joke complete. Two approaches exist on record: period-instrument versions that treat the tunes as folk song, and theatre versions with actors who half-speak. The second is closer to what 1728 heard."],"handel-giulio-cesare":["Three acts, close to four hours uncut: Caesar arrives in Egypt, Pompey is murdered, Cleopatra seduces Caesar to secure her throne, and her brother Ptolemy is destroyed. The score is a chain of da capo arias, each one a portrait. Cleopatra has more of them than anyone, from flirtation to the desolate Piangerò la sorte mia. The famous colours are a solo horn stalking through Caesar's hunting aria and an onstage band, harp and theorbo among it, for her seduction scene.","Handel had been in London a decade, running the Royal Academy of Music, a joint-stock company importing Italian singers for an aristocratic subscription audience. The 1724 cast was extraordinary: the castrato Senesino as Caesar and Francesca Cuzzoni as Cleopatra, stars whose fees swallowed the budget. Nicola Haym's libretto reworked an older Venetian text. The Academy would collapse within five years, undone by costs, a rival company, and in 1728 by The Beggar's Opera.","Va tacito e nascosto gives Caesar a solo horn, a hunting instrument, so the audience heard a man describing himself as a stalking hunter while the orchestra supplied the hunt around him. Cleopatra's seduction is staged as a concert inside the opera: she appears as the muse Virtue with a small band of instruments visible on stage. And Sesto, the boy sworn to avenge his father, was written for a woman's voice, so the fiercest vengeance arias come out an octave above where the drama expects them.","Giulio Cesare was a hit in 1724 and then vanished with the whole genre; Handel's operas went unstaged for two centuries while the oratorios kept his name alive. A Göttingen revival in the early 1920s began the return, at first heavily rewritten, with castrato parts sung an octave down by baritones. Countertenors and period instruments changed that after 1970. It is now the most-performed Baroque opera, and the arguments are about staging.","Do not attempt four hours first. Start with three arias: Va tacito for the horn, V'adoro pupille for the onstage band, and Piangerò la sorte mia for what Handel does with a woman who has lost everything. Then hear the whole work with a good countertenor Caesar and a period orchestra. Watch a staged video if you can; the arias turn out to be drama, not display, when somebody is acting them."],"handel-alcina":["Three acts, about three hours. The sorceress Alcina keeps an island where discarded lovers have been turned into rocks, streams and beasts; she falls genuinely in love with the knight Ruggiero, loses her powers when he leaves, and the island dissolves. The great arias are hers: Ah! mio cor for the moment she understands she has been abandoned, and Ombre pallide, where she summons spirits that do not come. French-style ballet music runs through all three acts.","Handel wrote it for his company at the Covent Garden theatre, in direct competition with the rival Opera of the Nobility, which had poached most of his singers. He had the dancer Marie Sallé and her troupe from Paris, which is why the score is full of dances. The libretto derives from Ariosto's Orlando furioso, the poem behind his Orlando and Ariodante too. London's appetite for Italian opera was already fading, and within a few years Handel switched to English oratorio.","Alcina's magic is expressed as musical convention: while she is powerful she sings brilliant coloratura, and as control slips her arias grow simpler and more exposed, until Ombre pallide leaves her calling into an orchestra that answers with silence. Listen for the moment Ruggiero sees through the enchantment, where the scoring thins almost to nothing. The role of Oberto, a boy searching for his lost father, was written for a treble, most unusually for opera seria.","Alcina ran well in 1735 and then disappeared until the twentieth century. Its modern fame owes much to Joan Sutherland's Venice performances of 1957 and the Zeffirelli production that followed, which established the title role as a vehicle for a big soprano. Period-instrument performances since have restored the dances and lighter textures and shifted attention to the opera's psychology. The cuts question is permanent: three hours of arias, and every production trims.","Start with Ah! mio cor, ten minutes of a woman working out that she has been left, and notice that the fast middle section is anger while the return of the opening is not the same music emotionally, though the notes repeat exactly. Then Verdi prati for Ruggiero, short and plain. Compare a big-voiced traditional recording with a period one; Alcina survives both, which is the real test."],"handel-saul":["An English oratorio in three acts, close to three hours: King Saul's jealousy of the young David curdles into madness and destroys him and his son Jonathan. Charles Jennens's libretto is dramatic rather than devotional, and Handel scores it more lavishly than anything else he wrote, with trombones, a carillon of small bells, a huge pair of kettledrums and organ. The Dead March in Act III, a slow processional in the major key, is the most famous thing in it.","Handel wrote Saul in 1738 for the King's Theatre, at the point where Italian opera in London had stopped paying and English oratorio had not yet proved that it would. Jennens, a wealthy and prickly literary amateur who later assembled the text of Messiah, pushed him towards drama with a chorus at its centre. The audience was a paying public rather than a subscription aristocracy, and it wanted English words, biblical subjects, no staging, and Lenten legality.","Handel acquired a carillon, a keyboard instrument striking small bells, specifically for the chorus that welcomes David home, and Jennens complained in writing about the tinkling noise it made. Saul's madness is written into the orchestra: as he loses himself the accompaniment fragments into short stabbing figures with silences between them. And the scene where Saul consults the Witch of Endor draws the strangest harmony Handel ever wrote for a stage work.","Saul was well received and stayed in the repertory longer than most Handel oratorios, largely because of the Dead March, played at British state funerals into the twentieth century. Modern staging is now the live question: since the 1990s directors have treated the oratorios as operas, and Barrie Kosky's Glyndebourne production of 2015 made Saul a theatrical event again. Purists object that Handel deliberately wrote these works to be unstaged.","Start with the Dead March, three minutes, slow, and in the major key, which is exactly why it is unbearable rather than merely gloomy. Then the Act II chorus Envy, eldest born of hell, where the choir becomes the emotion instead of commenting on it. Choose a recording with a small agile chorus and period brass, then, if you can, a staged video, which settles the argument about whether this is drama."],"handel-israel-in-egypt":["Two and a half hours, and unlike any other oratorio: the chorus sings nearly all of it, much of the time in eight parts, with very few solo arias. The text is taken straight from Exodus and the Psalms with no invented dialogue and no named characters. The plagues are painted one by one, with violins buzzing for the flies, a lurching bass for the frogs, hailstones as hammering chords, and darkness as a slow blank chorus that barely moves at all.","Written in about a month in 1739, hard on the heels of Saul, for the same London oratorio audience, and it failed. Listeners wanted arias and star singers and got a wall of choral counterpoint on words taken directly from scripture, which some found irreverent in a theatre. Handel cut it, added Italian arias for a revival, and never fully rescued it in his lifetime. Only in the next century, with mass choral societies, did it become genuinely popular.","Large stretches are reworked from other composers, among them a magnificat attributed to Dionigi Erba and a keyboard canzona by Kerll, and from Handel's own funeral anthem for Queen Caroline, which served as the first part in the original version. Eighteenth-century borrowing was normal practice, but the scale here has kept scholars arguing for two centuries. Listen too for the double chorus writing, two four-part choirs answering each other across the hall.","The 1739 failure has never been quite reversed in the theatre, but Victorian England made it a choral-society staple, sung by thousands at the Crystal Palace Handel Festivals. Twentieth-century scholarship raised the borrowing question sharply; the current consensus treats reuse as period practice rather than plagiarism. The structural argument is about shape: whether to perform the three-part original with the funeral anthem first, or the two-part version most audiences know.","Go to the plagues, about twenty minutes in, and hear how literally the orchestra paints them; you can hear the flies before you are told about them. Then He gave them hailstones, for the sheer noise of it. A choir of forty with period instruments gives clarity, a Victorian-scale choir gives weight, and hearing both is the quickest lesson available in what performance practice does to Handel."],"handel-semele":["Three acts, about two and three quarter hours, sung in English throughout on Congreve's libretto. Semele is Jupiter's mortal lover; his wife Juno, in disguise, persuades her to demand that he come to her in his true divine form, and the sight burns her to death. The music is worldly and light-footed, with Where'er you walk for Jupiter, Endless pleasure and the mirror aria Myself I shall adore for Semele, and a chorus that comments like a crowd of gossips.","Handel gave it in Lent 1744 in the theatre, unstaged, which let him put a frankly erotic pagan story in front of an audience that had come for oratorio. The device fooled nobody: the devout objected to an Ovidian comedy sung during a penitential season, and the opera-loving aristocracy stayed away from anything billed as an oratorio. Congreve's libretto was by then thirty-seven years old, written in 1707 for John Eccles and never performed.","Myself I shall adore is Handel's sharpest joke: Semele, alone with a mirror, sings a love duet with her own reflection, the orchestra echoing her phrases back at her. Notice too how the parts are cast against type, with Juno given the fastest and angriest coloratura in the work while Jupiter, the god, sings the plainest and gentlest tune in it. And the ending turns tragedy into a party in three minutes, with a chorus about Bacchus born from the ashes.","Semele failed in 1744 and has spent the modern era being reclassified: it appears in opera houses as an opera and in concert halls as an oratorio, and both are defensible. Stagings since the 1980s, notably Robert Carsen's world of wealth and celebrity, have made the satire land with audiences. The scholarly question is whether Handel intended theatre or concert platform, and the evidence points to a deliberate and profitable ambiguity.","Start with Where'er you walk, two minutes and one of the plainest tunes Handel wrote, then Myself I shall adore for the opposite. Read a synopsis first; the plot is a comedy about ambition and plays as one. Compare a staged video with a concert recording, and, if the appetite holds, with Eccles's 1707 setting of exactly the same words."],"handel-solomon":["Three acts, about two and a half hours, each act a separate tableau rather than a continuous story: Solomon's marriage and the dedication of the Temple; the judgement between two women claiming one child; the Queen of Sheba's state visit. The chorus is in eight parts for the big numbers, which are the work's architecture. The orchestral sinfonia that opens Act III, two oboes chasing each other, is the piece everyone already knows by another name.","Handel wrote it in the summer of 1748 and gave it in 1749, the year of the Music for the Royal Fireworks. Britain had just ended the War of the Austrian Succession, and an oratorio about a wise king presiding over a prosperous, peaceful and well-married kingdom read as a compliment to George II and his country. The librettist is unknown. Handel was sixty-four, about four years from blindness and ten from death.","The judgement scene is the best dramatic writing in the work: the true mother and the false one sing similar words to opposite music, and the trio that follows lets you hear which of them is lying. Solomon himself is written for a woman's voice, an alto, which gives the king a curiously gentle presence throughout. And the famous Sheba sinfonia is not a march at all but a two-oboe conversation, written to introduce a scene rather than to be a concert item.","Solomon was received politely and then dismissed for two centuries as a national pageant with one detachable hit, played at weddings and, in 2012, at the London Olympics opening ceremony. Recordings since the 1980s, John Eliot Gardiner's especially, argued that the double choruses are among Handel's finest choral writing, and the work is back in the repertory. The cut question remains: complete it is long, and Act I is usually trimmed.","Everyone already knows the Arrival of the Queen of Sheba, so the discovery lies elsewhere. Go to the Act I chorus May no rash intruder, the nightingale chorus, with the choir imitating birds at evening, and then the judgement trio in Act II. A period-instrument recording with a chorus of about thirty gives the eight-part writing enough room to be heard as counterpoint."],"handel-theodora":["Three acts, close to three hours. Theodora, a Christian noblewoman in Roman Antioch, refuses to sacrifice to the emperor; she is condemned to a brothel, rescued by the Roman soldier Didymus who loves her, and both go to their deaths. The music is slow, inward and largely in minor keys, with two choruses, Christians and Romans, set against each other. It ends not in triumph but with the duet Streams of pleasure ever flowing, sung by two people about to be executed.","Handel gave it in Lent 1750 at Covent Garden and it emptied the house. The audience that had supported his Old Testament oratorios had no stake in a Christian martyr story, and a run of earthquakes in London that spring had frightened fashionable society out of town. He is said to have remarked that the Jews would not come because it was a Christian story and the ladies would not come because it was a virtuous one. It was his last oratorio but one.","He saw the lovely youth, the chorus that closes Act II, is the one Handel himself reportedly rated above the Hallelujah chorus. It sets the raising of a widow's son as a slow minor-key lament that turns to the major mid-phrase as the boy revives, and the switch is audible as a physical change of light. Listen also to how little the Roman music enjoys itself: the persecutors get bright, empty choruses that ring hollow beside the Christians' harmony.","Three performances in 1750 and near silence afterwards; Handel reportedly told a friend who complained of the empty hall that the music would sound the better for it. The turn came with Peter Sellars's Glyndebourne staging of 1996, which presented the martyrdom as an American execution and made the piece a modern favourite. Scholars now rank it with Jephtha at the summit of the late oratorios, and argue chiefly about pacing.","Give it the slow-movement treatment: start with Angels, ever bright and fair and He saw the lovely youth, then the closing duet. Do not expect momentum, since the work's subject is people refusing to move. The Sellars staged video with Dawn Upshaw and Lorraine Hunt Lieberson converted a generation; a period-instrument concert version gives the choruses more definition and less theatre."],"handel-jephtha":["Three acts, about three hours. Jephtha vows that if he wins his battle he will sacrifice the first creature he meets on returning home, and it is his daughter. The centre of the work is the Act II chorus How dark, O Lord, are Thy decrees, which begins as a lament and ends with the line Whatever is, is right, repeated as if the singers were trying to believe it. An angel intervenes at the last moment, so the close is relief rather than joy.","Handel composed it in 1751 while going blind. On the manuscript, at the very point where the chorus sings of darkness, he noted in German that he had been unable to continue because of the sight of his left eye; he broke off for about ten days and then resumed. He was sixty-six and had suffered a stroke years earlier, and this was the last major work he completed. The London oratorio audience was by then a settled institution, and the 1752 premiere went well.","Whatever is, is right is lifted from Alexander Pope's Essay on Man and dropped into a biblical libretto: Enlightenment optimism inserted into a story about a father preparing to kill his child, and the music makes it sound like a question rather than a creed. Listen also for the accompanied recitatives, where the orchestra interrupts the singer mid-sentence. It is the technique of Handel's Italian operas, used forty years later on a man in shock.","Jephtha was admired at the premiere and then eclipsed by Messiah, as everything of Handel's was. Twentieth-century opinion reversed that judgement: it is now often called the deepest of the oratorios, and the darkness chorus one of the great pieces of eighteenth-century music. The libretto's angel, borrowed from the Isaac story and absent from the Book of Judges, still divides opinion, as a moral evasion or as the only ending an eighteenth-century audience would accept.","Go straight to How dark, O Lord, are Thy decrees, at the end of Act II, and follow it as it moves from grief to a consolation it does not sound convinced by. Then Waft her, angels, for the father's tenor voice alone. Once those two are familiar, hear the whole work. Choose a recording with a tenor who can act; Jephtha is a speaking part that happens to have notes."],"greene-lord-let-me-know-mine-end":["A verse anthem of about eight minutes on Psalm 39. Two high voices sing almost the whole piece over an organ, in close imitation, one line trailing the other by a beat so that the harmony becomes a chain of gentle collisions. The full choir enters only near the end, and then quietly. The words are a plea to be told how short life is, and the setting never once raises its voice to make the point.","Greene was organist of St Paul's Cathedral and Master of the King's Musick, the leading English church composer of Handel's London, and a rival more than a colleague, since the two men fell out. He published Forty Select Anthems in 1743, a deliberate act of collection at a time when English cathedral music was thin, scattered and largely unprinted. The verse anthem, alternating solo voices with choir, was by then a native form a century old.","The two solo voices are written so close together that they cross repeatedly, the lower rising above the upper and dropping back, which is why the piece sounds like one person thinking rather than two people singing. Listen for the setting of the line about every man living being altogether vanity: the music does not turn bitter, it simply thins out. Greene also mentored the young William Boyce, who inherited his manuscripts and his cathedral-music project.","Greene has been treated as a provincial shadow of Handel for most of the intervening time, and his anthems survived mainly in cathedral choir libraries rather than in print. English choral recordings since the 1980s have argued for him as a real composer with a narrow but distinct manner. The performance question is voices: boy trebles, as English cathedrals do it, or adult sopranos, which clarifies the harmony but loses the fragility.","Eight minutes, best heard in a resonant building. Follow the upper voice first, then listen again to the lower and notice how often they swap places. Compare it with Purcell's Hear my prayer, O Lord for what a generation changed: Purcell's dissonance is a wound, Greene's is a sigh. An English cathedral choir recording with boys' voices is the traditional and probably the intended sound."],"boyce-solomon-serenata":["A serenata in two parts, about an hour and a half, for a soprano, a tenor, chorus and orchestra. There is no plot: the two voices court each other through Edward Moore's paraphrase of the Song of Songs, in a long sequence of airs, duets and choruses. The best-known number, Softly rise, O southern breeze, gives the tenor a solo bassoon winding round him for six unhurried minutes, one of the loveliest things in English eighteenth-century music.","Boyce was in his early thirties, an organist and composer for the Chapel Royal working in a London wholly dominated by Handel. Solomon appeared in 1742, the year of Messiah's Dublin premiere, and stayed popular enough to be revived for decades. Its frankness caused trouble later: Victorian editors found a biblical text used as erotic poetry intolerable and either bowdlerised it or dropped it, which is one reason the work vanished so completely.","Boyce's model is the Italian secular cantata rather than Handel's oratorio, so the chorus is decorative and the two soloists carry everything; the work is really an hour-long love duet with interruptions. Listen for the bassoon in Softly rise, a solo part of real difficulty at a time when the instrument was mostly used to reinforce the bass line. And there is no God in the piece at all, despite the title, since Solomon appears only as the poem's supposed author.","Solomon was among the most performed English works of its century and then fell out of use entirely, buried by both the Handel comparison and Victorian prudery. Recordings since the 1990s have revived it, and the modern verdict is that Boyce is not a lesser Handel but a lighter and different composer. Scholars debate the scale of performance, chamber forces or a full oratorio chorus, since the surviving parts suggest something fairly modest.","Start with Softly rise, O southern breeze and let it be slow; the bassoon is the second voice, not an accompaniment. Then the opening duet, to hear how conversational the writing is. This is music for low intensity, an hour and a half of courtship with no crisis in it. A period band with light-voiced English singers, rather than oratorio-sized forces, catches the right scale."],"boyce-symphony-no-1":["Eight minutes in three short movements, fast, slow and fast, for strings, oboes and horns with a continuo harpsichord. The first movement bustles in even quavers with no real contrasting second theme; the middle is a brief singing slow section; the finale is a dance in triple time, over almost before it has begun. It is a symphony in the older sense, an overture detached from the vocal work it once introduced, with the tunes and energy of theatre music.","Boyce assembled the Eight Symphonys, Op. 2 in 1760 out of overtures he had written over the previous twenty years for odes, oratorios and stage pieces. By then he was Master of the King's Musick and going deaf, which pushed him towards editing and publishing; his three-volume Cathedral Music, completing the project Maurice Greene began, is the other monument of his later years. Publishing old overtures as concert symphonies was ordinary commercial practice.","These are not symphonies conceived as such. Each began life as the curtain-raiser to a vocal work, and the joins show, in endings that stop rather than conclude because a chorus originally followed straight on. The scoring assumes a harpsichord holding the harmony together, which is a Baroque habit, while the tunes and phrase lengths are already Classical. Boyce is audibly standing between two styles and does not appear much troubled by it.","Neglected as period furniture until Constant Lambert edited and championed the set in the 1920s and 1930s, after which they became popular concert curtain-raisers and have stayed so. Critics divide over whether Boyce was a conservative holding out against the new Continental symphony, or simply an efficient theatre composer with no symphonic ambition whatever. The performance argument is the continuo: harpsichord or not, since English orchestras were beginning to drop it.","Eight minutes, so play it as a palate cleanser before something bigger, which is exactly what it was written for. Listen for the horns, which mostly hold long notes and give the whole thing an outdoor brightness. Compare it with a J. C. Bach symphony of the same decade to hear the difference between an English overture and a Continental symphony. Small period orchestras take these best."],"boyce-heart-of-oak":["A strophic song of about three minutes: a solo verse answered by a chorus everyone can join, on the words heart of oak are our ships, heart of oak are our men. The tune moves in confident dotted rhythms at walking pace, sits comfortably in an untrained voice's range, and repeats the refrain often enough that a theatre audience had it by the second verse. Boyce's setting is plain by design; David Garrick's words do most of the work.","1759 was Britain's year of victories, at Quebec, Quiberon Bay and Minden, and Garrick wrote the song for his pantomime Harlequin's Invasion at Drury Lane, celebrating them almost as the news arrived. Boyce, Master of the King's Musick, supplied the music. The theatre was where national feeling was manufactured in eighteenth-century London, and a topical song in a Christmas entertainment reached far more people than any official court ode could.","The lyric's reference to this wonderful year dates the song exactly to 1759, and later verses were added and dropped as the wars changed, which is why printed versions disagree with each other. The tune's shape, a rising figure repeated a step higher each time, is the same device used by half the patriotic songs of the period, Rule, Britannia! included. Boyce is deliberately writing to a formula, and it worked: it is still the Royal Navy's official march.","As with Rule, Britannia!, criticism of Heart of Oak has been about what the song was used for rather than how it was made. Musicologists note that Boyce, a serious contrapuntist, could turn out a functional theatre tune without condescension or fuss. Its later life in naval ceremonial, films and terrace singing has stripped Garrick's specific 1759 references out of it, and most singers no longer know which year they are celebrating.","Three minutes; hear a naval band version and then a period-instrument theatre version with a solo tenor, and notice how much of the swagger turns out to be arrangement rather than composition. Compare it directly with Rule, Britannia!, written nineteen years earlier by Arne, the same job done twice in the same theatre world by the two leading English composers of the day."],"arne-rule-britannia":["The closing number of the masque Alfred: a solo verse and a chorus refrain, Rule, Britannia! Britannia, rule the waves, running about four minutes with its verses. The tune begins by holding the first syllable while the orchestra waits, then drops and climbs again, and the chorus line is written to be roared rather than sung. Arne scores it lightly; almost every version heard today is a later and much heavier arrangement.","Arne wrote Alfred in 1740 for a private performance at Cliveden, the country house of Frederick, Prince of Wales, marking a family birthday and a dynastic anniversary. The masque celebrates Alfred the Great as the founder of English naval power, at a moment when Britain was at war with Spain over trade and the navy was the country's image of itself. James Thomson and David Mallet wrote the words, and the song was extracted and sung separately within months.","The text is an imperative, not a description. Britannia, rule the waves is a command addressed to a country that did not yet control them, which is why the usual misquotation, Britannia rules the waves, inverts the meaning entirely. Musically, notice the pause on the first syllable of Rule, where the singer holds while the orchestra stops: that is a theatrical device, not a patriotic one. Beethoven wrote a set of piano variations on the tune in 1803.","The song outgrew its masque immediately and has been contested ever since: an embarrassment to the anti-slavery generation given the line about slaves, a Victorian imperial standard, and now an annual argument about the Last Night of the Proms. Musically it is barely discussed. Scholars note that Arne's original is lighter and faster than the ceremonial tempo it later acquired, and period performances of Alfred restore something closer to a theatre song.","Hear the original masque version first, quick and light with a solo soprano, and the distance from the Proms roar is startling. Then Beethoven's variations, where a German composer takes the tune apart for five minutes. Listen for the held first note, since all the swagger comes from that pause. Compare with Boyce's Heart of Oak, the same job done nineteen years later."],"arne-artaxerxes":["Three acts, about two and a half hours, sung throughout in English with no spoken dialogue: a Metastasio opera seria translated and set for London. The plot is court intrigue in ancient Persia, with a murdered king, a wrongly accused friend and lovers on opposite sides. The arias are long and florid, Italian in shape and English in language. The soldier tir'd of war's alarms, with its trumpet-like leaps, was a soprano display piece for a century.","Arne staged it at Covent Garden in 1762. He had spent his career writing songs and stage music for the London theatres and had studied Italian methods closely; Artaxerxes was his attempt to prove that an all-sung opera in English could pay, which nobody had managed since Eccles's Semele was abandoned in 1707. It ran, was revived for decades, and remained the only English opera seria that anyone performed.","Arne translated Metastasio's Italian libretto himself, which is why the words sit awkwardly on the music in places, English stresses fighting Italian phrase shapes. The title role was written for a castrato in an otherwise English cast, so the opera's central voice was the very foreign thing the whole venture was meant to make unnecessary. Much of the original score burned in the Covent Garden fire of 1808 and survives only through printed vocal editions.","Artaxerxes was the most successful English opera between Purcell and the twentieth century, played into the 1830s, and then vanished with the whole opera seria convention. Modern revivals, including a Classical Opera recording under Ian Page, have argued that it is better than its reputation suggests. The main scholarly problem is textual: with the full score burned, editors must reconstruct the orchestration from partial and later sources.","Begin with The soldier tir'd of war's alarms, three minutes of coloratura written to be applauded, then Water parted from the sea, the one tune that stayed popular for generations after the opera left the stage. The English text means you need no translation, which is exactly the argument the opera was making. Ian Page's period recording is the accessible modern version."],"avison-concerti-grossi-after-scarlatti":["Twelve concertos for string orchestra with a small solo group, each in four movements, made by taking Domenico Scarlatti's harpsichord sonatas and rewriting them for strings. Avison redistributes a keyboard's two hands across violins, violas, cello and continuo, adds inner parts Scarlatti never wrote, and arranges the borrowed movements into the slow, fast, slow, fast shape of a concerto. Complete, the set runs about two hours; a single concerto lasts ten to twelve minutes.","Avison spent his working life as organist of St Nicholas's Church in Newcastle upon Tyne, running a subscription concert series in a provincial city grown rich on coal. He published the set in 1744 by subscription, the standard way an English musician outside London financed printing. Scarlatti's sonatas had reached England through a London edition of 1739 and were fashionable, and Avison's arrangements let amateur string bands play music written for a virtuoso's hands.","Avison had to invent a great deal. A Scarlatti sonata is two lines of keyboard writing, so the viola and second violin parts are entirely his, and the harmonies they fill in are often richer than the original implies. Some movements come not from the printed sonatas but from manuscripts Avison had access to, which makes the set a source for Scarlatti pieces surviving nowhere else. A few movements are not Scarlatti at all, and not every source has been identified.","Eighteenth-century England admired Avison as a writer as much as a composer: his Essay on Musical Expression of 1752 was the first substantial work of music criticism in English and caused a pamphlet war by rating Geminiani and Marcello above Handel. The concertos were popular, then forgotten, then revived by string orchestras in the twentieth century. The purist objection, that arranging Scarlatti is vandalism, has faded as period practice came to accept arrangement as normal.","Take one concerto rather than the whole set, and hear how the keyboard's wiry brilliance turns into string weight and warmth. Then find the Scarlatti sonata behind one movement and play the two back to back. A small string band of about a dozen players with harpsichord gives the right scale; large romantic string-orchestra versions turn Scarlatti's nervous energy into plush."],"bach-cantata-106":["A funeral cantata of about twenty minutes, scored with no violins at all: two recorders, two violas da gamba and continuo, a sound at once soft-edged and old. It opens with a short Sonatina, then moves through choruses and solos on Old Testament texts about death as a fixed appointment, before turning to the New Testament promise. The centre is a collision, the choir insisting on the old law while a soprano sings over them of Jesus coming.","Mühlhausen or Arnstadt, probably 1707 or 1708, written for a funeral whose occasion is not documented, though the death of Bach's uncle Tobias Lämmerhirt has been proposed. Bach was in his early twenties and had recently walked to Lübeck to hear Buxtehude. The scoring with recorders and gambas belonged to a north German funeral tradition already going out of fashion, and the text is a compilation of scripture and hymn rather than the modern poetry he would later set.","At the great central moment three things happen at once: the choir sings that it is the old covenant, a soprano answers that Jesus should come, and the recorders quietly play the chorale melody \"Ich hab mein Sach Gott heimgestellt\". Then the voices fall away one by one until the soprano is left alone. The Old Testament literally stops singing. Few passages anywhere make a theological argument so plainly in sound.","The nickname Actus tragicus is not Bach's and first appears on a later copy. Because no autograph survives, the date rests on style and on the archaic scoring, and estimates spread across several years. It was among the first cantatas revived in the nineteenth century, and its reputation as the finest thing Bach wrote before Weimar has barely been challenged since, unusual agreement in a field that argues about everything.","Listen for the recorders; their breathy, slightly unequal tuning is part of the meaning, and modern flutes flatten it. Go straight to the central movement and follow the choir until they stop, then sit in the silence they leave. Period recordings with one voice per part let you hear each strand of the collision separately."],"bach-cantata-4":["The seven verses of Luther's Easter hymn set one after another with no added poetry, a chorale cantata through all the verses. A short instrumental Sinfonia opens; then chorus, duet, solo, chorus, solo, duet, chorus, an arch with the fourth verse at its centre. The strings are dark, two violins and two violas, with no oboes or flutes. Every movement takes the same hymn tune and reworks it, so the ear keeps meeting the same melody in new clothes.","Bach was barely twenty-two, auditioning at Mühlhausen or still at Arnstadt, and the piece probably belongs to Easter 1707 or 1708. The manner is deliberately old, the seventeenth-century chorale variation of Pachelbel and Buxtehude. Lutheran congregations knew the tune in their bones, so a composer could count on recognition rather than having to explain. The surviving performing parts come from a Leipzig revival two decades later.","Listen for the word Halleluja that closes every one of the seven verses; Bach sets it differently each time, from a headlong choral fugue to a near whisper. In the fourth verse the alto carries the melody almost unaltered while the other voices scramble around it. The opening Sinfonia already quotes the hymn's first phrase, so the tune arrives before any singing starts.","Long treated as juvenilia, the cantata is now heard as the first work in which Bach's mature manner is fully audible. The Leipzig performing parts complicate the dating and keep scholars arguing about how much was rewritten. Performance divides over whether to use one voice per part, as Joshua Rifkin proposed, or a small choir; the archaic string scoring sounds startlingly good either way.","Follow the tune. Hear the plain hymn once, then let the cantata run and try to catch where the melody hides, top voice, bass line, inner part. The central verse is a fierce little tug-of-war between life and death. One-voice-per-part recordings make the counterpoint transparent, and gut strings give the E minor its bite."],"bach-cantata-21":["Eleven movements in two parts, about forty minutes, the largest of the early cantatas. A Sinfonia for oboe and violin over sighing strings opens it, then a chorus that repeats the word \"Ich\" several times before the sentence can continue. The first part is grief throughout; after the sermon the second turns, by way of a dialogue between the Soul, a soprano, and Jesus, a bass, to a final chorus of trumpets and drums on words from Revelation.","Weimar, 1714, the year Bach was promoted to Konzertmeister and required to produce a cantata every four weeks. He was working through the new Italian style, since Vivaldi's concertos were being copied out at the Weimar court just then, and grafting it onto Lutheran church music. He kept returning to the piece, giving versions in Köthen, Hamburg and Leipzig, transposing and rescoring as available forces changed.","The Soul-and-Jesus duet is a love duet in everything but name, modelled on opera; Bach lifts the erotic vocabulary of the Song of Songs into church without apology. Listen too for the chorale sung by the tenors while the other voices weave free counterpoint around it, and for the moment the trumpets finally enter in the last movement, after nearly half an hour of restraint.","Johann Mattheson mocked the opening chorus in print in 1725 for its stammering repetitions of \"ich, ich, ich\", a rare contemporary notice of Bach and a hostile one. Later critics defended the stammer as the point, since grief cannot finish its sentence. The multiple versions make a single definitive text impossible, and conductors still choose between Weimar's small forces and Leipzig's larger ones.","Give it the full forty minutes and treat the two parts as before and after. Notice how little the music moves in the first half, then how the bass voice of Christ changes the harmonic weather within a few bars. Compare the final chorus with the end of a Handel oratorio: the same trumpets, a very different density. Period instruments keep the oboe plaintive rather than sweet."],"bach-cantata-199":["A solo cantata for soprano, oboe and strings in eight movements, about twenty-eight minutes, moving from the darkest self-reproach to open joy. The oboe is the second character throughout, arguing and consoling by turns. At the centre the singer takes a hymn tune while a solo viola plays an elaborate line around it, an unusual and slightly veiled colour. A short, dancing final aria closes the piece almost abruptly.","Weimar, 12 August 1714, on a text by Georg Christian Lehms published at Darmstadt three years earlier. Bach's new Konzertmeister duties meant regular cantatas, and Lehms's poetry, first-person, penitential and operatic in temperature, suited the Italianate manner he was then absorbing. He performed the work again in Köthen and Leipzig, transposing it and changing the obbligato instrument, which is why the surviving sources disagree with one another.","The choice of a viola for the chorale obbligato is peculiar; in a later version Bach gave the line to a cello instead, changing the movement's whole colour. In the opening recitative the strings sustain nearly motionless chords under the voice for a long stretch, so the singer seems to be speaking into an empty room. The final aria's dance rhythm arrives with no preparation whatever.","The cantata circulated in several versions, so the modern performing text is a choice rather than a given; the Copenhagen and Leipzig sources differ in key and scoring. Rediscovered relatively late, it has become a favourite of sopranos looking for something beyond BWV 51, and period-instrument recordings have argued convincingly for it as one of the finest of the Weimar works.","Hear it as a monologue rather than a church piece, one person talking themselves out of despair across half an hour. Follow the oboe, which answers every phrase the voice sings. The turn comes with the chorale movement, and nothing after it sounds the same. Seek a light, unforced soprano rather than an operatic one."],"bach-cantata-147":["Ten movements in two parts, about half an hour, opening with a chorus in which a solo trumpet leads a bright ritornello before the voices enter fugally. Arias follow for each soloist, one with violin obbligato, one with two low oboes. Each of the two parts ends with the same chorale setting: the hymn tune sung plainly by the choir while the orchestra plays a rocking triplet melody around and between its phrases.","Leipzig, for the Feast of the Visitation on 2 July 1723, weeks after Bach took up the Thomaskantor post. He expanded it from a Weimar Advent cantata of 1716 that had been left unperformed when the court cut its Advent music. The new job required a cantata almost every Sunday plus a school full of boys to teach, so borrowing from himself was not laziness but survival.","The famous chorale movement is one piece of music heard twice with different words, which is why it can feel both like an arrival and like a refrain. Its flowing triplet melody is Bach's own invention wrapped around a hymn tune by Johann Schop, not part of the original hymn at all. The trumpet that opens the cantata then vanishes until the very end; its silence across the middle is deliberate.","Arranged for piano by Myra Hess in 1926 as \"Jesu, Joy of Man's Desiring\", the chorale became one of the most played pieces in the world and detached itself entirely from the cantata around it. Purists complain the arrangement slows it to a crawl, since Bach's tempo is a walking pace at worst. The remaining twenty minutes stay far less known than those famous few.","Hear the whole thing once so that the familiar tune arrives in its place, twice, as a hymn rather than a wedding recessional. Notice how differently the two texts sit on identical music. Then compare a period band's brisk triplets with the Hess piano arrangement to learn how much a tempo can change a piece's meaning."],"bach-st-john-passion":["Two hours in two parts, divided by the sermon. An Evangelist tells John's account in recitative; the choir plays the crowd, snapping out short violent choruses; arias and chorales stop the action to reflect. It opens with a churning chorus over a grinding bass and a wail of woodwind, and ends twice, first with a long lullaby for the buried Christ and then with a final chorale. The trial scene is the tightest drama Bach ever built.","First performed on Good Friday, 7 April 1724, at the Nikolaikirche in Leipzig, in Bach's first year as Thomaskantor. The town had only recently permitted elaborate Passion music, and the council quarrelled with him about which church should host it. He revised the work at least three times across twenty-five years, swapping movements in and out, so no single authorised version exists.","The crowd choruses are not separate numbers but variations on a handful of shapes, reused as the mob repeats itself, which makes the crowd sound mechanical rather than human. Around the centre of the trial Bach arranges chorales and choruses in a symmetrical arch, with the chorale about Christ's kingdom at its apex. The aria \"Es ist vollbracht\" turns mid-way into a battle march before dying back.","The premiere left no record of scandal, but later history is fraught: the crowd's cries and John's phrase about the Jews have made performances a site of argument about antisemitism, and some conductors now frame the piece with a spoken preface. Musically the debate is about versions, since the 1725 revision opens with a completely different chorus, and about whether the fiercer St John or the vaster St Matthew is the greater work.","Start with the trial, roughly the first half of Part Two, and follow the alternation of crowd and chorale, which is the piece's engine. Then go back to the opening chorus, which makes far more sense afterwards. A period band with a small choir gives the mob its bite, and the viola da gamba should sound like an instrument struggling to speak."],"bach-cantata-78":["A chorale cantata in seven movements, about twenty-three minutes. The opening chorus is built over a ground bass that sinks chromatically, four notes at a time, repeated more than twenty times while the choir sings Johann Rist's hymn above it. Then comes the surprise: a duet for soprano and alto over a bouncing continuo, one of the lightest things Bach wrote, followed by darker recitatives and arias and a plain closing chorale.","Leipzig, 10 September 1724, in the middle of Bach's second annual cycle, the chorale cantatas, in which every week took a Lutheran hymn as its frame. He was producing one of these a week while running the choir school and teaching Latin. The falling lament bass of the opening chorus was a standing figure of grief across seventeenth-century Europe, used by Purcell and Monteverdi; Bach makes it carry a congregational hymn.","The famous duet is scored for continuo alone with no upper strings, which is why it trips so lightly, and its text is about hurrying forward with weak but eager steps. Listen for the chromatic descent under the opening chorus continuing beneath the choir even where the harmony seems to have moved elsewhere. In the tenor aria the flute plays a line so ornamented it nearly conceals the pulse.","The duet has had an independent life in recitals and on records since the nineteenth century, often sung with piano, which strips out the point of the bare continuo. Work on the Leipzig chronology, above all Alfred Dürr's, reordered our sense of when these cantatas were written. Why Bach left the chorale cycle unfinished is still argued over, with no agreed answer.","Start with the opening chorus and count the descending bass figure; once you hear it you cannot stop, and the movement turns into architecture. Then let the duet arrive as relief. Compare the ground bass with Purcell's Dido's Lament, built on the same descending idea for the opposite purpose."],"bach-cantata-56":["A solo cantata for one bass voice, about twenty minutes: two arias, two recitatives and a closing four-part chorale that is the only moment a choir sings at all. The first aria labours, a stumbling uphill line over strings, the cross being carried. The second, in a lilting compound metre, sets the words about the ship reaching harbour and moves with sudden ease. Between them a recitative accompanied by a cello that keeps rocking like water.","Written for the nineteenth Sunday after Trinity, 27 October 1726, in Bach's fourth Leipzig year, when he was writing fewer new cantatas and experimenting with solo forms. Solo cantatas suited a choir school whose best voices were unreliable, since one strong adult bass could carry a Sunday. The pilgrimage imagery of cross, sea voyage and harbour was standard Lutheran devotional language, but Bach illustrates it more literally here than usual.","In the recitative the cello's undulating figure stops dead at the word for the shore, and the singer arrives on land in silence. The oboe in the first aria doubles and shadows the voice rather than answering it, so the burden is shared rather than commented on. The final chorale, which welcomes death as the brother of sleep, is harmonised with a surprisingly restless bass line for a piece about rest.","Long a favourite of great baritones, Dietrich Fischer-Dieskau recorded it repeatedly, the cantata has been pulled between the lieder tradition and the period-instrument one, which asks for lighter tone and less rubato. The nickname Kreuzstab cantata comes from the first line, not from Bach. Scholars rank it among the finest of the solo cantatas without claiming any particular innovation for it.","This is the way in for anyone who finds the church cantatas forbidding: one voice, twenty minutes, a plain journey from burden to release. Listen for the exact moment the second aria starts and the metre changes; the relief is physical. Try a period-instrument version and a Fischer-Dieskau one back to back, both convincing and barely the same piece."],"bach-cantata-82":["Three arias and two recitatives for a single bass voice with oboe and strings, about twenty-two minutes. The first aria winds slowly upward and falls back, the oboe leading and the voice following after. The central aria is a lullaby of extraordinary stillness, long held notes over a rocking accompaniment, the singer telling his own eyes to close. The last aria is quick and almost cheerful: death welcomed rather than feared.","Written for the Feast of the Purification, 2 February 1727, whose gospel is the old man Simeon holding the infant Christ and saying he can now depart in peace. Bach was in his fourth Leipzig year and had already buried children of his own. He returned to the piece for years, making versions for soprano in E minor and for mezzo, and copied the lullaby aria into Anna Magdalena's household notebook.","The lullaby's accompaniment keeps declining to settle where the ear expects; the sleep it describes is less restful than suspended. In the second recitative the strings drop out and leave the voice nearly bare, and the word for peace lands on the plainest harmony in the whole work. The oboe's opening phrase returns a few bars later in the singer's mouth, note for note.","Probably the most recorded of all the cantatas, and the one that made the genre respectable in the concert hall. Hans Hotter and Fischer-Dieskau sang it as though it were Schubert; period performers argue that the slow tempos of that tradition make the lullaby sag. The soprano version has its own advocates, and asking which is the version is unanswerable, since all of them are Bach's.","Play only the middle aria first, at night, and let it run twice. Then hear the whole thing to feel how the final aria's briskness reframes the sleep that came before. Compare a bass version with a soprano one: the same notes, an entirely different character of consolation. Any recording with a fine oboist will serve."],"bach-cantata-51":["Five movements for one soprano, one trumpet, strings and continuo, about eighteen minutes. The first movement is a duel: voice and trumpet trade the same brilliant figures at the top of both their ranges. A recitative and a gentle aria over walking strings follow, then a chorale sung by the soprano while two violins spin decoration above it, and finally an Alleluia in which the trumpet returns and the singer has to run to keep up.","Leipzig, probably 1730, and marked for any occasion as well as for the fifteenth Sunday after Trinity, unusual licence for a church piece. The trumpet part suits Gottfried Reiche, Bach's virtuoso town musician. Who sang it is a genuine puzzle, since Leipzig church choirs were made up of boys and the tessitura is punishing; suggestions include a visiting singer or a performance somewhere outside the city.","The closing Alleluia is not an appendage but the trumpet's revenge, since the instrument sits silent through the middle two movements and returns to reclaim its material from the first. In the chorale movement the melody is sung plainly while the two violins play what amounts to a trio sonata around it. Underneath all of it the continuo keeps an unvarying tread.","For a long time this was a soprano showpiece detached from any liturgical setting and recorded by opera singers with a modern orchestra. Period practice pulled it back towards a lighter, faster reading, and Emma Kirkby's recording marked the turn. The question of the original singer remains open, and the boy-soprano hypothesis has both firm defenders and firm sceptics.","Listen first for balance: this is chamber music, not a concerto with accompaniment, and the trumpet should never bury the voice. Start with the final Alleluia for the thrill, then go back to the third movement, where the same voice sounds like a different person. Compare a period recording with a mid-century one to hear how much tempo changes the character."],"bach-cantata-80":["Eight movements on Luther's most famous hymn, about half an hour. The opening chorus is enormous: a fugue on the hymn's first line, each voice entering in turn, while the melody itself is hammered out in canon by instruments above and below. The second movement sets the hymn in canon again, oboe over continuo, around a bass aria. A chorale sung by the whole choir in unison, further arias and a duet, and a plain final verse close it.","Assembled in Leipzig around 1730 for Reformation Day, 31 October, out of an earlier Weimar cantata of 1715 on a text by Salomon Franck. Luther's hymn was the anthem of German Protestantism, so setting it for the festival commemorating the Reformation was a public act as much as a devotional one. Bach's sons later added trumpets and drums to make the piece grander for their own performances.","The trumpet and timpani parts most people know are not Bach's; Wilhelm Friedemann Bach added them, and modern performances have to decide whether to keep them. In the second movement the hymn tune runs in strict canon between oboe and bass instruments while the singer sings entirely different words, so two texts sound at once. The unison chorale at the centre is the only place all four voices share one line.","The layers of authorship make this the most disputed of the great cantatas. Editors strip out the added brass and audiences often miss it. The Weimar original survives only in part, so any reconstruction involves guesswork. Reformation anniversaries reliably produce fresh recordings and fresh arguments about how loud and how militantly Lutheran the piece ought to sound.","Listen to the opening chorus twice: once for the choir, once ignoring them entirely and following the instrumental canon underneath. Then find a recording without the added trumpets and hear how much leaner Bach's own design is. Hum the hymn first; the whole cantata becomes an argument with a tune you already know."],"bach-magnificat":["Twelve short movements in under half an hour, with no da capo arias and nothing repeated: one idea per movement and then out. Three trumpets and drums open and close it in D major. In between come a soprano aria over unison strings, a duet, a chorus that piles the words about all generations on top of each other until they crowd, an alto aria with flutes, and a bass aria over a stalking bass line.","Bach's first Christmas in Leipzig, 1723, produced a version in E flat with four interpolated Christmas texts for the vespers service. Around 1733 he transposed it to D, dropped the interpolations and replaced recorders with the more modern transverse flutes, making the work usable at any of the three high feasts. The five-part choir, unusual for him, matches Leipzig's grandest occasions.","In the movement about God upholding Israel the oboes play an ancient chant, the tonus peregrinus used for the Magnificat in the liturgy, straight through while three upper voices sing around it: the plainsong the work replaces, quoted inside it. At the words about casting down the mighty the violins plunge in a single scale, and where the rich are sent empty away the flutes end their phrase one note short.","The E flat original with its Christmas insertions has been revived on record, and some argue it is the better piece, but the D major version remains standard. Debate turns on choir size, on whether the movements should run without pause, and on tempo, since the work can sound either like a compact concerto or like a string of miniatures depending on how briskly it is driven.","Thirty minutes, twelve pieces, no fat: a good first Bach choral work if the Passions look daunting. Listen for the joke where the hungry are sent away, since the missing note is audible once you know it is coming. Compare the opening with Vivaldi's Gloria, written for a Venetian orphanage a few years earlier: the same festive D major, a very different density of thought."],"bach-christmas-oratorio":["Six cantatas, about two and a half hours altogether, written to be spread across the six feast days from Christmas Day to Epiphany rather than played in one sitting. Trumpets and drums open Part One; Part Two begins instead with a long pastoral Sinfonia in which oboes and strings imitate shepherds' pipes. An Evangelist tells the story while chorales, arias and choruses surround it. The last part ends with the choir singing over blazing trumpets.","Leipzig, Christmas 1734 into January 1735. Much of the music already existed, since Bach reworked movements from secular congratulatory cantatas written for the Saxon court, including a birthday piece for the Electress. Fitting new words to old music was normal practice and the poet almost certainly worked to the existing melodies. Bach had a libretto printed so that listeners could follow the story across two weeks of services.","The opening chorus began life as a drum-and-trumpet salute to a Saxon princess, so the timpani flourish that starts the Christmas Oratorio was written to praise a living woman. At the very end the choir sings a hymn tune that Leipzig listeners knew as the Passion chorale, so the Nativity closes with the melody of the crucifixion. Part Two's pastoral uses the lilting compound metre that meant shepherds to every European listener.","Nineteenth-century critics were uneasy about the borrowings and ranked the work below the Passions for that reason; twentieth-century scholarship reversed the judgment, arguing that Bach improved what he reused. The main practical question is whether to perform all six parts in one evening, which Bach never intended, or spread across the season. Most recordings and most concerts do the former anyway.","Take one part at a time, as designed. Part Two, with its shepherds' Sinfonia and cradle-song aria, is the most immediately lovable; Part One the most brilliant. Listen for the Passion chorale at the very end and hear how the piece looks past Christmas. Choirs of moderate size and period trumpets keep the textures clear."],"bach-toccata-fugue-f":["About fifteen minutes, and the most expansive of Bach's organ toccatas. It opens with two long canons over held pedal notes, calm and glittering, then hands the pedals two enormous solos in which the feet play alone at speed for page after page. The fugue that follows has two subjects, the second arriving only after the first is fully worked out, and the two are eventually combined. The piece is built on space rather than drama.","Probably Weimar, somewhere between 1712 and 1717, when Bach was court organist with the time and the instrument to write on this scale; the pedal writing implies an organ with a wide and responsive pedalboard. The solos may record something of how he actually played, since contemporaries described his feet flying over the pedals as though they were hands. No autograph survives.","Each pedal solo covers a compass that not every German organ of the day possessed, which is one reason the dating is argued over. The toccata never uses a full-organ crash to begin, the usual gambit, so the canons enter quietly and the piece grows rather than announces itself. The fugue's second subject is chromatic where the first is plain, and that contrast is exactly what the combination exploits.","Overshadowed by the D minor Toccata whose authorship is now widely doubted, this one is unquestionably Bach and preferred by many organists on musical grounds. Registration is the live argument: how much to change stops during the canons, whether to use reeds in the pedal solos. The tangled manuscript situation forces editors to choose between conflicting copies at several points.","Listen for the feet. In the two pedal solos everything else stops and the lowest voice becomes the melody, an effect almost no other instrument can produce. Then follow the fugue's two subjects separately before hearing them stacked together. A large German baroque organ recorded with room around it serves this better than a close, dry sound."],"bach-orgelbuchlein":["Forty-six short chorale preludes, all that were finished of a planned one hundred and sixty-four, arranged through the church year from Advent to the last things. Each takes one hymn tune and states it once, usually in the top voice and almost undecorated, over an accompaniment built from a single figure held for the whole piece. Most last under two minutes. Played complete they run about an hour and a half, but they were made to be used one at a time.","Begun at Weimar around 1713 and worked at for years, with a few added later. The title page describes a book in which a beginning organist learns to work out a chorale in every kind of way and to master the pedal. Bach may have compiled part of it during the month he spent in the ducal jail in 1717 after pressing too insistently to be released from his post. The blank pages for the unwritten preludes survive with their hymn titles already entered.","The accompanying figure in each piece is chosen to fit the words rather than the tune: a falling line for the fall of man, a stumbling one for the crucifixion, a rising one for the ascension. In the prelude on \"O Mensch, bewein dein Sünde gross\" the melody is so heavily ornamented that it becomes an operatic lament, the one place where the plain principle is abandoned. Several preludes are strict canons hidden inside the texture.","Long treated as a teaching manual first and music second, the collection now reads as Bach's most concentrated statement of how music can carry meaning. Schumann's admiration helped its nineteenth-century revival, and twentieth-century organists argued the pieces into the concert repertoire. The unfinished plan has tempted others to complete it, and a modern project has commissioned settings for all the missing chorales.","Do not play the whole book. Take five or six and read the hymn text beside each; the link between the words and the accompanying figure is the entire point, and once you hear it you can hear it everywhere else in Bach. Begin with \"O Mensch, bewein dein Sünde gross\". A modest, clear organ suits these far better than a monster instrument."],"bach-english-suites":["Six suites, each beginning with a substantial prelude, several of them concerto movements in all but name, with main sections that return like an orchestra between solo episodes. Then come allemande, courante, sarabande, a pair of lighter dances such as bourrées or gavottes, and a gigue. Several sarabandes carry a written-out ornamented repeat, so you hear the plain version and then the decorated one. About two and a half hours complete.","Probably written around 1715, at Weimar or in the first Köthen years, though the sources are late copies and published datings vary by a decade. Bach was absorbing Italian concerto style at exactly that time. The name is not his: an early copy carries a note that they were made for an Englishman, and his son Johann Christian owned a manuscript bearing the word, but no English patron has ever been identified.","The first suite has two courantes, the second with two elaborate variations attached, a survival of an older French practice. The preludes borrow the shape of a Vivaldi concerto, a returning main section alternating with contrasting episodes, which is why they feel orchestral under two hands. The gigue of the sixth suite inverts its own fugue subject halfway through, so the second half is the first turned upside down.","The relationship between the English and French Suites is a chronology puzzle, since the English are more ambitious yet may be earlier, which spoils any tidy story of development. The English title is now generally treated as meaningless. Performance debate runs on the usual lines of ornament and instrument, with the added question of how orchestral the big preludes should be made to sound.","Start with the prelude of the second suite in A minor and hear the imaginary orchestra. Then take a sarabande with its decorated repeat and listen twice, once for the bones and once for the ornament; this is Bach teaching you how baroque decoration works. A bright harpsichord suits the preludes, and the sarabandes reward a slow, patient reading."],"bach-brandenburg-concerto-no-2":["Twelve minutes, three movements, and the most unbalanced solo group Bach ever assembled: a high trumpet, a recorder, an oboe and a violin, four instruments of wildly different carrying power treated as equals. The outer movements are bright and relentless, with the trumpet near the top of its range throughout. In the slow movement the trumpet drops out entirely and the other three play a quiet trio over continuo, an act of mercy for the player.","Dedicated on 24 March 1721 to Christian Ludwig, Margrave of Brandenburg, as part of a presentation set of six concertos in a beautifully written score. Bach had met him two years earlier and was probably angling for a post. The Margrave seems never to have had the pieces played; they sat in his library and were valued at almost nothing when he died. Bach was then at Köthen with an unusually good band at his disposal.","The trumpet part is written for a clarino player working in the instrument's highest register, where the natural harmonics lie close enough together to make a scale, a specialised skill that died out and had to be reconstructed in the twentieth century. Modern piccolo trumpets make it possible but change the tone. The balance problem is not a flaw: Bach places the trumpet's rests exactly where the recorder needs to be heard.","For decades the concerto was thought unplayable as written, and performances substituted a soprano saxophone or a clarinet for the trumpet. The revival of natural trumpet technique in the 1970s changed that, though few players relish the part. In 1977 the first movement was placed on the Voyager Golden Record as its opening track, sent out of the solar system as a sample of human music.","Count the four soloists and follow each in turn through the first movement; the piece is a conversation among unequals. Then enjoy the slow movement as the trumpet's absence rather than its rest. Compare a natural-trumpet recording with a piccolo-trumpet one, since the older instrument is riskier and blends far better with its companions."],"bach-brandenburg-concerto-no-4":["Sixteen minutes in three movements, with a solo violin and two recorders, which Bach labels echo flutes, set against strings. The recorders lead the opening, blending into the strings and then separating out; the violin's role grows until, near the end of the first movement, it takes off in a passage of pure display that leaves everyone behind. The finale is a fugue that gathers the whole ensemble into a single argument.","Part of the presentation set sent to the Margrave of Brandenburg in 1721 but written earlier at Köthen, where Bach had a small, excellent orchestra and a violinist good enough for the solo part, possibly himself. He later reworked the concerto for harpsichord and two recorders in F, transposing everything down a tone, which suggests he thought well enough of it to want it playable in other circumstances.","What the term echo flutes meant has never been settled: possibly a pair of recorders played to produce echo effects, possibly a specific instrument now lost. The slow movement is built on an echo idea, loud phrases answered quietly, which supports the first reading. In the finale the violin's fastest writing sits underneath a fugue rather than on top of an accompaniment, so the display is structural rather than decorative.","The instrumentation puzzle keeps generating articles; some performances use flutes, most now use recorders, and the pitch of the instruments chosen affects the balance considerably. The harpsichord arrangement gives a second authorial view of the same music. Among the six, this one is often called the most perfectly proportioned, though the second and fifth are played more often.","Listen for the moment in the first movement when the violin stops being one of the group and becomes a soloist; it is sudden and unmistakable. Then follow the finale's fugue subject as it passes through the instruments. A period band with real recorders keeps the blend Bach designed, while modern flutes make the soloists too prominent too early."],"bach-brandenburg-concerto-no-6":["The odd one out: no violins at all, so the whole seventeen minutes sit in the middle and lower register, a warm and slightly clouded sound. It opens with the two violas playing the same figure a beat apart, chasing each other in strict canon over a walking bass while the violas da gamba fill in quietly beneath. A slow movement without the gambas follows, then a fast finale in the swing of a gigue for the two violas again.","Sent to the Margrave of Brandenburg in 1721 with the other five, but probably the earliest of the set. The viola da gamba was an aristocratic amateur's instrument and already going out of fashion; Prince Leopold of Köthen played one, and a long-standing theory holds that the easy gamba parts let the prince join in while the professionals took the difficult viola lines. Bach himself preferred to play the viola in ensembles.","The opening canon is at the distance of a single beat, so close that the two violas nearly collide, and the effect is of one line stumbling over itself. Removing the violins is not merely a matter of colour: it takes away the register in which a listener expects to find the melody, so the ear has to reach downward. The gambas are given nothing virtuosic, and in the slow movement they are dismissed altogether.","The ranking of the six concertos shifts with fashion, and the sixth has risen as period instruments made its sound world audible again; on modern instruments it can seem muddy. Whether the gamba parts really point to Prince Leopold is speculation that has hardened into anecdote in programme notes. The dating, earlier than the rest, rests on style rather than on documents.","Listen to the first minute with your attention on the two violas alone and hear how tightly the canon is packed. Then notice what is missing, since there is no bright top line anywhere. Play it straight after the second concerto for the contrast: the same composer, the same year, opposite ends of the spectrum. Gut strings and a small room suit it."],"bach-violin-partita-no-2":["Four dances, allemanda, corrente, sarabanda and giga, and then a fifth movement as long as the other four together: the Ciaccona, some fourteen minutes of variations over a repeating harmonic pattern only four bars long. It travels from D minor through a long stretch in the major and back, and asks one violinist to sound like three or four players, with chords spread across all four strings and passages of unbroken arpeggios.","Completed at Köthen; Bach's fair copy of the six sonatas and partitas for solo violin is dated 1720, the year his first wife Maria Barbara died while he was away travelling with the prince. He wrote the set for himself and the court's violinists, having trained as a violinist and played in ensembles all his life. Unaccompanied violin music had a German tradition behind it, but nothing on this scale.","Helga Thoene has argued that Lutheran chorale melodies lie hidden inside the Ciaccona's lines, making the movement a memorial for Maria Barbara; the theory is contested but performances built on it are affecting. The four dances before it are in ordinary proportions, so the Ciaccona's arrival breaks the suite's shape completely. The repeating bass pattern is never played on its own; the ear assembles it.","Brahms called the Chaconne, in a letter to Clara Schumann, a whole world drawn out of one small staff; he arranged it for the left hand alone, Busoni made a vast piano version, and dozens followed. The transcriptions raised the question of whether the original is complete as it stands, which players now answer with a firm yes. Thoene's chorale thesis divides scholars sharply.","Give the Ciaccona one uninterrupted listen with nothing else happening. Wait for the moment it turns to the major, roughly two-thirds through, when the light changes. Then hear Busoni's piano version, which spells out the harmony the violin only implies. A baroque violin with gut strings and a lighter bow makes the chords speak rather than crunch."],"bach-cello-suite-no-3":["Six movements for one cello with nothing else, about twenty minutes. The Prelude begins with a scale that falls two octaves in a single breath, then settles onto a low held note while the melody circles above it, building to a long climb at the close. An allemande, courante and sarabande follow, then a pair of bourrées, the first brisk and major, the second shadowy and minor, and finally a headlong gigue.","Köthen, around 1720, where Bach had a good orchestra, no church duties and a prince who liked instrumental music. The cello was still finding its status, mostly a bass instrument and rarely a soloist, so writing six unaccompanied suites for it was a bet on an instrument nobody thought of that way. No autograph survives; the main source is a copy in Anna Magdalena's hand, without bowings or much articulation.","C major puts the music on the cello's open strings, so the instrument rings under the fingers in a way it does not in other keys, and that resonance is why this suite sounds the most extrovert of the six. In the Prelude single notes imply two or three parts at once, so the ear hears harmony that is never actually played. The second bourrée is the same dance stripped of its confidence.","The suites were ignored as exercises until Pablo Casals found a copy as a boy in Barcelona and spent twelve years studying them before performing any in public; his recordings of the 1930s transformed the instrument's repertoire. Because the sources lack articulation marks, every performer effectively edits the piece. Period players use lighter bows, less vibrato and faster tempos, and the argument with the Casals tradition is still live.","The Prelude alone is a complete experience; hear how a single line makes you imagine chords. Then take the two bourrées as a pair and notice the change of mood. Compare Casals with a modern period player: the same notes, one weighty and rhetorical, the other dance-like. This is good music for walking."],"bach-cello-suite-no-5":["The darkest of the six, about twenty-five minutes. It opens not with a free prelude but with a French overture: solemn dotted rhythms, then a long fugue for a single instrument, which requires the player to imply several voices with one bow. The sarabande is the extreme case, a bare melodic line with no chords anywhere, the thinnest music Bach wrote. A pair of gavottes and a gigue follow, but the sarabande is what stays with you.","Köthen, around 1720, part of the set of six. Bach also arranged this suite for lute in G minor, and that version survives in his own hand, giving a rare chance to see what harmony he thought lay under the cello's single line. Tuning the top string down a tone was a common seventeenth-century device for changing an instrument's resonance and making otherwise impossible chords lie under the hand.","With the top string lowered, the whole instrument sounds darker and slightly veiled, and players who ignore the retuning have to rewrite passages to reach the notes. The sarabande's single line implies a harmonic progression that the lute version spells out, so hearing the two together shows Bach's thinking laid bare. The overture's fugue subject is turned upside down for the second half.","The retuning is the practical controversy, since many modern cellists play in standard tuning, losing the colour but keeping security. The lute version's status is debated too, whether it is for lute, for lute-harpsichord, or a keyboard reduction. Since Casals the sarabande has become a piece played at memorials, which gives it a solemnity that its dance origins do not quite support.","Go to the sarabande first and notice how much harmony you supply yourself from a line that has none. Then hear the lute version of the same movement and find out whether you guessed right. For the whole suite, seek a player who uses the original tuning; the difference in resonance is immediately audible."],"bach-french-suites":["Six suites with no preludes at all: they begin straight into an allemande and run through courante, sarabande, a group of lighter dances and a gigue. The textures are thin, often just two voices, and the demands on the hands are modest by Bach's standards. About an hour and a half complete. The character is graceful rather than grand, dances you could imagine actually dancing to, played in a room rather than a hall.","Köthen, in the early 1720s. The first five appear in the little keyboard book Bach compiled for Anna Magdalena in 1722, the year they married, and she copied several out herself. Köthen was a Calvinist court with no elaborate church music to write, so those years went on instrumental and teaching pieces. These sit between a household album and a published collection, and circulated widely in manuscript.","The sixth suite exists in versions with different numbers of movements, and some sources add dances that may not be Bach's, so the complete set is a modern editorial decision. Several movements appear in the Anna Magdalena books in simpler forms, letting you watch a piece being refined. The title French appears only after Bach's death and describes the style loosely at best; the courantes are more often Italian in type than French.","Traditionally ranked below the English Suites and Partitas as domestic music, which underrates them, since the economy is deliberate. Textual scholarship is unusually messy because the sources are teaching copies made by pupils, each carrying its own ornaments. Clavichord recordings have argued that these were meant for the quietest keyboard of all, an instrument almost inaudible beyond a small room.","Play them as background and then stop and listen properly; the sarabandes especially reward attention out of proportion to their size. The fifth suite in G is the friendliest way in. Try a clavichord recording once for the intimacy, then a harpsichord for the clarity, and the difference will tell you a great deal about how this music was actually used."],"bach-six-partitas":["Six suites of dances, published one a year and then together, about two and a half hours complete. Each opens with a different kind of prelude, called in turn Praeludium, Sinfonia, Fantasia, Ouverture, Praeambulum and Toccata, so no two begin the same way; then allemande, courante, sarabande, optional lighter dances and a closing gigue. The writing is the most demanding Bach published for keyboard, with wide leaps, dense textures and hands crossing.","Bach issued them singly from 1726, one each year, then collected them in 1731 as the first part of his Clavier-Übung and called it his Opus 1, though he was forty-six and had already written most of his church music. He sold them himself in Leipzig to amateurs and connoisseurs. A contemporary wrote that they made a great noise among musicians and that nothing like them had been seen before.","The keys of the six spread outward from B flat in alternating directions, up a step then down a third and so on, a pattern that only shows itself when they are laid out in order. The Sinfonia opening the second partita packs a French overture, a fugue and an Italian aria into a few minutes. The gigue of the sixth is written in a notation so awkward that performers still disagree about how its rhythms should actually sound.","Contemporaries treated the set as a public demonstration of what one composer could do with an old-fashioned genre; later pianists adopted them in spite of the harpsichord idiom. The argument over instrument is unresolved and probably unresolvable, since Glenn Gould's piano recordings are as convincing as any harpsichordist's. Ornamentation is the other flashpoint, because Bach wrote out more than usual but not everything.","Do not attempt all six at once. Take the first, the friendliest, and hear how its opening Praeludium sets a mood the dances then vary. Then jump to the sixth for the extremes of the set. Compare a harpsichord recording with a piano one: the harpsichord clarifies the counterpoint, the piano shapes the long lines."],"bach-harpsichord-concerto-no-1-d":["Three movements, about twenty-two minutes. The first begins with everyone playing the same line in unison, a hard driving figure with no harmony at all, before the harpsichord breaks away into elaborate solo writing. The slow movement lays a long-breathed melody over a bass that repeats one stalking figure from beginning to end. The finale returns to the unison manner and closes with a written-out solo passage of real ferocity.","Copied out in Leipzig around 1738 for the Collegium Musicum, the student and professional ensemble Bach directed at Zimmermann's coffee house, a weekly concert in a commercial venue rather than a church. The concertos gave his sons and pupils solo material to play. This one is almost certainly an arrangement of a lost violin concerto, and the solo writing still lies under a violinist's hand in places.","Two of its movements turn up as sinfonias with organ solo in Leipzig cantatas of 1726, which is how we know the material predates the harpsichord version. The unison opening is a violinistic gesture translated to the keyboard, and some passages ask a harpsichordist to imitate the effect of a string player rocking across two strings. Reconstructions of the lost violin original are regularly performed.","The set of harpsichord concertos is the first substantial body of keyboard concertos in existence and the ancestor of everything from Mozart onward, which is why the borrowing bothers nobody now. Harpsichord or piano is a matter of taste; the harpsichord's inability to swell keeps the balance right, which is not always true on a modern instrument. The violin reconstructions have their own advocates.","Start with the slow movement and follow the repeating bass, the same shape over and over while the melody above never repeats itself. Then hear the first movement for the shock of that bare unison. Compare a harpsichord recording with a piano one and, if you can find one, a reconstructed violin version: the same music under three different instrumental logics."],"bach-prelude-fugue-e-flat-st-anne":["A prelude and fugue that frame a large collection rather than sit side by side; in the print, twenty-one chorale settings come between them. The prelude is a French overture, dotted and ceremonial, alternating with two lighter episodes, one an echo passage and one fugal. The fugue falls into three sections on three different subjects, each faster than the last, and the first subject returns at the end so that all three are heard together.","Published in 1739 as the outer pillars of the third part of the Clavier-Übung, a set of organ chorales on the Lutheran catechism issued at Bach's own expense for a market of amateurs and organists. Leipzig had a printing trade and he used it. The volume is the most systematic thing he had published so far, and the framing prelude and fugue give it the shape of a service: entrance, teaching, dismissal.","Threes are everywhere: three flats in the key signature, three sections in the prelude, three subjects in the fugue, three statements of the material, a Trinity design that Lutheran readers in 1739 would have recognised without being told. The fugue's first subject happens to resemble the English hymn tune \"St Anne\", which gave the piece its nickname, though Bach almost certainly never heard that tune.","The triple-fugue reading is standard, but scholars disagree about whether the three sections form one fugue or three linked ones, since each has its own subject and only the first returns. The Trinity symbolism has been pressed into numerology by some writers and dismissed as coincidence by others. Organists argue about playing prelude and fugue together in concert, as almost everyone does, rather than as the print intends.","Follow the fugue's three sections and notice how the first subject keeps returning underneath the new material, until by the final page you are hearing all three ideas woven together. The prelude's dotted rhythms should sound crisp and unhurried. On a large German organ with strong reeds the piece carries the weight it was designed for."],"bach-well-tempered-clavier-book-2":["Twenty-four preludes and fugues, one in every major and minor key, climbing chromatically from C. About two and a half hours complete, though nobody hears it that way. The preludes are longer and more varied than Book I's: several are full binary movements with repeats, some are dances, one is a two-part invention. The fugues range from a compact three-voice piece to sprawling four-voice arguments using every contrapuntal device Bach knew.","Assembled in Leipzig between about 1739 and 1742 out of pieces written across decades, transposed where necessary to fill gaps in the key scheme. Bach never published it; the collection circulated in manuscript among pupils and stayed unprinted until the nineteenth century. The so-called London autograph, which ended up in the British Library, is the central source and shows him revising as he copied.","Several pieces were transposed from other keys to fit the sequence, and the seams occasionally show in awkward stretches for the hand. The F sharp minor pair takes a rising chromatic subject and combines it with two others, a triple fugue disguised as a modest piece. The term well-tempered refers to a tuning in which every key is usable but not identical, so each key keeps a slight flavour of its own.","Book II has always been the connoisseur's book, less immediately winning than Book I and richer on repeated hearing. Tuning dominates modern debate: a decorative squiggle on the title page of Book I has been read as a temperament diagram, and rival reconstructions have produced very different-sounding recordings. Whether the collection is a cycle or an anthology remains argued.","Take one pair a day. Play the prelude, then the fugue, then the prelude again, since the relationship between them is the design. Try a recording in an unequal temperament and hear how the remote keys sound coloured rather than merely distant. Compare Gould's piano, Landowska's harpsichord and a clavichord version; the music survives all three."],"bach-musical-offering":["A collection built on one theme, a long angular chromatic subject in C minor. It contains a three-voice fugue, a six-voice fugue, ten canons of various kinds and a trio sonata for flute, violin and continuo, about fifty-five minutes if everything is played, in an order nobody can be certain of. The canons are printed as puzzles: a single line with a clue, leaving the performer to work out how the other voices fit around it.","In May 1747 Bach visited his son Carl Philipp Emanuel at the court of Frederick the Great in Potsdam. The king gave him a theme and asked for a fugue on the spot; Bach improvised in three voices, declined to attempt six, then went home, wrote the whole thing out and had it printed as a gift. Frederick played the flute, which is why there is a flute part, and his court was the centre of the new galant taste Bach was resisting.","The dedication's Latin sentence, saying that the theme was given by royal command and the rest resolved in canonic style, makes an acrostic spelling RICERCAR. One canon rises by a whole tone at each repetition, so that after six repetitions it arrives back where it began an octave higher, an endlessly rising loop that Douglas Hofstadter built a book around. The king's theme may itself have been shaped by C.P.E. Bach.","Because the original print gathers the pieces on separately engraved sheets in no fixed order, every performance amounts to an edition. Whether Frederick really invented the theme, and whether the visit was a friendly summons or a quiet humiliation of an old-fashioned musician, are both argued. Anton Webern's 1935 orchestration of the six-voice ricercar remains the most famous act of interpretation.","Start with the trio sonata, the most conventionally beautiful part, then the six-voice ricercar, which is slow and dense and improves on a second hearing. Listen to Webern's orchestration afterwards: he splits the melody among instruments so the line changes colour every few notes, which is either revelation or vandalism depending on your taste."],"bach-schubler-chorales":["Six chorale preludes, about eighteen minutes in all, published as a set. Each treats the hymn tune as a trio: the melody in one voice, a flowing countermelody in another, the bass in the pedals. The first, on \"Wachet auf\", gives the tune to the pedals in the tenor register while the right hand plays a long striding melody above it, a texture that sounds like three players rather than one person at a keyboard.","Engraved and sold by Johann Georg Schübler around 1748, near the end of Bach's life, when he was publishing rather than writing new church music. At least five of the six are arrangements of movements from his own cantatas, adapted for a single player. Printed music was a way of consolidating a reputation for posterity, and Bach's own copy of the print, corrected by hand, survives.","Because these are cantata movements in disguise, the organ is playing music originally sung. In the \"Wachet auf\" setting the pedal line is the chorale that a choir of tenors once sang, and the melody above it was written for unison violins and viola. Knowing that changes how the piece should be registered, since the tune needs to sound like a voice rather than a bass part.","The set was long dismissed as arrangements rather than compositions, and the source for one of the six has never been traced to a cantata, leaving open the possibility that it was written fresh. Modern organists treat the transcriptions as recompositions. The print carries errors that Bach's hand-corrected exemplar helps resolve, making this a rare case of a text we can call settled.","Begin with \"Wachet auf\" and try to hear the three lines as three different people. Then find the cantata movement it came from and compare; the organ version is leaner and, some argue, clearer. Eighteen minutes for the whole set makes this an easy first encounter with Bach's organ music if the big toccatas feel forbidding."],"bach-art-of-fugue":["Fourteen fugues and four canons, all on the same plain D minor subject, arranged so that each explores a different technique: the subject inverted, stretched out, compressed, combined with new themes. About eighty minutes. No instruments are specified and the music is printed in open score, one voice per stave, so the counterpoint can be read as easily as played. The last fugue breaks off in the middle of a bar.","Compiled during Bach's last decade and left unfinished at his death in 1750. His sons published it in 1751 and it sold badly; the engraved plates were eventually sold for the value of the metal. He was by then blind or nearly so, past writing weekly church music, and working on abstract projects that summarised a lifetime's craft while fashion moved on to the galant style his own sons were writing.","The unfinished fugue introduces a third subject spelling B–A–C–H in German note names and stops a few bars later; C.P.E. Bach wrote on the manuscript that his father died at this point. The story is tidier than the truth, since the handwriting suggests he simply stopped. Four of the pieces are mirror fugues, playable upside down as well as the right way up, and the original prints them both ways.","Everything about the work is disputed: the order of the movements, whether it was meant to be played or only studied, what instrument it wants, and whether the missing conclusion can be reconstructed. Several completions exist and some are persuasive. The keyboard-versus-ensemble argument has produced organ, harpsichord, string quartet, saxophone quartet and orchestral versions, all defensible from the score's silence.","Do not start at the beginning. Take Contrapunctus 9, quick and exhilarating, then 11, then the unfinished one, and stop when it stops; the silence is worth sitting in. Hear a keyboard version and a string version of the same piece, since the lines separate differently. Reading along with the open score, even without training, shows you the shapes."],"haydn-symphony-6-le-matin":["Four movements, about twenty minutes, beginning with a slow rising figure that gathers instrument by instrument into a plain sunrise before the fast music arrives. Haydn keeps dissolving the orchestra into a chamber group: flute, oboe, solo violin, solo cello and even the double bass step out of the ranks and back again. The slow movement opens as a mock music lesson, a solo violin picking its way up a scale, then softens into a duet for violin and cello over quiet strings.","Haydn had just entered the service of Prince Paul Anton Esterházy as Vice-Kapellmeister, and this is among the first things he wrote for the household band at Eisenstadt. It belongs to a trilogy of times of day, morning, noon and evening, and the solo writing looks like a diplomatic gift to the dozen or so players he had inherited, each flattered with a moment in the light. Concerto grosso textures were still recent memory rather than antique style.","Listen for the trio of the minuet, where the double bass, normally condemned to double the cellos an octave lower, gets a full solo with the bassoon. Almost no eighteenth-century symphony asks this. The scale that opens the slow movement is a private joke about beginners' exercises, and the joke only lands because the violinist plays it deliberately squarely.","For two centuries these counted as apprentice pieces, footnotes before the real symphonies. Period-instrument surveys by Hogwood, Kuijken and later Antonini changed that, showing how much the writing depends on the transparency of gut strings and narrow-bore horns. Scholars still argue over whether Prince Paul Anton proposed the times-of-day scheme, as an early biographer claimed, or whether Haydn devised it himself.","Follow the soloists as a roll call: note which instrument speaks each time the full band drops away. Then compare the effect with a Handel or Bach concerto grosso, where the same alternation is the whole point of the form. A small period band suits this far better than a modern orchestra, which drowns the double bass exactly where it matters."],"haydn-symphony-22-philosopher":["The oddest sound in Haydn's early symphonies: instead of oboes he writes for two cor anglais, whose reedy, slightly nasal tone sits under the horns like a small chapel organ. The first movement is a slow walking Adagio in even tread, with the winds intoning a hymn-like line while the strings pace steadily beneath. Three faster movements follow, the finale a bustling hunt-flavoured Presto, so the symphony's whole weight sits at the front rather than the end.","Written at Eisenstadt in Haydn's early thirties, when he was still testing what a symphony could be and had a private orchestra to test it on. The church-sonata pattern of beginning slow was going out of fashion in Vienna, but the Esterházy chapel repertory kept it alive. Cor anglais players were on the payroll, so exotic instrumentation cost him nothing; the nickname was attached later by copyists or audiences, not by Haydn.","Listen closer to the walking bass under that opening Adagio: it never stops, and the two cor anglais answer the horns in strict alternation, as though two groups were reciting a text back and forth. That call-and-response is where the philosopher image probably came from, an imagined disputation rather than any programme Haydn wrote down.","Nineteenth-century critics ignored the symphony because its slow opening seemed archaic. Modern conductors treat it as evidence that Haydn's experimental streak arrived early, not with the minor-key works of the 1770s. A practical problem persists: cor anglais of the 1760s were curved, quiet instruments, and modern ones balance quite differently, which is why period bands make a stronger case for the piece.","Start at the beginning and simply hold on to the walking pulse; if you find yourself counting steps, the movement is working. Then notice how bright the finale sounds after the dark reeds. Seek a period-instrument recording, where the cor anglais keep their pungency rather than blending into a modern woodwind blur."],"haydn-symphony-44-trauer":["Four taut movements in about twenty-four minutes, driven by a bare unison figure that the whole orchestra hurls out in the first bars and then argues over for the rest of the movement. The minuet comes second and is written as a strict canon, the upper strings shadowed a bar later by the lower ones. The Adagio in E major is the still centre, muted strings moving in long, even phrases, and the finale returns to E minor with a hard-edged, breathless rush.","One of the minor-key symphonies of Haydn's late twenties and thirties, written for the Esterházy court in the years when he was producing operas, quartets and symphonies at a punishing rate. German literature was in its Sturm und Drang phase and the theatre troupes at Eszterháza traded in extremes; whether Haydn was reading Goethe or simply following the fashion for the tragic tone in instrumental music remains an open question.","Listen closer to the minuet: it is a canon in strict imitation, but Haydn hides the device so smoothly inside a dance that most listeners hear only a slightly stiff-legged elegance. The nickname comes from a report that Haydn wanted the Adagio played at his own funeral, an anecdote first printed decades after his death and impossible to confirm.","Nineteenth-century writers reached for the funeral story to explain the symphony's grip; twentieth-century scholarship dismantled the anecdote without weakening the music. Tempo remains contested: conductors who take the outer movements at the ferocious speed the writing suggests find a piece closer to Beethoven's C minor manner, while broader readings make it sound elegiac. Both approaches now sit side by side on record.","Begin with the minuet and try to hear the delayed echo in the cellos and basses; once you catch it you cannot unhear it. Then take the Adagio, ideally muted and unhurried, and finally the finale. Compare with Mozart's G minor symphony, written seventeen years later, to hear how much of that tone Haydn had already staked out."],"haydn-symphony-48-maria-theresia":["Ceremonial C major from the first bar, with two horns pitched high enough to sound like trumpets blazing over the strings. The opening movement is all fanfare and glitter; the long Adagio for muted strings that follows is nearly its opposite, spun out at an unhurried pace with the winds barely intervening. A broad minuet leads to a finale of rapid, hard-driven figures where the horns return to the top of their range and the timpani punch the cadences home.","Composed at Eszterháza in 1769, when the Esterházy household was building the theatre and opera establishment that would occupy Haydn for two decades. Nothing links the piece to the Empress Maria Theresa beyond her celebrated 1773 visit, when a Haydn symphony was performed; the nickname stuck to this work later. C major with high horns was the standard Austrian language of state occasions, borrowed from church and military music.","Listen closer to the horns. They are written for instruments in C alto, an octave above the usual court horn, so what sounds like brilliant trumpet writing is actually being taken by hand-horn players at the very edge of what the instrument allows. In the Adagio, the mutes on the strings turn the same orchestra into something that barely seems related to the fanfares.","For over a century the symphony was dated to 1773 to fit the empress's visit; the discovery of earlier manuscript copies pushed it back to 1769 and detached it from the story. Performers argue about whether the horn parts should be played on genuine C alto instruments, which are treacherous, or transposed down, which loses the glare that gives the piece its character.","Notice the horn colour in the opening minute and then listen for how completely it vanishes under the mutes of the slow movement. Compare with the trumpet-and-drum brilliance of Haydn's later C major writing. A period band, where hand horns crack and gleam rather than sounding uniformly smooth, brings out the risk built into the parts."],"haydn-symphony-49-la-passione":["Four movements that never leave the shadow: slow, fast, minuet, fast, with F minor holding almost throughout. The long opening Adagio is a grieving procession over a steady bass, the violins sighing downward again and again. The Allegro that follows is short-winded and agitated, all leaping figures and abrupt silences. Even the minuet is grim, its trio in the major offering only a moment's relief, and the finale drives to a hard, unconsoled close.","Written at Eszterháza in 1768, in the middle of the run of minor-key works that mark Haydn's early maturity. The old sonata da chiesa layout, beginning slow rather than fast, connects it to Holy Week practice, and the nickname is attested from a German performance later in the century. Court audiences in the 1760s expected symphonies to entertain before dinner; a work this uniformly dark asked something else of them.","Listen closer to the bass line under the opening Adagio: it walks in near-constant even notes for the whole movement, a device from Baroque lament rather than from the galant symphony. Notice too that Haydn refuses a major-key ending, which by convention was almost obligatory for a minor-key work of these years. The relentlessness is the argument.","Nineteenth-century critics knew Haydn as a genial humourist and simply did not programme pieces like this. Rediscovery came with the Sturm und Drang reading of the 1960s, which grouped the F minor symphony with No. 44 and No. 45 as a crisis period, an interpretation later scholars have questioned as too biographical. What is not disputed is that the work was widely copied and circulated in Haydn's lifetime.","Give the first movement its full length without expecting an event; the point is that nothing rescues it. Then hear how the Allegro's agitation grows out of the same downward sigh. Compare with Bach's passion writing, which the title invites, and with Mozart's later minor-key symphonies. Period strings, drier and less lush, keep the grief from turning romantic."],"haydn-symphony-82-the-bear":["The first of the six symphonies Haydn wrote for Paris, and the biggest orchestra he had yet handled: trumpets and drums alongside flute, oboes, bassoons and horns. The opening movement strides out in confident C major with a leaping theme built on wide intervals. The slow movement alternates two related themes, one in the major and one in the minor, elaborating both. The finale sets a skirling tune over a droning bass, the sound of a street bagpipe, which is where the nickname comes from.","Commissioned by the Concert de la Loge Olympique, a Parisian masonic concert society with an orchestra far larger than anything at Eszterháza, reportedly with dozens of string players in uniform. Haydn, then in his mid-fifties and internationally famous through printed editions, was paid handsomely and never travelled to hear them. He was writing, for the first time, for an audience of paying subscribers in a capital city rather than for a prince.","Listen closer to the finale's bass: it holds two notes a fifth apart, the fixed drone of a bagpipe, while the tune dances above. French listeners heard a performing bear led through a fair, and the name attached itself to the whole symphony. The slow movement, meanwhile, is a double variation set, the form Haydn was refining in these years and would use again in his most famous slow movements.","The Paris symphonies were a public triumph and were reprinted quickly across Europe. Nineteenth-century critics preferred the later London set, and the Paris six spent long stretches out of the repertory; conductors returned to them in the later twentieth century, arguing that the larger French orchestra pushed Haydn towards a grander, more public manner. The nicknames, all of them French and none Haydn's, still shape how the pieces are programmed.","Go straight to the finale and find the drone before the tune; once you hear the two, the picture assembles itself. Then return to the first movement for the wide leaps in the main theme. Compare with Mozart's Paris-influenced symphonies. A large band suits this music, and Haydn expected one, so a full modern orchestra is no betrayal here."],"haydn-symphony-92-oxford":["A slow introduction that seems to be searching for the key opens onto one of Haydn's most buoyant first movements, its main theme a light three-note upbeat that he then takes apart bar by bar. The Adagio in D major is broad and songlike until a sudden loud minor-key eruption cuts across it. The minuet's trio plays games with off-beat accents, and the finale is a fast rondo full of feints, false endings and sudden hushes before the real close.","Written for the Concert de la Loge Olympique's Parisian audience alongside two companion symphonies, not for England at all. In July 1791, on his first London visit, Haydn travelled to Oxford to receive an honorary doctorate of music, and this symphony was performed at the ceremonies, apparently because there was no time to rehearse anything new. The nickname records the occasion rather than the commission.","Listen closer to the slow movement's interruption: the orchestra breaks into a fierce minor-key outburst with drums and trumpets, then simply resumes the song as though nothing had happened. Haydn does not develop the eruption or explain it. In the finale, count how many times the music appears to finish before it actually does; the joke depends on the audience's confidence about where a piece ends.","Contemporary English reports of the Oxford performance were admiring but vague, and later writers embroidered them freely. The symphony has held the repertory continuously since, which none of the Paris six can claim. Recent performance debate centres on the slow introduction: whether to play it as genuine harmonic confusion, which needs a flexible tempo, or as a stately curtain-raiser, which flattens the surprise of the arrival.","Listen for the moment the introduction resolves and the fast music slips in, almost too casually. Then wait for the loud interruption in the slow movement. The finale rewards a second hearing, when you know where the false endings lie. Compare with the London symphonies that followed; a mid-sized orchestra, modern or period, serves the wit better than a heavy one."],"haydn-symphony-96-miracle":["Probably the first symphony Haydn presented in London, and pitched to charm: a short solemn introduction, then a light-footed Allegro whose theme keeps skipping upward. The slow movement grows unexpectedly stormy in the middle before a passage where solo violin, flute and oboe hold a delicate cadenza-like conversation. The minuet has a trio led by the oboe over a rustic lilt, and the finale runs on a quick, quiet rondo tune that repeatedly bursts into full-orchestra brilliance.","Haydn arrived in London on New Year's Day 1791 at the invitation of the violinist and impresario Johann Peter Salomon, having left Esterházy service after Prince Nikolaus's death. London had a commercial concert life with subscription series, newspaper reviews and rival promoters, entirely unlike a princely court. Haydn was nearly sixty, the most famous composer in Europe, and writing to prove it to a crowd that had paid at the door.","Listen closer to the slow movement's late passage for solo violin with flute and oboe, an intimate trio dropped into a public symphony; Salomon himself would have led it. The nickname comes from a story that a chandelier fell during the premiere without injuring anyone, the crowd having pressed forward to the stage. Later research placed that incident at a performance of a different symphony years afterwards.","The London press greeted the first Salomon concerts with unusual enthusiasm, and Haydn's symphonies became the fixed centrepiece of the series. The chandelier legend attached itself to this work in the nineteenth century and has proved impossible to dislodge, despite the documentary case against it. Modern performances argue mainly about repeats and about how far to point the finale's sudden dynamic contrasts.","Wait for the solo violin in the slow movement, the moment the symphony shrinks to chamber scale. In the finale, notice how often Haydn plays quietly for long stretches so the loud answers land harder. Compare with the later London symphonies. Recordings with a band of Salomon's size, around forty players, get the balance between soloist and orchestra right."],"haydn-symphony-100-military":["The nickname comes from the second movement, where a pretty tune for flute and oboes is suddenly overrun by triangle, cymbals and bass drum, and then, after a violent minor-key crash, by a solo trumpet sounding a bugle call over a drum roll. The rest of the symphony is sunny G major: a graceful slow introduction, a first movement led by flute and oboes, a broad minuet, and a finale that brings the percussion back to end the piece in a blaze.","Written for Haydn's second London season under Salomon, at a moment when Britain was at war with revolutionary France and military bands were part of daily street life. Turkish percussion, the janissary instruments, had entered European theatres through Ottoman military music and was familiar from opera; putting it into a symphony was new. The first performances in spring 1794 were reported as a sensation, with the piece repeated by demand.","Listen closer to the trumpet call in the slow movement. It is an actual military signal of the kind heard in barracks, dropped into a concert piece without warning and answered by silence. Haydn also recycled the movement's main tune from a concerto he had written years earlier for the lira organizzata, an instrument played by the King of Naples, so a courtly novelty tune becomes a battlefield.","A London critic wrote of the hellish roar of war increasing to a climax of horrid sublimity, which is unusually violent language for a Haydn review. Later commentators split over whether the symphony celebrates military display or unsettles it, and the finale's cheerful percussion has been read both ways. Performance questions concern the size and placement of the janissary instruments, which can either colour or swamp the orchestra.","Go to the second movement first and wait for the percussion entry, then for the trumpet signal after the crash. Notice how quickly the movement resumes its politeness. Compare with Mozart's Turkish-style writing, which is comic rather than alarming. Recordings differ enormously in how loud the bass drum sits; the more frightening the better."],"haydn-symphony-103-drumroll":["It opens with a solo timpani roll on a single note, unaccompanied, out of which the cellos and basses creep upward in a slow, dark line before the fast music arrives in bright E flat. The slow movement sets two folk-flavoured themes, one minor and one major, against each other and varies both in turn. The minuet is heavy-footed and rustic, and the finale runs almost entirely on a horn call and one skipping tune, spun into a continuous, glittering fabric.","From the last London season, given at the King's Theatre in March 1795 with an orchestra of around sixty, larger than Salomon's earlier band. Haydn was sixty-three, wealthy, celebrated and about to return to Vienna for good. He had heard massed Handel performances in Westminster Abbey a decade of English tradition had built up, and the scale of the late symphonies owes something to that experience of sheer numbers.","Listen closer to the very end of the first movement, where the slow introduction returns, drum roll and all, just before the close, as though the symphony briefly remembered where it started. The minor-key theme of the slow movement resembles a Croatian folk tune, part of the repertory Haydn absorbed on the Hungarian estates; and the finale's whole argument is generated from the two-note horn call heard in its first seconds.","The London Morning Chronicle praised the introduction's novelty at once. Nineteenth-century editors sometimes cut the return of the introduction as illogical, an intervention no one would now defend. The Croatian folk-tune claim, pressed hard by early twentieth-century scholars, is now treated cautiously: the resemblance is real, the direction of borrowing uncertain. Timpanists still argue over whether the opening roll should crescendo, since Haydn's marking is ambiguous.","Listen to the first thirty seconds twice, once for the drum and once for the low strings climbing out of it. Then follow the two themes of the slow movement and try to keep track of which is being varied. In the finale, count how long the horn call keeps returning in disguise. A big band suits it; this is Haydn writing for a full theatre."],"haydn-quartet-op-20-no-5":["Four movements, roughly twenty minutes, in a bleak F minor that Haydn sustains almost throughout. The first movement moves in a slow-burning triple time, the first violin spinning a long line while the lower parts press against it. A minuet follows, then a set of variations on a plain siciliano-like theme in the major, and the quartet ends with a fugue on two subjects, marked to be played quietly until the very last page, when the four instruments finally break into full voice.","The six Op. 20 quartets were composed at Eszterháza in 1772 and circulated in manuscript before publication; an early edition's title page showed a rising sun, giving the set its Sun nickname. Haydn was forty, writing for the small circle of court players and for connoisseurs across Europe who bought quartets to play at home. The genre barely existed as a serious form when he began and was largely his invention by the time he finished.","Listen closer to the fugal finale: for almost its whole length the players are instructed to stay hushed and to keep their bows off the string in a dry articulation, so the counterpoint is heard rather than proclaimed. Three of the Op. 20 quartets end in fugue, an old church device deliberately imported into a fashionable private genre, and Haydn never wrote a set like it again.","Nineteenth-century writers treated Op. 20 as the moment the string quartet became an equal conversation among four instruments rather than a first violin with accompaniment, and that reading has held. Modern debate is about the fugues: whether they represent an experiment Haydn abandoned as too learned, or a demonstration of technique he had absorbed and no longer needed to display. Period-instrument groups have made the quiet finale far more effective than steel-string performances once did.","Start with the variation movement for relief, then go back to the opening and let its length work on you. Save the fugue for last and listen for the moment the volume finally opens out. Compare with Beethoven's late quartets, which take the same idea of a fugal ending much further. A period-instrument quartet suits this best."],"haydn-quartet-op-33-no-2-joke":["Under twenty minutes, light on its feet, and built to end in comedy. The first movement is compact and conversational; a scherzo-like second movement replaces the usual courtly minuet with something rougher and quicker; the slow movement is a warm duet led by the first violin. Then comes the finale, a fast rondo whose cheerful tune is chopped into fragments separated by silences, until the piece stops in the middle of a phrase and simply does not resume.","Haydn announced the Op. 33 set in 1781 as written in a new and entirely special manner, a phrase he used in letters soliciting subscriptions, and he timed publication carefully for the Viennese market. He was by then dividing his year between Eszterháza and Vienna, where quartet playing among amateurs and aristocrats was a social habit. Mozart, hearing these pieces, responded with the six quartets he dedicated to Haydn four years later.","Listen closer to the finale's ending: Haydn writes the opening phrase again with long pauses between its fragments, so that players and audience lose track of which silence is the last. The joke only works if the performers keep a straight face and the listeners believe the piece is still running. It is a joke about concert etiquette, aimed precisely at the moment applause should begin.","The new and special manner claim has been argued over ever since: some hear a genuine breakthrough in how motifs generate whole movements, others suspect a sales pitch for a set Haydn needed to sell. What is agreed is the influence on Mozart. Performers face a practical question in the finale, since a hesitant ending ruins the gag and an overly knowing one makes it arch.","Play the finale for someone who does not know the piece and watch when they clap. Then listen again for how the second movement dispenses with courtly grace altogether. Compare with the Op. 20 quartets of nine years earlier to hear how much lighter the touch has become. Any good quartet will do; the timing matters more than the instruments."],"haydn-quartet-op-33-no-3-bird":["The nickname comes from the first movement, where the first violin decorates its line with tiny pecking grace notes over a quietly repeating accompaniment, a chirping that returns all through the movement. The second movement is a slow-moving scherzo whose trio is scored for the two violins alone, the lower instruments silent, so the texture thins to a duet. A songlike Adagio follows, and the finale is a quick rondo on a tune with the swing of a Slavonic dance.","One of the six quartets Haydn published in 1781, sold by subscription to Viennese and foreign amateurs and dedicated to Grand Duke Paul of Russia, which gave the set its alternative Russian name. Quartets were domestic music, played around a table by four friends or by a patron with hired professionals, and Haydn's writing assumes competent amateurs rather than virtuosos, which is part of why the set sold so well.","Listen closer to the trio of the second movement, where the two violins play alone in close harmony over nothing at all: with no cello or viola, the ear loses its sense of the ground and the music seems to float. The chirping figures in the first movement are ornaments, not melody, so the bird effect comes entirely from decoration rather than from any tune Haydn wrote out.","Op. 33 No. 3 has long been the most played of the set, partly because of its nickname and partly because the writing lies well under the hands. Scholars note that the birdsong reading was applied by publishers and audiences, not by Haydn, who left no programme. Recent performance practice has restored a lighter, faster reading of the second movement, which nineteenth-century tradition slowed into something closer to a minuet.","Listen to the first minute for the grace notes, which are easy to miss as ornament rather than subject. Then wait for the two-violin trio and notice how the ground drops away. Compare with the companion Joke quartet for a different kind of humour. Anything from a period group to a modern quartet works, if they keep the first movement light."],"haydn-quartet-op-50-no-6-frog":["Twenty minutes, brilliant and dry. The first movement takes a short rising figure and worries it through the whole argument; the slow movement is a gentle set of variations; the minuet is broad and public. The finale gives the piece its name: the first violin plays a repeated note alternately on two different strings, so the same pitch keeps changing colour, producing a croaking, buzzing effect that runs almost without pause to the end.","The six Op. 50 quartets were dedicated to King Friedrich Wilhelm II of Prussia, a keen cellist who had sent Haydn a gold ring, and were published in 1787 while Haydn was also finishing the Paris symphonies. The dedication explains the unusually rewarding cello parts. Haydn was by now negotiating directly with publishers in Vienna, London and Berlin, sometimes selling the same works twice, and the quartets were prime commercial property.","Listen closer to the finale's repeated notes: the technique, called bariolage, has the violinist play one pitch alternately on a stopped string and an open one, so the timbre flickers even though the note does not change. It is a violinist's trick imported into serious argument, and the croaking nickname was supplied by publishers. The device also demands an instrument tuned so the open string genuinely rings.","Op. 50 sat in the shadow of Op. 33 and Op. 64 for most of the nineteenth century and only entered the standard repertory later, helped by the discovery of Haydn's autograph for part of the set in Australia in the 1980s. Modern quartets differ on the finale: gut strings make the bariolage rough and buzzy as intended, while modern setups can smooth it into mere brilliance.","Skip to the finale and listen for the note that stays the same while its colour keeps shifting; once you hear the alternation you will hear nothing else. Then go back for the first movement's single small figure and track how often it returns. A gut-string quartet gives the croak its edge."],"haydn-quartet-op-64-no-5-lark":["The best known of Haydn's quartets after the Emperor. The three lower instruments begin with a short chattering accompaniment, and the first violin enters high above them with a long soaring line: the lark of the nickname. The slow movement is broad and hymnlike, the minuet has a shadowed minor trio, and the finale is a perpetual-motion Presto in which the players race in continuous even notes for three minutes, with a brief fugal passage in the middle before the dash to the end.","The six Op. 64 quartets date from 1790, the year Prince Nikolaus Esterházy died and the court orchestra was disbanded, leaving Haydn free to accept Salomon's London offer. They were written for Johann Tost, a violinist formerly of the Esterházy band who had gone into business and acted as Haydn's agent in Paris, which explains the demanding and prominent first violin part throughout the set.","Listen closer to the opening: the famous entry works because the violin comes in after the accompaniment has already started, floating in above music that is under way. Haydn withholds the melody for two bars and the whole effect depends on that delay. In the finale, the sudden turn to minor-key counterpoint halfway through is easy to miss at speed, but it is the movement's only moment of shade.","Nineteenth-century amateurs adopted the Lark as a favourite and it has never left the repertory. Critics have sometimes held its popularity against it, hearing charm where Op. 20 offers substance. Performance argument concentrates on the finale's tempo: fast enough to be a genuine tour de force risks blurring the fugal episode, while a safe speed loses the sense of a chase.","Listen for the violin's entry over the already-running accompaniment, and then for the same trick when the theme returns. Take the finale last and simply enjoy the speed. Compare with the Bird quartet, another piece named for its first violin. Recordings by long-established quartets tend to take the finale faster than period groups; try both."],"haydn-quartet-op-74-no-3-rider":["The nickname belongs to the galloping rhythm that drives the outer movements, a short-long pattern that never lets up, first in a restless G minor and finally in a finale that hammers the same gait until it breaks into the major for a bright close. Between them sits a Largo assai in E major, remote from the home key and far slower than anything around it, the four instruments moving in long sustained chords like a hymn heard from a distance.","Written in Vienna in 1793 between Haydn's two London visits, and published with dedication to Count Anton Georg Apponyi. Haydn had learned in London that quartets could be played in public concert halls rather than only in private rooms, and these pieces are correspondingly bolder, with wider dynamics and more orchestral gestures than the domestic sets of the 1780s. He was writing for professionals now as much as for amateurs.","Listen closer to the slow movement's key. E major stands at the greatest possible distance from the surrounding G minor, and Haydn does not modulate into it, he simply arrives, which is why the movement feels like stepping into a different building. The galloping rhythm of the outer movements is the same figure in both, so the finale is answering the first movement rather than merely resembling it.","The Apponyi quartets marked the point where critics began describing Haydn's chamber music as symphonic, a word used approvingly and disapprovingly ever since. Nineteenth-century performers often took the Largo assai too slowly, turning it into a devotional set piece; modern groups generally keep it moving. The Rider nickname, like most of Haydn's, was applied by publishers rather than by the composer.","Feel the galloping rhythm in the first minute and then check whether the finale is using the same one. In between, let the slow movement's key change land without trying to analyse it. Compare with Beethoven's Op. 18 quartets, written five years later under this music's influence. A quartet willing to play loudly serves this better than a delicate one."],"haydn-quartet-op-76-no-2-fifths":["The first violin opens alone with two wide falling intervals, a bare skeleton of a theme, and that shape governs the entire first movement, turned upside down, stretched, and passed between all four players. The slow movement is warm and songful. The minuet is the shock: violins in unison against viola and cello in unison, one bar apart, a strict canon in two harsh voices, which players nicknamed the Witches' Minuet. The finale is a driving Hungarian-flavoured dance that finally turns to the major.","The six Op. 76 quartets were composed in 1797 for Count Joseph Erdődy and published two years later, after Haydn's return from London and while he was at work on The Creation. He was sixty-five, at the height of his European fame, and no longer writing for a patron's private amusement: these quartets assume performers of professional skill and listeners prepared to follow a difficult argument.","Listen closer to the minuet, where the four instruments become two lines rather than four, and those two lines play the same music a bar apart in bare octaves. The result is deliberately ugly, all edges and no harmony, which is why the witch nickname stuck. The falling intervals that begin the quartet also lie hidden inside that canon, so the joke and the argument are the same material.","Op. 76 has been treated since the early nineteenth century as the summit of Haydn's quartet writing, and Charles Burney wrote admiringly of the set on its publication. Debate now centres on how harsh to make the minuet: some quartets smooth the octaves for tonal comfort, while others lean into the grinding sound, which recent scholarship regards as Haydn's evident intention.","Listen for the two falling intervals in the opening seconds, then try to spot them returning in the middle of the movement. Go to the minuet next and hear how the four players collapse into two. Compare with the Emperor quartet from the same set. Choose a recording that lets the minuet sound rough rather than polished."],"haydn-quartet-op-76-no-4-sunrise":["The lower three instruments lay down a soft sustained chord and the first violin rises slowly out of it in a long unhurried line, the image that gave the quartet its name. The movement keeps returning to that gesture, sometimes inverted so the melody sinks instead. A serene slow movement in E flat follows, then a minuet with a rustic drone-based trio, and a finale that starts almost lazily before accelerating twice, ending far faster than it began.","From the Erdődy set of 1797, written in Vienna in the years between the London journeys and the two late oratorios. Napoleon's armies were in northern Italy and Vienna felt threatened; Haydn composed the Emperor's Hymn that year, which appears in the companion quartet of this set. Quartet publishing had become a serious international business, and Op. 76 appeared in Vienna and London within months of each other.","Listen closer to the finale's tempo. Haydn writes the same dance tune three times at progressively faster speeds, so the movement does not accelerate by pushing but by restarting quicker, and the effect is of a machine winding up. In the first movement, the rising line is later played in mirror image, falling from the top, so the sunrise sets before the recapitulation brings it back.","The Sunrise has been among the most performed of Haydn's quartets since the nineteenth century, valued for its warmth rather than its ingenuity, though later analysts have shown how tightly the opening gesture controls the movement. Performers argue mainly about the finale's stepped accelerations, which can sound comic if exaggerated or be lost entirely if the first tempo is set too fast.","Listen to the opening thirty seconds for the held chord and the line emerging above it, then wait for the same line turned upside down. Take the finale and watch the speed change in steps rather than gradually. Compare with the Fifths quartet from the same set to hear how differently Haydn can begin. Any accomplished quartet serves."],"haydn-quartet-op-77-no-1":["It opens with a marching tune over a plain walking bass, cheerful and slightly military, which Haydn then dismantles across the movement. The Adagio in E flat is one of his most expansive slow movements, the melody passed around all four instruments in turn. The minuet is really a scherzo, fast and off-balance, its accents falling in the wrong places, and the finale is a swift, densely worked movement built on a folk-flavoured tune that keeps threatening to turn into counterpoint.","Prince Franz Joseph Maximilian Lobkowitz commissioned a set of six quartets in 1799; Haydn completed two, began a third and abandoned the project, telling correspondents that his strength was failing. He was sixty-seven and finishing The Creation. Lobkowitz would soon be Beethoven's patron too, and the young composer's own Op. 18 set was written for the same circle at almost the same moment, which may have contributed to Haydn's reluctance.","Listen closer to the minuet, which is marked as a minuet but moves at a speed no one could dance: the bar lengths are even, yet the accents contradict them so persistently that the meter dissolves. This is the scherzo in all but name, arrived at independently of Beethoven. In the finale, the plain tune keeps sprouting imitative entries and then thinking better of it.","The two Op. 77 quartets have been praised since the nineteenth century as Haydn's most concentrated chamber writing, and are often heard as an answer to the younger generation rather than a farewell. Why the set stopped at two remains debated: exhaustion, the pressure of the oratorios, or, as some have argued, the arrival of Beethoven's Op. 18 quartets in the same household.","Start with the minuet and try to conduct along; the difficulty is the point. Then hear the Adagio for the way the tune migrates from instrument to instrument. Compare directly with Beethoven's Op. 18, written for the same patron, to hear two generations in the same room. Modern or period quartets both work."],"haydn-piano-sonata-hob-xvi-20":["Three movements of unusual weight for a keyboard sonata of its time. The first is dark and unsettled, its material announced quietly and then broken up by sudden loud interruptions and long silences. The slow movement in A flat is elaborately decorated, the right hand spinning ornaments over slow-moving harmony. The finale returns to C minor and stays there, driving in short breathless phrases to an ending that offers no consolation and simply stops.","Composed in 1771, though not published until 1780 in a set of six issued by Artaria in Vienna and dedicated to the sisters Katharina and Marianna von Auenbrugger, whom Haydn praised as fine players. It is his first keyboard sonata with dynamic markings that require a fortepiano rather than a harpsichord, an instrument only then displacing the older one in Viennese houses. Domestic keyboard music was expected to please; this does not try to.","Listen closer to the silences in the first movement. Haydn writes rests where the phrase should continue, and the loud-soft contrasts he marks are physically impossible on a harpsichord, which is why the sonata is evidence for the arrival of the fortepiano in his writing. The slow movement's ornaments are not decoration added to a tune; strip them away and there is very little tune left.","The sonata was long overshadowed by Haydn's late London keyboard works and by Mozart's C minor sonata, which it precedes by more than a decade. Twentieth-century pianists including Brendel argued for its stature, and the fortepiano revival has strengthened the case, since the instrument's rapid decay makes the silences genuinely empty rather than merely quiet. Whether to play it on a modern piano at all remains contested.","Listen first for what is not there: the gaps in the opening movement, and how long Haydn is willing to leave them. Then hear the slow movement as decoration that has swallowed its subject. Compare with C.P.E. Bach's keyboard fantasias, whose abrupt manner Haydn knew. A fortepiano recording makes the argument better than a concert grand."],"haydn-piano-sonata-hob-xvi-50":["Three movements, brisk and inventive. The first grows out of a deliberately plain three-note idea that Haydn refuses to replace with a second tune, preferring to keep rebuilding the same scrap in different registers. The Adagio is spacious and highly ornamented. The finale is a short, fast movement full of abrupt silences, wrong-sounding chords and sudden shifts of register, ending almost before the listener has settled into it.","Written during Haydn's second London stay for Therese Jansen Bartolozzi, a formidable pianist and pupil of Clementi who had married an engraver and settled in London; Haydn signed the register at her wedding. English pianos of the 1790s, made by Broadwood and others, had a heavier action, more sustain and a greater range than the Viennese instruments Haydn had at home, and he wrote accordingly.","Listen closer to the first movement, where Haydn twice marks the dampers to be raised and left up, blurring the harmonies into a haze. Written pedal indications are almost unknown at this date, and the passage sounds like a mistake unless the player commits to it. The whole movement, meanwhile, is monothematic: the second subject is the first one in disguise, which is why nothing new ever seems to arrive.","Nineteenth-century editors sometimes removed the pedal marks as errors or as unplayable on later instruments with longer sustain. The English sonatas were slow to enter the repertory, since Haydn's keyboard music was long treated as teaching material next to Beethoven's. Fortepiano specialists have made the blurred passages central to the sonata's reputation, and pianists now argue over how to reproduce the effect on modern instruments.","Listen for the two hazy passages in the first movement, where the harmony smears rather than clears. Then track how seldom a genuinely new tune appears. The finale rewards close attention to its silences. Compare a fortepiano recording with a modern-piano one; the difference in how the blur behaves is the whole interest."],"haydn-piano-trio-hob-xv-25-gypsy":["Fifteen minutes in three movements. The first is a set of variations on a lilting theme that keeps alternating major and minor. The Poco adagio is a broad, singing movement in E major with the violin doubling and decorating the piano's line. The finale, marked Rondo in the Hungarian style, is the reason the trio is famous: a fast, stamping dance with driving repeated notes, sudden minor-key episodes and an ending that accelerates into a hectic scramble.","Published in London in 1795 as one of three trios dedicated to Rebecca Schroeter, a widow with whom Haydn was on close terms during his English visits and whose letters he later copied into a notebook. In these trios the piano leads and the strings support, a balance normal at the time. The Hungarian style meant the music of Romani bands heard on the Esterházy estates, which Haydn had known for thirty years.","Listen closer to the finale's repeated notes and snapping short-long rhythms, which imitate a cimbalom and a village band rather than any notated tradition. Haydn had spent decades within earshot of Romani musicians in western Hungary, and this movement is the most direct trace of it in his output. The trio's slow movement, by contrast, sits in a key far from the others, a private interlude between two public dances.","The Gypsy Rondo was for a century the single most played piece of Haydn's chamber music, familiar to amateurs in arrangements for every combination. Its popularity has since worked against the rest of the trio, which is rarely discussed. Modern scholarship has also questioned the label: what Haydn imitated was a repertory played by Romani professionals, filtered through Viennese taste, not a folk tradition he collected.","Play the finale first, then go back and hear how much the earlier movements differ from it. Notice how completely the piano dominates and the violin doubles. Compare with Brahms's Hungarian Dances, which take the same source material a century later. Fortepiano recordings restore the balance Haydn expected; modern instruments make the piano louder still."],"haydn-andante-con-variazioni-f-minor":["A single movement of about thirteen minutes, and among the most searching pieces Haydn wrote for keyboard. Two themes alternate: a dotted, halting melody in F minor and a smoother one in F major, each varied in turn with increasing decoration. The pattern seems settled until, after the last variation, the music breaks open into a long unstable coda of runs, harmonic detours and abrupt stops, ending quietly in the minor with the opening figure bare.","Composed in Vienna in 1793, between the London visits, and published later as a set of variations, though Haydn once referred to it as a sonata. It dates from the months after the death of Marianne von Genzinger, a Viennese doctor's wife and accomplished pianist with whom Haydn had exchanged unusually warm letters while stranded at Eszterháza. Whether the piece is connected to her death is inference, not documentation.","Listen closer to the coda. Everything before it follows the double-variation pattern Haydn had used for decades, alternating minor and major; then the form simply gives way, and the music wanders through keys with no variation structure holding it. The last page reduces the theme to its bare dotted rhythm, unharmonised. Nothing else in his keyboard music ends by dismantling itself like this.","Long catalogued as a minor variation set, the piece gained stature in the twentieth century through advocacy from pianists including Brendel and Richter, who treated the coda as the point of the work. The Genzinger connection appeals to biographers but rests on chronology alone. Editions differ over the ending, since Haydn left an alternative shorter close, and performers must choose which to play.","Follow the alternation of the two themes until you can predict it, then listen for the moment the prediction fails. The last two minutes are where the piece happens. Compare with Beethoven's late variation writing. A fortepiano gives the bare final page a starker sound; a modern piano gives the coda more weight. Both are defensible."],"haydn-the-seasons":["An oratorio in four parts, over two hours, following a farming year through Spring, Summer, Autumn and Winter. Three characters carry it: Simon, an old farmer, his daughter Hanne, and the young countryman Lukas. Haydn writes a thunderstorm with real menace, a hunting chorus with horns in full cry, a drunken harvest-home dance, and, at the end, a winter of long shadows in which the tenor's spinning song and a bass aria about approaching death give way to a final chorus about the coming of eternal morning.","Written in Vienna at the age of sixty-nine, immediately after The Creation, to a German text assembled by Baron Gottfried van Swieten from James Thomson's English poem of the 1720s. Van Swieten pushed hard for scene-painting effects, and Haydn complained about being made to write frogs into the score. The premiere in April 1801 at the Schwarzenberg Palace was a public success, but the effort exhausted him and he composed little afterwards.","Listen closer to the winter section, where the cheerful frame breaks. The orchestral introduction to Part Four depicts thick fog in slow, groping chromatic lines, and Simon's late aria compares the end of the year to the end of a life without any of the oratorio's earlier jollity. Haydn also quotes the drum-roll figure and the sunrise music of his own recent works, so the piece keeps glancing back at him.","Contemporary Vienna preferred The Creation, and later critics dismissed The Seasons as picturesque, a judgment that hardened when nineteenth-century taste turned against tone painting. Haydn himself is reported to have grumbled about van Swieten's insistence on imitative effects. Recent conductors, particularly on period instruments, have argued that the winter music is as dark as anything he wrote and that the rustic comedy is deliberate contrast, not concession.","Start at Part Four and listen to the fog music and Simon's aria before going back to the sunlit parts; the whole design makes more sense in that order. Then hear the summer thunderstorm for its sheer noise. Compare with The Creation. A period-instrument recording with a modest chorus keeps the words audible, which matters in a work this pictorial."],"haydn-nelson-mass":["Forty minutes, and the darkest of Haydn's late masses. It opens in D minor with trumpets and drums hammering out a military figure while the chorus sings the Kyrie against them, and the soprano soloist enters high and exposed above the whole texture. The Benedictus, usually the gentlest movement of a mass, here turns violent, with trumpet fanfares breaking in. The Dona nobis pacem finally arrives in the major, but the plea for peace has been made to sound like anything but a formality.","Composed in the summer of 1798 for the name day of Princess Maria Hermenegild Esterházy, wife of Haydn's last employer, in a period when the prince had dismissed the court wind band for economy, leaving Haydn with strings, trumpets, drums and organ. Haydn's own title was Missa in angustiis, Mass in time of anxiety. Napoleon's Egyptian campaign was under way; news of Nelson's victory at the Nile probably reached Austria as the work was finished.","Listen closer to the organ in the original version. Deprived of oboes, clarinets and horns, Haydn gives the organist elaborate solo passages that stand in for the missing winds, so the instrument is a concertante voice rather than a support. Later arrangements added wind parts and buried it. The Benedictus fanfares are the same trumpet-and-drum figure from the Kyrie, returning where it is least expected.","The mass acquired its nickname because Nelson visited Eisenstadt in 1800 and the work was probably performed during his stay; the music was written before he arrived, so the title commemorates a coincidence. Nineteenth-century performances used added wind parts as a matter of course. The restoration of the organ-led original has been one of the clearer gains of period performance, and most recordings now use it.","Listen to the first three minutes for the drums and trumpets under the Kyrie, and to how high the soprano is asked to sing. Then jump to the Benedictus and wait for the interruption. Compare with Haydn's Mass in Time of War. Seek a recording using the original organ-and-brass scoring rather than the nineteenth-century wind arrangement."],"haydn-missa-in-tempore-belli":["Forty minutes of festive C major with something wrong underneath. The Kyrie and Gloria are bright and busy, and a solo cello opens the Qui tollis with an unusually intimate line. The disturbance comes in the Agnus Dei: timpani enter alone, quiet and insistent, like distant artillery, and the trumpets gather behind them while the chorus pleads for mercy, so that the closing Dona nobis pacem has to force its way into the light against a military noise that will not stop.","Written in Vienna in 1796, the first of the six late masses composed annually for the Esterházy princess's name day. Austria had been at war with revolutionary France since 1792; in the autumn of 1796 French armies were advancing through Bavaria and Vienna feared attack, and the city was raising militia. Haydn's German nickname for the piece, Paukenmesse, the drum mass, records what listeners noticed first.","Listen closer to the timpani entry in the Agnus Dei. Haydn marks it quietly and unaccompanied, so the sound arrives from nowhere; the drum is not accompanying anything, it is the intrusion itself. Notice also the solo cello in the Gloria, an instrument rarely given a line of its own in a mass, which turns a liturgical text into a moment of private address.","The mass was heard from the start as a response to the war, and Beethoven, who knew it, seems to have drawn on the device of military intrusion in the Agnus Dei of his own Missa solemnis. Nineteenth-century church practice cut and rescored the late masses freely. Debate now concerns performing forces, since Haydn's Eisenstadt chorus was small and modern choral-society performances change the balance with the drums entirely.","Go to the Agnus Dei and wait for the drums, then notice how the final section has to fight to become celebratory. Listen for the solo cello earlier on. Compare with Beethoven's Missa solemnis, which pushes the same idea further. A period band with a chamber-sized chorus lets the timpani sound genuinely threatening."],"haydn-harmoniemesse":["The last large work Haydn completed, and the most opulently scored of his masses: flutes, oboes, clarinets, bassoons, horns and trumpets in full complement, which is what the German title refers to, the wind band. It begins with an unusually long, slow Kyrie in which the winds set the atmosphere before a voice is heard. Throughout, solo quartet and chorus interlock rather than alternate, and the Gloria and Credo run as continuous spans rather than as separate numbers.","Composed in 1802 for the name day of Princess Maria Hermenegild at Eisenstadt, the sixth and last of the annual masses. Prince Nikolaus II had restored the court wind band, so Haydn suddenly had the forces he had lacked when writing the Nelson Mass four years earlier and used every one of them. He was seventy, in declining health, and after this wrote almost nothing, leaving a final quartet unfinished.","Listen closer to the opening Kyrie: it is slow, long and orchestral before the chorus enters, closer to a symphonic slow movement than to a liturgical opening, and the winds carry the argument. Elsewhere Haydn dissolves the boundary between soloists and choir so that the four singers emerge from the texture and sink back into it, a technique that makes the mass feel through-composed rather than sectional.","The late masses were long dismissed as too symphonic for church, an objection dating from nineteenth-century reform movements that wanted liturgical music plainer; they disappeared from Catholic services and reappeared in concert halls. Modern scholarship treats the six as a coherent late cycle comparable to the London symphonies. Performance debate centres on tempo in the Kyrie, where too slow a reading loses the thread across its length.","Give the Kyrie its full span and listen for how long the winds hold the stage alone. Then follow one of the solo singers and notice how rarely they get a clean solo entrance. Compare with the Nelson Mass, written without any winds at all, to hear what Haydn does when he gets them back."],"haydn-seven-last-words":["Nine orchestral movements, seven of them slow, framed by an introduction and a short, violent finale depicting the earthquake at the moment of Christ's death. Each of the seven sonatas takes one of the sayings from the cross and works it as a self-contained slow movement, with the rhythm of the Latin words shaping the opening melody. Playing them in sequence produces over an hour of almost unbroken slow music, and the final movement's fury lasts barely two minutes.","Commissioned in 1786 for the Good Friday devotions at the Oratorio de la Santa Cueva in Cádiz, where a bishop pronounced each saying from the pulpit, prostrated himself, and the orchestra played while the congregation knelt in a church hung with black. Haydn later described the difficulty of writing seven consecutive adagios without wearying the listener. He arranged the work for string quartet in 1787 and made a choral version around 1796.","Listen closer to the melodies that begin each sonata: they are built to fit the Latin words, so the tune could be sung to the saying it represents, and a listener who knows the texts can hear them without a singer. Haydn ends the whole cycle not with consolation but with the earthquake, marked to be played as fast as possible, an abrupt eruption after an hour of restraint.","The work sold widely across Europe in all its versions and was among Haydn's most admired pieces in his lifetime, later slipping from view because its length and uniform slowness resist normal concert programming. Scholars debate which version has priority, since Haydn approved all of them. Some performances restore spoken meditations between movements, arguing that the music was designed to accompany a ritual rather than to stand alone.","Do not try to hear all nine at once first time; take two or three sonatas and let the slowness register. Then hear the earthquake and feel how short it is. The quartet version is more intimate and the orchestral more ceremonial; the oratorio adds voices. A recording with the spoken sayings between movements shows what the piece was for."],"haydn-cello-concerto-no-2":["Three movements, about twenty-five minutes, and the most singing of Haydn's concertos. The first movement gives the soloist a long lyrical theme that climbs high into the cello's upper register, where the instrument sounds almost like a tenor voice, and keeps it there for much of the movement. The Adagio is a gentle, decorated song with the orchestra reduced to strings. The finale is a light rondo, quick and unemphatic, ending without any grand gesture.","Written in 1783 for Antonín Kraft, the principal cellist of the Esterházy orchestra and a fine player who later joined Prince Lobkowitz's household. Cello technique had advanced rapidly, with thumb position opening the upper range, and the concerto exploits it constantly. Haydn was then deep in opera production at Eszterháza and wrote few concertos; this one exists because he had a soloist worth writing for.","Listen closer to how high the solo line sits. Much of the first movement is played in thumb position, above where the cello normally speaks, so the instrument's warm lower range is deliberately withheld and only returns for emphasis. The orchestra, meanwhile, is kept deliberately thin, oboes and horns marking corners rather than competing, so the soloist is almost never covered.","For over a century the concerto was attributed to Kraft himself, on the strength of a claim by his son, and appeared under his name in editions. Haydn's autograph score surfaced in 1954 and settled the question. Nineteenth-century performing versions rewrote the solo part and reorchestrated the accompaniment; those editions have largely been abandoned, though cadenzas remain a free choice for the soloist.","Listen for how rarely the cello drops into its low register in the first movement, and how much that changes the instrument's character. Then take the Adagio for the sheer line. Compare with Haydn's earlier C major cello concerto, more athletic and less vocal. Period-instrument recordings, with gut strings and no endpin, give a lighter and more speech-like sound."],"haydn-keyboard-concerto-d-hob-xviii-11":["Twenty minutes, three movements, and by far the most played of Haydn's keyboard concertos. The first movement is bright and orderly, the soloist decorating rather than dominating. The Adagio in A major is a long expressive song over quiet strings. The finale, headed Rondo all'Ungarese, is a fast dance with stamping accents, sudden lurches into the minor, and drone effects imitating an eastern European band, the keyboard hammering repeated figures against the strings.","Published in Vienna, Paris and London in the mid-1780s, when printed concertos were sold to amateurs who played them at home with a handful of friends rather than to concert virtuosos. The title pages offered it for harpsichord or fortepiano, since publishers wanted both markets. Haydn wrote it while running the Esterházy opera and had no interest in a soloist's career of his own, which is partly why he produced so few concertos.","Listen closer to the finale's Hungarian material. The drones and snapping rhythms come from the Romani bands of the Burgenland region around the Esterházy estates, but scholars have also traced specific tunes in it to Balkan and Croatian sources, so the movement is a mixture rather than a quotation. The keyboard's repeated chords imitate a struck cimbalom, a sound no Viennese concerto had used this way.","The concerto's popularity has always rested on its finale, which was published separately and arranged endlessly. Critics have sometimes treated the outer movements as conventional next to Mozart's concertos of the same decade, a comparison Haydn was not attempting. The instrument question stays open: harpsichord recordings sound crisper in the finale, fortepiano ones more expressive in the Adagio, and modern-piano performances remain common.","Go to the finale and listen for the drone and the hammered repeated notes. Then hear the Adagio, which is the concerto's real substance. Compare with Mozart's piano concertos of the early 1780s to hear two quite different ideas of what a soloist is for. Try a harpsichord version and a fortepiano version back to back."],"mozart-piano-concerto-9-jeunehomme":["Three movements, about half an hour. The surprise arrives in the second bar: instead of waiting through a long orchestral introduction, the piano answers the opening fanfare immediately, as though it had walked in mid-conversation. The central Andantino turns to C minor, muted strings underneath a piano line that behaves like a singer in a tragic opera scene. The finale is a fast rondo that stops dead halfway for a slow minuet, then bolts off again.","Written in Salzburg in January 1777, when Mozart was twenty and increasingly frustrated in the service of Archbishop Colloredo. The concerto was made for a visiting French pianist, long known only as Mademoiselle Jeunehomme; research published in 2004 identified her as Victoire Jenamy, daughter of the choreographer Jean-Georges Noverre, whom Mozart knew. Within months he left on the long, unsuccessful job-hunting journey to Mannheim and Paris that ended with his mother's death.","Listen closer: the famous opening is a role reversal that keeps paying off. Throughout the first movement, orchestra and soloist keep interrupting each other's sentences, and near the end the piano begins its cadenza before the orchestra has quite finished. The C minor slow movement uses canon between the two violin sections, and its vocal line is written out with the ornaments a singer would improvise, unusually specific for Mozart at this date.","Alfred Einstein called it Mozart's Eroica, meaning the moment a young composer suddenly writes at full adult scale, and the phrase stuck. Scholars now question whether one work can be that clean a dividing line, since the Salzburg concertos before it are less conventional than the story requires. The Jenamy identification, made from Noverre family records, has been widely accepted and quietly rewritten the concerto's nickname in programme books.","Start at the very beginning and count two bars: hearing the piano jump in early tells you what the whole piece is about. Then go to the Andantino, which is the emotional centre and unlike anything Mozart had written before. Compare it with the D minor concerto to hear how far he pushed the same conversational idea a decade later."],"mozart-piano-concerto-17-g":["Half an hour in three movements, and the sunniest of the great Vienna concertos. The first movement opens with a marching tune passed between strings and wind, and the wind band is a full partner rather than an accompaniment throughout. The C major slow movement begins with a question that hangs unanswered, then wanders into dark, distant keys. The finale is a set of variations on a chirping tune that ends with a long, fast comic-opera coda.","Completed on 12 April 1784, during the busiest concert season of Mozart's life; he gave more than twenty public appearances that spring. It was written for his pupil Barbara Ployer, daughter of a Salzburg agent in Vienna, who played it at her father's house in Döbling that June with Mozart present. Vienna's subscription concerts were then a genuine market, and Mozart wrote concertos the way a modern composer writes for a season.","Listen closer: in May 1784 Mozart bought a pet starling and noted in his expense book the tune it sang, which is nearly the finale's theme with one note altered. Whether the bird learned it from him or the reverse is unresolved, since the concerto was finished first. In the slow movement, notice how often the music simply stops on a bare chord and waits, a silence he uses as an event.","Nineteenth-century audiences preferred the stormy concertos and heard this one as merely charming. Twentieth-century writers, above all Charles Rosen and Donald Tovey, argued the reverse: that the wind writing here is the most sophisticated of any concerto before Beethoven and that the harmonic detours in the Andante are as strange as anything in the minor-key works. It is now among the most frequently recorded of the set.","Listen first to the finale, then go back to the beginning; knowing the comic ending changes how the opening march sounds. Follow the oboes and bassoons rather than the piano for one movement and you will hear how much of the argument they carry. Fortepiano recordings suit it well, since the lighter instrument lets the wind band through."],"mozart-piano-concerto-19-f":["Around twenty-eight minutes. A quiet dotted rhythm opens the first movement and then refuses to leave, appearing in almost every bar in some guise, so the music seems to march even when it is singing. The middle movement is a gentle Allegretto rather than a true slow movement, built on a rocking wind and piano dialogue. The finale is the surprise: a fizzing rondo that repeatedly collapses into full-blown fugues for the whole orchestra.","Entered in Mozart's own catalogue on 11 December 1784, at the end of the year in which he joined the Freemasons and moved to the expensive Domgasse apartment. He played it, with the later Coronation Concerto, at Frankfurt in October 1790 during the imperial coronation festivities, which is why the two are sometimes bracketed together. The Frankfurt concert lost money; by then Vienna's appetite for his subscription series had faded.","Listen closer: the fugal outbursts in the finale are not decoration but structural jokes. Mozart writes a comic-opera tune, then treats it with the full apparatus of church counterpoint, letting the mismatch generate the humour, and the second fugue inverts the subject. The dotted figure of the first movement returns transformed inside the finale, so the concerto is far more tightly argued than its light surface suggests.","Long overshadowed by its neighbours in D minor and C minor, it was championed by pianists who valued its wit over drama, and the finale is now routinely cited as the model Beethoven studied for combining fugue with sonata rondo. Debate continues over the cadenzas: Mozart left his own for the outer movements, and some performers still prefer nineteenth-century substitutes that sit oddly with the style.","Go straight to the finale and wait for the moment the fun stops and the whole orchestra starts a fugue; then listen for it happening again, upside down. After that the first movement's insistent tapping rhythm becomes impossible to miss. A recording with a smaller band and clear woodwind will let the counterpoint register rather than blur."],"mozart-piano-concerto-22-e-flat":["The longest of the concertos, around thirty-five minutes, and the first in which clarinets replace oboes, which darkens and softens the whole sound. The opening movement is ceremonial, with trumpets and drums, but keeps dissolving into private wind-band episodes. The C minor Andante is a set of variations in which the piano falls silent for long stretches while wind soloists take over. The hunting-horn finale is twice interrupted, once by a slow wind serenade.","Entered in Mozart's catalogue on 16 December 1785, between the completion of Figaro's first act and the opera's premiere, and written for his own Advent subscription concerts in Vienna. Clarinets were still a novelty in the Viennese orchestra; Mozart had fallen for them in Mannheim and now had players good enough to write for. Leopold Mozart, writing home, reported that the slow movement had to be repeated at the first performance.","Listen closer: the Andante is really a concerto for wind band with piano obbligato. Two of its variations exclude the soloist entirely, one for wind alone and one for muted strings, and when the piano returns it plays what sounds like an accompaniment to itself. In the finale, the interpolated slow section in A-flat is scored like a serenade for a garden party, a deliberate change of genre in mid-movement.","Nineteenth-century critics found the piece diffuse and preferred the tighter C minor concerto that followed within months. Modern opinion has largely reversed, hearing its length as generosity and its wind writing as the bridge between the Gran Partita and the clarinet works of 1789 and 1791. The absence of an authentic cadenza has kept the piece a proving ground for performers who improvise their own.","Start with the C minor Andante and notice how the piano keeps stepping aside for the wind players; if that movement grips you, the rest will. Then listen for the moment in the finale when the chase stops and a serenade breaks out. Try a period-instrument recording, where the softer clarinets and gut strings make the contrast with trumpets far sharper."],"mozart-piano-concerto-24-c-minor":["Thirty minutes, and the largest orchestra Mozart ever used in a concerto: oboes and clarinets together, trumpets and drums. The first movement opens with a groping unison theme in bare octaves that never settles into a comfortable tune. The E-flat Larghetto is almost naive by contrast, a simple song broken up by wind interludes. Instead of a rondo, the finale is a march-like set of variations that stays in the minor to the last bar.","Completed in March 1786, weeks before the premiere of Figaro, and played by Mozart at a Burgtheater concert that spring. C minor was already his key for public seriousness, used for the unfinished Mass and later the wind serenade. Vienna's concert audience was thinning; within two years the subscription model that had supported twelve concertos in three seasons had collapsed and Mozart was borrowing money from fellow Freemasons.","Listen closer: the opening theme uses all twelve notes of the chromatic scale before it repeats anything, an exhaustiveness rare before the nineteenth century. Notice too that the finale refuses the expected happy ending; Mozart writes a coda in the major and then pulls it back into C minor. The autograph is unusually messy, full of crossings-out, and the piano part is sketchy in places, so every performance involves editorial decisions.","Beethoven is reported by the pianist J. B. Cramer to have said, on hearing it, that we shall never be able to do anything like that, and his own C minor concerto borrows its key, mood and finale strategy. The rough autograph has produced rival editions and genuine disagreement about what Mozart intended the soloist to play. No cadenza survives, so the first movement's biggest moment is always someone else's invention.","Listen to the first sixty seconds twice before going further; the unsettled opening theme sets a puzzle the whole work tries to solve. Then jump to the last two minutes to hear the ending refuse consolation. Compare it directly with Beethoven's Third Concerto, in the same key, to hear a debt being acknowledged."],"mozart-piano-concerto-25-c":["About half an hour, and the most orchestrally imposing of the concertos. The first movement is enormous, with a long ceremonial introduction whose confident C major keeps sliding into C minor within a bar or two, so the grandeur never feels secure. The F major Andante is spare and hymn-like. The finale takes a tune from Mozart's own earlier ballet music and builds a rondo of unusual breadth, with a central episode in the minor for the wind.","Entered in Mozart's catalogue on 4 December 1786, the same season as the Prague Symphony, and the last concerto he wrote for a Viennese subscription series of his own. Figaro had been a qualified success in Vienna and a triumph in Prague; the Vienna concert market, however, was contracting, and Mozart was already planning the Bohemian journey that would produce Don Giovanni. He wrote no concerto at all in 1787.","Listen closer: the march-like second theme of the first movement outlines the same four notes that would later open the Marseillaise, written six years afterwards, which is coincidence rather than quotation but hard to unhear. More important is the constant major-minor flicker; count how many times a bright chord is shadowed by its minor twin within seconds. The finale's rondo theme is lifted from the gavotte of his Paris ballet Les petits riens.","The concerto was neglected for a century because it lacks a memorable opening tune, then rehabilitated by writers who valued exactly that: Tovey and later Rosen argued that its material is deliberately plain so that the architecture can carry the weight. It is now often called the most symphonic of the concertos and grouped with the Jupiter Symphony as evidence of Mozart's late fascination with monumental C major.","Give the first movement your full attention in one sitting; it is long and rewards patience rather than sampling. Listen specifically for the shivers into the minor key inside otherwise triumphant passages. Then compare the opening with the Jupiter Symphony written two years later, and you will hear the same ceremonial C major being tested in the same way."],"mozart-piano-concerto-27-b-flat":["His last piano concerto, half an hour, scored more modestly than the great Vienna series: no trumpets, no drums, no clarinets. The first movement begins with strings murmuring under a horn call before the main theme arrives almost apologetically, and the harmony wanders into remote keys with unusual freedom. The E-flat Larghetto is stripped to melody and simple accompaniment. The finale is a hunting rondo on a tune of nursery-rhyme plainness.","Dated 5 January 1791 in Mozart's catalogue, though sketch studies suggest much of it was drafted around 1788. He played it on 4 March 1791 at Ignaz Jahn's hall in a concert given by the clarinettist Joseph Bähr, his last appearance as concerto soloist. That year brought the two final operas and the Requiem commission; he died in December, aged thirty-five, with the Vienna concert life he had built largely gone.","Listen closer: the finale's theme reappears within weeks as the song Sehnsucht nach dem Fruhling, a children's poem welcoming the return of May, so the concerto's cheerful ending carries a text about waiting out the winter. In the first movement, listen for how often a phrase begins in one key and finishes in another without any dramatic gesture, a quiet harmonic drift that few earlier concertos risk.","The traditional reading, encouraged by the date and the modest scoring, treats it as a valedictory work full of autumnal resignation. Sketch evidence dating substantial portions to 1788 has weakened that story, and scholars now warn against hearing farewell in music written three years before the fact. What survives the argument is the harmonic strangeness, which nobody disputes and which sets it apart from its predecessors.","Start with the finale and then listen to the song Sehnsucht nach dem Fruhling straight afterwards; the pairing tells you more than any commentary. In the first movement, follow the horns, which quietly frame every important arrival. A recording with a fortepiano and a small string band suits the reduced scoring better than a full modern orchestra."],"mozart-symphony-25-g-minor":["Twenty-five minutes, four movements, and the first Mozart symphony that sounds genuinely agitated. It opens with syncopated repeated notes in the strings, off the beat and breathless, before the oboes cut across them. Four horns rather than the usual two thicken the sound and let the wind hold long notes under the storm. The minuet is grim and unsmiling; its trio, for wind alone, offers a sudden patch of calm before the fast finale returns to the opening unrest.","Dated 5 October 1773 in Salzburg, written just after a fruitless summer visit to Vienna in search of employment, where Mozart heard the minor-key symphonies then fashionable in Austria, including Haydn's. He was seventeen and still nominally a court musician under Archbishop Colloredo. Minor-key symphonies were a brief vogue rather than personal confession; what is unusual is how completely the young composer adopted its vocabulary and how little he softened it.","Listen closer: the four horns are tuned in two different keys, a practical trick that gives Mozart far more usable notes and lets the wind sustain harmonies that two horns could not reach. Notice also that the finale, like the first movement, begins quietly and builds rather than announcing itself; and that nothing in the symphony resolves into the major, which was still the expected courtesy at the end of a minor-key work.","For most of its life this was a minor early piece, catalogued and ignored. The 1984 film Amadeus opened with its first movement and made it instantly recognisable to millions, a rare case of cinema resetting a work's reputation. Scholars debate how much it owes to Haydn's Sturm und Drang symphonies, which Mozart may or may not have heard by October 1773; the resemblance is real, the transmission unproven.","Listen to the first thirty seconds, then to the opening of the later G minor symphony written fifteen years afterwards, and hear the same key handled by a boy and by a man. Within this work, wait for the wind-only trio in the minuet, the one moment of relief. Any recording with real four-horn bite will do; period bands make the syncopation cut harder."],"mozart-symphony-29-a":["Scored for strings with only oboes and horns, and lasting under half an hour, this is chamber music thinking on a symphonic scale. The first movement begins with a quiet theme that drops an octave and then climbs back, a shape that recurs everywhere afterwards. The Andante is muted and conversational. The minuet is sharply dotted and almost military, and the finale, marked with leaping octaves and racing scales, ends with a swaggering flourish for the horns.","Completed in Salzburg on 6 April 1774, six months after the G minor symphony, when Mozart was eighteen and writing for the archbishop's small, capable orchestra. The scoring reflects what Salzburg could supply: no clarinets, no trumpets, few players. Italian opera overtures were still the model for symphonies in most of Europe; Mozart here writes something closer to the Austrian symphonies of Haydn, with a proper four-movement argument and full development sections.","Listen closer: the falling octave that opens the piece is a seed. It returns as the leaping figure that starts the finale, upside down and at speed, so the last movement is the first movement's opening turned into a joke. The development of the first movement is unusually contrapuntal for its date, with the theme chasing itself between violins and basses, and the finale's coda quotes the horn call from its own opening bars.","Long treated as the best of the pre-Vienna symphonies and little more, it has risen steadily in esteem as period-instrument groups have shown how much drama a small band can generate in it. Conductors argue about the repeats, which are extensive and which Mozart's own Salzburg practice probably observed; taking them all adds several minutes and, defenders say, restores the intended proportions.","Play the opening bars and then the first bars of the finale back to back; recognising that they are the same idea unlocks the piece. Listen for the muted violins in the slow movement, a colour Mozart rarely used this early. A period-instrument recording with about twenty players will give you something closer to what the archbishop's musicians produced."],"mozart-symphony-31-paris":["Three movements and under twenty minutes, but the biggest orchestra Mozart had yet commanded, including clarinets, trumpets and drums for the first time in a symphony of his. It opens with a unison sweep upward from the whole band, deliberately calculated for effect. The middle movement is a graceful Andante. The finale begins with the strings alone, quiet and fast, so that the entry of the full orchestra a few bars later lands like a door being kicked open.","Written in Paris and premiered at the Concert Spirituel on 18 June 1778, during the miserable journey on which Mozart failed to find a post and his mother died in their lodgings three weeks later. Paris had the largest orchestra he had heard, and a fashion for the premier coup d'archet, a grand unison opening stroke that Mozart mocked in letters home while using it anyway. He wrote to please a market he despised.","Listen closer: there are two authentic slow movements. Joseph Legros, who ran the Concert Spirituel, found the original Andante too long and too modulating, so Mozart supplied a replacement, and both survive. Editions differ over which to play. In the finale, the quiet fugal opening for strings was a gamble: Mozart reported in a letter that the audience shushed itself in anticipation, then burst into applause at the loud entry, exactly as he had planned.","Mozart's own letters, unusually explicit about calculating audience reaction, have shaped every reading since; some hear cynical crowd-pleasing, others a young composer learning to write for a public rather than a court. Debate over the two Andantes continues, with no scholarly consensus, since Mozart seems to have preferred the second while the first is the more adventurous. Period bands have restored the sheer size of sound that made it a hit.","Listen for the opening upward rush, then read that Mozart thought it a vulgar Parisian habit; the double consciousness is part of the fun. In the finale, notice how long the quiet string passage lasts before the orchestra crashes in. If you can, hear both slow movements and decide for yourself which belongs."],"mozart-symphony-35-haffner":["Twenty minutes, four movements, brilliant and outward-facing. The first movement is built almost entirely from one idea: a theme that leaps up two octaves and stamps back down, hammered by the whole orchestra in unison and then treated in imitation, so the movement runs on a single gesture. The Andante is courtly and light. After a short, square minuet comes a finale marked to be played as fast as possible, a whirling comic-opera chase.","Begun in Salzburg in July 1782 as a serenade to celebrate the ennoblement of Sigmund Haffner the younger, son of a family friend, and written in frantic instalments while Mozart was simultaneously finishing Die Entfuhrung and preparing to marry Constanze Weber. When he wanted a symphony for a Vienna concert the following March, he asked his father to send the score back, removed the opening march and one of the two minuets, and added flutes and clarinets.","Listen closer: the seams of the conversion are still visible. The symphony has no slow introduction because a march originally preceded it, and the surviving minuet sounds like one of a pair. When the score came back from Salzburg, Mozart wrote to his father that it had positively amazed him, because he had forgotten every note of it. The flutes and clarinets appear only in the outer movements, the parts he revised.","Its origin as occasional music has always coloured judgment, with critics dividing between those who hear a serenade in symphonic clothes and those who point to the monothematic first movement as evidence of tight construction. The lost march, K. 408 No. 2, is sometimes restored in performance, which changes the proportions considerably. Tempo for the finale is contested: Mozart's instruction to play it as fast as possible is not a metronome mark.","Listen to the first movement's opening theme, then keep counting how often it returns; almost everything you hear grows from it. Save the finale for when you want speed, and compare it with the Entfuhrung overture written the same summer. A recording that takes Mozart's as-fast-as-possible instruction seriously is more exciting and more faithful than a comfortable one."],"mozart-symphony-36-linz":["About half an hour. It begins with the first slow introduction in any Mozart symphony, a solemn dotted procession that clears to reveal a bustling Allegro. The F major Andante is unusual for having trumpets and drums inside a slow movement, giving its gentle rocking motion a faint tread of ceremony. The minuet is broad and Austrian, its trio a wind serenade, and the finale runs on rapid string figures punctuated by hunting-horn calls.","Written in Linz at the beginning of November 1783 while Mozart and Constanze were breaking their journey home from Salzburg, where he had presented his wife to a father who never fully accepted the marriage. Old Count Thun offered a concert at four days' notice and Mozart, having no symphony with him, wrote one at breakneck speed. It was performed on 4 November, probably within a week of the first note being set down.","Listen closer: the slow introduction is a debt to Haydn, whose London symphonies were still to come but whose earlier practice Mozart had absorbed, and it changes the whole balance of the piece by making the fast music an answer to a question. Notice the trumpets and timpani quietly present in the Andante, an effect so rare that many listeners register only a strange weight without identifying its source.","The four-day composition story, documented in Mozart's own letter, has sometimes been used to patronise the work as hasty, and sometimes to marvel at fluency; neither reading engages much with the music. Recent scholarship notes that Mozart may have reused material drafted earlier, which would soften the legend. Its standing has risen with the recognition that it opens the sequence of late symphonies rather than closing the early ones.","Begin with the slow introduction and notice the moment the music tips into speed. In the Andante, listen specifically for the drums, which most listeners feel rather than hear. Then compare the opening with Haydn's London symphony, which uses the same device more grandly a decade later, to hear an idea being passed between two friends."],"mozart-symphony-38-prague":["Three movements only, with no minuet, and about half an hour long. The slow introduction is the longest and darkest Mozart wrote, wandering through minor keys before the Allegro arrives with a quiet, syncopated theme that never stops being worked over. The G major Andante is restless rather than restful, full of chromatic sighs. The finale is a headlong comic chase whose main theme is close kin to a duet from Figaro, written the same year.","Entered in Mozart's catalogue on 6 December 1786 and performed in Prague on 19 January 1787, during the visit when the city's enthusiasm for Figaro made him briefly a popular hero and led to the commission for Don Giovanni. Prague's orchestra was strong and its audience less conservative than Vienna's; Mozart wrote to a friend that people there talked of nothing but Figaro. The Vienna concert market, meanwhile, had all but dried up.","Listen closer: the finale's opening theme is almost the tune of Susanna and Cherubino's panicked duet Aprite, presto aprite from Figaro's second act, so the symphony ends by waving at the opera that made the city love him. The first movement is the most densely contrapuntal in any Mozart symphony except the Jupiter, with the main theme's accompaniment figure detaching itself and becoming the subject of long imitative passages.","The missing minuet has prompted centuries of speculation: that it was lost, that Prague preferred three movements, that Mozart wanted an Italian-overture shape. No evidence settles it. Nineteenth-century critics found the work austere; modern writers treat the first movement as one of the summits of Classical symphonic argument. Its relationship to Figaro, once dismissed as coincidence, is now generally accepted as deliberate.","Start with the slow introduction and let its length work on you; the pay-off is the moment the fast music finally appears. Then listen to the finale immediately after the Figaro duet Aprite, presto aprite and the joke becomes obvious. Look for a recording that observes the first movement's repeat, since the movement's scale is much of its point."],"mozart-symphony-39-e-flat":["First of the final three symphonies, half an hour, and the only one with clarinets instead of oboes, which gives it a warm, slightly veiled sound. A grand dotted introduction with scales crashing downward gives way to a lilting Allegro in triple time. The Andante is calm until it is interrupted twice by sudden violence. The minuet is broad and public; its trio is a clarinet Landler, a country dance. The finale spins from one scampering phrase without a second theme.","Dated 26 June 1788 in Mozart's catalogue, the first of three symphonies finished in about six weeks that summer, while he was moving to cheaper lodgings in the Alsergrund suburb and writing begging letters to his fellow Freemason Michael Puchberg. His infant daughter Theresia died that month. Whether the three symphonies were written for a specific concert series that may never have taken place remains unknown; no documented performance in his lifetime survives.","Listen closer: the absence of oboes is the key decision. Clarinets in their low register give the E-flat chords a woody thickness that connects the symphony to Masonic music, where E-flat and pairs of clarinets or basset horns carried ritual meaning for Vienna's lodges. The finale is monothematic, so once the opening flourish has gone by you have heard all the material there is; everything after is transformation.","Long the least played of the last three, partly because it lacks the pathos of the G minor and the fireworks of the Jupiter. Robert Schumann's description of it as Grecian and light-limbed set a tone that stuck. Scholars now question the romantic story that the three symphonies form a deliberate trilogy or a summing-up; the evidence for any unifying plan is circumstantial, and Mozart may simply have been stocking a portfolio.","Listen to the trio of the minuet first, where a single clarinet plays a rustic dance tune over a drone; it is the most immediately lovable minute. Then go back for the introduction, and notice how heavy and slow it is compared with everything that follows. Compare the sound with the G minor symphony of the same summer, which uses oboes and no clarinets in its first version."],"mozart-symphony-41-jupiter":["His last symphony and largest, around thirty-five minutes. The first movement alternates military fanfares with a tender, almost apologetic answering phrase, and drops a jaunty tune from one of his own comic arias into the middle of the argument. The F major Andante is muted and clouded by sudden minor-key shadows. After a chromatic minuet comes the finale, which takes five separate melodic ideas and, in its last two minutes, combines all five at once in five-part counterpoint.","Dated 10 August 1788, completed within weeks of the E-flat and G minor symphonies, while Mozart was in serious debt and writing to Puchberg for loans. Vienna's aristocratic concert life had contracted sharply with the Turkish war; the emperor's money and attention were elsewhere. No performance during Mozart's lifetime is documented, though he may have played it in Frankfurt in 1790. The nickname came later, probably from the London impresario Johann Peter Salomon.","Listen closer: the finale's first four notes are an old plainsong-derived figure Mozart had used since childhood and which appears in Haydn and in church music across Catholic Europe. He turns this scrap of common property into the subject of the most elaborate combination in Classical music. In the first movement, the cheerful interloping tune is quoted from his own concert aria Un bacio di mano, written for another composer's opera that spring.","Nineteenth-century writers made the finale a symbol of order and reason, and Brahms kept a copy of its final pages on his wall. Twentieth-century analysts established that the five-theme combination is not a fugue in the strict sense but a sonata-form coda saturated with invertible counterpoint. The romantic reading of the last three symphonies as a spiritual testament has been steadily dismantled; the finale's achievement has not.","Go to the last two minutes of the finale first, listen twice, then start the symphony from the beginning knowing where it ends. In the first movement, wait for the little comic-opera tune that does not belong and enjoy how seriously it is later taken. A large modern orchestra makes the finale sound grand; a period band makes the five strands audible, which matters more."],"mozart-idomeneo":["A three-act opera seria of about three hours. Idomeneo, king of Crete, vows in a storm to sacrifice the first person he meets ashore and finds it is his son Idamante. The score is dominated by the chorus, which represents the terrified Cretan people, and by orchestral storms and marches that link the numbers into continuous scenes. The heart of the opera is a quartet in which four characters sing entirely different thoughts at once and none of them resolves.","Commissioned for the Munich court carnival and premiered at the Residenztheater on 29 January 1781, when Mozart was twenty-four and briefly free of Salzburg. Munich had the Mannheim orchestra, the finest in Europe, newly relocated, and clarinets and a large wind section to write for. The librettist, the Salzburg cleric Giambattista Varesco, adapted a French text; Mozart's letters to his father are a running battle to cut Varesco's verbosity.","Listen closer: the voice of the oracle at the end is accompanied by trombones, an instrument then reserved for church and the supernatural, and Mozart wrote four successive versions, each shorter than the last, before settling on the terse one. Elettra's final rage aria is often cut in performance, which removes the opera's most unhinged music. The tenor Anton Raaff was sixty-five and Mozart tailored the title role to what an old voice could still do.","It vanished after Munich; Mozart revised it for a Vienna concert performance in 1786, recasting Idamante for tenor, and the two versions remain in competition, with most modern stagings using the original castrato role for a mezzo. Nineteenth-century opera houses ignored it as an obsolete genre. Its return dates from the mid-twentieth century, when conductors including Fritz Busch and later period specialists argued it is the first opera seria to survive as drama rather than as a vehicle.","Start with the quartet Andro ramingo e solo, which Mozart himself thought his best work in the score, then the storm choruses that frame the acts. Notice how rarely the music stops for applause; scenes run on. Choose a recording that includes Elettra's last aria and states clearly which version it uses, since the differences are substantial."],"mozart-entfuhrung-aus-dem-serail":["A Singspiel in three acts, roughly two and a half hours, with spoken dialogue between the numbers. Belmonte sets out to rescue Konstanze from the household of Pasha Selim; the plot is thwarted, and the Pasha unexpectedly forgives everyone. The score is loud with Turkish colour, piccolo, cymbals, triangle and bass drum, in the overture and choruses. Konstanze's Martern aller Arten is a defiant aria with four instrumental soloists, and Osmin's rages are the best comic bass writing of the century.","Premiered at the Vienna Burgtheater on 16 July 1782 under Joseph II's short-lived project for a German-language national opera. Mozart had arrived in Vienna the previous year after his violent break with Archbishop Colloredo, and married Constanze Weber, whose name the heroine shares, three weeks after the premiere. Vienna had been besieged by Ottoman armies within living memory, and Turkish subjects were both fashionable and safely historical.","Listen closer: Osmin's great rage aria begins securely in one key and, as his fury tips into incoherence, lurches into a completely unrelated one, a musical depiction of losing control that Mozart described in a letter to his father. The Pasha, the opera's moral centre, never sings a note; he is a speaking role. Martern aller Arten opens with an orchestral introduction so long that staging it has defeated directors for two centuries.","The emperor is said to have told Mozart the opera had very many notes, to which the composer reportedly replied exactly as many as are necessary; the anecdote is late and probably embroidered. It was Mozart's greatest popular success in his lifetime and spread across the German-speaking stage within a few years. Modern productions struggle with the plot's exoticism, and many rewrite or heavily cut the spoken dialogue.","Start with the overture, which gives you the Turkish percussion in ninety seconds, then Osmin's rage aria for the comedy and Martern aller Arten for the defiance. Notice how much of the drama happens in spoken words, which means the dialogue version you hear matters. Recordings differ wildly in how much dialogue they keep."],"mozart-le-nozze-di-figaro":["Four acts, about three hours, covering one chaotic day in a Spanish count's household as the servant Figaro tries to marry Susanna before his master can exercise an old feudal claim on her. The engine of the piece is the ensemble: the second-act finale runs for some twenty unbroken minutes, adding one character at a time until seven are singing. Aria after aria is short, fast and interrupted, and the orchestra comments continuously on what the singers will not say.","Premiered at the Vienna Burgtheater on 1 May 1786. Lorenzo Da Ponte adapted Beaumarchais's play, which Joseph II had banned from the Vienna stage as politically inflammatory, and persuaded the emperor that the opera version had removed the sedition. Much of it survives anyway in the servants' refusal to be treated as property. Vienna gave it a respectful run; Prague made it a sensation later that year, which changed Mozart's fortunes for a season.","Listen closer: the Countess's forgiveness of her husband, four bars near the very end, is the point the whole opera has been building toward, and the music that carries it has not been heard before. Notice how Mozart uses key to organise the plot, with the second-act finale sliding downward through a chain of keys as the situation deteriorates. Cherubino's aria Voi che sapete is accompanied by pizzicato strings imitating Susanna's guitar, a stage prop made audible.","It has never left the repertory and has been used as evidence for almost every position: revolutionary tract, Enlightenment comedy of forgiveness, study in class and sexual power. Twentieth-century productions moved it steadily from farce toward something bleaker. Scholarly editions have restored numbers cut in Mozart's own revivals, including two arias for Marcellina and Basilio in Act IV that most houses still omit for length.","Listen to the whole second-act finale in one go, with a synopsis to hand, and count the characters accumulating; it is the best twenty minutes of ensemble writing before Verdi. Then find the last three minutes of Act IV for the Countess's pardon. Compare a period-instrument recording with an older studio version to hear how much faster and lighter the comedy has become."],"mozart-don-giovanni":["Two acts, about three hours, labelled a jolly drama. It opens with a murder and ends with the murdered man's statue dragging his killer down to hell, and everything between is comedy. The overture begins with the terrifying D minor chords that will return at the end. Act I closes with a ball at which three onstage bands play three different dances in three different metres simultaneously, a minuet for the nobility, a country dance and a waltz, layered so that the classes literally cannot keep step.","Commissioned after Prague's rapture over Figaro and premiered at the Estates Theatre there on 29 October 1787, with Da Ponte's libretto drawing on a well-worn Don Juan legend and a recent Venetian version by Bertati. Mozart's father had died that May. The story that the overture was written the night before the premiere comes from Constanze and cannot be verified; the copyists certainly received it very late. Vienna saw a revised version the following May.","Listen closer: at the Don's last supper his private band plays three tunes by other composers, popular hits of 1787 by Martin y Soler and Sarti, and then Non piu andrai from Figaro, at which Leporello remarks that he knows this one only too well. It is Mozart quoting himself as the latest fashion. The trombones return with the statue, the same church-and-supernatural instrument he had used for the oracle in Idomeneo.","E. T. A. Hoffmann's 1813 story recast the Don as a demonic romantic seeker and set the interpretive agenda for a century. The main practical dispute is the ending: Vienna's 1788 revision reordered numbers, added two arias and, in some sources, dropped the final moralising sextet, so the opera can end in flames or in a shrug. Modern productions choose, and the choice determines whether the work is a tragedy or a comedy.","Listen to the overture and then jump straight to the final scene to hear the same music return with the statue. After that, the Act I ball finale rewards headphones: try to follow one of the three dance bands at a time. Compare two recordings that make different decisions about the final sextet."],"mozart-cosi-fan-tutte":["Two acts, about three hours, and the most symmetrical opera ever written: two sisters, two officers, a cynical philosopher and a scheming maid. The men pretend to leave for war, return in disguise, and each seduces the other's fiancee on a bet. Almost everything is scored for pairs, two sopranos in thirds, two clarinets shadowing them, and the score's beauty runs directly counter to the plot's cruelty. The farewell trio Soave sia il vento is the still centre.","Premiered at the Vienna Burgtheater on 26 January 1790, the third and last Da Ponte collaboration, reportedly commissioned by Joseph II. The emperor died six weeks later and the theatres closed, cutting the run short. Mozart was deep in debt and had written little for the stage in two years. The libretto is original rather than adapted, which is unusual for Da Ponte, and its philosophical framing owes something to Enlightenment debates about whether virtue can be tested.","Listen closer: the title phrase, meaning all women behave like this, is sung to a short motto that first appears in the overture and returns at the moment of proof, so the opera's thesis is planted before a word is spoken. Fiordiligi's Come scoglio parodies old-fashioned opera seria with absurdly wide leaps, mocking her own steadfastness. Nothing in the final ensemble tells you which couples end up together, and the music deliberately withholds the answer.","The nineteenth century found the plot immoral and the music too good for it; Beethoven and Wagner both objected, and houses performed it with rewritten librettos to make the story respectable. Rehabilitation came in the twentieth century, first through Richard Strauss and Thomas Beecham, then through directors who read it as a study in how people discover what they are capable of. It is now often considered the subtlest of the three Da Ponte operas.","Start with the trio Soave sia il vento, three voices and shimmering strings, and note that two of the singers know it is a fraud. Then Fiordiligi's Come scoglio for comedy. Listen to how often the clarinets double the sisters. A recording that keeps the recitatives complete is worth the extra length, because the plot's cruelty lives there."],"mozart-die-zauberflote":["A Singspiel in two acts with spoken dialogue, running under three hours. A prince and a bird-catcher set out to rescue a princess from a supposed villain, discover that the villain is a sage and the grieving mother a tyrant, and undergo trials of fire and water. The musical range is deliberately extreme: folk-song for the bird-catcher, coloratura at the top of the soprano range for the Queen of the Night, solemn wind chorales for the priests, and a chorale-prelude for two armoured men.","Written for Emanuel Schikaneder's suburban Theater auf der Wieden and premiered on 30 September 1791, with Schikaneder himself as Papageno and Mozart conducting. This was popular theatre with machinery and animals, not a court house. Both men were Freemasons, and the trials, the three chords, the emphasis on wisdom over vengeance all draw on lodge ritual at a moment when Austrian Freemasonry was under imperial pressure. Mozart died nine weeks after the premiere.","Listen closer: the two armoured men sing their warning over a strict Lutheran chorale melody, treated as an old-fashioned church piece with the tune in long notes while the orchestra weaves counterpoint around it, the most severe music in any Mozart opera. Groups of three run everywhere, three chords, three ladies, three boys, three flats in the key signature. Papageno's magic bells are a keyed glockenspiel that Mozart himself played, mischievously, at one performance.","The libretto's apparent change of villain halfway through has been explained as a hasty rewrite, as deliberate misdirection, and as Masonic allegory; no explanation is agreed. Its treatment of Monostatos and of women has become the central problem for modern staging, and productions increasingly cut or rewrite dialogue. Goethe planned a sequel; Bergman filmed it; it is among the most performed operas in the world.","Listen to Papageno's entrance song and the Queen of the Night's second aria back to back to grasp the deliberate collision of registers. Then find the armoured men's scene in Act II, which almost nobody expects. A recording with a light, quick approach and clear spoken dialogue suits the piece better than a heavy one."],"mozart-la-clemenza-di-tito":["Two acts, about two and a quarter hours. The Roman emperor Titus is betrayed by his closest friend Sextus, who has been manipulated into an assassination plot by the woman he loves, and pardons him. The score is leaner than the Da Ponte operas, with short arias and a first-act finale in which the Capitol burns offstage while the chorus sings from a distance. Two arias carry elaborate solo clarinet and basset horn parts that duet with the singer.","Commissioned at short notice for the coronation of Leopold II as king of Bohemia and premiered at the Prague Estates Theatre on 6 September 1791, three weeks before Die Zauberflote. Mozart adapted Metastasio's fifty-year-old libretto with the poet Caterino Mazzola, who compressed it heavily and added ensembles. He was working on the Requiem at the same time; the simple recitatives were probably written by his assistant Franz Xaver Sussmayr.","Listen closer: the two great arias were written for the clarinettist Anton Stadler, who travelled to Prague, and use his basset clarinet and basset horn, instruments with extra low notes that no ordinary clarinet can play, so the obbligato descends below where the singer can go. In the finale of Act I the chorus is placed offstage and marked to sound as if from far away, an early instance of music used as stage distance.","The empress reportedly dismissed it as German rubbish, and the work was long treated as a hasty commission written by a dying man with help. Scholarship has largely reversed this: the compression of Metastasio into ensembles was a real reform of opera seria, and the recitatives, not the arias, are the delegated portion. Performances collapsed in the nineteenth century and revived strongly after the 1960s, aided by singers willing to take the coloratura.","Start with Sextus's aria Parto, parto, where a clarinet argues with the singer for several minutes, then Vitellia's Non piu di fiori with its basset horn. Notice how short most numbers are compared with Figaro. Look for a recording using period basset instruments, since the low notes are the whole point and modern substitutes lose them."]});